window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t": "考試攻略 Exam Strategy", "u": "ch0.html", "p": "Strategy", "x": "Chapter 0 — 考試攻略（Exam Strategy） 🗓️ COMP3511 Midterm：29-Oct-2026（Thu）8:00 PM – 10:00 PM（2 小時） 📍 L1 → LTD｜L2 → LSKG012｜L3 → LTB（唔好同 lab section LAx 搞亂！唔記得 → Canvas > People） 📚 Coverage：Chapters 1–6（up to Synchronization Tools） 📖 Open-book, open-notes（printed materials）；calculator OK（唔准手機/平板）；自備 rough work sheets（booklet 雙面）；答案寫喺 booklet 指定位置。 💻 可能有 PA / lab API coding 題（fork, exec, wait, pipe, dup, open/read/write/close, pthread, semaphore）→ 印埋 lab notes / 本 cheat book 嘅 API 章。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. 試卷結構預測（根據 4 份 past papers）", "u": "ch0.html#s1", "p": "Strategy", "x": "Section S26（今個 instructor 出） F20 F19 Sp21 預測 F26 MC 25 × 1 = 25 15 × 1 — 30 × 1.5 = 45 ~25 分 Short questions 16（race condition、caching、custom policy、swap spinlock） 20（microkernel、linker/loader、heavyweight、SMP queues、MLFQ） 10 + 部分 — ~15 分 Process & thread（fork） 24（fork tree、fork+exec cases、pipe/dup 填空） 23（output orderings、count “a”） 25 18 ~24 分 CPU scheduling 27（MLFQ、RM/EDF、dynamic priority） 27（FCFS/SJF/SRTF、2-level FQ aging、priority+RR） 40 26 ~27 分 Synchronization 8（semaphore ordering 填空） 15（race values） 25 11 ~8–15 分 🎯 S26（Spring 2026）係最近一份、由 lecturer 直接提供，格式同今次 announcement 一致（open-book、calculator、rough sheets）→ 最有參考價值 → 預期題型：25 MC + 4 short + 3 fork 題 + MLFQ + RM/EDF + custom priority + s", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. 時間分配（120 分鐘）", "u": "ch0.html#s2", "p": "Strategy", "x": "部分 建議時間 策略 掃一次全卷 3 min 圈出 calculation 題，估分 MC（25 題） 25 min 每題 ≤ 1 min；唔肯定 → 用 MC 陷阱表（每章 §「常見陷阱」）；做唔到先跳 Short questions 15 min 抄 Model answer（英文），寫 2–3 句 + 例子 fork 題 20 min 用 Ch3 §9 解題大法；畫 process tree Scheduling 35 min 用 Ch5 §13 流程；寫晒每個 boundary time；每個 avg 寫 working Synchronization 12 min 用 Ch6 §6 Check 10 min 檢查 Gantt 總時間、WT ≥ 0、MC 答案格", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. 題型 → 去邊度搵方法（Index）", "u": "ch0.html#s3", "p": "Strategy", "x": "見到… 去 \"interrupt / DMA / trap / dual mode / privileged\" Ch3 §0 \"cache / hit ratio / locality / average access time\" Ch1 §4 \"von Neumann / storage hierarchy / SMP / NUMA / multicore\" Ch1 §2, §3, §5 \"API / system call / printf / parameter passing / linker / loader\" Ch2 §3, §5 \"monolithic / layered / microkernel / LKM / hybrid\" Ch2 §7（比較表） \"process state / PCB / scheduler（long/short/medium）/ context switch\" Ch3 §2–6 \"fork() … how many processes / lines / tree / outputs\" Ch3 §9（公式表 + 例題） \"exec 成功/失敗 scenarios\" Ch3 §9.7 \"pipe / dup / open / read / write 填空\" Ch3 §11 + API 章 \"zombie / orphan / IPC / message passing / socket\" Ch3 §8, §10–12 \"thread shares / benefits / user vs kernel / models / signa", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. 答題技巧（拎分位）", "u": "ch0.html#s4", "p": "Strategy", "x": "MC：題目問 NOT / false 要特別留神；有 \"always / only / never / exactly / all\" 嘅選項通常係錯。 Short questions：英文作答；先一句定義（keywords 要出現），再一句解釋/例子。S26 race condition 4 分 = 定義 2.5 + 例子 1.5。 fork 題：寫 process tree + 數式 + 最終答案；有 partial credit（S26：tree 4 分、數目 1 分、break 1 分）。 Gantt chart：由左至右評分，錯一格後面冇分（F20 grading rule）→ 慢慢做、逐格 check；寫 每段起止時間；MLFQ 標 queue。 Average：寫 (x1+x2+…)/n = 答案（保留 2 位小數）；1 分 only if Gantt 啱。 Ties / ambiguity：寫低 assumption（e.g. \"Assume: on tie, the running process continues; new arrivals enter the RR queue before the preempted process\"）。 Response time：用 course definition（end of first run − arrival）；如有疑問可以寫一句說明。 Synchronization values：列出 每個 case 嘅 interleaving + 值；每個 distinct 值都有分。 Semaphore 填空：S2", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. 24 小時溫習計劃（Oct 28 晚 → Oct 29 8pm）", "u": "ch0.html#s5", "p": "Strategy", "x": "時間 做咩 今晚 2h 讀 Ch5 §8, §11, §13–14；用網站 Scheduler 工具做 S26 MLFQ、RM/EDF 一次 今晚 1.5h 讀 Ch3 §9；自己計 S26 Q3.1、F19 Q2.7、Sp21 Q1.2，對答案 今晚 1h Ch6 §6：F19 Q4.3、F20 Q5、F19 Q4.4、S26 Q5 明早 2h MC 練習（網站 Quiz 全部做一次；錯嘅睇解釋） 明午 2h Ch1、Ch2、Ch4 陷阱表 + short question model answers 明日 5pm 打印 cheat book（雙面、加 tab / highlighter）；收拾 calculator、rough paper、Student ID 7:30pm 去對 venue（L1 LTD / L2 LSKG012 / L3 LTB）", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. 考場必帶清單", "u": "ch0.html#s6", "p": "Strategy", "x": "[ ] Student ID card [ ] Cheat book（打印） + lecture notes / lab notes（printed） [ ] Calculator（非手機） [ ] Rough work sheets（自備！） [ ] 筆（藍/黑 + 備用）、highlighter、尺（畫 Gantt chart） [ ] 手錶（唔可以用手機睇時間）", "w": 3}, {"t": "考場一頁通 Essentials", "u": "essentials.html", "p": "Essentials", "x": "Exam-Day Essentials（考場一頁通）", "w": 1}, {"t": "E1. 必背公式卡 Formula Card", "u": "essentials.html#e1-formula-card", "p": "Essentials", "x": "主題 公式 例 Average access time [1.32] AMAT=hThit​+(1−h)Tmiss​（若畀 penalty：Tmiss​=Thit​+penalty） 0.9×2+0.1×50= 6.8 ns Amdahl's Law [4.12] speedup≤S+(1−S)/N1​；N→∞：S1​；S = serial S=0.25, N=2⇒ 1.6 Amdahl 反解 S=1−1/N1/X−1/N​ X=3, N=4⇒S=1/9 Turnaround [5.8] TAT=C−A（completion − arrival） Waiting [5.8] WT=TAT−burst（= Gantt 上嘅 gaps 總和） Response（本科） [5.12] RT=(end of FIRST run)−A（RR/MLFQ：第一個 quantum 完；non-preemptive：= TAT） RR4：P1=4, P2=7, P3=10 Exponential averaging [5.19] τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)τn​ α=.25, tn​=6, τn​=10⇒ 9 Little's formula [5.57] n=λ×W λ=3/s, n=12⇒W= 4 s RR bound [5.11] 冇 process 等多過 (n−1)q RT utilization [5.49] U=∑i​pi​ti​​；U>1⇒ 一定 miss 25/50+35/80=0.94 RM bound（課本） 保證可排：U≤N(21/N−1)：N=1 1.000、2 ", "w": 1}, {"t": "E2. 本科 Conventions（考試照用，唔確定就寫 assumption）", "u": "essentials.html#e2-conventions-assumption", "p": "Essentials", "x": "Response time = completion of the first run − arrival（slide 5.12；S26 官方答案照用）。 RR：quantum 用完 → tail；同一時刻有新 arrival → 新 arrival 先入 queue。 MLFQ：新 job → Q0 tail；用晒 quantum 仍未完 → 降一級 tail；被高 queue 新 arrival preempt → 返自己 queue 嘅 HEAD，保留剩餘 quantum（Q1）/ 剩餘 burst（Q2），唔會降級；Q0 job 唔會被 preempt；Q1 job 唔會 preempt Q2 job。 Priority：細數字 = 高 priority（除非題目話 larger = higher，例如 S26 dynamic priority）。 Ties：題目有講就跟（S26：smaller ID；dynamic priority：larger ID）；冇講：FCFS（早到先）→ 細 ID；SRTF / EDF 平手 → 唔 preempt running process。 Soft real-time：miss 咗 deadline 嘅 job 繼續跑完，但計一次 miss。 fork 題：除非另有說明，假設 printf 即時 flush、fork 全部成功。 Gantt chart：每段寫 start / process / end；idle 都要畫；MLFQ 標 queue（P1@Q0）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "E3. 最易中伏 Top Traps（MC 前睇一次）", "u": "essentials.html#e3-top-traps-mc", "p": "Essentials", "x": "❌ 錯誤講法 ✅ 正確 Main memory is nonvolatile / registers slower than memory volatile；registers 最快；main memory byte addressable von Neumann：instructions 同 data 分開存 same memory（stored-program） Speedup with N CPUs = N < N（contention / overhead） NUMA：所有 memory 同樣 latency remote memory slower VM 一定比 native 快 minor slowdown printf() 係 system call printf calls write() system call API 比 system call 快 / 可以 bypass protection 優點 = portability + hide details（convenient） Microkernel performance 好過 monolithic worse（message passing） Layered：任何 layer 可 call 任何 layer 嗰個係 LKM；layered 只 call 下層 LKM 要 recompile kernel + reboot 可以 run time load/remove Policy = how Policy = what；mechanism = how Interrupts 全部同一 priority", "w": 1}, {"t": "E4. 答題模板 Answer Templates", "u": "essentials.html#e4-answer-templates", "p": "Essentials", "x": "Short question（2–4 分）：[Definition sentence with keywords]. [Why / how — one sentence]. [Example or advantage/disadvantage]. Gantt + averages： Gantt: 0 P1 4 P2 8 P3 12 ... (每個 boundary 都寫) P | Arr | Burst | Comp | TAT=C-A | WT=TAT-B | RT=end1-A avg WT = (w1 + w2 + ... + wn) / n = x.xx avg TAT = (t1 + ... + tn) / n = x.xx Assumptions: ties -> ..., new arrival enters queue before preempted process. fork 題：Draw tree → count per level → formula → final number；output 題：constraints (wait / same process order) → list all interleavings。 Race values：Case 1 (branch …): interleaving … → c = … 逐個列。 Semaphore 填空：用 init-0 semaphore：先做者 signal(s)，後做者開頭 wait(s)；冇需要嘅 blank 寫 /* Nothing */。", "w": 1}, {"t": "解題 Playbooks", "u": "playbooks.html", "p": "Playbooks", "x": "Playbooks（解題流程 + 預先計好嘅表）", "w": 1}, {"t": "P1. fork() Playbook", "u": "playbooks.html#p1-fork-playbook", "p": "Playbooks", "x": "Step 1 — 規則 fork() 返回：child 0 / parent child-PID (>0) / fail −1（只有 parent，冇 child）。 Child 由 fork() 下一個 operation 開始，複製晒 variables（包括 loop 嘅 i）。 短路：A && B → A 係 0（child）唔做 B；A || B → A 非 0（parent）唔做 B。 return / exit / break 會停止該 process 嘅 loop；exec 成功 = 之後 code 唔執行。 Step 2 — Pattern 表（每個 iteration、所有 process 都繼續 loop） 每 iteration 每 process 變 n 次後 例 fork(); ×2 2n n=3 → 8；n=10 → 1024 fork(); fork(); ×4 4n n=3 → 64 if (fork()) fork(); ×3 3n n=3 → 27 if (fork() == 0) fork(); ×3 3n n=3 → 27（Sp21 MC8） fork() && fork(); ×3 3n parent 做兩次 fork() || fork(); ×3 3n child 做第二次 if (fork() == fork()) printf ×4 4n；lines (4n−1)/3 F22 Q3 if (fork()) {fork(); fork();} else fork(); ×6 6n Lab 3 Ex3 if (fork()) fo", "w": 1}, {"t": "P2. Pipe / dup / exec 填空 Playbook", "u": "playbooks.html#p2-pipe-dup-exec-playbook", "p": "Playbooks", "x": "要做 Code 建 pipe（fork 之前） int fd[2]; pipe(fd); → fd[0] read，fd[1] write Writer close(fd[0]); write(fd[1], &x, sizeof(x)); close(fd[1]); Reader close(fd[1]); read(fd[0], &x, sizeof(x)); close(fd[0]); stdin ← file int fin = open(name, O_RDONLY); close(0); dup(fin);（或 dup2(fin, 0)） stdout → file int fo = open(name, O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0644); dup2(fo, 1); close(fo); stdout → pipe dup2(fd[1], 1); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(...) stdin ← pipe dup2(fd[0], 0); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(...) 等指定 child waitpid(pid, &status, 0); exec 一個 program execlp(\"ls\", \"ls\", \"-l\", (char *)NULL); / execvp(argv[0], argv);（argv 尾要 NULL） 數偶數（S26） if (number % 2 == 0) cnt++;", "w": 1}, {"t": "P3. CPU Scheduling Playbook", "u": "playbooks.html#p3-cpu-scheduling-playbook", "p": "Playbooks", "x": "萬用步驟：(1) 抄表；(2) 由 t=0 起逐個 event（arrival / completion / quantum expiry / check point）；(3) 每個 event 寫 ready queue 內容；(4) 照算法揀；(5) 畫 Gantt（每個 boundary）；(6) C → TAT → WT → RT；(7) 平均連 working；(8) 檢查：最後完成時間 =∑burst+idle。 Algorithm 揀邊個 幾時重新揀 注意 FCFS 最早到 完成時 non-preemptive；convoy effect SJF 已到達嘅最短 burst 完成時 一旦開始跑到完 SRTF 最短 remaining 完成 + 每個 arrival tie → keep running RR (q) queue head 完成 / quantum 用完 新 arrival 先入 queue，再放被 preempt 嘅 Priority NP 最高 priority（細數字） 完成時 tie → FCFS Priority P 最高 priority 完成 + 高 priority arrival Priority + RR 最高 priority；同級 RR 完成 / quantum / 高 priority arrival 同級得一個就繼續跑 MLFQ 最高非空 queue 嘅 head 完成 / quantum / 高層 arrival preempted → head + 剩餘 quantum Dynamic priority 計 init+α", "w": 1}, {"t": "P4. Synchronization Playbook", "u": "playbooks.html#p4-synchronization-playbook", "p": "Playbooks", "x": "Race 可能值（F19 Q4.3 / F20 Q5 type） 將 T1 拆做 atomic steps：read for condition → read b → read a → write c（c = b + a 由左至右讀）。 T2 嘅 writes（次序固定）可以插入任何 gap。 分 branch（condition read 前 / 後寫）→ 每個 read 見新值 / 舊值 → 計 c。 列晒所有 distinct 值 + 每個值嘅 interleaving。 Semaphore 次序：每條 dependency「X 要喺 Y 前」→ semaphore s = 0：X 尾 signal(s)、Y 頭 wait(s)；多個前置 → 多個 wait。 Semaphore 序列可行性：逐字追蹤每個 semaphore 嘅值同每個 process 做到邊；任何 wait 時值必須 > 0；有 loop 嘅 process 可以重複。 Spinlock 任何 atomic instruction：while (instr(...) 返回「攞唔到」) ; + release 寫返 free 值： Instruction（return） acquire release test_and_set(&lock)（old） while (test_and_set(&lock)) ; lock = false; compare_and_swap(&lock,0,1)（old） while (compare_and_swap(&lock,0,1) != 0) ; lock = 0;", "w": 1}, {"t": "P5. 預先計好嘅表 Pre-computed Tables", "u": "playbooks.html#p5-pre-computed-tables", "p": "Playbooks", "x": "Amdahl speedup =S+(1−S)/N1​（S = serial fraction；parallel =1−S） S\\N 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 10 16 32 ∞ 0.05（par 95%） 1.90 2.73 3.48 4.17 4.80 5.38 5.93 6.90 9.14 12.55 20.00 0.10（par 90%） 1.82 2.50 3.08 3.57 4.00 4.38 4.71 5.26 6.40 7.80 10.00 0.15（par 85%） 1.74 2.31 2.76 3.13 3.43 3.68 3.90 4.26 4.92 5.66 6.67 0.19（par 81%） 1.68 2.17 2.55 2.84 3.08 3.27 3.43 3.69 4.16 4.64 5.26 0.20（par 80%） 1.67 2.14 2.50 2.78 3.00 3.18 3.33 3.57 4.00 4.44 5.00 0.25（par 75%） 1.60 2.00 2.29 2.50 2.67 2.80 2.91 3.08 3.37 3.66 4.00 0.30（par 70%） 1.54 1.88 2.11 2.27 2.40 2.50 2.58 2.70 2.91 3.11 3.33 0.35（par 65%） 1.48 1.76 1.95 2.08 2.18 2.26 2.32 2.41 2.56 2.70 2.86 0.39（par 61%） 1.44 1.69 1.84 1.95 2.03 2.10 2.14 2.22", "w": 1}, {"t": "攻略：fork / pipe 題", "u": "howto-fork.html", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "攻略：fork / pipe 題點用 cheat book 攞分 🎯 呢頁唔係教原理，係教你點「查書 → 照做 → 照寫」。所有頁數 = 印出嚟嗰本 cheat book（173 頁版）。見到 fork / pipe 題：先認類型（§1）→ 翻指定頁 → 跟步驟 → 用「答卷寫法」寫。", "w": 1}, {"t": "0. 入場前：貼 5 張 sticky notes", "u": "howto-fork.html#s0", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "貼喺 頁 用嚟 P1 fork Playbook（pattern 表 + 已驗證答案） p.14 數 process / 數 print 次數 P2 pipe / dup 填空表 p.15 pipe / dup2 / open 填空 P5 Powers 表（2n、3n、4n、6n…） p.16 唔使計次方，直接查 Ch3 §9 fork 解題大法 p.102 break / 短路 / 輸出次序 / exec XC 完整 C programs p.71 「寫 program」題直接抄改", "w": 1}, {"t": "1. 先認類型（睇題目字眼，10 秒）", "u": "howto-fork.html#s1", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "題目字眼 類型 跳去 \"How many processes are created / in total (including the parent)?\"、\"How many times is … printed?\" A 數目 §2 \"Draw the process tree\" B Process tree §3 \"List all possible outputs\"、\"Which output is (not) possible?\" C 輸出次序 §4 \"If exec() succeeds / fails…\"、\"if fork() fails…\" D exec / fail 情況 §5 Code 有 BLANK，周圍有 pipe / dup / open / read / write E pipe 填空 §6 \"Write a program…\"、\"Complete the code so that…\" F 寫 code §7 fork 同 pthread_create 一齊出現 G fork + threads §8", "w": 1}, {"t": "2. 類型 A — 數 process / 數 print（最常見）", "u": "howto-fork.html#s2", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "步驟（全部係查表，唔使理解） 抄低 loop 入面嗰段 code（for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { 呢度 }）同 n。 翻 P1 Step 2 pattern 表（p.14）：喺「每 iteration」一欄搵一模一樣嘅 code → 讀「n 次後」formula → 代 n。 次方數唔使計：翻 P5 Powers 表（p.16），行 = n，欄 = formula（2n、3n、4n、6n、2n+1−1、2(3n+1)、(4n−1)/3 都有）。 表冇 → 翻 P1 Step 3「其他已驗證答案」（p.14） 同 Ch3 §9.8（p.105）：好多係歷屆原題（S26、F19、F20、Sp21、F22、F24、S25、Lab），一樣就直接抄答案。 仍然冇 → 用「一輪法」（純機械）： 喺 rough paper 由 1 個 process 開始，只做一個 iteration：照 Ch3 §9.1 規則（p.102） 逐句行（child 拎到 0；A && B：A 係 0 就唔做 B；A || B：A 非 0 就唔做 B）。 數 k = 做完一輪仲會繼續 loop 嘅 process 數，同 s = 停咗嘅（break / return / exit / exec 成功）。 s = 0：答案 =kn（查 P5）。 s > 0：翻 Ch3 §9.3（p.103） 嗰個「active 追蹤表」，逐輪填 iteration · active · 新增 · total。 問 print 幾多次： printf 喺 loop 之後 → 次數 = 行到嗰句嘅 process", "w": 1}, {"t": "3. 類型 B — 畫 process tree", "u": "howto-fork.html#s3", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "翻 P1 Step 4（p.14）：每個 node 寫名（P0、P1、P2…），旁邊寫 佢喺第幾個 iteration（i 值）出世；child 由 fork 下一句開始、繼承 i 值繼續 loop。 由 P0 開始，一個 iteration 畫一層：每個仍然 active 嘅 node 照嗰輪 code 逐句行，邊個 process 執行 fork()，就由佢拉一條箭咀去新 child（child 再 fork 就由 child 再拉，所以會有孫）。 有 break / return → 嗰個 node 之後唔再生。例子：Ch3 §9.3（p.103）、原題 S26 Q3.1（p.143）。 自我檢查：node 總數 = §2 計到嘅 total。 答卷寫法：箭咀 parent → child，每條線標 i = 0 / 1 / 2；最底寫 \"Total: N processes\"。", "w": 1}, {"t": "4. 類型 C — 所有可能輸出 / 次序", "u": "howto-fork.html#s4", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "翻 P1 Step 5（p.14） 同 Ch3 §9.6（p.104）。 喺 rough paper：每個 process 一行，寫低佢印嘅字（同一 process 嘅次序固定）。 加約束（用箭咀連）： parent 喺 wait() 之後印嘅 → 一定喺 child（同 child 嘅子孫）所有輸出之後； fork 之前已印嘅 → 最先； read() 一條 pipe → 要等對方 write() 之後（例：X3-50（p.40））。 逐個位揀「而家可以印邊個」→ 列晒所有次序；數量可以用 Ch3 §9.6 嘅「插空位」方法計。 同類原題：F20 Q3.1（p.148）、Sp21 Q1.3（p.149）；threads 版：X4-24（p.45）。 答卷寫法：\"Assume each printf is flushed immediately.\" 然後 (1)、(2)、(3)… 逐個 output 列出；問「幾多個」就寫總數 + 點數。", "w": 1}, {"t": "5. 類型 D — exec 成功 / 失敗、fork 失敗", "u": "howto-fork.html#s5", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "翻 P1 Step 6（p.15） + Ch3 §9.7（p.105）；原題 S26 Q3.2（p.144）。 照三個情況逐個寫： (I) fork ✓、exec ✓：child 只印 exec 之前嘅嘢（exec 之後嘅 code 永遠唔行）+ parent 嘅輸出。 (II) fork ✓、exec ✗：exec 返回 −1，child 繼續行 exec 之後嘅 code（loop 入面會繼續 fork！見 X3-31（p.37））。 (III) fork ✗：冇 child，只有 pid < 0 嗰個 branch 嘅輸出。 答卷寫法：三個 bullet，每個寫「印咩 + 一句原因」（\"exec replaces the process image, so the code after it never runs\"）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "6. 類型 E — pipe / dup / open 填空", "u": "howto-fork.html#s6", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "翻 P2 表（p.15），逐個 BLANK 對「要做」一欄： BLANK 喺邊 通常填 fork() 之前 pipe(fd); 會 write 嘅 process close(fd[0]); → write(fd[1], &x, sizeof(x)); → close(fd[1]); 會 read 嘅 process close(fd[1]); → read(fd[0], &x, sizeof(x)); → close(fd[0]); 要 stdout 去 pipe / file dup2(fd[1], 1); 或 dup2(fo, 1); 要 stdin 由 pipe / file dup2(fd[0], 0); 或 close(0); dup(fin); 開檔 open(name, O_RDONLY) / open(name, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644) 點知邊個係 writer / reader：睇 BLANK 之後嗰句用 read 定 write、用邊個 fd。 兩條 pipe（雙向）→ 抄 XC-07（p.73） / 原題 Lab 4 EX1（p.153）。 交卷前 checklist： 每個 process 都 close 自己唔用嗰端（否則 reader 收唔到 EOF、永遠 block）； read / write 第二個參數係 address（&x / buf），第三個係 size（sizeof(x)）； fd[0] = read，fd[1] = write； 個 BLANK 唔使填嘢 → 寫 /* Nothing ", "w": 1}, {"t": "7. 類型 F — 叫你寫 program", "u": "howto-fork.html#s7", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "翻 XC（p.71），搵最似嘅 program 成段抄，再改字串 / 檔名 / 數目： 題目要求 抄 parent 等 child、印 exit status XC-01（p.71） 開 N 個 children / 一條 chain XC-02（p.71） / XC-03（p.71） 用 exec 行一個 command XC-04（p.71） parent → child 傳字串 XC-05（p.72） child 計完傳結果畀 parent XC-06（p.72） 雙向溝通 XC-07（p.73） cmd < in > out redirection XC-08（p.73） cmd1 | cmd2（例如 ls | wc -l） XC-09（p.73） PA1 多段 pipe XC-10（p.74） 簡單 shell / cd / Ctrl-C XC-11（p.74） named pipe (FIFO) XC-12（p.75） zombie / orphan 示範 XC-19（p.78） 答卷寫法：保留 comments（graders 睇得明 = 攞分）；記得 wait()、exec 之後 perror(...); exit(1);、close 唔用嘅 pipe ends。API 參數唔記得 → A 部 API 速查（p.164）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "8. 類型 G — fork + pthread_create 混合", "u": "howto-fork.html#s8", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "直接翻 X4-32 至 X4-36（p.47–p.48）：已解好「幾多 process / thread / 印幾多次」嘅五種寫法。規則（抄落答卷）：\"fork() in a multithreaded process duplicates only the calling thread (Linux).\"；\"Threads share globals, processes get a copy.\"（X4-34（p.47））。", "w": 1}, {"t": "9. 交卷前 30 秒 checklist", "u": "howto-fork.html#s9", "p": "fork 攻略", "x": "child 拎 0、parent 拎 child PID、失敗 −1（E3 traps，p.12）。 && / || 短路有冇計錯？ break / return 只停嗰個 process。 exec 成功 → 之後唔行。 有冇寫 assumption：\"all fork() calls succeed; printf is flushed immediately.\"", "w": 1}, {"t": "攻略：CPU scheduling 題", "u": "howto-sched.html", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "攻略：CPU scheduling 題點用 cheat book 攞分 🎯 呢頁唔係教原理，係教你點「查書 → 照做 → 照寫」。頁數 = 印出嚟嗰本 cheat book（173 頁版）。Scheduling 係最多分嘅部分（歷屆約 27 分），而且 Gantt chart 由左至右改，錯一格之後冇分（F20 改卷規則），所以每一步都照書做、逐格對。", "w": 1}, {"t": "0. 入場前：貼 6 張 sticky notes", "u": "howto-sched.html#s0", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "貼喺 頁 用嚟 E2 本科 Conventions p.12 tie、RR 新 arrival、MLFQ 規則、response time 定義 E4 答題模板 p.13 Gantt + 表 + 平均數寫法 P3 Scheduling Playbook p.15 每個 algorithm「揀邊個 / 幾時重揀」 P5 RM bound 表 p.16 RM 可唔可以排 Ch5 §13 Gantt 萬用流程 p.125 逐步 + tie-breaking Ch5 §14 歷屆計算全集 p.126 同類已解題（對答案用）", "w": 1}, {"t": "1. 先認類型", "u": "howto-sched.html#s1", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "題目字眼 跳去 FCFS / SJF / SRTF / RR / (non-)preemptive priority + \"Gantt chart\"、\"average waiting / turnaround / response time\" §2 \"priority scheduling … processes with the same priority are scheduled RR\" §3 \"multilevel feedback queue\"、Q0 / Q1 / Q2、\"aging\"、\"two-level\" §4 \"rate-monotonic\"、\"EDF\"、\"period\"、\"deadline\"、\"CPU utilization\" §5 \"predict the next CPU burst\"（α、τ）、Little's formula、context-switch overhead、RR bound §6 自訂 policy：dynamic priority（α、β）、\"least used first\"、公式 fi​ §7 冇數字：why / compare / which algorithm… 題目搜尋表 p.17 → X5（p.50） 直接抄", "w": 1}, {"t": "2. FCFS / SJF / SRTF / RR / Priority：Gantt + 平均數", "u": "howto-sched.html#s2", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "Step 0 — 先寫 assumptions（照抄 E2，p.12），放喺答案最頂： \"Assumptions: ties are broken by FCFS, then smaller process ID; in RR a newly arrived process enters the ready queue before the preempted process; response time = end of the first run − arrival time.\" Step 1 — 喺 rough paper 抄個表：P、arrival、burst（、priority）。 Step 2 — 翻 P3 表（p.15），搵你個 algorithm 嗰行，抄低兩樣嘢：「揀邊個」同「幾時重新揀」： 你個 algorithm 揀邊個 幾時重新揀 FCFS 最早到 有人完成 SJF 已經到咗嘅最短 burst 有人完成 SRTF 最短 remaining 有人完成 + 每個 arrival（平手唔 preempt） RR q queue 頭 完成 / 用完 q（用完 → 排 queue 尾） Priority NP / P 最細數字 完成 /（P：較高 priority 嘅 arrival） Step 3 — 喺時間軸點出所有 arrival 時間；之後一路行一路加 完成時間同（RR）quantum 用完時間。 Step 4 — 每個 event 時間，寫低 ready queue（照次序），再照 Step 2 揀下一個。RR：用完 q 嘅排尾；同一時刻有新 arriv", "w": 1}, {"t": "3. Priority + RR（同 priority 輪流）", "u": "howto-sched.html#s3", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "照 §2 步驟，但每個 priority level 一條 queue（細數字 = 高，E2 第 4 點）。 規則（X5-26，p.53 有完整示範）： 被更高 priority 嘅 arrival preempt → 返自己 level 嘅 queue 頭，保留剩餘 quantum； 用完 quantum → 排自己 level 嘅 queue 尾； 同一 level 得一個 process → 佢繼續行。 同類原題：S23（p.152）、F20 Q4.3（p.148）、Lab 5 L5.2（p.140）。 最常錯：preempt 完重新畀返成個 quantum（錯，書入面有標）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "4. MLFQ（每年必考）", "u": "howto-sched.html#s4", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "抄低題目規則（幾多條 queue、每條 quantum、最後一條 FCFS、有冇 aging）。題目冇講嘅 → 用 E2 第 3 點（p.12），寫做 assumption。 Rough paper 畫 P3 嘅 MLFQ 追蹤表（p.15）： t | event | Q0 | Q1 (rem q) | Q2 | running (queue, q left, burst left) 每個 event 照呢幾條（E2 第 3 點）： 新 job → Q0 尾； 用完 quantum 仲未完 → 降一級，排尾； 被高層 queue 新 arrival preempt → 返自己 queue 頭，保留剩餘 quantum，唔降級； 只係最高非空 queue 先可以行；Q0 job 唔會被新 arrival preempt。 Gantt 標 queue：0 P1@Q0 4 P2@Q0 8 P1@Q1 …（E2 第 8 點）。 表 + 平均數同 §2 一樣。 對答案用：X5-34（p.54） 完整示範（連 queue 狀態）、Ch5 §8.2 lecture example（p.121）、§8.4 checklist（p.121）；原題 S26 Q4.1（p.144）、Sp21 Q2.2（p.149）、HW2 Q3.3（p.151）；two-level + aging：F20 Q4.2（p.148）。 純文字題（\"why MLFQ\"、\"parameters of MLFQ\"、\"advantages\"）→ X5-31 至 X5-33（p.54） 直接抄。", "w": 1}, {"t": "5. RM / EDF（real-time）", "u": "howto-sched.html#s5", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "計 U：U=∑i​ti​/pi​（E1，p.12），寫晒條式。 判斷： RM：查 P5 RM bound 表（p.16）（N = 2 → 0.828、N = 3 → 0.780…）。U≤ bound → \"schedulable by RM (guaranteed)\"；U> bound → \"not guaranteed — draw the timeline\"。 EDF：U≤1（deadline = period）→ \"schedulable by EDF\"。 U>1 → \"overloaded: no algorithm can meet all deadlines\"。 要畫 timeline：照 P3「RM / EDF 追蹤」（p.15）： 每個 task 列 release 時間 0,p,2p,… 同 deadline = release + d（題目 d < p 就用 d！見 S26 Q4.2，p.144）； 逐個時間單位揀：RM = period 最短（固定）；EDF = absolute deadline 最早；平手 → 唔 preempt（E2 第 5 點）。 Deadline miss：完成時間 > deadline 就係 miss；soft real-time → 嗰個 job 繼續行完，計一次 miss（E2 第 6 點）。 答卷寫法：U 條式 → 同 bound 比較一句 → Gantt → 逐個 miss 寫「P2 misses its deadline at t = 7」。 對答案用：X5-53（p.58）（同一組 task 嘅 RM vs EDF ", "w": 1}, {"t": "6. 公式題（直接代入）", "u": "howto-sched.html#s6", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "題目 公式頁 已解示範（照格式抄） Exponential averaging（α、τ） E1 p.12、P5 權重表 p.16 X5-19（p.52）、X5-20（p.52） Little's formula n=λW E1 X5-55（p.58）（記得先轉單位） RR + context switch：efficiency / utilization — X5-13（p.51）、X5-14（p.52） RR 等待上限 (n−1)q E1 X5-11（p.51） Response time 定義 E2 第 1 點 X5-07（p.51） 答卷寫法：formula → 代數 → 答案（連 unit）。逐步寫，錯數都有 method 分。", "w": 1}, {"t": "7. 自訂 policy（dynamic priority、LUF、fi​ 公式）", "u": "howto-sched.html#s7", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "P3 表有「Dynamic priority」同「LUF」兩行（p.15）。 每個決策時間點列一個表：每個 process 嘅 priority / 用咗幾多 CPU / fi​ 值 → 照題目規則揀 → 平手照題目講（例如 S26：larger ID）。 對答案用：Ch5 §14.2 至 §14.4（p.126）；原題 S26 Q4.3（p.144）、S26 Q2.3（p.143）、F19 Q3.5（p.146）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "8. 交卷前 30 秒 checklist", "u": "howto-sched.html#s8", "p": "Scheduling 攻略", "x": "有冇寫 assumptions（tie、RR 新 arrival、response time 定義）？ Gantt 每個 boundary 有冇寫？有冇漏 idle？ 每個 process 嘅段數加埋 = burst？最後完成時間 = 總 burst + idle？ RT 係「第一段完結 − arrival」（唔係開始）？ MLFQ：preempt 之後有冇錯降級 / 錯 reset quantum？", "w": 1}, {"t": "攻略：Synchronization 題", "u": "howto-sync.html", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "攻略：Synchronization 題點用 cheat book 攞分 🎯 呢頁唔係教原理，係教你點「查書 → 照做 → 照寫」。頁數 = 印出嚟嗰本 cheat book（173 頁版）。Sync 題（歷屆約 8–15 分）幾乎全部係固定套路：可能值、semaphore 次序、輸出序列、用 atomic instruction 寫 lock、三個 requirement 判斷。", "w": 1}, {"t": "0. 入場前：貼 4 張 sticky notes", "u": "howto-sync.html#s0", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "貼喺 頁 用嚟 P4 Synchronization Playbook p.15 可能值方法、semaphore 次序規則、spinlock 表 Ch6 §6 做題方法 p.133 歷屆題型逐步示範 X6 答案庫（判斷表、填空、例題） p.60 直接抄答案 / 對答案 XC mutex / semaphore programs p.76 寫 code 題", "w": 1}, {"t": "1. 先認類型", "u": "howto-sync.html#s1", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "題目字眼 跳去 \"What are all the possible values of c / x / counter?\" §2 可能值 \"Initialize the semaphores / fill in wait() and signal() so that S1 runs before S2…\"、precedence graph §3 semaphore 次序 \"Which outputs are possible?\"、\"Is the sequence … possible?\"（有 semaphores） §4 輸出序列 \"Implement a lock using test_and_set / compare_and_swap / swap / 新 instruction\"、填空 §5 spinlock \"Does this solution satisfy mutual exclusion / progress / bounded waiting?\" §6 三個 requirements \"Write a program with a mutex / semaphores\"、producer–consumer、readers–writers §7 code 定義 / 解釋：race condition、critical section、spinlock pros/cons、semaphore vs mutex 題目搜尋表 p.17 → X6（p.60） 直接抄", "w": 1}, {"t": "2. 所有可能值（race condition）", "u": "howto-sync.html#s2", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "照 P4「Race 可能值」（p.15） 四步，全部機械式： 拆 T1 做 atomic steps：if (a > 0) = 讀 a；c = a + b = 讀 a → 讀 b → 寫 c（由左至右讀）；c = b - a = 讀 b → 讀 a → 寫 c。寫成 R1, R2, R3, W。 T2 嘅 writes 次序固定（例如 a = -1; 先，b = 10; 後）；佢哋可以插入 T1 每個空位（R1 之前、R1–R2 之間…）。 逐個插法計：每個 read 見到新值定舊值 → 行邊個 branch → 計結果。如果 T2 都寫同一個變數（例如兩邊都寫 c），最後寫嗰個贏，記得加埋呢啲 case。 收集所有唔同嘅值。 答卷寫法（E4 模板，p.13）：逐個 case 列：\"Case 1: T1 reads a = 3, then T2 runs a = −1; b = 10; T1 reads b = 10 → c = 13.\" → 最後 \"Possible values: {4, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13}\"。每個 distinct 值都有分，所以寧願多列 case。 📝 完整示範：X6-41（p.66）（6 個值，連表）。同類原題：F19 Q4.3（p.147）、F20 Q5（p.148）、Lab 6 Q7（p.154）、HW1 Q3.1（p.150）；counter++ / counter-- 版：X6-02（p.60）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "3. Semaphore 次序 / precedence graph 填空", "u": "howto-sync.html#s3", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "照 P4「Semaphore 次序」（p.15）： 畫出要求嘅次序：每個「X 要喺 Y 之前」畫一條箭咀 X → Y。 每條箭咀 = 一個 semaphore，初始值 0：X 做完之後 signal(s)；Y 開始之前 wait(s)。 一個 statement 有兩個前置 → 兩個 wait（例：X6-37，p.66）。 輪流（T1 → T2 → T3 → T1 …）→ 第一個 semaphore = 1，其餘 = 0；每個 thread wait(自己) … signal(下一個)（X6-38，p.66；Lab 6 Q1，p.153）。 「最多 k 個同時」→ 一個 semaphore = k；mutual exclusion → 1（X6-24 / X6-26，p.63）。 唔使填嘢嘅 BLANK → 寫 /* Nothing */。 答卷寫法：每個 semaphore 寫「名 = 初始值」，再逐個 BLANK 填；加一句 \"S1 signals s after it finishes; S2 waits on s before it starts, so S2 always follows S1.\" 同類原題：S26 Q5（p.144）、Lab 6 Q2（p.153）；示範 X6-25（p.64）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "4. 有 semaphores 嘅輸出序列", "u": "howto-sync.html#s4", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "「呢個序列可唔可能？」 → 照 P4「Semaphore 序列可行性」： 寫低每個 semaphore 嘅初始值。 跟住個序列逐個字：搵出邊個 process 印 → 行到嗰度之前要經過嘅 wait，當時個值一定要 > 0（否則嗰個 process 要等，序列就唔得）；signal 就 +1。 全部行得通 → possible；卡住 → impossible，寫低卡喺邊個 wait、個值係幾多。 「列出所有可能輸出」 → X6-43（p.67） 方法： 每個 process 自己嘅次序固定； 每對 signal(s) → wait(s) 加一條「之前」約束； 列晒符合約束嘅次序； 檢查會唔會 deadlock（X6-44，p.67：數 permits，追到全部人卡住為止）。 同類原題：F19 Q4.4（p.147）、Lab 6 Q4（p.153）、Lab 6 Q5（p.154）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "5. 用 atomic instruction 寫 spinlock / 填空", "u": "howto-sync.html#s5", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "讀題目畀嘅 instruction 定義，問自己：「返回咩值代表我攞到 lock？」 套 P4 spinlock 表（p.15） 嘅格式： while (instr(...) 返回「攞唔到」嘅值) ; /* spin */ /* critical section */ lock = 「free」嘅值; /* release */ 表入面已經有四隻：test_and_set、compare_and_swap、swap、compare_and_reset；新 instruction（例如 fetch_and_add）→ X6-40（p.66）。 填空版 → X6-39（p.66）；bounded-waiting TAS 填空 / 改錯 → P4「Bounded-waiting TAS 檢查點」 + X6-15 至 X6-17（p.62）。 同類原題：S26 Q2.4 swap（p.143）、Sp21 Q3.1 compare_and_reset（p.149）、Sp21 Q3.2（p.149）。 答卷寫法：code + 一句 \"The lock is acquired only when the instruction returns the old value 'free'; the release stores the free value back.\"", "w": 1}, {"t": "6. 「滿唔滿足 ME / progress / bounded waiting？」", "u": "howto-sync.html#s6", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "先翻 X6-32 判斷表（p.65）：九種常見 protocol（只用 turn、flag 先 check 後 set、flag 先 set 後 check、Peterson、Peterson 調轉、TAS、CAS、bounded-waiting TAS、非 atomic acquire）。搵到一樣嘅就抄嗰行 Yes/No + reason。 搵唔到 → 用 X6-33 至 X6-36（p.65） 嘅「反例」寫法逐個試： ME：試搵一個 interleaving 令兩個都入到 CS（兩邊都過咗 check 先改變數）→ 搵到 = violated。 Progress：試一個 process 留喺 remainder section（唔想入），另一個入唔入到？入唔到 = violated；或者兩個互相等（deadlock）= violated。 Bounded waiting：一個 process 等緊，另一個可唔可以無限次再入？可以 = violated。 定義原文：X6-06（p.60）（三個 requirements + 點樣會違反）。 答卷寫法：每個 requirement 一行：\"Mutual exclusion: satisfied, because … / violated: P0 … then P1 …, both enter.\" 有反例就寫出 interleaving（攞分關鍵）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "7. 寫 code（mutex / semaphores / classic problems）", "u": "howto-sync.html#s7", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "題目要求 抄 兩個 thread 加同一個 counter，要保證正確 XC-15（p.76） 用 semaphores 保證 T1 → T2 → T3 XC-16（p.76） Producer–consumer（bounded buffer） XC-17（p.77）、pseudo-code X6-47（p.67） Readers–writers XC-18（p.77）、X6-48（p.68）、Lab 6 版 Lab 6 Q6（p.154） Mutex 填空（pthread_mutex_lock …） Lab 6 Q3（p.153） Condition variable X6-50（p.68）", "w": 1}, {"t": "8. 交卷前 30 秒 checklist", "u": "howto-sync.html#s8", "p": "Sync 攻略", "x": "可能值：有冇由左至右讀 operands？有冇漏「T2 全部喺前 / 全部喺後」兩個極端？ Semaphore 次序：所有「之前」關係都用 init 0 嘅 semaphore？輪流嗰個係咪得第一個 = 1？ Spinlock：test_and_set / compare_and_swap 返回 old value（E3 traps，p.12）。 wait / signal 調轉或者漏咗：X6-45（p.67） 有晒每種後果。", "w": 1}, {"t": "Ch1 Introduction", "u": "ch1.html", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Chapter 1 — Introduction（引言） 📌 考試範圍提示：Ch1 主要出 MC（2–4 題） 同 short question（定義題）。最常考：caching / locality / average access time、von Neumann、storage hierarchy、SMP / multicore / NUMA、multiprogramming vs timesharing、virtualization / cloud。 ⚠️ 留意：今個 term（Fall 2026）嘅 slides 將 interrupt / DMA / dual-mode 放咗喺 Ch3 slides 3.21–3.31（舊 paper 寫 \"1.40\" 係舊版 slide number）。呢部分喺 Ch3 notes §0，但 MC 一樣會考。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. What is an Operating System?（OS 係乜？）[1.20–1.24]", "u": "ch1.html#s1", "p": "Ch1", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.1 Computer system 四個組成部分 [1.20]", "u": "ch1.html#s1-1", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Component 解釋 Users 人、機器、其他電腦 / devices Application programs 定義點樣用 system resources 去解決問題（editors, compilers, web browsers, database, games） Operating system controls and coordinates use of computing resources among various applications and users Hardware basic computing resources：CPU, memory, I/O devices", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.2 OS 嘅定義 [1.21, 1.23]", "u": "ch1.html#s1-2", "p": "Ch1", "x": "OS is a system software that acts as an intermediary between users (or application software) and computer hardware.（OS 係用戶/應用程式同硬件之間嘅中介） OS hides the complicated details of the hardware 並提供 simple and easy-to-use interface（例如你處理 photos、web pages、files，而唔係 bytes 同 blocks）→ 呢個叫 abstraction。 There is no universally accepted definition of OS. \"Everything a vendor ships when you order an operating system\" 只係一個 approximation。 兩個角色（必背）： OS is a resource allocator — manages all resources (hardware and software); decides between conflicting requests for efficient and fair resource use. OS is a control program — controls execution of programs to prevent errors and improper use of the computer. 一句總結：\"O", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.3 OS 嘅兩大目標（Chapter summary）", "u": "ch1.html#s1-3", "p": "Ch1", "x": "User convenience — provides an environment for users to execute programs in a convenient, safe, protected and efficient manner. Resource allocation — allocates resources (hardware: CPU, memory; software: signals, locks) in a fair and efficient manner. 📝 HW1 MC3 例子：「幾個 job 喺 shared computer 等運行，OS 決定邊個 job 用 CPU、memory 點分配」→ OS acts as a resource allocator。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.4 唔同環境嘅 OS [1.22]", "u": "ch1.html#s1-4", "p": "Ch1", "x": "環境 重點 Shared computers（mainframe / minicomputer） 要令所有 user 滿意 → performance vs. fairness Individual systems（workstations） dedicated resources，重 performance 多過 fairness Mobile devices resource constrained；touch screen、voice（Siri）；optimized for usability and battery life Embedded systems 好少或無 UI；OS pre-installed in ROM（冷氣機、汽車、太空船） Real-time systems strict time constraints", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.5 常見名詞 [1.24]", "u": "ch1.html#s1-5", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Kernel — \"the one program running at all times on the computer\"（核心功能）。 Middleware — a set of software frameworks providing additional services to application developers（databases, multimedia, graphics）；mobile OS（iOS, Android）好常見。 System programs — ships with the OS but not part of the kernel（例如 compilers）。 Application programs — 同 OS 無關嘅程式（App Store 啲 app）。 OS = always-running kernel + middleware frameworks + system programs.", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. Computer System Organization（電腦系統組織）[1.25–1.27]", "u": "ch1.html#s2", "p": "Ch1", "x": "One or more CPU cores 同 device controllers 經 common bus 連接，share memory。[1.25] CPUs and devices execute concurrently，compete for memory cycles through the shared bus。 每個 device controller 管一類 device，有自己嘅 local buffer；OS 用 device driver 同 controller 溝通（device driver = kernel module that controls a device）。 [1.27] 圖：processor + cache ↔ bridge/memory controller ↔ memory；PCI bus 連 graphics controller、SCSI controller、IDE disk controller、expansion bus interface（keyboard, parallel/serial port）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.1 von Neumann Architecture [1.26] ⭐（MC 常客）", "u": "ch1.html#s2-1", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Stored-program architecture：instructions and data are stored in the same memory（同一個 memory space，用 address 存取）。 CPU contains an ALU (arithmetic logic unit) and processor registers — PC (Program Counter), AC (Accumulator), MAR (Memory Address Register), MDR (Memory Data Register)。 Control unit — IR (Instruction Register) and PC。 Memory stores data and instructions（along with caches）；external mass storage = secondary storage；input and output mechanisms。 Instruction execution steps（instruction–execution cycle）： Fetch instruction（from memory/cache → 放入 IR） Decode instruction Fetch data（operands → data registers） Execute instruction Write back（if any，結果寫返 memory/cache） Programs and data must be b", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. Storage（儲存）[1.28–1.35]", "u": "ch1.html#s3", "p": "Ch1", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.1 單位 [1.28]", "u": "ch1.html#s3-1", "p": "Ch1", "x": "bit（0/1）；byte = 8 bits（大部分電腦最細嘅 convenient chunk，冇 move 1 bit 嘅 instruction，但有 move 1 byte）。 word = 一個 architecture 嘅 native unit of data（64-bit registers → 64-bit (8-byte) word）。 KB = 210 = 1,024 bytes；MB = 10242 bytes；GB = 10243；TB = 10244；PB = 10245。 廠商通常 round off（MB ≈ 1 million bytes）；networking measured in bits（Mb/s, Gb/s）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.2 Storage Hierarchy [1.29] ⭐", "u": "ch1.html#s3-2", "p": "Ch1", "x": "按 speed, cost per unit, capacity (size), volatility 分層： registers ┐ cache │ primary storage (volatile) main memory ┘ - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - nonvolatile memory (NVM) ┐ secondary storage (nonvolatile) hard-disk drives (HDD) ┘ optical disk ┐ tertiary storage magnetic tapes ┘ 上面：smaller, faster, more expensive per byte 下面：larger, slower, cheaper per byte 越近 CPU → 越快、越細、每 byte 越貴；越落 → cost per byte 減、access time 增、capacity 增。 Storage hierarchy [1.29]", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.3 Main Memory [1.30]", "u": "ch1.html#s3-3", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Main memory is the only large storage media that the CPU can access directly.（CPU 係 indirectly 存取 secondary storage） Volatile；通常係 DRAM (Dynamic Random-Access Memory)。 load / store instructions to specific addresses；byte addressable — each address refers to one byte。⭐（S26 MC3 答案） 開機第一個程式 bootstrap program 存喺 EEPROM（firmware）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.4 Secondary Storage [1.31]", "u": "ch1.html#s3-4", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Extension of main memory providing large non-volatile storage capacity。 最常見：HDDs 同 NVM devices（例如 SSD）。 Mechanical（HDD, optical disk, magnetic tape）vs Electrical（flash, SSD, FRAM, NRAM → 統稱 NVM）。 Mechanical: larger and less expensive per byte. Electrical: more costly, smaller, more reliable, and faster.", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.5 Characteristics of storage [1.34]（睇表記「Managed by」）", "u": "ch1.html#s3-5", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Level 1 registers 2 cache 3 main memory 4 SSD 5 magnetic disk Typical size < 1 KB < 16 MB < 64 GB < 1 TB < 10 TB Technology custom memory, multiple ports CMOS on/off-chip CMOS SRAM CMOS SRAM（slide 原文） flash memory magnetic disk Access time (ns) 0.25–0.5 0.5–25 80–250 25,000–50,000 5,000,000 Bandwidth (MB/s) 20,000–100,000 5,000–10,000 1,000–5,000 500 20–150 Managed by compiler hardware operating system OS OS Backed by cache main memory disk disk disk or tape Movement between levels of storage hierarchy can be explicit or implicit.（例如 cache ↔ memory 由 hardware 隱式處理；memory ↔ disk 由 OS 顯式處理）", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.6 Range of Timescales [1.35]（Jeff Dean \"Numbers Everyone Should Know\"）", "u": "ch1.html#s3-6", "p": "Ch1", "x": "L1 cache 0.5 ns；branch mispredict 5 ns；L2 cache 7 ns；mutex lock/unlock 25 ns；main memory reference 100 ns；compress 1KB 3 µs；send 2KB over 1 Gbps 20 µs；read 1MB from memory 250 µs；round trip in datacenter 500 µs；disk seek 10 ms；read 1MB from disk 20 ms；packet CA→Netherlands→CA 150 ms。", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. Caching ⭐⭐（必考 short question）[1.32–1.33]", "u": "ch1.html#s4", "p": "Ch1", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.1 Fundamental idea", "u": "ch1.html#s4-1", "p": "Ch1", "x": "📝 Model answer：The fundamental idea of caching is to temporarily copy a subset of information from a slower but larger storage to a faster but smaller storage, so that frequently used data can be accessed more quickly.（make frequently used case faster, less frequent case less dominant） Caching performed at many levels：memory、address translation、file blocks、file names、file directories、network routes… 存取時先 check cache： Hit — information is in the cache → used directly (fast) Miss — not in cache → data copied from slower storage to cache and used there Cache is usually much smaller than the storage being cached. Cache management：cache size 同 replacement policy。 Major criterion: cache hit ratio（", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.2 Average access time（公式必背）", "u": "ch1.html#s4-2", "p": "Ch1", "x": "🧮 Average Access Time（AMAT） AMAT=(Hit Ratio×Hit Time)+(Miss Ratio×Miss Time) Miss Ratio=1−Hit Ratio。 HW1 例：hit ratio 90%、hit time 2 ns、miss time 50 ns → 0.9×2+0.1×50=1.8+5= 6.8 ns。 如果題目畀 miss penalty（額外時間）而唔係 miss time：Tmiss​=Thit​+penalty → AMAT=Thit​+(1−h)×penalty（兩條公式結果一樣）。 📝 S26 Q2.2 Model answer（high hit ratio 夠唔夠？）：No. Cache performance is not judged only by cache hit ratio. Although hit ratio is a major criterion, the overall performance should be evaluated using the average access time, which depends on both the hit time and the miss time (miss penalty). A cache with a high hit ratio but a long hit time or a huge miss penalty can still perform poorly.", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.3 Locality — why caching works ⭐", "u": "ch1.html#s4-3", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Temporal locality (locality in time) — recently accessed items are likely to be accessed again in the near future. 例：loop counter i、loop 入面重複執行嘅 instructions、成日 call 嘅 function。 Spatial locality (locality in space) — items near (contiguous to) the recently accessed items are likely to be accessed shortly（data 同 program 都係）。 例：順序讀 array a[0], a[1], a[2]…；順序執行 instructions。 Without access locality（e.g. all items accessed with equal probability）, cache would never work! F20 MC1：\"Locality is essential to ___\" → Cache。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. Processor Systems（處理器系統）[1.36–1.41]", "u": "ch1.html#s5", "p": "Ch1", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.1 Single-Processor Systems [1.36]", "u": "ch1.html#s5-1", "p": "Ch1", "x": "一個 CPU，single processing core；core 執行 instructions，有 registers 存 data。 仲有 special-purpose processors（device-specific：disk controller、GPU）→ 跑 limited instruction set，do not execute instructions from user processes。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.2 Multiprocessor Systems [1.37] ⭐", "u": "ch1.html#s5-2", "p": "Ch1", "x": "而家由 mobile 到 server 都係 multiprocessor 主導。 Speed-up ratio with N processors is less than N — 因為 overhead, e.g., contention for shared resources (bus or memory)。 三大優點（必背）： Increased throughput — more computing capability Economy of scale — share other devices（I/O devices, power supplies, housings, peripherals）→ 平過多部單 CPU 機 Increased reliability — graceful degradation or fault tolerance 缺點（summary）：hardware design 同 software（parallel programming）都複雜好多。 兩類： Asymmetric multiprocessing (AMP) — master–slave：master assigns specific tasks to slaves and handles I/O。（Ch5：master 可以變 bottleneck） Symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) — each processor performs all tasks, including OS functions and user processes", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.3 SMP [1.38]", "u": "ch1.html#s5-3", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Each CPU has its own set of registers and a private (local) cache; all processors share physical memory through the system bus.", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.4 Multicore [1.39] ⭐", "u": "ch1.html#s5-4", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Multiple computing cores reside on a single physical chip. 優點：faster on-chip communication than between-chip communication；uses significantly less power（mobile/laptop 重要）。 📝 F19 Q2.5：Multicore SMP systems are faster and consume less power than systems in which each CPU has its own physical chip (on-chip communication is faster).", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.5 NUMA (Non-Uniform Memory Access) [1.40] ⭐", "u": "ch1.html#s5-5", "p": "Ch1", "x": "問題：加更多 CPU 未必 scale，因為 contention for the system bus → bottleneck。 解決：each CPU (or group of CPUs) has its own local memory accessed via a small, fast local bus；CPUs connected by a shared system interconnect；all CPUs share one physical memory address space。 Drawback: increased latency when a CPU must access remote memory across the system interconnect → 影響 scheduling and memory management（Ch5 processor affinity）。 ⚠️ HW1 MC4：「In NUMA, every CPU accesses every part of memory with exactly the same latency」→ False（Non-uniform 就係唔同 latency）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.6 名詞定義 [1.41]（MC 易混）", "u": "ch1.html#s5-6", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Term Definition CPU The hardware that executes instructions Processor A physical chip that contains one or more CPUs Core The basic computation unit of the CPU（executes instructions + registers） Multicore Multiple computing cores on a single physical processor chip Multiprocessor Including multiple processors", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. Multiprogramming & Timesharing ⭐ [1.42]", "u": "ch1.html#s6", "p": "Ch1", "x": "所有 modern OS 都有兩個特徵： Multiprogramming (batch system) — needed for efficiency。 以前一次 load 一個 program；single program cannot keep CPU or I/O devices busy at all times。 Multiprogramming organizes jobs so that the CPU always has one to execute.（一個 job 等 I/O → CPU 轉做另一個 job） Mainframe：jobs submitted remotely and queued → job scheduling 揀邊啲 load 入 memory。 Timesharing (multitasking) — logical extension of multiprogramming in which the CPU switches jobs so frequently that users can interact with each job while it is running → interactive computing。 Response time should be under a certain threshold（通常 < 1 秒）。 相關概念： A program in execution → process Several processes ready at the same time → CPU scheduli", "w": 3}, {"t": "7. Virtualization ⭐ [1.43–1.46]", "u": "ch1.html#s7", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Virtualization abstracts the hardware of a single computer into multiple different execution environments，令每個 user/program 以為自己有部 \"private computer\"。 創造 virtual machine (VM)，OS 同 applications 可以喺上面跑。 It allows an operating system to run as an application within another operating system. ⭐（F20 MC2 答案：Virtualization，唔係 Emulation） Components [1.44]： Host — underlying hardware system Virtual machine manager (VMM) or hypervisor — creates and manages virtual machines by providing an interface identical to the host Guest OS — process provided with a virtual copy of the host（通常係一個 OS） Motivations [1.43]： Server consolidation — 將多個 OS 合併到較少 hardware 上 Desktop 上跑一個 OS（Linux/macOS）但仍用到另一個平台（Windows）嘅 n", "w": 3}, {"t": "8. Cloud Computing [1.47–1.48]", "u": "ch1.html#s8", "p": "Ch1", "x": "Delivers computing, storage, and apps as a service over a network. Logical extension of virtualization（用 virtualization 作基礎）；例：Amazon EC2（pay based on usage）。 Types： Public cloud — available via Internet to anyone willing to pay Private cloud — run by a company for its own use Hybrid cloud — both public and private components Service models（MC 易考 match）： SaaS (Software as a Service) — applications via Internet（e.g. word processor） PaaS (Platform as a Service) — software stack ready for application use（e.g. database server） IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service) — servers or storage over Internet（e.g. storage for backup） MaaS — Machine learning as a Service", "w": 3}, {"t": "9. I/O Subsystem（Chapter 1 summary 提及）", "u": "ch1.html#s9", "p": "Ch1", "x": "I/O 硬件三元素：buses, device controllers, devices。 搬 data（device ↔ main memory）：CPU in programmed I/O 或者 offloaded to a DMA controller。 Device driver = kernel module that controls a device。 Kernel I/O subsystem services：I/O scheduling, buffering, caching, spooling, device reservation, error handling。 Buffering — store data temporarily while it is being transferred；Caching — faster storage holding a copy of data（Lab 2）。⭐ A buffer may hold the only existing copy of a data item, whereas a cache only holds a copy of an item that resides elsewhere. Spooling — hold output for a device that cannot accept interleaved data（e.g. printer），coordinating concurrent output。 I/O hardware（Lab 2）：port（connection p", "w": 3}, {"t": "10. 常見陷阱 & MC 速查（Traps）", "u": "ch1.html#s10", "p": "Ch1", "x": "敘述 真/假 原因 Main memory is byte addressable ✅ each address refers to one byte [1.30] Main memory is nonvolatile ❌ DRAM 係 volatile Registers are slower than main memory ❌ registers 最快 HDD is smaller than volatile memory ❌ HDD 大好多 CPU can directly access secondary storage ❌ 只可 directly access main memory Instructions and data stored in same memory (von Neumann) ✅ stored-program Speed-up with N processors = N ❌ < N due to overhead/contention Multicore uses less power than multiple single-core chips ✅ [1.39] NUMA: same latency for all memory ❌ remote memory slower SMP: each processor has private cache, shares memory ✅ [1.38] AMP: master handles I/O ✅ [1.37] Timesharing is an extension of multiprog", "w": 3}, {"t": "11. Past-paper 題目（Ch1 相關）", "u": "ch1.html#s11", "p": "Ch1", "x": "S26 MC3：storage hierarchy 正確嘅係？→ D. Main memory is byte addressable。 S26 MC4：von Neumann 核心特徵？→ B. Instructions and data stored in the same memory space, accessed by address。 S26 Q2.2：caching 基本概念 + 高 hit ratio 夠唔夠？→ 見 §4。 F19 Q1.2：temporal vs spatial locality？→ 見 §4.3。 F20 MC1：Locality is essential to → Cache。 F20 MC2：___ allows a guest OS to run as an application on a native OS → Virtualization。 Sp21 MC1：von Neumann processing unit contains a/an ___ and processor registers → ALU。 HW1 MC3 resource allocator；MC4 NUMA same latency → false；MC8 VM always faster → false；Q2.1 AMAT = 6.8 ns；Q2.2 multiprogramming vs timesharing。", "w": 3}, {"t": "12. 自我測驗（Self-test）", "u": "ch1.html#s12", "p": "Ch1", "x": "寫出 average access time 公式；hit ratio 95%、hit time 1 ns、miss time 100 ns？（答：0.95 + 5 = 5.95 ns） 點解 N 個 processors speedup < N？（contention for shared resources e.g. bus/memory, overhead） Multiprocessor 三個優點？（throughput, economy of scale, reliability/graceful degradation） NUMA 嘅 drawback？（remote memory access latency） VMM 又叫咩？做咩？（hypervisor; creates and manages VMs, interface identical to host） Cache 由邊個 manage？Main memory 呢？（hardware；OS）", "w": 3}, {"t": "Ch2 OS Structures", "u": "ch2.html", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Chapter 2 — Operating-System Structures（作業系統結構） 📌 考試範圍提示：Ch2 每年都有 3–5 題 MC + 1–2 題 short question。必考：API vs system call（點解要分開）、printf() → write()、parameter passing 三種方法、linker vs loader、monolithic / layered / microkernel / LKM / hybrid 比較（優缺點）、policy vs mechanism、OS services。 ⚠️ Dual-mode / interrupt / trap 今個 term 放咗喺 Ch3 slides（3.x），見 Ch3 notes §0。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. Operating System Services（OS 服務）[2.4–2.7]", "u": "ch2.html#s1", "p": "Ch2", "x": "OS provides an environment for execution of programs and other services to programs and users。分兩組：", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.1 對 user 有用嘅服務（helpful to the user）", "u": "ch2.html#s1-1", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Service 解釋 User interface (UI) CLI (command-line), GUI, touch-screen Program execution load a program into memory and run it; end execution normally or abnormally (indicating error) I/O operations running program may require I/O（file 或 I/O device） File-system manipulation read/write/create/delete/search files & directories, list info, permission management Communications processes exchange information（同一部機或 network）via shared memory or message passing (packets moved by the OS) Error detection errors may occur in CPU and memory, in I/O devices, in user programs；OS takes appropriate actions；debugging facilities", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.2 確保系統本身有效運作（efficient operation via resource sharing）", "u": "ch2.html#s1-2", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Service 解釋 Resource allocation 多 user/job 同時跑 → 分配 CPU cycles, memory, file storage, I/O devices Logging (accounting) keep track of which users use how much and what kinds of computer resources Protection and security Protection: ensure all access to system resources is controlled；Security: defend from outsiders → user authentication, defend external I/O devices from invalid access ⚠️ S26 MC5 陷阱：「Errors only occur in memory and I/O devices」→ False（CPU、memory、I/O devices、user programs 都可以出錯）。 ✅「Logging service should keep track of computer resources that each user uses」→ True。 Summary 補充：OS services 兩大類 —（1）enforce protection among concurrent processes（process 只可以存取自己 address space；唔可以直接存取 ha", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. User Interfaces [2.8–2.12]", "u": "ch2.html#s2", "p": "Ch2", "x": "CLI / command interpreter — allows direct command entry。 Sometimes implemented in kernel, sometimes by system programs. 有多個 flavor → shells（C shell, Bourne-Again shell (bash), Korn shell）。 Shell 嘅主要功能：(1) fetches a command from user, (2) interprets it, (3) executes it. ⭐（PA1 myshell 就係做呢樣） GUI — user-friendly desktop metaphor；icons；mouse；invented at Xerox PARC (early 1970s)，first widely used in Apple Macintosh (1984)。Windows = GUI + CLI \"command\" shell；Mac OS X = \"Aqua\" GUI + UNIX kernel；Unix/Linux = CLI + optional GUI（KDE, GNOME）。 Touchscreen — gestures, virtual keyboard, voice command（Siri）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. System Calls ⭐⭐ [2.13–2.26]", "u": "ch2.html#s3", "p": "Ch2", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.1 定義", "u": "ch2.html#s3-1", "p": "Ch2", "x": "System calls provide the programming interface to the services provided by the OS.（Sp21 MC：「___ provide an interface to the services provided by an OS」→ System calls） 通常以 high-level language (C/C++) functions 提供；certain low-level tasks written in assembly for accessing hardware。 例：cp in.txt out.txt 涉及一連串 system calls（acquire input file name、open input file、create output file、loop read/write、close、write completion message、terminate normally）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.2 API (Application Programming Interface) [2.15]", "u": "ch2.html#s3-2", "p": "Ch2", "x": "API specifies a set of functions available to application programmers, including the parameters passed to each function and the return values it may expect. The functions that make up an API invoke the actual system calls on behalf of the programmer. 例：Windows CreateProcess() invokes NTCreateProcess() system call。 Programmer 經 OS 提供嘅 library 用 API；UNIX/Linux C → libc。 三大 API：Win32 API（Windows）、POSIX API（UNIX, Linux, Mac OS X）、Java API（JVM）。 [2.16] ssize_t read(int fd, void *buf, size_t count) — fd file descriptor；buf buffer；count max bytes；return 0 = end of file；−1 = error。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.3 點解用 API 而唔直接 call system call？⭐（F19 Q1.3、S26 MC6）[2.17]", "u": "ch2.html#s3-3", "p": "Ch2", "x": "📝 Model answer：(1) Program portability — a program using an API can compile and run on any system that supports the same API, even though the implementation of system calls varies from machine to machine. (2) Hide the complex details of system calls — actual system calls are often more detailed and difficult to work with; the caller only needs to obey the API and understand what the OS will do, not how the system call is implemented. S26 MC6 答案：APIs provide a more convenient programming interface。 ❌「APIs always execute faster than system calls」、❌「APIs can bypass kernel protection」、❌「APIs eliminate the need for linkers and loaders」。 F20 MC4：「Programmers interface with ___ to obtain services f", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.4 System Call Implementation [2.18–2.19]", "u": "ch2.html#s3-4", "p": "Ch2", "x": "大部分語言嘅 run-time environment (RTE)（a set of functions built into libraries）提供 system-call interface，作為連接 OS system calls 嘅 link。 Each system call is associated with an identity number；system-call interface maintains a table indexed by these numbers。 System-call interface 做三件事：intercepts function calls in the API → invokes the necessary system call within the OS → returns the status of the system call and return value(s)。 The caller knows nothing about how a system call is implemented.（只需要 obey API） API – system call – OS relationship [2.19] [2.19 圖]：user application 呼叫 open() → (user mode) system call interface → (kernel mode) 用 index i 查 table → implementation of open() → return。 📝 HW1 Q2.4：", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.5 System Call Parameter Passing ⭐ [2.20–2.21]", "u": "ch2.html#s3-5", "p": "Ch2", "x": "三種方法（F20 MC5：All of the above）： Registers — simplest；但可能 parameters 多過 registers。 Block (table) method — parameters stored in a block/table in memory, address of the block passed in a register（Linux & Solaris 用）。 Stack method — parameters pushed onto the stack by the program and popped off by the OS。 Block and stack methods do not limit the number or length of parameters.", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.6 Types of System Calls（六類）⭐ [2.22–2.25]", "u": "ch2.html#s3-6", "p": "Ch2", "x": "類別 例子 UNIX 例 Windows 例 Process control create/terminate process, end/abort, load/execute, get/set attributes, wait for time, wait/signal event, allocate/free memory, dump memory, debugger, locks fork(), exit(), wait() CreateProcess(), ExitProcess(), WaitForSingleObject() File management create/delete, open/close, read/write/reposition, get/set attributes open(), read(), write(), close() CreateFile(), ReadFile(), WriteFile(), CloseHandle() Device management request/release device, read/write, get/set attributes, attach/detach ioctl(), read(), write() SetConsoleMode(), ReadConsole(), WriteConsole() Information maintenance get/set time/date, system data, attributes getpid(), alarm(), sleep() Ge", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.7 Standard C Library Example ⭐（S26 MC8）[2.26]", "u": "ch2.html#s3-7", "p": "Ch2", "x": "C program invoking printf() library call, which calls the write() system call. ✅ \"printf() invokes write() system call to get service from OS\" ❌ 「printf() and write() are both system calls」（printf 係 library function） ❌ 「program enters kernel mode immediately when printf() is called」（printf 喺 user mode 處理 formatting/buffering，到 write() 先 trap 入 kernel） ❌ 「write() invokes printf()」", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. System Programs（系統程式）[2.27–2.30]", "u": "ch2.html#s4", "p": "Ch2", "x": "System services / system programs / system utilities provide a convenient environment for program development and execution. 有啲只係 user interfaces to system calls；有啲複雜得多。 The view of the OS seen by most users is defined by the application and system programs, not the actual system calls.（GUI 同 CLI 用同一套 system calls，但 user 睇落好唔同） 類別： File management — create, delete, copy, rename, print, list files/directories Status information — date, time, available memory, disk space, number of users；performance, logging, debugging info File modification — text editors；search/transform file contents Programming-language support — compilers, assemblers, debuggers, interpreters Program loading and execution ", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. Linkers and Loaders ⭐⭐（F19 Q1.1、F20 Q2.2 都考過）[2.31–2.32]", "u": "ch2.html#s5", "p": "Ch2", "x": "main.c --(compiler: gcc -c main.c)--> main.o (relocatable object file) main.o + other object files + libraries --(linker: gcc -o main main.o -lm)--> main (executable file) main --(loader: ./main)--> program in memory (+ dynamically linked libraries) Source code is compiled into relocatable object files（designed to be loaded into any physical memory location）。 Linker combines these relocatable object files into a single binary executable file（along with libraries if needed, e.g. -lm math library）。 Programs reside on secondary storage as binary executables, which must be brought into memory by the loader to be executed. Relocation assigns final addresses to program parts and adjusts code & dat", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. OS Design and Implementation [2.33–2.37]", "u": "ch2.html#s6", "p": "Ch2", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.1 Design goals [2.33]", "u": "ch2.html#s6-1", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Design 受 choice of hardware, type of system（desktop, mobile, distributed, real-time）影響；先定 goals and specifications。 User goals：convenient to use, easy to learn and use, reliable, safe, fast。 System goals：easy to design, implement, maintain; flexible, reliable, error-free, efficient。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.2 Policy vs Mechanism ⭐ [2.34]", "u": "ch2.html#s6-2", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Policy: What will be done? Mechanism: How to do it? 例：A timer is a mechanism for ensuring CPU protection, but deciding how long the timer is set for a particular user is a policy decision. Separation of policy from mechanism allows flexibility if policy decisions are changed later（同一個 mechanism 可以支持「I/O-intensive 優先」或者相反嘅 policy）。 總結：goals → determine policies → implemented through mechanisms。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.3 Implementation [2.35]", "u": "ch2.html#s6-3", "p": "Ch2", "x": "早期用 assembly；而家多數用 C/C++；最底層 kernel 可能仍用 assembly。Android：system libraries C/C++，frameworks Java。 Advantages of high-level languages：easier to port to other hardware；code written faster, more compact, easier to understand and debug；compiler 進步 → recompile 就變好。 Disadvantages：reduced speed、increased storage requirements（今日唔係大問題）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.4 Examples [2.36–2.37]", "u": "ch2.html#s6-4", "p": "Ch2", "x": "MS-DOS：single-tasking；開機 invoke shell；no process created；single memory space；load program overwriting all but the kernel；program exit → shell reloaded。 FreeBSD（Unix variant）：multitasking；user login → shell；shell executes fork() to create a new process, exec() to load a new program into the process；shell waits for the process to terminate or continues with user commands（background）；需要 CPU scheduling、process coordination、memory management。", "w": 3}, {"t": "7. Operating System Structure ⭐⭐⭐（必考比較題）[2.38–2.53]", "u": "ch2.html#s7", "p": "Ch2", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.0 一覽表", "u": "ch2.html#s7-0", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Structure 核心概念 優點 缺點 例子 Simple structure 無 well-defined structure most functionality in least space interfaces/levels 唔分明；app 可直接 access I/O；no dual mode/no hardware protection MS-DOS Monolithic all kernel functionality in a single, static binary file running in a single address space performance：system-call interface overhead 好少，kernel 內部溝通快 difficult to implement, debug, maintain/modify；tightly coupled Original UNIX, Linux, Windows（largely） Layered layer 0 = hardware … layer N = user interface；每層只用下層服務 simplicity of construction and debugging；information hiding 難定義 layers；performance overhead（traverse 多層） TCP/IP networks；(pure layered OS 好少) Microkernel remove nonessential components from ", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.1 Simple Structure — MS-DOS [2.39]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-1", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Written to provide the most functionality in the least space；not carefully divided into modules。 Application programs can access I/O directly（interfaces/levels 唔分開）。 Written for Intel 8088 — no dual mode and no hardware protection。", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.2 Monolithic Structure — Original UNIX ⭐ [2.40–2.42]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-2", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Traditional UNIX kernel = everything below the system-call interface and above the physical hardware. Original UNIX: monolithic structure [2.40] Place all functionality of the kernel into a single, static binary file that runs in a single address space. UNIX = two separable parts: kernel + system programs；kernel 再分成 interfaces 同 device drivers。 Drawback：enormous functionalities combined into one level → difficult to implement, debug, and maintain。 Advantage：distinct performance advantage — very little overhead in the system-call interface, and communication within the kernel is fast. → 所以 UNIX、Linux、Windows 仍然用。 Sp21 MC：Original UNIX 採用 → Monolithic structure。 Linux [2.42]：based on UNIX；mono", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.3 Modular Design [2.43]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-3", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Monolithic = tightly coupled system（改一部分影響其他部分）。 Loosely coupled system：分成細 components，每個有 specific/limited 功能 → changes in one component affect only that component → 更自由靈活。 Layered approach 係 modular design 嘅其中一種。", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.4 Layered Approach ⭐ [2.44–2.45]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-4", "p": "Ch2", "x": "OS divided into layers, each built on top of lower layers. Bottom layer (layer 0) = hardware; highest (layer N) = user interface. 每層有 data structures + functions，可被上層調用、可調用下層。 Information hiding：上層唔需要知下層點 implement，只需知 what these operations do（interface）；每層隱藏自己嘅 data structures、operations、hardware。 Main advantage：simplicity of construction and debugging（由最底層開始逐層 debug）。 成功例子：computer networks（TCP/IP）、web applications。 Few OSes use a pure layered approach because： Significant challenges in appropriately defining layers and their functionalities Overall performance affected by the overhead of traversing multiple layers to obtain a service Trend：fewer layers with more functionality，modularized ", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.5 Microkernel ⭐⭐ [2.46–2.48]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-5", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Kernel 變大難管理 → removing all nonessential components from the kernel and implementing them as system or user-level programs in separate address spaces → much smaller kernel。 Mach（CMU, mid-1980s）；best-known：Darwin（Mac OS X/iOS，包含 Mach microkernel）。 微核心通常提供：minimal process and memory management + communication facility (IPC)。 Main function：provide communication between client programs and services running in user space through message passing. 例：app 要讀 file → 同 file server 經 microkernel exchange messages，兩者唔直接 interact。 Microkernel: services in user mode talk via message passing [2.47] Advantages： Easier to extend — new services added to user space without modifying the kernel Kernel 要改時改動較少（ke", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.6 Loadable Kernel Modules (LKMs) — Modular Approach ⭐⭐ [2.49–2.51]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-6", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Best current methodology for OS design. Kernel has a set of core components and links in additional services via modules, either at boot time or during run time. 好處：dynamic linking is preferable to adding features directly to the kernel, which would require recompiling the entire kernel every time。例：kernel 內有 CPU scheduling + memory management，file systems 經 loadable modules 加入。 Similar to layered：each kernel section has well-defined, protected interfaces；but more flexible — any module can call any other module（無上下層限制）。 Similar to microkernel：primary module 只有 core functions + 知點 load/communicate 其他 modules；but more efficient — modules do not need message passing to communicate。 Linux uses L", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.7 Hybrid Systems [2.52–2.59]", "u": "ch2.html#s7-7", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Very few OSes adopt a single strictly defined structure → hybrid systems combine approaches for performance, security, usability。 Linux：monolithic（single address space → efficient）+ modular（LKM）。 Windows：largely monolithic，但保留 microkernel 特色 — separate subsystems (personalities) running as user-mode processes；亦支援 dynamically loadable kernel modules。 macOS / iOS [2.54–2.57]：kernel environment Darwin = Mach microkernel + BSD UNIX kernel（XNU kernel，hybrid）。 Two system-call interfaces：Mach system calls (traps) + BSD system calls (POSIX)。 Mach → memory management, CPU scheduling, IPC；BSD → POSIX API, networking, file system, security。 I/O kit for device drivers；dynamically loadable modules = kern", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.8 Summary in OS Design [2.53]（原句，MC 用）", "u": "ch2.html#s7-8", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Monolithic: no structure; all functionality in a single static binary in a single address space; difficult to modify; primary benefit is efficiency. Layered: discrete layers (bottom = hardware interface, top = user interface); not ideal for OS due to performance problems and difficulty in defining layers. Microkernel: minimal kernel; most services run as user-level applications; communication via message passing. LKM: services through modules loaded and removed during runtime. Many contemporary OSes are hybrid: monolithic kernel + modules.", "w": 3}, {"t": "8. History of OS [2.60]", "u": "ch2.html#s8", "p": "Ch2", "x": "硬件貴、人平：Hand programmed (1945–55) → Batch processing (1955–65) → Multiprogramming (1965–80) 硬件平、人貴：Time sharing → Personal computer → Distributed OS → Real-time OS", "w": 3}, {"t": "9. 補充：Protection & Security（Chapter 2 summary）", "u": "ch2.html#s9", "p": "Ch2", "x": "Protection — controlling access of users/processes to resources。Access right = permission to perform operations on an object；Domain = a set of access rights；process 喺 domain 入面執行。 Security — defend system against internal/external attacks（DoS, worms, viruses）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "10. 常見陷阱 & MC 速查", "u": "ch2.html#s10", "p": "Ch2", "x": "敘述 真/假 printf() is a system call ❌（library function，calls write()） API always faster than system calls ❌ API can bypass kernel protection ❌ Parameters can be passed by registers, block/table, stack ✅（all of the above） Block & stack methods limit number of parameters ❌（do not limit） Shell may be implemented in kernel or as system program ✅ Linker loads program into memory ❌（loader 做） DLLs are loaded once and shared ✅ Microkernel has better performance than monolithic ❌ Microkernel: services communicate by message passing ✅ Microkernel easier to port and extend ✅ Layered: any layer may call any other layer ❌（LKM 先可以 any module calls any module） Layered: main advantage = simplicity of construct", "w": 3}, {"t": "11. Past-paper 題目（Ch2）", "u": "ch2.html#s11", "p": "Ch2", "x": "S26 MC5 false about OS services → \"Errors only occur in memory and I/O devices\" S26 MC6 advantage of API → more convenient programming interface S26 MC7 false about microkernel → better performance than monolithic（C 亦接受） S26 MC8 printf/write → printf() invokes write() system call S26 MC9 layered → interfaces between adjacent layers are well defined F19 Q1.1 / F20 Q2.2 linker vs loader（§5） F19 Q1.3 點解分開 API 同 system call（§3.3） F19 Q1.4 LKM vs layered（§7.6） F20 MC4 APIs；MC5 all of the above；MC6 LKMs；Q2.1 microkernel（§7.5） Sp21 MC：two modes → user mode and kernel mode；interface to OS services → system calls；original UNIX → monolithic HW1 MC5 microkernel true statement；MC6 read() path；MC7 privil", "w": 3}, {"t": "12. 自我測驗", "u": "ch2.html#s12", "p": "Ch2", "x": "寫出 system call 六大類別。 點解 block/stack parameter passing 優於 registers？（no limit on number/length） LKM 同 microkernel 有咩相似同唔同？（both: core kernel small; LKM no message passing → efficient） 舉例說明 policy vs mechanism。（timer = mechanism; how long = policy） FreeBSD shell 點樣 run 一個 program？（fork → exec → wait）", "w": 3}, {"t": "Ch3 Processes", "u": "ch3.html", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Chapter 3 — Processes（進程 / 行程） 📌 考試範圍提示：Ch3 係分數最多嘅章節之一。必考： fork() 計數 / process tree / possible outputs（每年 2–3 題，8–13 分一題） fork + exec + wait 成功/失敗情況、pipe / dup 填空（S26 Q3.3） MC：process state、PCB、context switch、schedulers、zombie/orphan、pipes（ordinary vs named）、interrupt、DMA、dual mode ⚠️ 今個 term interrupt / DMA / dual-mode 喺 Ch3 slides（3.21–3.31）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "0. Computer-System Operation：I/O、Interrupt、DMA、Dual Mode [3.21–3.31]", "u": "ch3.html#s0", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "0.1 I/O 基本運作 [3.21]", "u": "ch3.html#s0-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "I/O devices and the CPU execute concurrently and asynchronously. Each device controller is in charge of a particular device type and has a local buffer. Device controller moves data between the peripheral devices and its local buffer storage.（I/O = device ↔ controller local buffer） CPU moves data from/to main memory to/from local buffers（typically for slow devices: keyboard, mouse）。 Device controller informs the CPU that it has finished an operation by causing an interrupt. Input device：data available in local buffer；Output device：I/O operation completed。", "w": 3}, {"t": "0.2 Interrupts ⭐⭐ [3.22–3.24]", "u": "ch3.html#s0-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "An I/O device may trigger an interrupt by sending a signal to the CPU（經 interrupt-request line）。CPU handles the interrupt and then returns to the interrupted instruction。 Interrupts are widely used to handle asynchronous events — device controllers and hardware faults. Interrupt transfers control to an interrupt service routine (ISR) or interrupt handler — part of kernel code. Interrupt vector（summary）：a table/array holding the addresses of the interrupt handlers；每個 interrupt 有 unique interrupt number 作為 index。 運作（summary）：CPU 每執行完一條 instruction 就 sense interrupt-request line → 有 signal → 讀 interrupt number → 用佢做 index 入 interrupt vector → jump 去 handler。 兩條 interrupt request lines（summary）：", "w": 3}, {"t": "0.3 DMA (Direct Memory Access) ⭐⭐ [3.25–3.26]", "u": "ch3.html#s0-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Programmed I/O — CPU runs special I/O instructions to move one byte at a time between memory and slow devices（keyboard, mouse）。 DMA — for fast devices and large amounts of data：DMA controller bypasses the CPU to transfer data between I/O device and memory directly；CPU/OS 只係 initialize DMA controller。→ relieves the CPU from slow data movement。 步驟：OS writes DMA command block into memory（source and destination addresses, read or write mode, number of bytes）→ writes location of command block to DMA controller → bus mastering（DMA controller grabs bus from CPU — momentarily prevents the CPU from accessing main memory, Lab 2）→ when done, sends an interrupt to the CPU to signal completion。 Six-step ", "w": 3}, {"t": "0.4 Dual-mode Operation ⭐⭐ [3.27–3.31]", "u": "ch3.html#s0-4", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Dual-mode operation allows the OS to protect itself and other system components. User mode vs kernel mode（亦叫 supervisor / system / privileged mode）— distinguish execution of OS code and user code。 Provides the basic means of protecting the OS from errant users, and errant users from one another. Mode bit provided by hardware：user mode = 1，kernel mode = 0. ⭐ Privileged instructions — can only be executed in kernel mode，例：I/O control, timer management, interrupt management（summary：set timer value, clear memory, disable interrupts, modify device-status table, access I/O devices）。 If a privileged instruction is attempted in user mode, the hardware treats it as illegal and traps it to the OS.（HW1", "w": 3}, {"t": "0.5 Three Types of Mode Transfer（user → kernel）⭐ [3.31]", "u": "ch3.html#s0-5", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Type 性質 例子 System call user process requests a service from OS；\"like a function call but outside the user process\" exit(), write(), read() Interrupt external, asynchronous events；independent of user process timer, I/O completion Trap / Exception internal, synchronous events in the process protection violation (segmentation fault), divide by zero", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. Process Concept（進程概念）[3.4–3.8]", "u": "ch3.html#s1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.1 Four Fundamental OS Concepts [3.4]", "u": "ch3.html#s1-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Process — an instance of an executing program；consists of an address space and one or more threads of control。 Thread — a single unique execution context；fully describes program state — program counter, registers, execution flags, stack。 Address space — program 喺自己嘅 address space 執行，distinct from other programs'（logical/virtual address，Ch9–10）。 Dual-mode operation — basic protection（見 §0.4）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.2 Process 嘅組成部分 [3.5] ⭐", "u": "ch3.html#s1-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Text section — the program code Current activity — program counter, processor registers Stack — temporary data when invoking functions: function parameters, return addresses, local variables ⭐（Sp21 MC 答案：stack） Data section — global variables Heap — memory dynamically allocated during run time（malloc() / free()） Program = passive entity（executable file on disk）；Process = active entity（有 PC 指住下一條 instruction + associated resources）。 A program becomes a process when an executable file is loaded into memory（double-click icon / shell command）。 One program can be executed by multiple processes. A process can be an execution environment for other programs（例：JVM 執行 Java program）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.3 Loading a program into memory [3.7]", "u": "ch3.html#s1-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Load code and static data（initialized variables）into the address space Allocate the runtime stack；fill in main() parameters argc, argv Allocate heap（malloc() / free()） Other initialization (I/O) — UNIX：每個 process 預設有 three open file descriptors: stdin (0), stdout (1), stderr (2) ⭐", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.4 Memory Layout of a C Program [3.8]", "u": "ch3.html#s1-4", "p": "Ch3", "x": "high address ┌──────────────────────┐ │ argc, argv │ (separate section for main() params) ├──────────────────────┤ │ stack ↓ │ local vars, params, return addr │ │ │ heap ↑ │ malloc() ├──────────────────────┤ │ uninitialized data │ (bss) ┐ global data │ initialized data │ ┘ ├──────────────────────┤ low address │ text (code) │ └──────────────────────┘ Global data section is divided into (1) initialized data and (2) uninitialized data.", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. Process State（進程狀態）⭐⭐ [3.9]", "u": "ch3.html#s2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "State 意思 New process is being created — allocating resources Ready waiting to be assigned to a CPU core Running instructions are being executed on a CPU Waiting waiting for some event(s) to occur（I/O completion, signal） Terminated finished execution — deallocating resources new --admitted--> ready <--interrupt-- running --exit--> terminated | ----scheduler dispatch----> | ^ v +--I/O or event completion-- waiting <--I/O or event wait--+ 轉換（F19 Q2.1 答案）： New → Ready：admitted Ready → Running：scheduler dispatch（CPU scheduling） Running → Ready：interrupt（e.g. timer / time slice expired / preempted） Running → Waiting：I/O or event wait Waiting → Ready：I/O or event completion Running → Terminated：exi", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. Process Control Block (PCB) ⭐ [3.10, 3.13]", "u": "ch3.html#s3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "PCB（又叫 task control block）= information associated with each process（kernel data structure）： Field 內容 Process state running, waiting, etc. Program counter location of the next instruction to execute CPU registers accumulators, index registers, stack pointers, general-purpose registers, condition codes CPU-scheduling information priorities, pointers to scheduling queues, scheduling parameters Memory-management information memory allocated（page tables / segment tables） Accounting information CPU used, clock time elapsed, time limits I/O status information I/O devices allocated, list of open files 每個 process 有 unique PID (process identifier)（UNIX/Linux 係 positive integer；PID 1 = init / systemd，", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. Threads（簡介，詳見 Ch4）[3.11–3.12]", "u": "ch3.html#s4", "p": "Ch3", "x": "A thread is represented by a program counter, registers, execution flags, and a stack — part of a process. 一個 thread 喺 CPU 執行 = 佢嘅 state 喺 CPU hardware registers。 Multiple threads of a process run within the same address space (share code, data, files); each thread has its own thread state.（multicore 上可以 parallel） 例：web browser 一個 thread display、一個 thread 由 network 拎 data。 傳統 process（single thread）= heavy-weight process。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. Process Scheduling（進程排程）⭐ [3.14–3.18]", "u": "ch3.html#s5", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.1 基本", "u": "ch3.html#s5-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Objective of multiprogramming：have some process running at all times to maximize CPU utilization. Process scheduler — selects an available process (in memory) for execution on a CPU core。 Single CPU core → never more than one process running at a time. Degree of multiprogramming = number of processes currently residing in memory. ⭐（Sp21 MC） I/O-bound process — more time doing I/O；many short CPU bursts。 CPU-bound process — infrequent I/O；few very long CPU bursts。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.2 Scheduling Queues [3.15–3.16]", "u": "ch3.html#s5-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Ready queue — processes in main memory, ready and waiting to execute Wait queues — processes waiting for an event（e.g. I/O completion） Processes migrate among the various queues during their lifetime. Queueing diagram [3.16]：ready queue → CPU → 出口有：I/O request（→ I/O wait queue → I/O → ready）、time slice expired（→ ready）、create child process（→ child termination wait queue）、wait for an interrupt（→ interrupt wait queue → interrupt occurs → ready）。 Queueing diagram [3.16] Summary：the state of a process can be determined by which queue it resides on（running 嗰個例外）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.3 Schedulers ⭐⭐ [3.17–3.18]", "u": "ch3.html#s5-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Scheduler 又叫 做咩 頻率 Long-term scheduler job scheduler selects which processes should be brought into memory（from job queue, after resource allocation）；dictates the degree of multiprogramming；aims for a good process mix of I/O-bound and CPU-bound；mainframes/minicomputers 用，PC 冇 very infrequent（seconds, minutes）→ may be slow Short-term scheduler CPU scheduler selects a process from the ready queue to execute next and allocates a CPU core very frequent（milliseconds）→ must be fast Medium-term scheduler — reduces degree of multiprogramming — removes a process from memory, stores it on disk, brings it back later = swapping（swap space） 需要時 F20 MC10：「___ swaps a process out of memory and temporarily ", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. Context Switch（上下文切換）⭐⭐ [3.19–3.20]", "u": "ch3.html#s6", "p": "Ch3", "x": "When the CPU switches to another process, the OS must save the state of the old process and load the saved state of the new process via a context switch. Context of a process is represented in the PCB（or thread）：registers, program counter, stack pointer。 Context-switch time is pure overhead — the system does no useful work while switching. The more complex the OS and the PCB, the longer the context switch. 速度取決於 memory speed, number of registers to copy, special instructions (load/store all registers) → highly dependent on hardware。 Some processors provide multiple sets of registers（SUN UltraSPARC）→ context switch = changing a pointer to another register set. ⚠️ S26 MC11 ✅「During context swi", "w": 3}, {"t": "7. Process Creation（建立進程）⭐⭐⭐ [3.32–3.46]", "u": "ch3.html#s7", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.1 概念", "u": "ch3.html#s7-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Parent process creates child processes → tree of processes. 每個 process 有 unique pid。 Linux tree [3.33]：systemd (pid 1) 有三個 children：logind (8415)、python (2808)、sshd (3028)；logind → bash (8416) → ps (9298)、vim (9204)；sshd (3028) → sshd (3610) → tcsh (4005)。 Child 需要 resources（CPU time, memory, files, I/O devices）；parent 可傳 initialization data。 Resource sharing 三種選擇 [3.34]： Parent and children share (almost) all resources — fork() Children share a subset of parent's resources — clone()（Ch4） Parent and child share no resources Execution 兩種選擇： Parent continues to execute concurrently with its children（例：a.out &） Parent waits until some or all of its children have terminated（例：shell 等 a.out 完成） A", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.2 fork() ⭐⭐⭐ [3.35–3.39]", "u": "ch3.html#s7-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "fork() creates a new process that duplicates the entire address space of the parent. Both processes continue execution at the instruction after fork(). Return value（each process receives exactly one return value）[3.36–3.37]： Return value 喺邊個 process 意思 > 0 parent 值 = PID of the newly created child = 0 child 新建嘅 child < 0（−1） parent (original) fork failed，no child created，errno set All state of the parent is duplicated in the child：memory (program, data), file descriptors…；每個 process 有自己一份 program 同 data（time consuming）。 Steps to implement fork() [3.38]： Create and initialize the PCB in the kernel Create a new address space（allocate memory） Initialize the address space with a copy of the pare", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.3 Copy-on-Write (COW) & clone() [3.46]", "u": "ch3.html#s7-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Process creation in UNIX is unique — separated into two system calls: fork() and exec().（其他 OS：create new address space + read executable + start 一次過） Linux implements fork() with copy-on-write (COW) pages — parent and child share a single copy；copying is delayed until a page is written。若 fork() 後即刻 exec()，pages never need to be copied → greatly speeds up process creation。 Linux also implements fork() via clone()（more general；flags 指定 share 邊啲 resources）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.4 exec / wait / exit / signal [3.41–3.43]", "u": "ch3.html#s7-4", "p": "Ch3", "x": "exec() / execlp() — changes the program being run by the current process；creates a new process image from a regular executable file（replaces the address space；成功就唔會 return，fail 先 return −1 並繼續執行下一行）。 wait() — wait for a (child) process to finish；pid = wait(&status); 返回 terminated child 嘅 PID，status 存 exit status。 exit() — terminate the current process and free all its resources。 signal() — send a notification to another process（slide 3.41 原文）。 ⚠️ slides 前後唔一致：slide 4.31 話真正 deliver signal 畀 process 嘅 UNIX function 係 kill(pid, signal)；而實際 UNIX 嘅 signal(SIGINT, handler) 係裝 handler（PA1 就係咁用）。MC 照題目用字對返邊張 slide 作答。 經典 code [3.42]： pid = fork(); if (pid < 0) { fprintf(stderr, \"Fork Failed\"); exit", "w": 3}, {"t": "8. Process Termination（終止）⭐⭐ [3.47–3.50]", "u": "ch3.html#s8", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Process 執行完最後一句 → exit() 要求 OS 刪除佢；可以經 wait() 回傳 status value 畀 parent。 Resources (memory, open files, I/O buffers) are deallocated and reclaimed by the OS. Parent can terminate children：abort()（UNIX/Linux）、TerminateProcess()（Windows）；parent 需要知 child PID（由 fork() 返回）。理由：child exceeded allocated resources；task no longer required；parent is exiting（有啲 OS 唔准 child 冇 parent）。 Cascading termination — if a process terminates, all its children must also be terminated（initiated by OS）。 Normal termination：exit() 直接或間接被 call（C runtime library 預設 call exit()）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.1 Zombie ⭐⭐", "u": "ch3.html#s8-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "A process that has terminated, but whose parent has not yet called wait(), is a zombie process. 佢嘅 entry in the process table, PID, and PCB still exist（因為 process table entry 存住 exit status）；其他 resources 已釋放；not runnable（冇 address space）。 All processes transition into the zombie state before termination, but generally only briefly. Once the parent calls wait(), the zombie's PID, process-table entry and PCB are released. S26 MC13 ✅「A zombie process has terminated but its parent has not yet collected its status」；✗ still executing user code；✗ created when exec() fails；✗ owns no PID。", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.2 Orphan ⭐", "u": "ch3.html#s8-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "If a parent terminates without invoking wait(), its child becomes an orphan（如果冇 cascading，可能仍 runnable，最終變 zombie）。 init / systemd is assigned as the new parent and periodically calls wait() → 收集 exit status，釋放 PID 同 process-table entry。", "w": 3}, {"t": "9. fork() 解題大法 ⭐⭐⭐（考試必用）", "u": "ch3.html#s9", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.1 基本規則", "u": "ch3.html#s9-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "每 call 一次 fork()，「每個執行到嗰句嘅 process」都會變兩個：parent（return child PID > 0 → true）、child（return 0 → false）。 Child 由 fork() 下一句開始行，有 parent 所有 variables 嘅 copy（包括 loop counter i）。 n 句連續 fork()（無條件）→ 2n processes。 Short-circuit evaluation ⭐： A && B：A false (0 → child) 就唔會執行 B；A true (parent) 先執行 B。 A || B：A true (parent) 就唔會執行 B；A false (child) 先執行 B。 printf 次數 = 執行到該句 printf 嘅 process 數目（每個 printf 位置分開計）。 return / exit() / break 會令 process 停止繼續 loop 或者唔再執行後面。 exec() 成功 → 之後嘅 code 唔會執行；失敗 → return −1 繼續。", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.2 常見 pattern 公式（全部已用真 C 程式驗證 ✅）", "u": "ch3.html#s9-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Code（每個 iteration，所有 process 都繼續 loop） 每 iteration 每個 process 變成 n 次後 processes fork(); 2 2n fork(); fork(); [3.44] 4 4n（slide：10 次 → 410） if (fork()) fork(); [3.45] 3（parent 再 fork 一次） 3n（slide：310） if (fork() == 0) fork();（Sp21 MC8） 3（child 再 fork） 3n（n=3 → 27） if (fork() && fork()) .../fork() || fork() 3 3n", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.3 有 break / return 嘅 loop（追蹤「仲 active」嘅 process）", "u": "ch3.html#s9-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "S26 Q3.1： for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (fork()) fork(); // parent: fork 多一次 else break; // 第一個 child: break（停 loop） } 每個 active process 每 iteration：第一個 fork 產生 child（→ else → break，停）；parent 再 fork 一次，新 child（第二個）繼續 loop（佢喺 if branch 入面，return 0 但已經喺 if 入面）。 所以 1 個 active → 3 個 processes，其中 2 個繼續 active、1 個 stopped。 Ak​=2k（iteration k 開始前 active 數）；每 iteration 新增 2k 個 break。 Total processes =active2n​​+stopped(2n−1)​​=2n+1−1；break 次數 =2n−1。 n = 2：7 processes，3 breaks ✅；n = 3：15 processes，7 breaks ✅。 Process tree（n=2）：P0 → P1 (break), P2 → {P5 (break), P6}, P3 (break), P4… （P0 第 0 輪產生 P1(break)、P2；第 1 輪產生 P3(break)、P4；P2 第 1 輪產生 P5(break)、P6） P0 ├── P1 (break at i=0) ├── P2 (continues) │ ", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.4 Short-circuit 題", "u": "ch3.html#s9-4", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Sp21 MC7：if (fork() || fork()) { fork(); } P0：fork1 → P0 得 >0（true）→ 跳過第二個 fork → 入 if → fork → 2 個 C1（fork1 得 0）→ 執行 fork2：C1 得 >0（true）→ 入 if → fork → 2 個；C2 得 0 → false → 唔入 if → 1 個 Total = 2 + 2 + 1 = 5 ✅ F19 Q2.7：if (fork() == fork() || fork() != fork()) fork(); printf(\"Fork!\\n\"); fork() == fork()：4 個 processes，返回值組合 (x,y)：P0 (c1,c2) 唔等；C2 (c1,0) 唔等；C1 (0,c3) 唔等；C3 (0,0) 相等 → true（只有收到兩次 0 嗰個 grandchild）。 C3：|| 短路 → 執行 body fork() → 2 個 process 印。 其餘 3 個 process 執行 fork() != fork() → 每個變 4 個 = 12；每組 4 個入面 3 個 true（(c,c')、(0,c)、(c,0)），1 個 false (0,0)。 9 個 true → fork → 18；3 個 false → 3。 Total = 2 + 18 + 3 = 23 lines ✅", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.5 Nested / combined", "u": "ch3.html#s9-5", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Sp21 Q1.1： pid1 = fork(); pid2 = fork(); if (pid1 > 0) if (pid2 == 0) fork(); 兩個 fork 後 4 個 processes；只有「pid1 > 0（P0 或 P0 嘅第二個 child）且 pid2 == 0」嘅 P0 第二個 fork 生出嘅 child 符合 → 再 fork 1 次 → 5 processes ✅ Sp21 Q1.2： if (fork() == 0) { for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) if (fork() == 0) fork(); } fork(); 第一個 fork：child 入 loop；loop 5 次，每次每個 process 變 3 個 → 35=243（包括 child 自己）。 加 original parent = 243 + 1 = 244；最後 fork 全部 ×2 → (35+1)×2=488 ✅ F20 Q3.2： if (!fork()) { for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (fork()) fork(); } fork(); printf(\"a\"); 同上：child branch 變 3n，加 parent → 3n+1，最後 fork ×2 → \"a\" 數 =2(3n+1) ✅（n=1 → 8；n=2 → 20） F19 Q2.6（loop 入面 parent 同 child 都 printf）： for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) { if (fork()) printf", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.6 Possible output orderings（輸出次序題）⭐", "u": "ch3.html#s9-6", "p": "Ch3", "x": "方法： 畫 process tree，寫低每個 process 印咩、依咩次序（同一 process 內次序固定）。 加 wait() 約束：parent wait() 之後嘅 print 一定喺 child 全部 print 之後。 Fork 之前印嘅一定最先（假設 flushed）。 列出所有符合約束嘅 interleavings（topological orders）。 F20 Q3.1： if (fork() == 0) { // child C if (fork() > 0) { printf(\"1\"); wait(NULL); printf(\"2\"); } // C else printf(\"3\"); // grandchild G } else printf(\"4\"); // parent P 約束：C 印 \"1\" 然後等 G（印 \"3\"）→ 再印 \"2\"。即 \"2\" 喺 \"1\" 同 \"3\" 之後；\"4\" 隨意。 \"2\" 最後：1、3、4 任意排 = 3! = 6 種：1342, 1432, 3142, 4132, 3412, 4312 \"4\" 喺 \"2\" 之後：1324, 3124 Total = 8（unbuffered）。若 buffered（\"1\" 同 \"2\" 同一 buffer 一齊 flush）→ 只剩 4312, 3412, 3124（3 種）。 Sp21 Q1.3：printf(\"A\\n\"); if (fork()==0) printf(\"B\\n\"); else { printf(\"C\\n\"); ___A___ } printf(\"D\\n\"); (", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.7 fork + exec 成功/失敗情況（S26 Q3.2）⭐", "u": "ch3.html#s9-7", "p": "Ch3", "x": "int ret = fork(); if (ret == 0) { printf(\"Hello1\\n\"); exec(\"some_executable\"); printf(\"Hello2\\n\"); } else if (ret > 0) { wait(NULL); printf(\"Hello3\\n\"); } else { printf(\"Hello4\\n\"); } Case Output fork 成功、exec 成功 Hello1, Hello3（Hello2 唔印：image replaced） fork 成功、exec 失敗 Hello1, Hello2, Hello3（exec 返回 −1，繼續） fork 失敗 Hello4（only parent, ret < 0） 因為有 wait(NULL)，Hello3 一定喺 child 嘅輸出之後。", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.8 更多歷屆 fork 題（Lab 3 review 提供：F22 / F24 / S25，全部驗證 ✅）", "u": "ch3.html#s9-8", "p": "Ch3", "x": "題目 Code（重點） 答案 Fall 2022 Q3 for(i<n){ if (fork() == fork()) printf(...); } processes 4n；printed lines 40+41+⋯+4n−1=34n−1​ Fall 2024 P3.1 for(i<M){ pid = fork(); if(!pid) for(j<N) fork(); } child 行 line Y；M=N=1 → 3；一般 (2N+1)M Spring 2025 MC12 if (fork() || fork()) { if (fork() && !fork()) fork(); } 9（外層 2 入 if × 每個變 4 + 1 入 else） Lab 3 Ex3 for(i<n){ if(fork()){fork();fork();} else fork(); } 6n（parent 變 4 + child 變 2） 詳細解釋：Labs notes L3.7。Fall 2022 嘅 lines：每輪只有收到兩個 0 嘅 grandchild 印，第 k 輪有 4k−1 個這樣嘅 process。", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.9 Buffering 陷阱", "u": "ch3.html#s9-9", "p": "Ch3", "x": "printf 冇 \\n 或者 output 去 file/pipe → stdout buffer 未 flush 就 fork → buffer 被複製 → 重複印。 考試通常講明「stdout is flushed after each printf」或「printf not buffered」。如果冇講，可以寫一句 assumption。", "w": 3}, {"t": "10. Interprocess Communication (IPC) ⭐⭐ [3.51–3.64]", "u": "ch3.html#s10", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.1 Independent vs Cooperating", "u": "ch3.html#s10-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Cooperating processes can affect or be affected by other processes（e.g. share data）。 Reasons for cooperation：information sharing（copy & paste）、computation speedup（parallel subtasks）、modularity（+ convenience）。 需要 IPC mechanism 交換 data。兩大模型： Shared memory — a region of memory is shared；processes read/write the shared region（implicit communication；快，但要自己 synchronize） Message passing — explicit messages exchanged（useful in distributed environment；Mach OS 主要用；唔需要 share address space）", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.2 Producer–Consumer Problem（Bounded Buffer, Shared Memory）[3.53–3.56]", "u": "ch3.html#s10-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Unbounded buffer：no practical limit；bounded buffer：fixed size。 #define BUFFER_SIZE 10 item buffer[BUFFER_SIZE]; int in = 0, out = 0; /* Producer */ while (true) { while (((in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE) == out) ; /* buffer full → do nothing */ buffer[in] = next_produced; in = (in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE; } /* Consumer */ while (true) { while (in == out) ; /* buffer empty → do nothing */ next_consumed = buffer[out]; out = (out + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE; } ⭐ This solution only allows at most BUFFER_SIZE − 1 items in the buffer at the same time（因為 in == out 表示 empty，要留一格分辨 full）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.3 Message Passing [3.57–3.64]", "u": "ch3.html#s10-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Mechanism for processes to communicate and synchronize without sharing the address space. 兩個 operations：send(message)、receive(message)；message size fixed or variable。 P、Q 要通訊：establish a communication link → exchange via send/receive。 Implementation 選擇：direct or indirect；synchronous or asynchronous；automatic or explicit buffering。 Direct communication [3.59]： Processes must name each other explicitly：send(P, message)、receive(Q, message)。 Link established automatically；associated with exactly one pair；between each pair exactly one link。 Disadvantage：limited modularity — 改一個 process 嘅 identifier 要檢查所有其他 processes。 Indirect communication [3.60–3.61]： Messages sent to and received from mailboxes", "w": 3}, {"t": "11. Pipes ⭐⭐⭐ [3.65–3.69]", "u": "ch3.html#s11", "p": "Ch3", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.1 設計問題（4 個）", "u": "ch3.html#s11-1", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Unidirectional or bidirectional? If bidirectional：half-duplex（一次一個方向）or full-duplex（同時雙向）？ Must there be a relationship (parent–child)? Can pipes be used over a network, or only on the same machine?", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.2 Ordinary Pipes vs Named Pipes（必背表）", "u": "ch3.html#s11-2", "p": "Ch3", "x": "Ordinary pipe Named pipe 方向 Unidirectional（one-way） Bidirectional（UNIX FIFO：half-duplex；Windows：full-duplex） 關係 Requires parent–child relationship No parent–child relationship required 存取 Cannot be accessed from outside the process that creates it Several processes can use it（multiple writers） 生命週期 Ceases to exist after processes finish communicating and terminate Continues to exist after processes finish, until explicitly deleted 機器 Same machine UNIX：same machine；Windows：same or different machines UNIX API pipe(int fd[2])：fd[0] = read end，fd[1] = write end mkfifo()；appear as typical files in the file system（FIFOs） Windows anonymous pipes（ReadFile()/WriteFile()） CreateNamedPipe() UNIX treats", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.3 Pipe example（Parent → Child）[3.68]", "u": "ch3.html#s11-3", "p": "Ch3", "x": "int pfds[2]; char buf[30]; pipe(pfds); /* 1. create pipe BEFORE fork */ pid_t pid = fork(); /* 2. child inherits pfds[0], pfds[1] */ if (pid != 0) { /* parent = writer */ close(pfds[0]); /* close unused READ end */ write(pfds[1], \"test\", 5); wait(NULL); } else { /* child = reader */ close(pfds[1]); /* close unused WRITE end */ read(pfds[0], buf, 5); printf(\"CHILD: read \\\"%s\\\"\\n\", buf); } 📝 HW1 Q2.5 Model answer：(1) The parent calls pipe(fd) before fork() so that both processes share the same pipe (fd[0] read end, fd[1] write end). (2) Call fork(); the child inherits both file descriptors. (3) Parent (writer) closes fd[0] and writes data with write(fd[1], ...), then closes fd[1]. (4) Child (r", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.4 dup / dup2 & I/O redirection（Lab 3 / PA1）⭐⭐", "u": "ch3.html#s11-4", "p": "Ch3", "x": "File descriptor table：0 = stdin，1 = stdout，2 = stderr。 dup(oldfd) — 複製 oldfd 到 lowest-numbered unused fd，返回新 fd。 dup2(oldfd, newfd) — 令 newfd 指向 oldfd 嘅 file（如 newfd 已開會先 close）。 Input redirection 經典 pattern：close(0); dup(fin); ⟹ fd 0 變成 file → scanf 由 file 讀。等價 dup2(fin, 0); Output redirection：close(1); dup(fout); 或 dup2(fout, 1); Pipe 接 stdout → stdin（ls | wc）：writer child：dup2(fd[1], 1); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(\"ls\",...)；reader child：dup2(fd[0], 0); close(fd[1]); close(fd[0]); execlp(\"wc\",...)；parent 要 close 兩端（否則 reader 永遠收唔到 EOF）。 S26 Q3.3（填空）：child 處理 1.txt、parent 處理 2.txt，數 even numbers，child 經 pipe 傳結果： int fin = open(file_name[i], O_RDONLY); close(0); // Blank 1 dup(fin);", "w": 3}, {"t": "12. Client–Server Communication：Sockets & RPC ⭐ [3.70–3.72]", "u": "ch3.html#s12", "p": "Ch3", "x": "A socket is defined as an endpoint for communication. A pair of processes over a network uses a pair of sockets。 A socket is identified by an IP address and a port number. Server always running，listens to a well-known port；接受 request → 建立 socket connection。 Well-known ports（< 1024）：SSH 22、FTP 21、HTTP 80。 Only clients can initiate communication. Client 揀一個 port number > 1024（未用緊）。 IP：IPv4 32-bit（128.32.244.172）、IPv6 128-bit。 ⭐ The 4-tuple (source IP address, source port number, destination IP address, destination port number) uniquely determines a pair of communication.（F19 Q2.2 答案） RPC (Remote Procedure Call) — procedure-call mechanism between systems with network connections；message-based；常", "w": 3}, {"t": "13. 常見陷阱 & MC 速查", "u": "ch3.html#s13", "p": "Ch3", "x": "敘述 真/假 Program = active, process = passive ❌（program passive；process active） Stack holds function params, return addresses, local variables ✅ Heap holds global variables ❌（data section；heap = dynamic allocation） Ready → Waiting transition exists ❌ Long-term scheduler controls degree of multiprogramming ✅ Short-term scheduler invoked infrequently ❌（very frequently, ms） Medium-term scheduler does swapping ✅ Context switch = user→kernel mode switch ❌ Context-switch time is useful work ❌（pure overhead） fork() returns 0 to parent ❌（0 to child；child PID to parent） fork() fails → returns −1 to parent, no child ✅ After fork, child shares parent's variables ❌（copy；COW 只係 implementation 優化） Parent aut", "w": 3}, {"t": "14. Past-paper 題目（Ch3）", "u": "ch3.html#s14", "p": "Ch3", "x": "S26：MC1 interrupts（C）、MC2 DMA（B）、MC10 fork（C：7, X=6, Y=1）、MC11 context switch（B）、MC12 ordinary pipes false（B）、MC13 zombie（B）、MC14 leave running（A）、Q3.1 fork tree（7 / 3 / 2n+1−1）、Q3.2 fork+exec cases、Q3.3 pipe/dup 填空 F19：Q2.1 state transitions、Q2.2 socket 4-tuple、Q2.4 fork+exec、Q2.6（2n+1−2）、Q2.7（23） F20：MC3 interrupt、MC7 fork、MC8 address space（all of above）、MC9 named pipes、MC10 medium-term、Q3.1（8 outputs）、Q3.2（2(3n+1)） Sp21：MC2 interrupt、MC degree of multiprogramming、MC own address space etc.（all）、MC stack、MC short-term、MC fork incorrect（B）、MC pipes not true（C）、MC leave CPU（all）、MC7（5）、MC8（27）、Q1.1（5）、Q1.2（488）、Q1.3 outputs HW1：MC1 DMA、MC2 interrupts、MC7 privileged、MC9 fork、MC10 pipes、Q2.3 st", "w": 3}, {"t": "15. 自我測驗", "u": "ch3.html#s15", "p": "Ch3", "x": "for (i=0;i<4;i++) fork(); 有幾多個 processes？（16） fork(); fork() && fork(); fork(); 有幾多？（2 × 3 × 2 = 12：第一個 fork → 2；fork() && fork() 每個變 3；最後 ×2） Zombie 同 orphan 分別？ 點解 bounded buffer 只可以放 BUFFER_SIZE − 1 個 item？ Named pipe 喺 UNIX 叫咩？點建立？（FIFO；mkfifo()） 寫出三種 mode transfer。（system call, interrupt, trap/exception）", "w": 3}, {"t": "Ch4 Threads", "u": "ch4.html", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Chapter 4 — Threads & Concurrency（線程與並行） 📌 考試範圍提示：Ch4 通常 3–4 題 MC + 有機會 1 題 short question。必考： thread 有乜自己私有 / 乜同其他 thread 共享、benefits（4 個）、concurrency vs parallelism、data vs task parallelism、Amdahl's Law 計算、multithreading models（many-to-one / one-to-one / many-to-many）、signals（sync vs async）、thread cancellation（asynchronous vs deferred）、fork()/exec() semantics、Linux clone()、heavyweight vs lightweight。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. Motivation & Overview（動機）[4.4–4.6]", "u": "ch4.html#s1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.1 點解要 multithreading？", "u": "ch4.html#s1-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "好多 applications 本身係 multithreaded： Web browser：一個 thread display images/text，另一個 thread 由 network retrieve data。 Word processor：display graphics、respond to keystrokes、background spelling & grammar checking。 Most OS kernels are multithreaded（Linux boot 時建立多個 kernel threads：managing devices、memory management、interrupt handling）。 Take advantage of processing capabilities on multicore systems（data mining、graphics、AI）。 ⭐ Process creation is heavy-weight while thread creation is light-weight — threads of the same process share code, data and other resources。 其他例子 [4.5]：embedded systems（elevators, planes）、OS kernels、database servers、network servers（file/web server、airline reservation）、parallel prog", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.2 Thread 定義 ⭐⭐ [4.6]", "u": "ch4.html#s1-2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "A thread is a basic unit of CPU utilization — independently scheduled and run（an instance of execution）。 每個 thread 私有（per-thread）：thread ID, program counter (PC), register set, stack 同一 process 嘅 threads 共享（shared）：code (text), data (global variables), heap, open files, signals（及其他 OS resources） single-threaded process multithreaded process ┌──────┬──────┬──────┐ ┌──────┬──────┬──────┐ │ code │ data │ files│ │ code │ data │ files│ ← shared ├──────┼──────┼──────┤ ├──────┼──────┼──────┤ │ regs │ PC │stack │ │ regs │ regs │ regs │ ← private └──────┴──────┴──────┘ │stack │stack │stack │ (per thread) │ PC │ PC │ PC │ └──────┴──────┴──────┘ 📝 F19 Q2.3：unique to each thread → program counter, regis", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. Benefits（四大好處）⭐⭐ [4.7]", "u": "ch4.html#s2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Benefit 解釋 Responsiveness may allow continued execution if part of a process (one thread) is blocked — 對 interactive / UI applications 特別重要 Resource sharing threads share the resources of a process by default — 比 shared memory / message passing 容易（同一 address space） Economy thread creation is much cheaper than process creation（less time & memory）；context switching between threads is faster than between processes Scalability process can take advantage of multicore architectures（threads run in parallel） 📝 HW2 MC1：app 喺一個 thread blocked 時仍然 respond user → Responsiveness。", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.1 Heavyweight vs Lightweight（F20 Q2.3）⭐", "u": "ch4.html#s2-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "📝 Model answer：A process is called a heavyweight process because creating it requires allocating a new address space and duplicating the program, data and other resources (PCB, memory, open files) — time- and resource-consuming. A thread is lightweight because it only contains an execution unit (thread ID, PC, registers, stack, TCB) and shares the code, data and open files of its process; creating a thread needs no duplication of program and data, and context switching between threads of the same process is cheaper since the address space stays the same.", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. Multicore Programming（多核心編程）⭐ [4.8–4.11]", "u": "ch4.html#s3", "p": "Ch4", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.1 五大挑戰 [4.9]", "u": "ch4.html#s3-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Dividing tasks — how to divide into separate, concurrent tasks Balance — each task performs an \"equal\" amount of work（value） Data splitting — data must be divided to run on separate cores Data dependency — if there is a data dependency, synchronization is required Testing and debugging — different execution paths make debugging difficult", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.2 Concurrency vs Parallelism ⭐⭐ [4.9–4.10]", "u": "ch4.html#s3-2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Parallelism — a system can perform more than one task simultaneously（需要 multicore / multiprocessor）。 Concurrency — supports more than one task making progress；on a single core, the scheduler provides concurrency by multiplexing over time。 Single-core：only concurrency is possible；multicore：parallelism possible. Concurrency without parallelism 係可以嘅；parallelism 一定 concurrent。 Concurrency vs parallelism [4.10] Single core (concurrency): T1 T2 T3 T4 T1 T2 T3 T4 T1 ... (time →) Two cores (parallelism): core 0: T1 T3 T1 T3 ... core 1: T2 T4 T2 T4 ... ⚠️ S26 MC16：✅「Concurrency can be supported on a single processor core through time multiplexing by the scheduler」。 ✗「Concurrency means performing more", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.3 Data vs Task Parallelism ⭐ [4.11]", "u": "ch4.html#s3-3", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Data parallelism — distributes subsets of the same data across multiple cores, same operation on each core（e.g. 分開 sum 一個 array 嘅兩半；distributed ML）。 Task parallelism — distributes threads (tasks) across cores, each thread performing a unique operation。 Not mutually exclusive — an application may use both（hybrid）。 📝 HW2 MC2：「Threads on different cores perform the same operation on different subsets of an array」→ data parallelism。「One thread handles input, another does spell-check」→ task parallelism。", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. Amdahl's Law ⭐⭐⭐（計數題必考）[4.12–4.13]", "u": "ch4.html#s4", "p": "Ch4", "x": "🧮 Amdahl's Law speedup≤S+N1−S​1​,N→∞lim​speedup=S1​ S = serial portion，1−S = parallel portion，N = number of processing cores。 As N→∞, speedup → 1/S. Serial portion has a disproportionate effect on performance gained by adding cores. Amdahl's Law identifies theoretical speedup in latency at a fixed workload。", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.1 例子（全部計算過）", "u": "ch4.html#s4-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "題目 S N 計算 Speedup Slide：75% parallel 0.25 2 0.25+0.75/21​=0.6251​ 1.6 Sp21 MC：30% serial 0.3 7 0.3+0.7/71​=0.41​ 2.5 HW2 Q3.1(a)：20% serial 0.2 4 0.2+0.8/41​=0.41​ 2.5 HW2 Q3.1(b) 0.2 ∞ S1​=0.21​ 5 S26 MC17 A：81% parallel 0.19 3 0.19+0.81/31​=0.461​ 2.17 S26 MC17 B：81% parallel 0.19 5 0.19+0.81/51​=0.3521​ 2.84 S26 MC17 C：61% parallel 0.39 3 0.39+0.61/31​=0.59331​ 1.69 ✅（1.6–1.7） S26 MC17 D：61% parallel 0.39 5 0.39+0.61/51​=0.5121​ 1.95", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.2 解題 tips", "u": "ch4.html#s4-2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "留意題目畀 parallel % 定 serial %（S = serial！）。 反向題：要 speedup = X with N cores → 解 S：S=1−1/N1/X−1/N​。 例：想 4 cores 有 3× speedup → S=1−1/41/3−1/4​=3/41/12​=91​ ≈ 11.1% serial。 Speedup 上限永遠 ≤N 同 ≤1/S。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. Multithreaded Process & TCB ⭐ [4.14–4.18]", "u": "ch4.html#s5", "p": "Ch4", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.1 Multithreaded process [4.14]", "u": "ch4.html#s5-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "More than one instance of execution，每個有自己 PC。 Threads share the same address space → can access the same data. Each thread has its own private set of registers. Context switch between two threads (T1 → T2) of the same process：save T1's register state, restore T2's；address space remains the same → overhead is much smaller。Switching to a thread of a different process → more overhead. Overlap of I/O with other activities within a single program（一個 thread 用 CPU，另一個做 I/O）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.2 Thread Control Block (TCB) ⭐ [4.15]", "u": "ch4.html#s5-2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Thread = single unique execution context = lightweight process：PC, registers, execution flags, stack。 Thread 喺 processor 執行 = resident in the registers；PC register holds the address of the executing instruction。 Each thread has a TCB： Execution state：CPU registers, program counter, pointer to stack Scheduling info：state, priority, CPU time Accounting info Various pointers（implementing scheduling queues） Pointer to enclosing process (PCB) ⚠️ Sp21 MC：NOT in TCB → Process identifier（PID 喺 PCB；TCB 只有 pointer to enclosing process）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.3 Shared vs Private state [4.16] ⭐", "u": "ch4.html#s5-3", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Threads encapsulate concurrency — \"active\" component；address space encapsulates protection — \"passive\" part. Shared by all threads in a process：contents of memory (global variables, heap)；I/O state (file descriptors, network connections) Private to each thread (kept in TCB)：CPU registers (including PC)；execution stack (parameters, temporary variables, saved PC) If a process has multiple threads, there is no \"process state\", only the state of each thread（summary）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.4 Thread lifecycle [4.17–4.18]", "u": "ch4.html#s5-4", "p": "Ch4", "x": "new → ready → running → waiting → terminated（同 process 一樣）。 \"Active\" threads are represented by their TCBs; TCBs are organized into queues by state（ready queue、各 device queues：tape unit、disk unit、ethernet…，每個 queue 有自己 scheduling policy）。 📝 HW2 Q2.1：同一 process 兩個 threads 可唔可以有唔同 PC / register 值？→ Yes — 每個 thread 係獨立 flow of execution，有自己 PC、register set、stack；唔 running 時存喺 TCB (Thread Control Block)。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. User Threads vs Kernel Threads ⭐⭐ [4.19–4.20]", "u": "ch4.html#s6", "p": "Ch4", "x": "User threads — independently executable entities within a program, created and managed by a user-level thread library；visible only to programmers and unknown to the kernel → cannot be scheduled on a CPU directly。 Kernel threads — supported and managed by the OS；can be scheduled and executed on a CPU（Windows, Linux, macOS, iOS, Android 都支援）。 ⭐ OS only manages and schedules kernel threads.（F19 Q3.1：OS 排程嘅係 kernel-level threads） Three primary thread libraries：POSIX Pthreads、Windows threads、Java threads（對應 POSIX API、Win32 API、Java API）。 F20 MC12：provides API for creating and managing threads → Thread library (e.g. POSIX Pthreads)。 If a program defines no user threads, there is no kernel thread t", "w": 3}, {"t": "7. Multithreading Models ⭐⭐⭐ [4.21–4.26]", "u": "ch4.html#s7", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Model Mapping 優點 缺點 例子 Many-to-One many user threads → 1 kernel thread thread management 喺 user space（efficient） one thread blocking (blocking system call) blocks the entire process；cannot run in parallel on multicore（only one kernel thread active at a time） Solaris Green Threads, GNU Portable Threads（few systems now） One-to-One each user thread → its own kernel thread maximum concurrency（another thread runs when one blocks）；parallel on multicore creating a user thread requires creating a kernel thread → overhead；number of threads per process may be restricted Windows, Linux（most common now） Many-to-Many many user threads → smaller or equal number of kernel threads flexible；OS 可預先建立足夠 kernel", "w": 3}, {"t": "8. Threading Issues ⭐⭐ [4.27–4.33]", "u": "ch4.html#s8", "p": "Ch4", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.1 fork() and exec() semantics [4.28]", "u": "ch4.html#s8-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Does fork() called by a thread duplicate only the calling thread or all threads? Some UNIX systems have two versions of fork(). If exec() is called immediately after forking → duplicating only the calling thread is appropriate（exec 會取代成個 process）。 exec() works as normal — replaces the entire process including all threads.", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.2 Signal Handling ⭐⭐ [4.29–4.31]", "u": "ch4.html#s8-2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Signals notify a process that a particular event has occurred. Synchronous signals — illegal memory access, division by 0 → delivered to the same process that performed the operation causing the signal。 Asynchronous signals — generated by an event external to the running process：terminating with 、timer expires（kill）。 處理：有啲 ignore，有啲（illegal memory access）terminate program。 Signal handling pattern：(1) generated by an event → (2) delivered to a process → (3) handled by a default or user-defined handler。 Every signal has a default handler (kernel); a user-defined handler can override the default. Multithreaded process 要 deliver 去邊？四個選擇： To the thread to which the signal applies To every thread ", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.3 Thread Cancellation ⭐ [4.32–4.33]", "u": "ch4.html#s8-3", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Terminating a thread before it has finished；the thread to be cancelled = target thread. Asynchronous cancellation — terminates the target thread immediately（可能喺 critical update 中途 → OS 未必可以 reclaim 所有 resources）。 Deferred cancellation — the target thread periodically checks whether it should terminate, allowing it to terminate itself in an orderly fashion（preferred）。 Invoking cancellation only requests it; actual cancellation depends on thread state and type. Pthreads：if cancellation is disabled, it remains pending until enabled；default type is deferred. Code [4.33]： #include <pthread.h> pthread_t tid; pthread_create(&tid, 0, worker, NULL); /* worker = start routine */ pthread_cancel(tid); /", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.4 Thread-Local Storage（課本補充，Sp21 MC 考過）", "u": "ch4.html#s8-4", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Thread-local storage (TLS) is data that is unique to each thread（類似 static data 但每個 thread 一份；同 local variables 唔同，local 只喺一個 function call 內存在）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "9. OS Examples [4.34–4.38]", "u": "ch4.html#s9", "p": "Ch4", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.1 Windows Threads", "u": "ch4.html#s9-1", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Windows application = separate process，每個 process 可有多個 threads；Windows uses the one-to-one mapping。 Thread 組成：thread ID, register set, program counter, separate user and kernel stacks, private data storage area（run-time libraries / DLLs 用）。 Register set + stacks + private storage area = context of the thread. 數據結構： ETHREAD (executive thread block) — pointer to process & to KTHREAD（kernel space） KTHREAD (kernel thread block) — scheduling & synchronization info, kernel-mode stack, pointer to TEB（kernel space） TEB (thread environment block) — thread id, user-mode stack, thread-local storage（user space）", "w": 3}, {"t": "9.2 Linux Threads ⭐ [4.37–4.38]", "u": "ch4.html#s9-2", "p": "Ch4", "x": "Linux does not differentiate between threads and processes — both are called tasks. A thread in Linux is a process that may share certain resources with other threads；每個 thread 有自己 task_struct。 對比 Windows：Windows 一個 PCB（shared resources）指向兩個 TCB；Linux 就係兩個 normal task_struct，set up to share resources。 clone() 用 flags 指定 share 咩： clone(CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND, 0); CLONE_VM：share address space；CLONE_FS：share file-system info；CLONE_FILES：share open files；CLONE_SIGHAND：share signal handlers。 Instead of copying all data structures like fork(), the new task points to the parent's data structures depending on the flags. A normal fork() can be implemented as clone(SIGCHLD, ", "w": 3}, {"t": "10. Pthreads API 速查（Lab 4 / Ch6 都用）", "u": "ch4.html#s10", "p": "Ch4", "x": "#include <pthread.h> int pthread_create(pthread_t *tid, const pthread_attr_t *attr, void *(*start)(void *), void *arg); int pthread_join(pthread_t tid, void **retval); /* wait for thread tid */ void pthread_exit(void *retval); int pthread_cancel(pthread_t tid); pthread_t pthread_self(void); /* mutex */ pthread_mutex_t m; pthread_mutex_init(&m, NULL); pthread_mutex_lock(&m); /* critical section */ pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); /* semaphore */ #include <semaphore.h> sem_t s; sem_init(&s, 0 /*shared between threads*/, 1 /*initial value*/); sem_wait(&s); /* P / wait: decrement or block */ sem_post(&s); /* V / signal: increment */ 編譯：gcc prog.c -lpthread（或 -pthread）。 典型 pattern： void *worker(void *ar", "w": 3}, {"t": "11. 常見陷阱 & MC 速查", "u": "ch4.html#s11", "p": "Ch4", "x": "敘述 真/假 Each thread has its own address space and file descriptors ❌（share） Threads of the same process cannot run concurrently on multiple cores ❌（one-to-one 可以 parallel） Thread creation is usually faster than process creation ✅（S26 MC15） Multithreaded process can't use multicore ❌ Stack is shared among threads ❌ Heap / global data shared ✅ Concurrency possible on single core ✅ Parallelism possible on single core ❌ Data parallelism = same operation on different data subsets ✅ As N→∞, Amdahl speedup → 1/S ✅ Speedup can exceed N ❌ OS schedules user threads directly ❌（kernel threads） Many-to-one allows parallelism on multicore ❌ Many-to-one: one blocking call blocks whole process ✅ One-to-one: ", "w": 3}, {"t": "12. Past-paper 題目（Ch4）", "u": "ch4.html#s12", "p": "Ch4", "x": "S26 MC15：true about multithreaded process → C. Thread creation is usually faster than process creation S26 MC16：concurrency/parallelism → C S26 MC17：Amdahl 1.6–1.7 → C (61%, 3 cores) = 1.69 S26 MC18：false about signals → C. All signals delivered to all threads F19 Q2.3：unique vs shared（§1.2）；Q3.1：OS schedules kernel threads；user-thread → kernel-thread/LWP scheduling = PCS (process-contention scope)，within the same process, based on priority set by programmer；CPU scheduling (SCS) 喺全系統 kernel threads 之間揀 F20 MC11 stack；MC12 thread library；MC13 user thread → multiple kernel threads（false）；Q2.3 heavyweight vs lightweight Sp21 MC：Amdahl 30% serial 7 cores → 2.5；TCB 唔包括 process identifier；single p", "w": 3}, {"t": "13. 自我測驗", "u": "ch4.html#s13", "p": "Ch4", "x": "列出 multithreading 四個 benefits。 50% serial 嘅 program 用 4 cores speedup？（0.5+0.5/41​=0.6251​ = 1.6）；無限 cores？（1/S = 2） Many-to-one model 兩大缺點？ 點解 exec() 緊接 fork() 時只複製 calling thread 就夠？ Deferred vs asynchronous cancellation？邊個 preferred？點解？ Linux 點樣建立 thread？（clone() with CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND）", "w": 3}, {"t": "Ch5 CPU Scheduling", "u": "ch5.html", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Chapter 5 — CPU Scheduling（CPU 排程） 📌 考試範圍提示：Ch5 係分數最重嘅一章（S26：27/100；F19：40/100；F20：27+；Sp21：26）。必考： Gantt chart + average waiting / turnaround / response time：FCFS、SJF、SRTF、RR、Priority（± RR）、MLFQ（每年都有！）、自創 policy（LUF、dynamic priority） RM / EDF real-time Gantt + missed deadlines MC / short：preemptive vs non-preemptive、convoy effect、starvation & aging、MLFQ 優點、RR vs FCFS、exponential averaging、Little's formula、PCS vs SCS、processor affinity、load balancing、hard vs soft real-time ⚠️ Response time 本科定義 = 第一次運行「完結」時間 − arrival time（slide 5.12），唔係課本「第一次開始」！", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. Basic Concepts（基本概念）[5.4–5.7]", "u": "ch5.html#s1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.1 CPU–I/O Burst Cycle", "u": "ch5.html#s1-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Objective of multiprogramming：have a process running at all times → maximize CPU utilization. Process execution consists of cycles of CPU execution and I/O wait — CPU burst and I/O burst. [5.5] Histogram of CPU-burst times：many short CPU bursts, few long ones (long-tail distribution)。I/O-bound → many short CPU bursts；CPU-bound → a few long CPU bursts. 呢個分佈對設計 scheduling algorithm 好重要。 CPU idle 時，OS 由 ready queue 揀一個 process（除非 ready queue empty）→ 由 CPU scheduler（process scheduler / short-term scheduler） 揀。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.2 When does scheduling happen? ⭐⭐ [5.6]", "u": "ch5.html#s1-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "CPU scheduling 可能喺四種情況發生： Running → waiting（e.g. I/O request, wait()） Process terminates Running → ready（e.g. interrupt / timer） Waiting → ready（I/O completion）或者 new → ready（新 process 到達，priority 更高） Scheduling under 1 and 2 is non-preemptive (cooperative) — process gives up the CPU voluntarily。 Scheduling under 3 and 4 is preemptive. Preemptive 要考慮：access to shared data (race condition, Ch6)、preemption while in kernel mode、interrupts during crucial OS activities。 📝 Sp21 MC：CPU scheduling decisions may take place in → all of the above（running→waiting、terminates、running→ready）。 📝 HW2 MC5：preemptive 例子 → time quantum expires, running process moved back to the ready queue（A terminate、B I/O、D w", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.3 Dispatcher [5.7]", "u": "ch5.html#s1-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Dispatcher module gives control of the CPU to the process selected by the CPU scheduler： switching context from one process to another switching to user mode jumping to the proper location in the user program to restart it Dispatch latency — time for the dispatcher to stop one process and start another（overhead）。 應該越快越好。 vmstat（Linux）可睇 context switches 數目（每秒幾百次）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. Scheduling Metrics（排程指標）⭐⭐⭐ [5.8, 5.12]", "u": "ch5.html#s2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Metric 定義 目標 CPU utilization fraction of the time that the CPU is busy max Throughput number of processes completed per unit time（favors short processes） max Turnaround time (TAT) time to execute a particular process = completion time − arrival time min Waiting time (WT) total time spent waiting in the ready queue = TAT − CPU burst min Response time (RT) time from submission until the first response is produced min Fairness adequate allocation for different jobs/processes —", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.1 公式（本科 convention，必背）⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s2-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "🧮 Metrics（本科 convention） TurnaroundWaitingResponse​=Completion−Arrival=Turnaround−CPU burst=(end of the FIRST run)−Arrival​ Response time 嘅「first run」完結 = 第一次上 CPU 嘅嗰段（RR / MLFQ 就係 end of first quantum，或者 burst 細過 quantum 就係完成時間）[5.12]。 Slide 5.13 例：RR q=4，P1(24), P2(3), P3(3) 全部 t=0 到 → RT：P1 = 4, P2 = 7, P3 = 10，avg = 7。 S26 MLFQ 官方：P1 response = 4 − 0 = 4（P1 跑 0–4）。 非 preemptive (FCFS/SJF) 嘅 RT = TAT（第一次 run 就跑完）。 Waiting time 另一個計法（gap method）：將 Gantt chart 上 process 唔喺 CPU 嘅時間（由 arrival 起）加埋。兩個方法結果一樣： S26 P2：gap = (4−2)+(21−8)+(40−29)+(57−45)+(64−58) = 44 ✔；formula = 71 − 2 − 25 = 44 ✔。 S26 MC19 ✅「Response time is the amount of time from when a request is submitted until the first respo", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. FCFS — First-Come, First-Served ⭐ [5.9–5.10]", "u": "ch5.html#s3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "按 arrival order 排；non-preemptive（跑完 current CPU burst 先放）。 例：P1 = 24, P2 = 3, P3 = 3（全部 t=0） 次序 P1, P2, P3：0 P1 24 P2 27 P3 30 → WT = 0, 24, 27 → avg 17；TAT avg =324+27+30​=27 次序 P2, P3, P1：0 P2 3 P3 6 P1 30 → WT = 6, 0, 3 → avg 3；TAT avg = 13 ⭐ Convoy effect — short processes stuck behind a long process，令 average waiting time 好長，完全取決於 arrival order。One CPU-bound + many I/O-bound → low I/O device utilization。（例：銀行排隊存張支票，前面有人開戶） 📝 HW2 MC6：convoy effect → several short processes wait behind a long process that arrived first。 📝 F19 Q3.2：FCFS 問題 = convoy effect（short process stuck behind a long process）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. RR — Round Robin ⭐⭐⭐ [5.11–5.16]", "u": "ch5.html#s4", "p": "Ch5", "x": "FCFS + preemption：每個 process 得 time quantum q（通常 10–100 ms）；用完 → preempted and added to the end (tail) of the ready queue。 n processes → each gets 1/n of CPU time in chunks of at most q；no process waits more than (n − 1)q time units ⭐（ignoring context switch）。 Timer interrupts every quantum；如果 burst（或 remaining）< q，process 自己完成就放 CPU。 例 [5.13]：q = 4，P1 24, P2 3, P3 3 → 0 P1 4 P2 7 P3 10 P1 14 P1 18 P1 22 P1 26 P1 30 WT：P1 = 6, P2 = 4, P3 = 7 → avg 5.67；TAT avg =330+7+10​=15.67；RT = 4, 7, 10 → avg 7 RR：average waiting time can be long, but more \"fair\"；better for short jobs than FCFS；better average response time → good for interactive jobs.", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.1 Time quantum 大細 [5.14, 5.16] ⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s4-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "q large → RR degenerates to FCFS.（Sp21 MC 答案：FCFS） q small → many context switches; q must be large w.r.t. context-switch time (< 10 µs), otherwise overhead too high.（q 極細 = processor sharing：好似 n 個 processor 每個 1/n 速度） Average turnaround time does not necessarily improve as q increases. TAT improves if most processes finish their CPU burst within a single quantum. Rule of thumb：80% of CPU bursts should be shorter than q.", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.2 FCFS vs RR ⭐（F19 Q3.2 考過）[5.15]", "u": "ch5.html#s4-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Is RR always better than FCFS? No! 例：10 jobs start at the same time, each needs 100 s, q = 1 s → average turnaround time much worse under RR（全部差唔多 1000 s 先完；FCFS 平均 550 s），but average response time much better。 一般而言 no clear winner between FCFS and RR in TAT/WT — 取決於 burst times、arrival pattern、quantum size。 📝 F19 Q3.2 Model answer：RR performs worse than FCFS in turnaround time when all processes have the same (equal) CPU burst times that are longer than the time quantum — the jobs are interleaved and all finish near the end, whereas FCFS finishes them one by one.", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.3 RR 做 Gantt chart 規則（考試用）", "u": "ch5.html#s4-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "用 queue（寫低每個時刻 queue 內容！）。 新到達 process 加入 queue tail；quantum 用完嘅 process 都去 tail。 ⚠️ 同一時刻「新 process 到達」同「running process quantum 用完」→ convention：新到達嗰個先入 queue，然後先放被 preempt 嗰個（寫低 assumption）。 Process burst 完 → 即刻揀下一個（唔使等 quantum 完）。 Queue 空而 running process quantum 用完 → 佢繼續跑（再得一個 quantum）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. SJF / SRTF — Shortest Job First ⭐⭐⭐ [5.17–5.24]", "u": "ch5.html#s5", "p": "Ch5", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.1 SJF（non-preemptive）", "u": "ch5.html#s5-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Associate each process with the length of its next CPU burst; schedule the shortest.（更準確叫 shortest-next-CPU-burst） ⭐ SJF is optimal — gives the minimum average waiting time for a given set of processes. Difficulty：knowing the length of the next CPU request.（所以 practical 難 implement；F20 MC15 答案 SJF difficult to implement） Sketch proof：moving a short process before a long one decreases the waiting time of the short process more than it increases the waiting time of the long process → average waiting time decreases. 例 [5.18]：P1 = 6, P2 = 8, P3 = 7, P4 = 3。Slide 個 chart 當全部 t = 0 到：0 P4 3 P1 9 P3 16 P2 24 → avg WT =43+16+9+0​=7（slide 答案）。 ⚠️ 如果真係跟表入面 arrival（0, 2, 4, 5）：t=0 只有 P1 → 0 P1 6 P4 9 P", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.2 SRTF — Shortest-Remaining-Time-First（preemptive SJF）⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s5-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "New arrival with burst shorter than the remaining time of the running process → preempts it. Scheduling 喺 every arrival (condition 4) 都要 invoke。 例 [5.23]：P1(0,8), P2(1,4), P3(2,9), P4(3,5) → 0 P1 1 P2 5 P4 10 P1 17 P3 26 avg WT =4(10−1)+(1−1)+(17−2)+(5−3)​=426​=6.5", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.3 比較 [5.24] ⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s5-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "SJF/SRTF are the best for minimizing average waiting time (and average turnaround time). Provably optimal：SJF among non-preemptive, SRTF among preemptive. SRTF is always at least as good as SJF. SJF/SRTF = FCFS if all processes have the same CPU burst. Can lead to starvation of long processes if shorter processes keep arriving；fairness cannot be enforced. F20 MC14：best (min) average waiting time → SJF（SRTF 亦接受）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.4 Exponential Averaging（預測下一個 CPU burst）⭐⭐ [5.19–5.22]", "u": "ch5.html#s5-4", "p": "Ch5", "x": "🧮 Exponential averaging τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)τn​,0≤α≤1 tn​ = actual length of the n-th CPU burst；τn+1​ = predicted value for the next burst；通常 α=1/2。 α=0⇒τn+1​=τn​（recent history does not count）；α=1⇒τn+1​=tn​（only the last burst counts）。 展開 → all past values count but recent ones weigh more；past effect diminishes exponentially： τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)αtn−1​+⋯+(1−α)jαtn−j​+⋯+(1−α)n+1τ0​ 例：α=0.5，10 rounds 後 (1/2)10=1/1024 → 可忽略。 預測取決於 (1) all previous actual bursts with different weights, (2) the initial estimate τ0​。 Slide 5.20 例（α=1/2, τ0​=10）： i 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 CPU burst ti​ 6 4 6 4 13 13 13 … guess τi​ 10 8 6 6 5 9 11 12 HW2 MC9：τn​=10, tn​=6, α=0.25⇒τn+1​=0.25×6+0.75×10=1.5+7.5= 9 ms。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. Priority Scheduling ⭐⭐ [5.25–5.27]", "u": "ch5.html#s6", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Each process has a priority number；CPU allocated to the highest priority（本科：smallest integer = highest priority，除非題目另講）。 Preemptive（higher-priority arrival preempts）或 non-preemptive。 Equal priority → FCFS order（或者 RR）。 SJF is a special case of priority scheduling where priority = inverse of predicted next CPU burst. ⭐ Problem：Starvation (indefinite blocking) — low-priority processes may never execute. ⭐ Solution：Aging — gradually increase the priority of a process as it waits（需要每個 process 喺 ready queue 有 timer）。 Sp21 MC：main problem of priority scheduling → some processes may never execute (starvation)；HW2 MC7：aging → address starvation by gradually increasing the priority of a waiting proce", "w": 3}, {"t": "7. Multilevel Queue（多級佇列）⭐ [5.28–5.29]", "u": "ch5.html#s7", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Priority scheduling combined with RR；priority assigned statically；a process remains in the same queue for its whole runtime. Partition processes into queues based on process type（例：real-time → system → interactive → batch）。 Each queue can have its own scheduling algorithm. Scheduling among queues：(1) fixed-priority preemptive scheduling（高 queue 空先輪到低 queue → 可能 starvation）或 (2) time-slice（each queue gets a certain % of CPU time，e.g. 60%, 20%, 10%, 10%）。 Sp21 MC：Multilevel queue（固定）有 starvation（free from starvation 嘅只有 FCFS 同 RR）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "8. MLFQ — Multilevel Feedback Queue ⭐⭐⭐（每年必考 Gantt）[5.30–5.35]", "u": "ch5.html#s8", "p": "Ch5", "x": "A process can move between queues；aging can be implemented this way → flexibility. MLFQ 由以下 parameters 定義： number of queues scheduling algorithm for each queue method to upgrade a process（to higher queue） method to demote a process（to lower queue） method to determine which queue a process enters when it needs service", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.1 Slide example 規則（考試照用）[5.31] ⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s8-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Q0 – RR（slide 5.31: q = 8；例子 5.32–5.34 用 q = 4）；Q1 – RR（q = 16 / 8）；Q2 – FCFS A new job always enters Q0 (RR, FCFS order)；it also preempts jobs from Q1 or Q2 currently running. Gets q0​ in Q0；if not finished → moved to Q1. In Q1 gets q1​ additional；if still not complete → preempted and moved to Q2. ⭐ If a job from Q1 or Q2 is preempted by a new job from Q0, it joins the HEAD of Q1 or Q2, with the remaining quantum (Q1) or remaining CPU burst (Q2).（唔會攞一個全新 quantum；唔會因為被 preempt 而 demote） Q1 job 只會喺 Q0 空先跑；Q2 job 只會喺 Q0 和 Q1 都空先跑。 Q1 job 永遠唔會 preempt Q2 job（因為冇 process 直接 arrive 去 Q1）— summary 重點。 A job in Q0 is never preempted（Q0 內部只有 RR）。 Burst ≤q0​+q1​（e.g. 4 + 8 = 12）嘅 job 永遠唔會落 Q2。 This a", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.2 Lecture example [5.32–5.34]（Q0 q=4, Q1 q=8, Q2 FCFS）", "u": "ch5.html#s8-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Process Arrival Burst P1 0 10 P2 2 15 P3 5 2 P4 12 14 P5 18 6 0 P1 4 P2 8 P3 10 P1 12 P4 16 P1 18 P5 22 P1 24 P2 32 P4 40 P5 42 P2 45 P4 47 Q0 Q0 Q0 Q1 Q0 Q1 Q0 Q1 Q1 Q1 Q1 Q2 Q2 t=4 P1 用完 Q0 quantum → Q1；t=8 P2 → Q1；t=10 P3 完；P1 喺 Q1 跑；t=12 P4 到 → preempt P1（P1 返 Q1 head，剩 6 quantum）；t=16 P4 → Q1 tail；P1 繼續（剩 quantum 6）；t=18 P5 到 → preempt P1；t=22 P5 → Q1；P1 跑到 24 完成（Q1 總共用 2+2+2 = 6 ≤ 8）；P2 24–32 用完 Q1 quantum → Q2；P4 32–40 → Q2；P5 40–42 完成；P2 42–45 完；P4 45–47 完。 WT：P1 = 14, P2 = 28, P3 = 3, P4 = 21, P5 = 18 → avg =584​=16.8 ✅", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.3 MLFQ advantages ⭐⭐（F19 Q3.3、F20 Q2.5 必背）[5.35]", "u": "ch5.html#s8-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Used in BSD Unix, Solaris, Windows NT and later。 It does not need prior knowledge of the next CPU burst time（SJF/SRTF 需要） It handles interactive jobs well — better response time than RR（short jobs 喺 top queue 完成） It produces similar performance to SJF/SRTF It is \"fair\" — makes progress on CPU-bound jobs Starvation 可以用 reshuffling jobs periodically（e.g. 一段時間後將所有 jobs 搬返 top queue）解決。 📝 F20 Q2.5 Model answer：(1) MLFQ resembles the performance of SJF/SRTF without requiring prior knowledge of the next CPU burst time; (2) like RR, it gives good response time to interactive (short, I/O-bound) jobs since they finish in the top queues; (3) it is fair — all processes, long or short, make progress (an", "w": 3}, {"t": "8.4 MLFQ 做題 checklist ✍️", "u": "ch5.html#s8-4", "p": "Ch5", "x": "每個時間點寫低：Q0、Q1、Q2 內容（同次序）、running process、佢喺邊個 queue、剩餘 quantum、剩餘 burst。 事件：arrival（入 Q0 tail；如果 running 係 Q1/Q2 → preempt，被 preempt 嘅返 head of own queue，保留剩餘 quantum）；quantum 用完（未完成 → 降一級，入 tail）；完成。 Gantt 上標 process@Queue（HW2 要求 label queue）。 同一 process 由 Q0 用完 quantum 立即喺 Q1 繼續跑（因為 Q0 空）→ Gantt 可合併，但計 response time 要用 Q0 quantum 完結時間（S26 P5：49 − 45 = 4）。 題目規則永遠優先（S26 第 8 條、F20 aging timer、HW2 「retains unused quantum」）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "9. Thread Scheduling ⭐⭐ [5.36–5.37]", "u": "ch5.html#s9", "p": "Ch5", "x": "On modern OSes, kernel-level threads are scheduled (by the OS); user-level threads are managed by the thread library. LWP (lightweight process) — intermediate data structure；appears as a virtual processor on which user threads are scheduled to run；each LWP attached to a kernel thread。 ⭐ Process-Contention Scope (PCS) — under many-to-one and many-to-many models, the thread library schedules user-level threads onto an LWP；competition among threads belonging to the same process；typically by priority set by programmers（thread library 通常唔會調整 priority）；PCS typically preempts the running thread in favour of a higher-priority user thread。 ⭐ System-Contention Scope (SCS) — the kernel schedules kernel", "w": 3}, {"t": "10. Multiple-Processor Scheduling ⭐⭐ [5.38–5.46]", "u": "ch5.html#s10", "p": "Ch5", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.1 AMP vs SMP [5.38–5.39]", "u": "ch5.html#s10-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Multiprocessor 而家包括 multicore CPUs, multithreaded cores, NUMA systems, heterogeneous multiprocessing。 Asymmetric multiprocessing (AMP) — only one processor (the master server) accesses kernel data structures（唔使 data sharing）；all scheduling decisions, I/O processing, system activities handled by the master；other processors execute only user code；⭐ the master server can become a potential bottleneck。 Symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) — each processor is self-scheduling，with one common ready queue or a private ready queue per processor；每個 processor 嘅 scheduler examine ready queue 揀 thread。 Common queue：must ensure two processors don't choose the same thread（race condition） 同 processes 唔會 lost。 Al", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.2 Multicore & Multithreaded cores (CMT) ⭐ [5.40–5.44]", "u": "ch5.html#s10-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Multiple cores on the same chip — faster and consume less power, but complicate scheduling. ⭐ Memory stall：when a processor accesses memory, it spends a significant amount of time waiting for data to become available（modern processors much faster than memory, esp. on cache miss）。（處理器可能 50% 時間喺等 memory） Chip multithreading (CMT)（Intel = hyperthreading）：multiple hardware threads per core；each hardware thread has its own architectural state (PC, register set) and appears as a logical CPU。 If one thread stalls waiting for memory, the core switches to another hardware thread. Dual-threaded dual-core → 4 logical processors；quad-core × 2 threads → 8 logical processors；UltraSPARC T3：16 cores × 8 thr", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.3 Processor Affinity ⭐⭐ [5.45]", "u": "ch5.html#s10-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "When a thread has run on a processor, that processor's cache holds its memory accesses → the thread has affinity for that processor. High cost of invalidating and repopulating caches → most SMP systems try to avoid migrating processes between processors. ⭐ Per-processor ready queues provide processor affinity for free! Soft affinity — OS attempts to keep a process on the same processor but doesn't guarantee it（可以 migrate during load balancing）。 Hard affinity — a process specifies a subset of processors it may run on.（Linux：soft affinity + sched_setaffinity() for hard affinity） NUMA：CPU 存取近佢嘅 memory 較快 → affinity to both processor and memory。 📝 HW2 Q2.4：hard affinity {CPU0, CPU1}，CPU2 idle → ", "w": 3}, {"t": "10.4 Load Balancing ⭐ [5.46]", "u": "ch5.html#s10-4", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Keep workload evenly distributed（只喺 per-processor private queues 時需要）。 Push migration — a specific task periodically checks loads and pushes tasks from overloaded to idle/less-busy CPUs. Pull migration — an idle processor pulls a waiting task from a busy processor. Not mutually exclusive — often both implemented (Linux CFS). ⭐ Load balancing often counteracts the benefits of processor affinity（natural tension）。 S26 MC22 ✗「The motivation of placing multiple cores on the same chip is to simplify the scheduler」（其實 complicate scheduling）；✅ AMP master bottleneck；✅ memory stall → run another thread；✅ affinity keeps process on same CPU for cache。", "w": 3}, {"t": "11. Real-Time CPU Scheduling ⭐⭐⭐ [5.47–5.52]", "u": "ch5.html#s11", "p": "Ch5", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.1 Hard vs Soft ⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s11-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Real-time scheduling demands performance guarantee — predictability. Hard real-time — a task must be serviced by its deadline; service after the deadline is the same as no service. Soft real-time — no guarantee when a critical real-time process will be scheduled; only guarantees it will be given preference over noncritical processes. Real-time OS scheduler must support a priority-based algorithm with preemption（但 preemptive priority-based 只保證 soft real-time）。 Sp21 MC ✅「Soft real-time systems guarantee only that the process will be prioritized over non-critical processes」；✗ doesn't demand guarantee；✗ hard RT can service after deadline；✗ priority without preemption。", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.2 Periodic tasks [5.48]", "u": "ch5.html#s11-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "參數：processing time t, deadline d, period p，0≤t≤d≤p；rate = 1/p。 Admission control：process announces deadline；scheduler admits only if it can guarantee completion by the deadline。 CPU utilization of task i = ti​/pi​；total（U>1 → 一定有 miss）： U=i∑​pi​ti​​", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.3 Rate-Monotonic (RM) ⭐⭐ [5.49–5.50]", "u": "ch5.html#s11-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Static priority = inverse of period：shorter period → higher priority（rationale：tasks requiring CPU more often get higher priority）。Preemptive. RM is optimal among static-priority algorithms：if a set can't be scheduled by RM, no other static-priority algorithm can. Only handles periodic processes with constant processing time. 例 [5.49]：P1 (p=50, t=20), P2 (p=100, t=35)：U = 0.40 + 0.35 = 75% → OK：0 P1 20 P2 50 P1 70 P2 75 idle 100 例 [5.50]：P1 (50, 25), P2 (80, 35)：U = 0.50 + 0.44 = 94% → 0 P1 25 P2 50 P1 75 P2 85 → P2 misses its deadline (80) by finishing at 85。 RM: P2 misses its deadline at 80 [5.50] 課本 bound（slides 冇，但好用）：RM guaranteed if U≤N(21/N−1)：N=1 → 1；N=2 → 0.828；N=3 → 0.780；N=4 → 0.7", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.4 EDF — Earliest Deadline First ⭐⭐ [5.51–5.52]", "u": "ch5.html#s11-4", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Dynamic priorities according to deadline：earlier (absolute) deadline → higher priority. Preemptive。 例 [5.51]（P1 50/25, P2 80/35）：0 P1 25 P2 60 P1 85 P2 100 P1 125 P2 145 idle 150 → no deadline missed（t=50 時 P2 deadline 80 < P1 新 deadline 100 → P2 繼續）。 EDF: same tasks, no deadline missed [5.51] EDF does not require processes to be periodic, nor a constant CPU time per burst；only requirement：announce its deadline when it becomes runnable. ⭐ EDF is theoretically optimal — can meet all deadlines with CPU utilization up to 100%（但實際上 context switching + interrupt handling 令佢做唔到 100%）。 S26 MC21：assigns priorities according to deadlines → EDF。 📝 Sp21 Q2.3(b)：EDF is better — unlike RM with static pri", "w": 3}, {"t": "11.5 RM / EDF 做題方法 ✍️", "u": "ch5.html#s11-5", "p": "Ch5", "x": "列出每個 task 嘅 release times（0, p, 2p, …）同 absolute deadlines（release + d；冇講 d 就 d = p）。 每個 time unit（或者每個事件：release / completion）揀： RM：ready jobs 入面 period 最短（固定）嗰個。 EDF：ready jobs 入面 absolute deadline 最早嗰個。 Ties：題目有講就跟（S26：smaller process ID）；冇講：keep the running job（唔 preempt）（F19、Sp21 官方答案都係咁），再唔得就 smaller ID。寫低 assumption。 Soft real-time：miss 咗 deadline 嘅 job 會繼續跑完（Sp21 hint；S26 官方亦係咁），但 count 為 missed。 數 missed deadlines：每個 deadline ≤ 時限範圍嘅 job，完成時間 > deadline（或者未完成）→ miss。 先計 U=∑i​ti​/pi​ 做 sanity check。 S26 Q4.2（P1 t=1 d=3 p=4；P2 2/4/5；P3 2/6/7；P4 1/5/8；0–16，ties → smaller ID）： RM（priority P1 > P2 > P3 > P4）：0 P1 1 P2 3 P3 4 P1 5 P2 7 P3 8 P1 9 P3 10 P2 12 P1 13 P3 14 P3 15 P2 16 P3 job ", "w": 3}, {"t": "12. Algorithm Evaluation [5.53–5.60]", "u": "ch5.html#s12", "p": "Ch5", "x": "先定 criteria（e.g. maximize CPU utilization subject to max response time 300 ms；maximize throughput such that TAT ∝ execution time）。 Deterministic modeling — takes a particular predetermined workload and defines the performance of each algorithm for that workload；simple and fast, gives exact numbers, but requires exact input and answers apply only to those cases。 例 [5.54–5.55]：5 processes at t=0：P1 10, P2 29, P3 3, P4 7, P5 12 → FCFS avg WT = 28；non-preemptive SJF = 13；RR (q=10) = 23。 Queueing models / analysis — CPU = server with ready queue；用 distributions（exponential, described by mean）；可計 utilization、average queue length、wait time；classes of algorithms and distributions handled are limited", "w": 3}, {"t": "13. Gantt Chart 萬用流程（考試步驟）✍️⭐⭐⭐", "u": "ch5.html#s13", "p": "Ch5", "x": "抄表：Process / Arrival / Burst（/ Priority）。 由 t = 0 開始逐個事件推進（events：arrival、completion、quantum expiry、check point）。每個事件寫低 ready queue（同 MLFQ 各層）。 揀 process 規則： Algorithm 揀邊個 Preempt? FCFS earliest arrival No SJF shortest burst among arrived No SRTF shortest remaining Yes, on arrival RR head of queue Yes, quantum expiry Priority (NP / P) highest priority P: on higher arrival Priority + RR highest priority；same → RR Yes MLFQ highest non-empty queue Yes（Q0 arrival preempts Q1/Q2） RM shortest period Yes EDF earliest absolute deadline Yes CPU idle：冇 process ready 就畫 idle（Sp21 FCFS 0 P1 2 idle 4 P2…）。 畫 Gantt：每段標 start time / process / end time；所有 boundary 時間都要寫（partial credit 靠呢個）。 計 table：Completi", "w": 3}, {"t": "13.1 Tie-breaking（寫 assumption！）", "u": "ch5.html#s13-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "同時到達、同 burst/priority：FCFS → lower process ID。 SRTF：新到達 burst = running remaining → 一般 keep running（唔 preempt）；F19 官方 preempt 咗，但兩種 avg WT 一樣 2.5。 RR：新到達 + quantum expiry 同一時刻 → 新到達先入 queue。 EDF：deadline 相同 → keep running，否則 smaller ID（或者跟題目）。 Dynamic priority（S26 Q4.3）：題目講「same priority → larger ID first」— 跟題目！", "w": 3}, {"t": "14. Past-paper 計算題全集（全部用程式驗證 ✅）", "u": "ch5.html#s14", "p": "Ch5", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.1 S26 Q4.1 MLFQ（Q0 RR4, Q1 RR8, Q2 FCFS；preempted → head of own queue）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-1", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P Arr Burst P1 0 9 P2 2 25 P3 6 7 P4 14 18 P5 45 15 P6 58 6 0 P1 4 P2 8 P3 12 P1 14 P4 18 P1 21 P2 29 P3 32 P4 40 P2 45 P5 57 P2 58 P6 64 P2 71 P4 77 P5 80 (Q0)(Q0)(Q0)(Q1)(Q0)(Q1)(Q1)(Q1)(Q1)(Q2)(Q0→Q1 at 49)(Q2)(Q0→Q1 at 62)(Q2)(Q2)(Q2) WT：P1 12, P2 44, P3 19, P4 45, P5 20, P6 0 → avg =6140​=23.33 RT（end of first run − arrival）：P1 4, P2 6, P3 6, P4 4, P5 4 (49−45), P6 4 (62−58) → avg =628​=4.67", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.2 S26 Q4.3 Dynamic priority（larger = higher；waiting rate α，running rate β；tie → larger ID）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-2", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P1 (0, 7), P2 (3, 5), P3 (6, 6), P4 (9, 4) (a) α=0, β=3（running process priority 上升，waiting 不變 → 一旦 run 就霸住）：0 P1 7 P3 13 P4 17 P2 22 t=7：P2, P3 都係 0 → tie → larger ID → P3；t=13：P2 (0), P4 (0) → P4。 (b) α=1, β=0，只考慮 P1、P2，每 3 個 unit check：0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 9 P2 11 P1 12 t=3：P1 = 0 (ran 3), P2 = 0 → tie → P2；t=6：P1 = 3, P2 = 0 → P1；t=9：P1 = 3, P2 = 3 → tie → P2；t=11 P2 完 → P1。 (c) α=1, β=−2，初始 30，每 3 unit check：0 P1 3 P2 6 P3 9 P4 12 P1 15 P2 17 P3 20 P4 21 P1 22 t=3：P1 = 30−6 = 24, P2 = 30 → P2；t=6：P1 27, P2 24, P3 30 → P3；t=9：P1 30, P2 27, P3 24, P4 30 → tie → P4；t=12：P1 33 → P1；t=15：P1 27, P2 33, P3 30, P4 27 → P2（17 完）；t=17：P1 29, P3 32, P4 29 → P3（20 完）；t=20：P1 32, P4 32 → tie → P4（21 完）；t=", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.3 S26 Q2.3 自訂 policy（揀 fᵢ 最大）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-3", "p": "Ch5", "x": "fi​=aci​+b1​(a,b>0) (a) Yes — I/O-bound processes have small average CPU bursts ci​ → larger fi​ → higher priority. (b) Yes — a CPU-bound process (large ci​, low fi​) can starve if higher-priority I/O-bound processes keep arriving.", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.4 F19 Q3.5 LUF (Least Used First)（揀用 CPU 最少嘅；tie → FCFS；preempt on arrival）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-4", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P1 (0, 25), P2 (15, 25), P3 (25, 5), P4 (40, 5) 0 P1 15 P2 25 P3 30 P2 40 P4 45 P1 55 P2 60 P TAT RT WT P1 55 15 30 P2 45 10 20 P3 5 5 0 P4 5 5 0 LUF vs SRTF：相似 — short jobs 較易有高 priority；分別 — LUF 令跑咗好耐嘅 process 易 starve；SRTF 令 long burst 嘅 process 一開始就 starve。", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.5 F19 Q3.6（P1 0/8, P2 7/13, P3 9/1, P4 11/5, P5 20/7, P6 27/1）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-5", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Alg Gantt WT (P1..P6) Avg FCFS 0 P1 8 P2 21 P3 22 P4 27 P5 34 P6 35 0,1,12,11,7,7 6.33 SJF 0 P1 8 P2 21 P3 22 P4 27 P6 28 P5 35 0,1,12,11,8,0 5.33 RR q=4 0 P1 8 P2 12 P3 13 P4 17 P2 21 P4 22 P5 26 P2 30 P5 33 P6 34 P2 35 0,15,3,6,6,6 6.0（corrected） SRTF 0 P1 8 P2 9 P3 10 P2 11 P4 16 P2 20 P5 27 P6 28 P2 35（官方，tie 時 preempt） 0,15,0,0,0,0 2.5 SRTF 另一答案（tie 時 keep P2）：… 16 P2 27 P6 28 P5 35 → WT 0,7,0,0,8,0 → avg 都係 2.5。 注意 SJF：t=8 只有 P2 到咗 → P2 跑到 21（non-preemptive）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.6 F20 Q4.1（P1 0/9, P2 3/3, P3 8/2, P4 10/6, P5 16/8, P6 20/5）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-6", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Alg Gantt WT Avg FCFS 0 P1 9 P2 12 P3 14 P4 20 P5 28 P6 33 0,6,4,4,4,8 4.33 SJF 0 P1 9 P3 11 P2 14 P4 20 P6 25 P5 33 0,8,1,4,9,0 3.67 SRTF 0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 8 P3 10 P1 14 P4 20 P6 25 P5 33 5,0,0,4,9,0 3.0", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.7 F20 Q4.2 Two-level feedback queue with aging（Q0 RR4；Q1 FCFS；Q1 timer 8 ms → 返 Q0；被 preempt 返 Q1 head 並 reset timer）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-7", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P1 0/9, P2 3/3, P3 8/2, P4 10/9, P5 13/4, P6 18/5 0 P1 4 P2 7 P1 8 P3 10 P4 14 P5 18 P1 22 P6 26 P4 30 P6 31 P4 32 關鍵：t=8 P1（Q1）被 P3 preempt → Q1 head，timer reset 8；t=16 P1 timer 到 8 → 升返 Q0；t=22 P4（14 入 Q1）timer 到 8 → 升 Q0。 WT = 13, 1, 0, 13, 1, 8 → avg = 6", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.8 F20 Q4.3 Priority + RR（smaller = higher；preemptive；same priority RR q=4）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-8", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P1 0/9/2, P2 3/3/1, P3 8/5/4, P4 10/2/4, P5 16/8/5, P6 20/5/3 0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 12 P3 16 P4 18 P3 19 P5 20 P6 25 P5 32 WT = 3, 0, 6, 6, 8, 0 → avg =623​=3.83", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.9 Sp21 Q2.1（P1 0/2, P2 4/12, P3 6/6, P4 9/4, P5 13/5）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-9", "p": "Ch5", "x": "Alg Gantt WT Avg FCFS 0 P1 2 idle 4 P2 16 P3 22 P4 26 P5 31 0,0,10,13,13 7.2 SJF 0 P1 2 idle 4 P2 16 P4 20 P5 25 P3 31 0,0,19,7,7 6.6 RR q=8 0 P1 2 idle 4 P2 12 P3 18 P4 22 P2 26 P5 31 0,10,6,9,13 7.6 SRTF 0 P1 2 idle 4 P2 6 P3 12 P4 16 P5 21 P2 31 0,15,0,3,3 4.2", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.10 Sp21 Q2.2 MLFQ（Q0 q=2, Q1 q=4, Q2 FCFS）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-10", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P1 0/10, P2 10/8, P3 8/30, P4 16/2, P5 22/8, P6 6/22 0 P1 2 P1 6 P6 8 P3 10 P2 12 P6 16 P4 18 P3 22 P5 24 P2 28 P5 32 P1 36 P6 52 P3 76 P2 78 P5 80 (Q0)(Q1)(Q0)(Q0)(Q0)(Q1)(Q0)(Q1)(Q0)(Q1)(Q1)(Q2)(Q2)(Q2)(Q2)(Q2) WT：P1 26, P2 60, P3 38, P4 0, P5 50, P6 24 → avg = 33", "w": 3}, {"t": "14.11 HW2（P1 0/7, P2 1/3, P3 4/5, P4 10/2）", "u": "ch5.html#s14-11", "p": "Ch5", "x": "SRTF：0 P1 1 P2 4 P3 9 P1 10 P4 12 P1 17 → WT 10,0,0,0 → avg WT 2.5；avg TAT 6.75 RR q=3：0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 9 P3 12 P1 13 P4 15 P3 17 → WT 6,2,8,3 → avg WT 4.75；avg TAT 9 MLFQ（Q0 4, Q1 8, Q2 FCFS；P1 0/20, P2 1/5, P3 11/2）：0 P1(Q0) 4 P2(Q0) 8 P1(Q1) 11 P3(Q0) 13 P1(Q1) 18 P2(Q1) 19 P1(Q2) 27 t=11 P1 被 P3 preempt → Q1 head（喺 P2 前面），剩 5 ms quantum（唔係新 8 ms）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "15. 常見陷阱 & MC 速查", "u": "ch5.html#s15", "p": "Ch5", "x": "敘述 真/假 Scheduling on running→waiting / terminate = non-preemptive ✅ Timer interrupt (quantum expiry) = preemptive ✅ FCFS is preemptive ❌（FCFS must be non-preemptive — Sp21 MC） SJF gives minimum average waiting time ✅ SJF easy to implement ❌（need next burst length） SRTF ≥ SJF in performance ✅ RR with very large q → FCFS ✅ RR always better TAT than FCFS ❌ RR: no process waits more than (n−1)q ✅ Small q → high context-switch overhead ✅ Priority scheduling problem = starvation；solution = aging ✅ Multilevel queue: processes move between queues ❌（MLFQ 先可以） MLFQ requires known burst times ❌ MLFQ: each queue different algorithm ✅ Starvation-free: FCFS and RR ✅（Sp21 MC 答案 A：1, 4） OS schedules user th", "w": 3}, {"t": "16. 自我測驗", "u": "ch5.html#s16", "p": "Ch5", "x": "P1(0,5), P2(1,3), P3(2,1)：SRTF Gantt？（0 P1 1 P2 2 P3 3 P2 5 P1 9；WT = 4,1,0 → 5/3） 10 個 jobs 各 100 s 同時到，RR q=1 vs FCFS 平均 TAT？（RR ≈ 995.5 s；FCFS = 550 s） α=0.5, τ0​=10，bursts 6, 4 → τ2​？（τ1​=8；τ2​=6） 兩個 tasks：A (t=2, p=5)、B (t=4, p=10)：U = 0.8，RM 得唔得？（0.8 ≤ 0.828 → guaranteed） PCS vs SCS？ Push vs pull migration？", "w": 3}, {"t": "Ch6 Synchronization", "u": "ch6.html", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Chapter 6 — Synchronization Tools（同步工具） 📌 考試範圍提示：Midterm 包到 Ch6（Synchronization Tools）。每年都有 1 大題（8–25 分）+ 3–4 題 MC。題型： Race condition — 列出 shared variable 所有可能值（F19 Q4.3、F20 Q5：每個值 2.5 分！） Semaphore 次序控制填空（S26 Q5：wait/signal 填 blank）、semaphore 輸出序列判斷（F19 Q4.4） 用 atomic instruction 寫 spinlock（S26 Q2.4 swap；Sp21 Q3.1 compare_and_reset） Bounded-waiting test_and_set 算法分析 / 改錯（S26 MC25、Sp21 Q3.2） 定義：race condition、critical section 三個條件、spinlock 優缺點、binary semaphore vs mutex Pthread mutex + semaphore 程式輸出（S26 MC23）", "w": 3}, {"t": "1. Background（背景）[6.4–6.8]", "u": "ch6.html#s1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Processes execute concurrently and may be interrupted at any time, partially completing execution. Concurrent access to shared data may result in data inconsistency. Maintaining data consistency requires mechanisms to ensure the orderly execution of cooperating processes.", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.1 Producer–Consumer with counter [6.5–6.6]", "u": "ch6.html#s1-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "用 counter 記 buffer 有幾多 item（可以用晒 BUFFER_SIZE 格）： /* Producer */ /* Consumer */ while (true) { while (true) { while (counter == BUFFER_SIZE) ; while (counter == 0) ; buffer[in] = next_produced; next_consumed = buffer[out]; in = (in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE; out = (out + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE; counter++; counter--; } } 問題：counter++ / counter-- 唔係 atomic。", "w": 3}, {"t": "1.2 Race Condition 例子 ⭐⭐ [6.7]", "u": "ch6.html#s1-2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "counter++ → register1 = counter; register1 = register1 + 1; counter = register1 counter-- → register2 = counter; register2 = register2 - 1; counter = register2 counter = 5 initially，interleaving： Step Executed Result S0 producer: register1 = counter register1 = 5 S1 producer: register1 = register1 + 1 register1 = 6 S2 consumer: register2 = counter register2 = 5 S3 consumer: register2 = register2 − 1 register2 = 4 S4 producer: counter = register1 counter = 6 S5 consumer: counter = register2 counter = 4（正確應該係 5！） 調轉 S4、S5 → counter = 6。結果取決於執行次序 → race condition。 Kernel race example [6.8]：P0、P1 同時 fork() → race on kernel variable next_available_pid → same pid could be assigned to two different", "w": 3}, {"t": "2. Race Condition & Critical-Section Problem ⭐⭐⭐ [6.9–6.12]", "u": "ch6.html#s2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.1 Race condition 定義（S26 Q2.1 原題，4 分）", "u": "ch6.html#s2-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Sp21 MC：「several processes access and manipulate the same data concurrently and the outcome depends on the order」→ race condition。 根本原因（summary）：high-level statements (count++) translate into multiple machine instructions, which can be interleaved arbitrarily. 📝 Model answer：A race condition is an undesirable situation where several processes (or threads) access and manipulate shared data concurrently, and the final outcome depends on the particular order (timing) in which the accesses take place. Because the execution order is unpredictable (e.g., context switches at untimely points), the result can be incorrect / non-deterministic. Example: two threads execute counter++ on the same shared ", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.2 Critical Section", "u": "ch6.html#s2-2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Critical section：a segment of code in which a process may be changing shared variables, updating a table, writing a file, etc. When one process is in its critical section, no other may be in its critical section.（mutual exclusion and critical section imply the same thing） Critical-section problem：design a protocol that processes can use to cooperate. General structure [6.10]： do { entry section /* request permission */ critical section exit section remainder section } while (true);", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.3 三個 requirements ⭐⭐⭐（F19 Q4.1 原題）[6.11]", "u": "ch6.html#s2-3", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Mutual exclusion — if process Pi​ is executing in its critical section, no other processes can be executing in their critical sections. Progress — if no process is executing in its critical section and some processes wish to enter, the selection of the process that will enter next cannot be postponed indefinitely（只有唔喺 remainder section 嘅 process 先參與決定）。 Bounded waiting — a bound must exist on the number of times other processes are allowed to enter their critical sections after a process has made a request to enter its critical section and before that request is granted.（i.e. 等待時間有上限，no starvation） Assumptions：each process executes at a nonzero speed；no assumption about the relative speed of", "w": 3}, {"t": "2.4 Critical sections in the kernel [6.12]", "u": "ch6.html#s2-4", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Kernel data structures（list of open files, memory allocation, process table, interrupt handling）都有 race condition。 Preemptive kernel — allows preemption of a process running in kernel mode；not free from race conditions，SMP 上更難；但 more responsive（適合 real-time）。 Non-preemptive kernel — runs until it exits kernel mode, blocks, or voluntarily yields → essentially free of race conditions in kernel mode（single-processor）。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3. Synchronization Hardware ⭐⭐ [6.13–6.21]", "u": "ch6.html#s3", "p": "Ch6", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.1 Disabling interrupts [6.13]", "u": "ch6.html#s3-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Uniprocessor：could simply disable interrupts → current code runs without preemption。 Generally inefficient on multiprocessor/SMP systems（要 disable 所有 CPU 嘅 interrupts，費時；影響 system clock）。 層次：Hardware（load/store, disable interrupts, test&set, compare&swap）→ High-level APIs（locks, semaphores）→ programs。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.2 Atomic instructions [6.14]", "u": "ch6.html#s3-2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Atomic = non-interruptible；保證 instruction 唔會被中途打斷 → no race condition。 即使兩個 CPU 同時執行，they are forced to execute sequentially (serialization)。 Building blocks for more sophisticated synchronization tools. 兩個常用：test_and_set()（test a memory word and set a value）、compare_and_swap()（swap contents of two words）。 S26 MC24：commonly used hardware primitive → test_and_set()。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.3 test_and_set ⭐⭐ [6.15–6.16]", "u": "ch6.html#s3-3", "p": "Ch6", "x": "boolean test_and_set(boolean *target) { /* executed atomically */ boolean rv = *target; *target = TRUE; return rv; /* returns the OLD value */ } /* lock initialized to FALSE */ do { while (test_and_set(&lock)) ; /* spin while old value was TRUE (lock held) */ /* critical section */ lock = false; /* release */ /* remainder section */ } while (true); 第一個 call 嘅 process 攞到 old = FALSE → 入 CS，同時 lock 變 TRUE；其他人攞到 TRUE → spin。 ✅ mutual exclusion；❌ bounded waiting（冇保證輪到邊個）。 Sp21 MC4（lock init 0）：entry section → while (test_and_set(&lock));（C）。✗ while (!test_and_set(&lock))。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.4 compare_and_swap (CAS) ⭐⭐ [6.17–6.18]", "u": "ch6.html#s3-4", "p": "Ch6", "x": "int compare_and_swap(int *value, int expected, int new_value) { /* atomic */ int temp = *value; if (*value == expected) *value = new_value; return temp; /* returns the OLD value */ } /* lock initialized to 0 */ do { while (compare_and_swap(&lock, 0, 1) != 0) ; /* spin until old value was 0 */ /* critical section */ lock = 0; /* remainder section */ } while (true); ⚠️ 留意 return 值：CAS 返回 old value → 用 != 0 判斷。Sp21 MC4 ✗ while (compare_and_swap(&lock, 0, 1) == 0)（相反）、✗ compare_and_swap(&lock, 1, 0) != 0。", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.5 Bounded-waiting Mutual Exclusion with test_and_set ⭐⭐⭐ [6.19–6.20]", "u": "ch6.html#s3-5", "p": "Ch6", "x": "/* shared: boolean waiting[n] (all false), boolean lock = false */ do { waiting[i] = true; key = true; while (waiting[i] && key) key = test_and_set(&lock); waiting[i] = false; /* critical section */ j = (i + 1) % n; while ((j != i) && !waiting[j]) /* scan in cyclic order */ j = (j + 1) % n; if (j == i) lock = false; /* no one is waiting, so release the lock */ else waiting[j] = false; /* unblock process j (hand the lock over directly) */ /* remainder section */ } while (true); Sketch proof [6.20]： Mutual exclusion：Pi​ enters CS only if waiting[i] == false or key == false. key becomes false only via test_and_set() — only the first process to execute test_and_set finds key == false；others wait", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.6 Atomic Variables [6.21]", "u": "ch6.html#s3-6", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Atomic variable：atomic (uninterruptible) updates on basic data types (integers, booleans)，用 CAS 實現： void increment(atomic_int *v) { int temp; do { temp = *v; } while (temp != compare_and_swap(v, temp, temp + 1)); /* retry if someone changed v */ }", "w": 3}, {"t": "3.7 用其他 atomic instruction 寫 spinlock（考試題型）⭐⭐", "u": "ch6.html#s3-7", "p": "Ch6", "x": "S26 Q2.4 — swap(x, v)：atomically sets x = v and returns the old value of x（locked = 1 held / 0 free）： while (swap(locked, 1) == 1) ; /* acquire: spin while the old value was 1 */ /* critical section */ locked = 0; /* release (or swap(locked, 0)) */ Sp21 Q3.1 — compare_and_reset(int *a, int old, int new)：if (*a == old) { *a = new; return 1; } else return 0; struct spinlock { int value = 0; }; void acquire(struct spinlock *lock) { while (compare_and_reset(&lock->value, 0, 1) == 0) ; /* spin until we changed 0 → 1 */ } void release(struct spinlock *lock) { lock->value = 0; } 萬用套路：搵出「成功攞到 lock」時 instruction 嘅 return 值 → while (instruction(...) 表示失敗) ; → release 就 set 返 free value。 Instruction Ac", "w": 3}, {"t": "4. Mutex Locks ⭐⭐ [6.22–6.23]", "u": "ch6.html#s4", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Simplest software tool most OSes use. Protect a critical region by first acquire() a lock, then release() it — both must be atomic（usually via hardware atomic instructions）。 Boolean variable available 表示 lock 有冇得用： acquire() { while (!available) ; /* busy wait */ available = false; } release() { available = true; } do { acquire lock; critical section; release lock; remainder section; } while (true); This solution requires busy waiting → the lock is called a spinlock. Wait if locked — 等耐咗應該 sleep / block 而唔係 spin。", "w": 3}, {"t": "4.1 Spinlock 優缺點 ⭐⭐⭐（S26 Q2.4b 原題）", "u": "ch6.html#s4-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "單 CPU 用 spinlock 冇乜意思（spin 緊嘅 thread 佔住 CPU，持有 lock 嘅 thread 跑唔到）。 📝 Model answer：Disadvantage: a spinlock wastes CPU cycles due to busy waiting (the waiting thread keeps looping). Advantage: no context switch is required when a process must wait on a lock, and a context switch may take considerable time. So spinlocks are useful when locks are expected to be held for short times, especially on multiprocessor systems, where one thread can spin on one processor while another thread executes its critical section on another processor.", "w": 3}, {"t": "5. Semaphores ⭐⭐⭐ [6.24–6.28]", "u": "ch6.html#s5", "p": "Ch6", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.1 定義 [6.24]", "u": "ch6.html#s5-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Semaphore S — a (non-negative) integer variable, a generalized lock；Dijkstra (late 1960s)；main synchronization primitive in original UNIX。 Accessed only through two atomic operations (apart from initialization)：wait() and signal()（originally P() = proberen, to test；V() = verhogen, to increment）。 Atomic → no more than one process can execute wait()/signal() on the same semaphore at the same time (serialization). wait(S) { signal(S) { while (S <= 0) ; S++; S--; } }", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.2 Counting vs Binary [6.25] ⭐", "u": "ch6.html#s5-2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Counting semaphore — value ranges over an unrestricted domain；control access to a resource with a finite number of instances（initialize to the number of available resources）；wait = 攞一個，signal = 還一個。 Binary semaphore — value only 0 or 1；can behave like a mutex lock，但亦可以用嚟做其他同步。 Ordering example（S1 喺 S2 之前執行）：synch = 0 P1: S1; signal(synch); P2: wait(synch); S2; 📝 F19 Q4.2：Binary semaphore = mutex lock? → No. A binary semaphore initialized to 1 can be used as a mutex lock, but a binary semaphore can also be used for other synchronization purposes, e.g., initialized to 0 to enforce execution order / signaling between processes. (Also a mutex lock is owned — the thread that locks it must unlock ", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.3 Implementation with no busy waiting ⭐⭐ [6.26–6.27]", "u": "ch6.html#s5-3", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Each semaphore has a waiting queue（each entry：value + pointer to next record）。 block() — place the invoking process on the waiting queue（suspend）；wakeup(P) — remove a process from the waiting queue and put it in the ready queue。 typedef struct { int value; struct process *list; } semaphore; wait(semaphore *S) { S->value--; if (S->value < 0) { add this process to S->list; block(); } } signal(semaphore *S) { S->value++; if (S->value <= 0) { remove a process P from S->list; wakeup(P); } } ⭐ Increment/decrement is done BEFORE checking the value（同 busy-waiting 版相反）。 ⭐ Semaphore value may become negative；if negative, its magnitude = number of processes waiting on it.（busy-waiting classical 版永遠唔會 n", "w": 3}, {"t": "5.4 Deadlock and Starvation ⭐ [6.28]", "u": "ch6.html#s5-4", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Deadlock — two or more processes waiting indefinitely for an event that can be caused only by one of the waiting processes. S = Q = 1 P0: wait(S); wait(Q); ... signal(S); signal(Q); P1: wait(Q); wait(S); ... signal(Q); signal(S); P0 wait(S)、P1 wait(Q) 之後：P0 等 Q（要 P1 signal），P1 等 S（要 P0 signal）→ deadlock（extremely difficult to debug）。 Starvation (indefinite blocking) — a process may never be removed from the semaphore queue，例如用 LIFO order 或者按 priority 移除 queue 入面嘅 processes。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6. 做題方法 ✍️⭐⭐⭐", "u": "ch6.html#s6", "p": "Ch6", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.1 Race condition 所有可能值（F19 Q4.3、F20 Q5）", "u": "ch6.html#s6-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "步驟： 將每個 thread 拆成 atomic reads/writes（c = b + a → read b, read a, write c；if 條件嘅 read 係另一個 read！）。 另一個 thread 嘅 writes 可以插喺任何 read 之間（次序保持）。 按 「條件 read 嗰陣見到嘅值」分 branch，再按「每個 read 見到新值定舊值」分 sub-case。 列出所有 distinct 值（唔好漏！每個值獨立計分）。 F19 Q4.3：a = 7, b = 2, c = 5；T1：if (a < 0) c = b - a; else c = b + a;；T2：b = 8; a = -6; Case T1 見到 c T2 全部喺 T1 之前 → a<0 branch b = 8, a = −6 8 − (−6) = 14 else branch；T2 喺 c 計之前完成 b = 8, a = −6 8 + (−6) = 2 else；T2 喺 T1 之後 b = 2, a = 7 9 else；write b 喺 read b 前，write a 喺 read a 後 b = 8, a = 7 15 else；read b 喺 write b 前，write a 喺 read a 前 b = 2, a = −6 −4 → {14, 2, 9, 15, −4} F20 Q5：a = 7, b = 2；T1：if (b < 0) c = b - a; else c = b + a;；T2：b = -8; a = 6; b<0 branch（wri", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.2 Semaphore 次序（dependency）題（S26 Q5）", "u": "ch6.html#s6-2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "規則：「X 必須喺 Y 之前完成」→ 用一個 init 0 嘅 semaphore s：X 完成後 signal(s)；Y 開始前 wait(s)。每個 dependency edge 一個 semaphore。 S26 Q5：A depends on B and C；B depends on C（即 C → B → A，且 C → A）；s0, s1, s2 全部 init 0。 workerA: /*BLANK1*/ wait(&s2); wait(&s1); printf(\"A\"); // 等 B (s2) 同 C (s1) workerB: /*BLANK2*/ wait(&s0); printf(\"B\"); signal(&s2); // 等 C (s0)，完成通知 A workerC: /*BLANK3*/ /* Nothing */ printf(\"C\"); /*BLANK4*/ signal(&s0); signal(&s1); BLANK1 = wait(&s2)，BLANK2 = wait(&s0)，BLANK3 = /* Nothing */，BLANK4 = signal(&s0) → 輸出一定係 C B A。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.3 Semaphore 輸出序列題（F19 Q4.4）", "u": "ch6.html#s6-3", "p": "Ch6", "x": "semaphore L = 3, R = 0; P1: while (true) { wait(L); putc('C'); signal(R); } P2: while (true) { wait(R); putc('A'); putc('B'); signal(R); } P3: while (true) { wait(R); putc('D'); } 方法：數 token。L = 3 → P1 最多印 3 個 C（冇人 signal L）。每個 C 產生 1 個 R token；P2 借 token 印 AB 後還返；P3 食咗 token 唔還。 (a) D 數目 = R token 總數 = 3 (b) A：最少 0（P3 搶晒 token），最多 infinite（P2 無限借還） (c) CABABDCDABCABD → No：印到 C A B A B D C D 時 C 印咗 2 次、D 食咗 2 個 token → R = 0，下一個 A（P2 需要 wait(R)）冇 token，要等第 3 個 C 先得。 (d) CABACDBCABDD → Yes：C(R=1) A(P2 借→0) B(還→1) A(借→0) C(→1) D(食→0) B(還→1) C(→2) A(借→1) B(還→2) D(→1) D(→0) ✓ 技巧：逐個字元追蹤 semaphore 值同每個 process「做到邊」；任何時刻 token 唔可以 < 0。", "w": 3}, {"t": "6.4 Pthread 程式輸出題（S26 MC23）", "u": "ch6.html#s6-4", "p": "Ch6", "x": "int counter = 0; pthread_mutex_t mtx; sem_t sem; /* sem_init(&sem, 0, 0) */ worker1: lock; counter += 2; printf(\"A %d;\", counter); unlock; sem_post(&sem); worker2: sem_wait(&sem); lock; counter += 3; printf(\"B %d;\", counter); unlock; sem 初始 0 → worker2 一定等 worker1 post → 輸出 A 2; B 5;（答案 A）。mutex 保證 counter 更新互斥。", "w": 3}, {"t": "7. 補充：Peterson（Lab 6）& classic problems（Ch7 預覽）", "u": "ch6.html#s7", "p": "Ch6", "x": "", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.1 Peterson's Solution（two processes, software-only；Lab 6 有教 + 證明，見 Labs notes L6.1）", "u": "ch6.html#s7-1", "p": "Ch6", "x": "int turn; boolean flag[2]; /* Pi uses j = 1 - i */ do { flag[i] = true; turn = j; while (flag[j] && turn == j) ; /* critical section */ flag[i] = false; /* remainder section */ } while (true); 滿足 mutual exclusion、progress、bounded waiting（兩個 process）；但 modern architectures may reorder instructions → not guaranteed to work without memory barriers。", "w": 3}, {"t": "7.2 Classic problems with semaphores（Ch7 預覽）", "u": "ch6.html#s7-2", "p": "Ch6", "x": "Bounded buffer：mutex = 1, full = 0, empty = n Producer: wait(empty); wait(mutex); add item; signal(mutex); signal(full); Consumer: wait(full); wait(mutex); remove item; signal(mutex); signal(empty); ⚠️ 調轉 wait(mutex) 同 wait(empty) 次序 → 可能 deadlock。 First readers–writers：rw_mutex = 1, mutex = 1, read_count = 0；第一個 reader wait(rw_mutex)，最後一個 reader signal(rw_mutex)；writers 可能 starve。 Condition variables (POSIX)：pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &mutex)（必須持有 mutex；wait 時釋放 mutex 並 sleep，醒後重新 acquire）；pthread_cond_signal(&cond)。", "w": 3}, {"t": "8. 常見陷阱 & MC 速查", "u": "ch6.html#s8", "p": "Ch6", "x": "敘述 真/假 Race condition: outcome depends on order of access ✅ counter++ is a single atomic machine instruction ❌ CS requirements: mutual exclusion, progress, bounded waiting ✅ \"Synchronization\" is one of the 3 CS requirements ❌ Bounded waiting implies progress ✅（summary） Disabling interrupts works well on multiprocessors ❌（only uniprocessor） Atomic = non-interruptible ✅ test_and_set returns the new value ❌（old value） CAS returns old value ✅ Simple TAS lock satisfies bounded waiting ❌ Bounded-waiting TAS: waiting process enters within n−1 turns ✅ test_and_set releases the lock ❌ Spinlock wastes CPU but avoids context switch ✅ Spinlocks useful on multiprocessors for short critical sections ✅ Sem", "w": 3}, {"t": "9. Past-paper 題目（Ch6）", "u": "ch6.html#s9", "p": "Ch6", "x": "S26：MC23（A 2; B 5;）、MC24（test_and_set）、MC25（A 錯）、Q2.1 race condition（4 分）、Q2.4 swap spinlock + 優缺點（4 分）、Q5 semaphore 填空（8 分） F19：Q4.1 三個條件（4 分）、Q4.2 binary semaphore vs mutex（3 分）、Q4.3 c 可能值（8 分）、Q4.4 semaphore 序列（10 分） F20：Q5 c 可能值（15 分！） Sp21：MC mutual exclusion、MC CS 唔需要 synchronization、MC race condition、MC4 TAS entry、Q3.1 compare_and_reset spinlock（4 分）、Q3.2 bounded waiting 改錯（7 分）", "w": 3}, {"t": "10. 自我測驗", "u": "ch6.html#s10", "p": "Ch6", "x": "寫出 CS 三個 requirements 嘅定義（英文）。 用 test_and_set 寫 lock；點解唔滿足 bounded waiting？ 一個 semaphore value = −3 代表咩？（3 個 processes 喺 waiting queue） x = 0；T1：x = x + 1；T2：x = x * 2（每句 read/write 分開）→ 可能值？（2: T1→T2；1: T2→T1；1: T1 read 0, T2 read 0 & write 0, T1 write 1；0: T2 read 0, T1 write 1, T2 write 0 → {0, 1, 2}） 三個 threads 要 A → B → C 次序，用最少 semaphores 寫出來。（s1 = s2 = 0；A; signal(s1) | wait(s1); B; signal(s2) | wait(s2); C）", "w": 3}, {"t": "Labs & PA1/PA2", "u": "labs.html", "p": "Labs", "x": "Labs 1–6 & PA1 / PA2（實驗課 + 功課考點） 📌 點解要睇：Midterm email 講明「There may be questions related to PA or coding questions using some APIs we discussed in our lab classes」；Lab 3 slide 亦講「PA-related questions in exam — it is crucial to have a thorough understanding of the project assignments」。S26 Q3.3 直接叫你「refer to the Lab 3 notes for open, pipe, read, write」。 ✅ 本頁所有 fork 數目、semaphore 輸出、race 值、Gantt chart 都已用真 C 程式 / 窮舉 / 模擬器驗證，同 lab 官方答案一致。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L1. Lab 1 — Linux 環境 & Shell 指令", "u": "labs.html#l1-lab-1-linux-shell", "p": "Labs", "x": "Terminal = 畫面/視窗；Shell = 解讀指令嘅程式（bash, tcsh…）→ shell fetches, interprets, executes commands（Ch2 [2.8]）。 常用指令： 指令 用途 指令 用途 pwd 顯示目前目錄 cd dir / cd .. / cd ~ 轉目錄 ls -l -h / ls -a 列出檔案（詳細 / 包括隱藏） mkdir / rmdir 建立 / 刪除目錄 cp a b / mv a b / rm f 複製 / 搬或改名 / 刪除 cat / less / head / tail 睇檔案 ps / ps aux 列出 processes（PID） kill PID 送 signal 畀 process wc -l 數行數 sort / grep x 排序 / 搵字 diff a b 比較檔案（PA 對答案） chmod u+x f 加執行權限 du -ah | sort -rh | less 睇 disk 用量（CS Lab 2 quota 100MB） vim / nano 文字編輯器", "w": 1}, {"t": "L2. Lab 2 — C in Linux、編譯流程、Ch1–3 Review", "u": "labs.html#l2-lab-2-c-in-linux", "p": "Labs", "x": "", "w": 1}, {"t": "L2.1 Compile pipeline（同 Ch2 linker/loader 掛鈎）", "u": "labs.html#l2-1-compile-pipeline-ch2-linker", "p": "Labs", "x": "最簡：gcc prog.c -o prog → ./prog（loader 將 executable 載入 memory）。 gcc -c main.c → main.o（relocatable object）；gcc -o main main.o -lm → linker 連 math library（-lm，用 <math.h> 時要加）。 -g：加 debugging info → gdb prog；Pthreads：gcc prog.c -lpthread（或 -pthread）。 Makefile：target: prerequisites + 下一行 TAB（唔可以用空格）+ command；make / make clean。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L2.2 I/O Subsystem 補充（Lab 2 review slides）", "u": "labs.html#l2-2-i-o-subsystem-lab", "p": "Labs", "x": "Buffering — store data temporarily while being transferred；Caching — faster device holding a copy of data。 ⭐ 分別：a buffer may hold the only existing copy of a data item, whereas a cache only holds a copy on faster storage of an item that resides elsewhere. Spooling — hold output for a device that cannot accept interleaved data（e.g. printer）。 I/O hardware：port（connection point）、bus（PCI / PCIe、expansion bus for slow devices）、controller (host adapter)；device registers：data-in, data-out, status, control。 Device driver presents a uniform device-access interface to the I/O subsystem（abstraction）。 Interrupt 補充：maskable vs nonmaskable；interrupt vector = table of interrupt-handler addresses；multilevel", "w": 1}, {"t": "L2.3 Lab 2 fork 題（已驗證 ✅）", "u": "labs.html#l2-3-lab-2-fork", "p": "Labs", "x": "int main() { pid_t pid = fork(); int cnt = 0; if (pid == 0) { cnt += 10; pid = fork(); if (pid != 0) { cnt += 10; pid = fork(); } } printf(\"%d \\n\", pid); printf(\"%d \\n\", cnt); return 0; } 4 個 processes 印出：P0（pid≠0, cnt 0）、C1（pid≠0, cnt 20）、C2（pid 0, cnt 10）、C3（pid 0, cnt 20）。 non-zero PID 印 2 次；'0' 印 3 次（P0 嘅 cnt + C2、C3 嘅 pid）；'10' 印 1 次。 填空：保證輸出 process 3 → 2 → 1（每個都印 x = 1）： if (fork()) { // BLANK1: fork()（> 0 亦可） x = x + 1; wait(0); // BLANK2: wait(0) / wait(NULL) printf(\"...process 1 is %d\\n\", x); } else if (fork()) { // BLANK3: fork() x = x + 1; wait(0); // BLANK4: wait(0) printf(\"...process 2 is %d\\n\", x); } else { x = x + 1; printf(\"...process 3 is %d\\n\", x); }", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3. Lab 3 — Linux Process & Pipe System Calls（⭐⭐⭐ PA1 + 考試 API）", "u": "labs.html#l3-lab-3-linux-process-pipe", "p": "Labs", "x": "", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.1 本 lab 用到嘅 system calls", "u": "labs.html#l3-1-lab-system-calls", "p": "Labs", "x": "Process：getpid, getppid, sleep, fork, wait, signal File：open, read, write, close, pipe, dup, dup2 Execute：execl, execlp, execle, execv, execvp, execvpe Call 重點 pid_t getpid(void) / getppid(void) 自己 / parent 嘅 PID unsigned int sleep(unsigned int s) 停 s 秒（usleep 用 µs；nanosleep 更細） pid_t fork(void) child 0、parent child PID、−1 error（set errno）；child 得到 parent address space（text, data, bss, heap, stack）同 open file descriptors 嘅獨立 copy pid_t wait(int *status) 等任何一個 child；wait(0) / wait(NULL) 唔要 status；返回 child PID；課程只用 wait()（waitpid 唔使） signal(SIGINT, handler) signal = software-generated interrupt；可以用 customized handler 取代 default；child inherits the customized handler", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.2 Zombie / Orphan demo（lab 示範）", "u": "labs.html#l3-2-zombie-orphan-demo-lab", "p": "Labs", "x": "Orphan：parent 先完（冇 wait）→ shell prompt 出咗嚟之後 child 先繼續印 → child 被 init (PID 1) adopt & reap。 用 wait() 正確：parent 印完 wait(0) → child 印完先返 shell。 Zombie：child 已完但 parent 仲 sleep(10) 未 wait() → child 喺嗰段時間係 zombie；parent wait(0) 後清走。 Exit status：voluntary — normal exit(0)（return from main / exit / _exit）、error exit(1)；involuntary — fatal error（divide by 0, segmentation fault, core dump）、killed by another process（kill()）。child 終止時：open files flushed & closed、resources de-allocated、parent notified via a signal、exit status via wait()。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.3 File operations（低階 vs stdio）", "u": "labs.html#l3-3-file-operations-vs-stdio", "p": "Labs", "x": "fd = open(\"hello.txt\", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, S_IRUSR | S_IWUSR); /* permission 600 */ write(fd, \"hello\\n\", 6); close(fd); fd = open(\"hello.txt\", O_RDONLY); read(fd, buffer, 6); close(fd); open/read/write/close（<fcntl.h>, <unistd.h>）= low-level system calls；fopen/fread/fwrite/fclose（<stdio.h>）= high-level C library。 ⭐ PA 唔准用 stdio file ops：FILE* 係 C library 嘅 struct，cannot be used in pipe operations。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.4 Pipes", "u": "labs.html#l3-4-pipes", "p": "Labs", "x": "Key concept：treat a pipe as 2 files；int pipe(int pfds[2]) → pfds[0] read end，pfds[1] write end。 Data received in the order it was sent；OS enforces mutual exclusion（only one process at a time）；processes sharing the pipe must have the same parent。 ⭐ read/write are blocking：冇 data 讀 → reader block；pipe 滿 → writer block（pipe size 有限：4 KB page / 64 KB）。 Two-way communication → create two pipes（每個方向一條）。 Shell pipe ps aux | grep root | sort | wc -l：前一個 command 嘅 output 做下一個嘅 input。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.5 exec family", "u": "labs.html#l3-5-exec-family", "p": "Labs", "x": "6 variants：execl, execlp, execle, execv, execvp, execvpe — replace the current process image；成功唔會 return；error return −1。 字母意思：l = arguments as a list（數目已知）；v = arguments in a vector/array（compile time 唔知數目，PA1 用 execvp）；p = search PATH；e = pass environment。 execlp(\"ls\", \"ls\", \"-l\", \"-h\", NULL); /* same as running \"ls -l -h\" */ char *args[] = {\"ls\", \"-l\", \"-h\", NULL}; execvp(\"ls\", args); printf(\"never printed if exec succeeds\\n\");", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.6 dup / dup2（redirection 核心）", "u": "labs.html#l3-6-dup-dup2-redirection", "p": "Labs", "x": "int dup(int oldfd) — copy 到 first unused (lowest-numbered) fd；int dup2(int oldfd, int newfd) — copy 到指定 newfd（如已開先 close）；成功返回新 fd，失敗 −1。 Default：0 = stdin，1 = stdout，2 = stderr。 Lab 題：以下程式輸出係咩？ fd = open(\"output.txt\", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, S_IRUSR | S_IWUSR); close(1); /* close stdout */ dup(fd); /* lowest free fd = 1 → stdout now refers to output.txt */ printf(\"Hello World!\\n\"); fflush(stdout); → 螢幕冇嘢顯示；\"Hello World!\" 寫入 output.txt。 ls | wc -l 實作（lab 版本：parent 做 wc）： pipe(pfds); if (fork() == 0) { /* child → ls */ close(1); dup(pfds[1]); /* stdout = pipe write end */ close(pfds[0]); execlp(\"ls\", \"ls\", NULL); } else { /* parent → wc -l */ close(0); dup(pfds[0]); /* stdin = pipe read end */ cl", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.7 Lab 3 fork 例題 + 更多歷屆 fork 題（全部驗證 ✅）", "u": "labs.html#l3-7-lab-3-fork-fork", "p": "Labs", "x": "題目 Code（重點） 答案 Ex1 for (i=0;i<3;i++) fork(); 23=8 Ex2 for(i<2){ fork(); for(j<3) if(!fork()){} } 8 個連續 fork → 28=256 Ex3 for(i<10){ if(fork()){fork();fork();} else fork(); } 每輪 ×6（parent 4 + child 2）→ 610 Fall 2022 Q3 for(i<n){ if (fork() == fork()) printf(...); } processes 4n；lines 40+41+⋯+4n−1=34n−1​（n=1,2,3：1, 5, 21 行） Fall 2024 P3.1 for(i<M){ pid = fork(); if(!pid) for(j<N) fork(); } (a) child 執行 line Y（!pid true）；(b) M=N=1 → 3；(c) (2N+1)M Spring 2025 MC12 if (fork() || fork()) { if (fork() && !fork()) fork(); } 9 processes（新建 8 個） Fall 2022 解釋：每輪每個 process 變 4 個（2 個 fork）→ 4n；每輪只有「兩個 fork 都返回 0」嗰個 grandchild 印 → 第 k 輪印 4k−1 行。 Fall 2024 解釋：設 f(k) = 剩 k 輪時一個 process 最終變幾多個：parent 自己繼續 → ", "w": 1}, {"t": "L3.8 PA1 — Simplified Linux Shell（考試可能問）", "u": "labs.html#l3-8-pa1-simplified-linux-shell", "p": "Labs", "x": "6 種 command pattern：cmd、cmd < in、cmd > out、cmd < in > out、cmd > out < in、cmd1 | cmd2 | …（最多 8 段）。 cd 唔可以用 exec 做（要改 shell 自己嘅 working directory）→ chdir(path)（0 成功 / −1 失敗）。 唔准用 system() / popen()（會開另一個 shell 代你做）。 Redirection = open + dup/dup2 取代 stdin/stdout；multi-level pipe = 將 2-level pipe 寫成 for-loop。 execvp 嘅 argv array 尾要放 NULL（所以 8 個 arguments 要 size 9）。 Ctrl-C → signal(SIGINT, handler)，SIGINT = 2；start/end PID 要一致（冇多餘 processes）。 Plagiarism：JPlag 檢測，提供同抄襲雙方都 0 分。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L4. Lab 4 — Named Pipes & Pthreads", "u": "labs.html#l4-lab-4-named-pipes-pthreads", "p": "Labs", "x": "", "w": 1}, {"t": "L4.1 Anonymous vs Named pipes（lab 版本）", "u": "labs.html#l4-1-anonymous-vs-named-pipes", "p": "Labs", "x": "Anonymous：unidirectional；cannot be accessed outside the creating process；parent–child；ceases after processes terminate。 Named (FIFO)：bidirectional；no parent–child needed；several processes（多 writers）；appear as files（mkfifo()）；exist until explicitly deleted。 ⭐ FIFO 細節： open / read / write 係 blocking：FIFO 要兩端都有 process open 先會 unblock（冇 reader 時 writer 會 block）；用 O_NONBLOCK 可以變 non-blocking。 Data read once then discarded（唔似 regular file 可以重讀）→ cannot broadcast to multiple recipients。 實際上 一條 FIFO 唔適合雙向（寫完即刻讀可能讀返自己個 message）→ 雙向用兩條。 Writes ≤ PIPE_BUF bytes are atomic；更大可能同其他 writer 交錯。 popen(cmd, \"r\"/\"w\") / pclose(fp)（<stdio.h>）：fork 一個 child 執行 cmd，返回 FILE* 連住佢嘅 stdout（\"r\"）或 stdin（\"w\"）；pclose 等佢", "w": 1}, {"t": "L4.2 Exam example — 兩條 ordinary pipes 雙向通訊（填空）", "u": "labs.html#l4-2-exam-example-ordinary-pipes", "p": "Labs", "x": "char buffer[10] = {}; int p2c[2], c2p[2]; pipe(p2c); /* BLANK1 */ pipe(c2p); /* BLANK2 */ pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { /* child */ const char msg[] = \"Child\"; close(c2p[0]); write(c2p[1], msg, 6); /* BLANK3（5 亦接受） */ close(p2c[1]); read(p2c[0], buffer, 7); /* BLANK4（6 亦接受） */ printf(\"Child received the message: %s\\n\", buffer); } else { /* parent */ const char msg[] = \"Parent\"; close(c2p[1]); read(c2p[0], buffer, 6); /* BLANK5（5 亦接受） */ close(p2c[0]); write(p2c[1], msg, 7); /* BLANK6（6 亦接受） */ printf(\"Parent received the message: %s\\n\", buffer); } 輸出：Parent received the message: Child / Child received the message: Parent。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L4.3 Pthreads", "u": "labs.html#l4-3-pthreads", "p": "Labs", "x": "int sum; /* shared by the threads */ void *runner(void *param) { int upper = atoi(param); sum = 0; for (int i = 1; i <= upper; i++) sum += i; pthread_exit(0); } int main(int argc, char *argv[]) { pthread_t tid; pthread_attr_t attr; pthread_attr_init(&attr); /* default attributes */ pthread_create(&tid, &attr, runner, argv[1]); /* id, attr, start routine, arg */ pthread_join(tid, NULL); /* wait for the thread */ printf(\"sum = %d\\n\", sum); } pthread_t = thread id；pthread_attr_t = attributes（stack size, scheduling info）；compile -lpthread。 Join 多個 threads：for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) pthread_join(workers[i], NULL); Thread trace：main 印 \"main: begin\" → create T1、T2 → join → \"main: end\"；threads run ind", "w": 1}, {"t": "L4.4 Exam example — thread vs process 共享 memory（MC）", "u": "labs.html#l4-4-exam-example-thread-vs", "p": "Labs", "x": "void *child(void *arg) { int *counter = (int *)arg; (*counter)++; return NULL; } int main() { int counter = 0; pthread_t thread; pthread_create(&thread, NULL, child, (void *)&counter); pthread_join(thread, NULL); printf(\"%d\\n\", counter); /* 1: thread shares memory */ counter = 0; pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid) wait(NULL); else { child((void *)&counter); exit(0); } printf(\"%d\\n\", counter); /* 0: child process has its own copy */ } 選項 A 0 0、B 1 0、C 0 1、D 1 1 → 答案 B（1 / 0）。Key：threads share memory space but processes have separate memory spaces.", "w": 1}, {"t": "L5. Lab 5 — Scheduling Review & PA2（MLFQ）", "u": "labs.html#l5-lab-5-scheduling-review-pa2", "p": "Labs", "x": "", "w": 1}, {"t": "L5.1 Review 例題（P1 7, P2 4, P3 1, P4 4，全部 t=0，已驗證 ✅）", "u": "labs.html#l5-1-review-p1-7-p2", "p": "Labs", "x": "Algorithm Gantt avg WT FCFS / RR q=8 0 P1 7 P2 11 P3 12 P4 16 40+7+11+12​=7.5 RR q=4 0 P1 4 P2 8 P3 9 P4 13 P1 16 49+4+8+9​=7.5 RR q=2 0 P1 2 P2 4 P3 5 P4 7 P1 9 P2 11 P4 13 P1 16 49+7+4+9​=7.25 SJF 0 P3 1 P2 5 P4 9 P1 16（P2、P4 同長 → 先 P2） 49+1+0+5​=3.75 Priority（4,1,2,3） 0 P2 4 P3 5 P4 9 P1 16 49+0+4+5​=4.5 SRTF（P1 0/7, P2 2/4, P3 4/1, P4 5/4）：0 P1 2 P2 4 P3 5 P2 7 P4 11 P1 16 → 49+1+0+2​=3。 觀察：q 大過最長 burst → RR = FCFS；q 細咗 avg WT 唔一定變差（7.25 < 7.5）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L5.2 ⭐ Priority + RR 有唔同 arrival time（HW / Exam 熱門）", "u": "labs.html#l5-2-priority-rr-arrival-time", "p": "Labs", "x": "⚠️ Common mistake：reset the time quantum of a preempted process. 正確：被高 priority 新 process preempt 嘅 process 要記住佢剩餘嘅 quantum（同 MLFQ 規則一樣）。 Spring 2023 midterm 官方答案（q = 2）：0 P1 1 P2 4 P1 5 P3 7 P1 8 P4 10 P1 11 P3 13 P5 15 P1 16 P3 18 P5 20 P3 22 P5 24 P1 喺 0–1 跑咗 1 unit 被 P2（高 priority）preempt → 4–5 只跑剩低嘅 1 unit quantum 然後輪到 P3（同 priority RR）。8–10 同樣：P4 preempt P1，P1 10–11 用返剩低 1 unit。 由 Gantt 推返嘅 table（模擬器重現完全一致 ✅）：P1 (0, 5, pr 2)、P2 (1, 3, pr 1)、P3 (2, 8, pr 2)、P4 (8, 2, pr 1)、P5 (9, 6, pr 2)，細數字 = 高 priority。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L5.3 RM / EDF 例題（deadline < period）", "u": "labs.html#l5-3-rm-edf-deadline-period", "p": "Labs", "x": "P1 (t=1, d=2, p=4)、P2 (2, 5, 6)、P3 (3, 7, 9)；U=41​+62​+93​=0.917。 RM（P1 > P2 > P3）：0 P1 1 P2 3 P3 4 P1 5 P3 6 P2 8 P1 9 P3 … → P3 first job finishes at 10 > deadline 7 → misses 1 deadline。 EDF：0 P1 1 P2 3 P3 4 P1 5 P3 7 P2 8 P1 9 P2 10 P3 12 … → no deadline missed。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L5.4 PA2 — N-level MLFQ simulator", "u": "labs.html#l5-4-pa2-n-level-mlfq", "p": "Labs", "x": "N = 1–4 queues，last queue must be FCFS（input 用大數 e.g. 1000 代表 FCFS），≤ 10 processes。 Input：queue_num = 3、time_quantum = 2 4 1000、process_table_size、process_table（P1 0 7 …）；# 開頭 / 空行忽略。 Assumptions：sorted by arrival；first arrival = 0；no two processes arrive at the same time；CPU never idle；Gantt ≤ 300 steps。 用法：./mlfq < input.txt > output.txt（input/output redirection）；用 diff --side-by-side your-out.txt sample-out.txt（--suppress-common-lines）對答案。 Output：parsed values + Gantt Chart = 0 P1 2 P2 3 P1 4 P3 5 P1 6 P4 8 P1 10 P4 11 P1 12。 Step-by-step 例子（Q0 q=2, Q1 q=4, Q2 FCFS；P1 0/7, P2 1/1, P3 4/1, P4 6/3），已用模擬器驗證 ✅： t=1 P2 到 → no preemption（P1 都喺 Q0）；t=2 P1 用完 Q0 quantum → Q1；P2 跑。 t=3 P2 完 → P1（Q", "w": 1}, {"t": "L6. Lab 6 — Synchronization Review（Sample Questions）", "u": "labs.html#l6-lab-6-synchronization-review-sample", "p": "Labs", "x": "", "w": 1}, {"t": "L6.1 Peterson's solution（software-only，2 processes）", "u": "labs.html#l6-1-peterson-s-solution-software", "p": "Labs", "x": "/* P_i (j = 1 - i) */ do { flag[i] = true; turn = j; while (flag[j] && turn == j) ; /* busy wait */ /* critical section */ flag[i] = false; /* remainder section */ } while (true); Shared：flag[2]（想入 CS）、turn（輪到邊個）；assumption：load and store are atomic。 Proof：ME — 兩個都想入時 turn 只可以係 0 或 1 → 只有一個過到 while；Progress — 冇人喺 CS 就即刻入；Bounded waiting — 最多等對方一次（對方出嚟會設 flag[j] = false，再想入就要設 turn = i）。 缺點：複雜（只限兩個 process）、每個 thread code 唔同、busy waiting 浪費 CPU → OS 提供 mutex / semaphore / condition variable。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L6.2 POSIX primitives 速查", "u": "labs.html#l6-2-posix-primitives", "p": "Labs", "x": "Mutex：pthread_mutex_init(&m, NULL) → lock → access → unlock → destroy；thread 持 lock 時 crash → 其他人 block。 Semaphore（<semaphore.h>）：sem_init(&s, pshared, value)（pshared = 0：同一 process 嘅 threads 共用）、sem_wait、sem_post、sem_destroy。 Initial value 行為 用途 1 一個 permit mutual exclusion（當 mutex） 0 wait() 一定 block 直至有人 signal ordering（等其他 thread 做完先） N N 個 permits 管理 N 個相同 resources Condition variable：pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &mutex) atomically releases the mutex and sleeps；signalled → re-acquires the mutex before returning；用 while (!ready) 包住；pthread_cond_signal / pthread_cond_broadcast。 Lab note：semaphore ≈ integer S + a mutex protecting it + a condition variable whose condition is \"S > 0\"。", "w": 1}, {"t": "L6.3 Sample Questions（官方答案，已驗證 ✅）", "u": "labs.html#l6-3-sample-questions", "p": "Labs", "x": "Q0：binary semaphore = mutex？→ No — init 1 可以當 mutex，但 init 0 可以做其他用途（ordering）。 Q1：T1：wait(S1); CS; signal(S2)；T2：wait(S2); CS; signal(S3)；T3：wait(S3); CS; signal(S1) 要 T1, T2, T3, T1… → S1 = 1, S2 = 0, S3 = 0。 Q2（P0 → P1 → P2 循環，填 BLANK）：S0 = 1, S1 = 0, S2 = 0；P0：wait(S0) … signal(S1)；P1：wait(S1) … signal(S2)；P2：wait(S2) … signal(S0)。 Q3（mutex 填空）：pthread_mutex_t* m = (pthread_mutex_t*) arg;、pthread_mutex_lock(m);、pthread_mutex_unlock(m); → purpose：protect the shared counter；final counter = 10,000（兩個 threads 各加 5,000）。（void* 要 cast 返 pthread_mutex_t*，因為 thread function signature 一定係 void *f(void *)。） Q4（X = Y = 0）：A：wait(X); \"b\"; signal(Y); wait(Y); \"c\"; signal(Y); B：signal(X); \"a\"; wait(Y)", "w": 1}, {"t": "L7. 常見陷阱（Labs / PA）", "u": "labs.html#l7-labs-pa", "p": "Labs", "x": "敘述 真/假 PA 可以用 fopen/FILE* 做 pipe ❌（FILE* 唔用得喺 pipe operations） wait(0) 同 wait(NULL) 一樣 ✅ dup(fd) 一定返回 1 ❌（返回 lowest unused fd；要先 close(1)） exec*() 成功後會返回 0 ❌（唔會 return；失敗 −1） execvp 嘅 argv 唔使 NULL 結尾 ❌ cd 可以用 execvp(\"cd\", …) 做 ❌（要 chdir） FIFO 開 write end 時冇 reader 會 block ✅ FIFO 啲 data 可以畀多個 reader 各讀一次（broadcast） ❌ Child 會繼承 parent 嘅 customized signal handler ✅ Threads share memory，processes 唔 share ✅ Priority + RR 被 preempt 後 quantum 重新計 ❌（保留剩餘 quantum） PA2：最後一層 queue 係 FCFS ✅ Peterson：assume load/store atomic ✅ sem_init(&s, 0, 0) 嘅 0（第二個）表示 threads 共用 ✅（pshared = 0） Condition wait 會放開 mutex ✅（醒返會重新攞）", "w": 1}, {"t": "API 速查 (C / Pthreads)", "u": "api.html", "p": "API", "x": "API Reference — Linux System Calls & Pthreads（Lab / PA 編程題速查） Announcement：「There may be questions related to PA or coding questions using some APIs we discussed in our lab classes」→ 呢頁就係用嚟填空 / 寫 code。", "w": 1}, {"t": "1. Process APIs", "u": "api.html#s1", "p": "API", "x": "Call Header Return 重點 pid_t fork(void) <unistd.h> child: 0；parent: child PID (>0)；fail: −1 child 由下一句開始；複製 address space（COW） int execlp(const char *file, const char *arg0, ..., (char *)NULL) <unistd.h> success: no return；fail: −1 用 PATH 搵 program；arg0 = program name；最後一定要 NULL int execvp(const char *file, char *const argv[]) <unistd.h> 同上 argv 最後一個元素要係 NULL（PA1：所以 array size 係 9 而唔係 8） int execl(path, arg0, ..., NULL) / execv(path, argv) <unistd.h> 同上 要 full path（冇 p = 唔搵 PATH） pid_t wait(int *status) <sys/wait.h> terminated child 嘅 PID；冇 child → −1 wait(NULL) 唔要 status；等任何一個 child pid_t waitpid(pid_t pid, int *status, int options) <sys/wait.h> 該 child PID；error −1 waitpid(pid, &status, 0) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "1.1 經典 fork–exec–wait", "u": "api.html#s1-1", "p": "API", "x": "pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid < 0) { perror(\"fork\"); exit(1); } else if (pid == 0) { /* child */ execlp(\"ls\", \"ls\", \"-l\", (char *)NULL); perror(\"exec failed\"); /* only reached if exec fails */ exit(1); } else { /* parent */ int status; waitpid(pid, &status, 0); /* or wait(NULL) */ }", "w": 1}, {"t": "1.2 Signal handler（PA1 Feature 2）", "u": "api.html#s1-2", "p": "API", "x": "void handle_sigint(int signum) { printf(\"Myshell (pid=%d) terminates by Ctrl-C with signal: %d\\n\", getpid(), signum); exit(0); } int main() { signal(SIGINT, handle_sigint); /* ... */ } SIGINT = 2（Ctrl-C）；SIGKILL (9)、SIGSTOP 唔可以 catch。", "w": 1}, {"t": "2. File I/O（POSIX，唔係 stdio）", "u": "api.html#s2", "p": "API", "x": "Call Return 重點 int open(const char *path, int flags[, mode_t mode]) <fcntl.h> lowest unused fd / −1 flags：O_RDONLY、O_WRONLY、O_RDWR、O_CREAT、O_TRUNC、O_APPEND；建立檔案要 mode（e.g. 0644） ssize_t read(int fd, void *buf, size_t count) bytes read；0 = EOF；−1 error blocking（pipe 冇 data 會等；所有 write ends close 晒先 return 0） ssize_t write(int fd, const void *buf, size_t count) bytes written / −1 int close(int fd) 0 / −1 用完要 close，尤其 pipe write end int dup(int oldfd) 新 fd = lowest unused close(0); dup(fin); → fd 0 指向 file int dup2(int oldfd, int newfd) newfd newfd 已開 → 先 close；dup2(fd, 1) = stdout 指向 fd fd 0 = stdin，1 = stdout，2 = stderr（STDIN_FILENO, STDOUT_FILENO, STDERR_FILENO）。 Lab 3 permission 寫法：open(\"he", "w": 1}, {"t": "3. Pipes", "u": "api.html#s3", "p": "API", "x": "Call 重點 int pipe(int fd[2]) <unistd.h> fd[0] = read end，fd[1] = write end；0 / −1；一定喺 fork() 之前 call int mkfifo(const char *path, mode_t mode) <sys/stat.h> named pipe（FIFO），之後用 open()/read()/write()；要 unlink() 刪除；0 / −1 int access(const char *path, int how) <unistd.h> F_OK 存在、R_OK/W_OK/X_OK 權限；0 = OK，−1 = 唔得（Lab 4：if (access(FIFO, F_OK) == -1) mkfifo(FIFO, 0777);） FILE *popen(const char *cmd, const char *type) / int pclose(FILE *fp) <stdio.h> fork 一個 child 跑 cmd；\"r\" 讀佢 stdout、\"w\" 寫佢 stdin；pclose 等佢完返回 status（PA1 禁用） FIFO 特性（Lab 4）：open/read/write blocking，要兩端都有 process open 先繼續（可用 O_NONBLOCK）；data 讀咗就冇（唔可以 broadcast）；≤ PIPE_BUF bytes 嘅 write 係 atomic；一條 FIFO 實際唔適合雙向（可能讀返自己寫嘅）→ 雙向用兩條。 兩條 ordin", "w": 1}, {"t": "3.1 Parent → child", "u": "api.html#s3-1", "p": "API", "x": "int fd[2]; pipe(fd); if (fork() == 0) { /* child: reader */ close(fd[1]); int n; read(fd[0], &n, sizeof(n)); close(fd[0]); exit(0); } else { /* parent: writer */ close(fd[0]); int n = 6; write(fd[1], &n, sizeof(n)); close(fd[1]); wait(NULL); }", "w": 1}, {"t": "3.2 Shell pipeline ls | wc -l", "u": "api.html#s3-2", "p": "API", "x": "int fd[2]; pipe(fd); if (fork() == 0) { /* child 1: ls → pipe */ dup2(fd[1], STDOUT_FILENO); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(\"ls\", \"ls\", (char *)NULL); } if (fork() == 0) { /* child 2: pipe → wc */ dup2(fd[0], STDIN_FILENO); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(\"wc\", \"wc\", \"-l\", (char *)NULL); } close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); /* parent MUST close both, else wc never sees EOF */ wait(NULL); wait(NULL); Multi-level pipe（PA1 Feature 5）：n segments → n−1 pipes；segment i：dup2(fds[i-1][0], 0)（if i > 0）、dup2(fds[i][1], 1)（if i < n−1）；每個 child close 所有 pipe fds；parent close 所有 fds 再 wait 全部。", "w": 1}, {"t": "3.3 常見錯誤", "u": "api.html#s3-3", "p": "API", "x": "pipe() 喺 fork() 之後 call → 兩條唔相關嘅 pipe（HW1 Q3.3c）。 冇 close 未用嘅 write end → reader read() 永遠唔會得到 EOF（hang）。 write(fd[0], …) / read(fd[1], …) 搞錯方向（0 讀 1 寫：記「0 = stdin-like，1 = stdout-like」）。 read/write 第二個參數要 address（&cnt），第三個係 size（sizeof(int)）。", "w": 1}, {"t": "4. Pthreads（#include <pthread.h>，compile with -pthread）", "u": "api.html#s4", "p": "API", "x": "Call 重點 int pthread_create(pthread_t *tid, const pthread_attr_t *attr, void *(*start)(void *), void *arg) return 0 = success；attr 用 NULL；start routine signature void *f(void *) int pthread_join(pthread_t tid, void **retval) 等 thread 結束（類似 wait）；retval 可 NULL void pthread_exit(void *retval) thread 自己結束 int pthread_cancel(pthread_t tid) request cancellation（default deferred） pthread_t pthread_self(void) 自己 tid int pthread_kill(pthread_t tid, int sig) signal 指定 thread int pthread_attr_init(pthread_attr_t *attr) default attributes（stack size, scheduling info）→ 再傳畀 pthread_create（Lab 4 sum example） pthread_cond_broadcast(&cond) 叫醒所有等緊嘅 threads（pthread_cond_signal 只叫醒一個） void *worker(void *arg) { ", "w": 1}, {"t": "4.1 Mutex", "u": "api.html#s4-1", "p": "API", "x": "pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; /* or pthread_mutex_init(&m, NULL) */ pthread_mutex_lock(&m); /* critical section */ pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); pthread_mutex_destroy(&m);", "w": 1}, {"t": "4.2 POSIX unnamed semaphore（#include <semaphore.h>）", "u": "api.html#s4-2", "p": "API", "x": "sem_t s; sem_init(&s, 0, 1); /* pshared = 0 → shared between threads of this process; initial value 1 */ sem_wait(&s); /* P(): if value > 0 decrement, else block */ sem_post(&s); /* V(): increment, wake a waiter */ sem_destroy(&s); Ordering：sem_init(&s, 0, 0)；先做嘅 thread 做完 sem_post(&s)，後做嘅 thread 開頭 sem_wait(&s)。 Mutual exclusion：init 1；sem_wait → CS → sem_post。 Counting resources：init = 資源數量 n。", "w": 1}, {"t": "4.3 Condition variables", "u": "api.html#s4-3", "p": "API", "x": "pthread_mutex_t m; pthread_cond_t c; int ready = 0; /* waiter */ pthread_mutex_lock(&m); while (!ready) pthread_cond_wait(&c, &m); /* releases m while sleeping, re-acquires on wake */ pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); /* signaller */ pthread_mutex_lock(&m); ready = 1; pthread_cond_signal(&c); pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); 一定要持有 mutex 先 call；用 while（唔係 if）再 check 條件。", "w": 1}, {"t": "5. Compile / Run（Lab 1–2）", "u": "api.html#s5", "p": "API", "x": "gcc -pedantic -Wall -Wextra -std=c99 -o myshell myshell.c gcc prog.c -o prog -lpthread # pthread programs (or -pthread) gcc buggy.c -o buggy -g # debug info, then: gdb buggy ./mlfq < input.txt > output.txt # PA2: input/output redirection diff --side-by-side my-out.txt sample-out.txt # compare outputs (--suppress-common-lines) gcc main.c -o main -lm # link math library (linker!) ./prog gcc -c main.c → main.o（compiler）；gcc -o main main.o -lm（linker）；./main（loader）[2.32]。", "w": 1}, {"t": "Accounting information", "u": "glossary.html#g-accounting-information", "p": "Glossary", "x": " PCB field: CPU used, clock time elapsed, time limits 記帳資訊 3.10 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Address space", "u": "glossary.html#g-address-space", "p": "Glossary", "x": " The memory a program executes in, distinct from other programs' (text, data, heap, stack) 位址空間 3.4–3.5 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Admission control", "u": "glossary.html#g-admission-control", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Real-time scheduler admits a task only if it can guarantee its deadline 准入控制 5.48 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Aging", "u": "glossary.html#g-aging", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Gradually increasing the priority of a process that waits a long time, to prevent starvation 老化 5.25 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Amdahl's Law", "u": "glossary.html#g-amdahl-s-law", "p": "Glossary", "x": " speedup≤S+(1−S)/N1​; as N→∞, speedup →1/S 阿姆達爾定律 4.12 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "API", "u": "glossary.html#g-api", "p": "Glossary", "x": " A set of functions available to programmers (parameters + return values) that invoke system calls on their behalf 應用程式介面 2.15 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Asymmetric multiprocessing (AMP)", "u": "glossary.html#g-asymmetric-multiprocessing-amp", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Master processor handles scheduling, I/O and kernel data; others run user code only; master may bottleneck 非對稱多處理 1.37, 5.38 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Asynchronous cancellation", "u": "glossary.html#g-asynchronous-cancellation", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Terminates the target thread immediately 非同步取消 4.32 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Asynchronous signal", "u": "glossary.html#g-asynchronous-signal", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Signal generated by an event external to the running process (Ctrl-C, timer expiry) 非同步信號 4.29 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Atomic", "u": "glossary.html#g-atomic", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Non-interruptible; executes as one uninterruptible unit 原子性 6.14 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Atomic variable", "u": "glossary.html#g-atomic-variable", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Provides atomic updates on basic data types (e.g., increment via CAS) 原子變數 6.21 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Average access time", "u": "glossary.html#g-average-access-time", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Hit ratio × hit time + miss ratio × miss time 平均存取時間 1.32 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Binary semaphore", "u": "glossary.html#g-binary-semaphore", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Semaphore whose value is only 0 or 1; can act like a mutex 二元信號量 6.25 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Blocking (synchronous) send/receive", "u": "glossary.html#g-blocking-synchronous-send-receive", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Sender blocks until the message is received; receiver blocks until a message is available 阻塞式 3.62 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Bootstrap program", "u": "glossary.html#g-bootstrap-program", "p": "Glossary", "x": " First program run at power-on, stored in EEPROM 開機程式 1.30 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Bounded buffer", "u": "glossary.html#g-bounded-buffer", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Fixed-size buffer shared by producer and consumer 有界緩衝區 3.53 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Bounded waiting", "u": "glossary.html#g-bounded-waiting", "p": "Glossary", "x": " A bound exists on how many times others may enter their CS after a process requests entry 有限等待 6.11 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Busy waiting", "u": "glossary.html#g-busy-waiting", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Looping continuously while waiting for a condition; wastes CPU 忙碌等待 6.22 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Cache", "u": "glossary.html#g-cache", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Faster, smaller storage holding a copy of a subset of slower, larger storage 快取 1.32 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Cascading termination", "u": "glossary.html#g-cascading-termination", "p": "Glossary", "x": " When a process terminates, all its children are also terminated by the OS 級聯終止 3.48 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Chip multithreading (CMT) / hyperthreading", "u": "glossary.html#g-chip-multithreading-cmt-hyperthreading", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Multiple hardware threads per core, each appearing as a logical CPU; hides memory stalls 晶片多執行緒 5.40–5.42 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "clone()", "u": "glossary.html#g-clone", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Linux call creating a task that shares selected resources with its parent (flags) 複製 3.46, 4.38 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Cloud computing", "u": "glossary.html#g-cloud-computing", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Delivers computing, storage and apps as a service over a network; extension of virtualization 雲端運算 1.47 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Compare-and-swap (CAS)", "u": "glossary.html#g-compare-and-swap-cas", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Atomically: if *value == expected set *value = new; return original value 比較並交換 6.17 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Concurrency", "u": "glossary.html#g-concurrency", "p": "Glossary", "x": " More than one task making progress (possibly by time multiplexing on one core) 並行（並發） 4.9 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Context switch", "u": "glossary.html#g-context-switch", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Saving the state of the old process/thread and loading the saved state of the new one; pure overhead 上下文切換 3.20 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Convoy effect", "u": "glossary.html#g-convoy-effect", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Short processes stuck waiting behind a long process (FCFS) 護航效應 5.10 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Cooperating process", "u": "glossary.html#g-cooperating-process", "p": "Glossary", "x": " A process that can affect or be affected by other processes 協作進程 3.51 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Copy-on-write (COW)", "u": "glossary.html#g-copy-on-write-cow", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Parent and child share pages until one writes; then the page is copied 寫入時複製 3.46 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "CPU burst", "u": "glossary.html#g-cpu-burst", "p": "Glossary", "x": " A period of CPU execution between I/O waits CPU 爆發 5.4 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "CPU-bound process", "u": "glossary.html#g-cpu-bound-process", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Spends most time computing; few long CPU bursts CPU 密集 3.14 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "CPU scheduler (short-term)", "u": "glossary.html#g-cpu-scheduler-short-term", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Selects a process from the ready queue and allocates a CPU core 短期排程器 3.17, 5.6 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "CPU utilization", "u": "glossary.html#g-cpu-utilization", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Fraction of time the CPU is busy CPU 使用率 5.8 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Critical section", "u": "glossary.html#g-critical-section", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Code segment in which shared data is accessed/modified 臨界區 6.9 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Data parallelism", "u": "glossary.html#g-data-parallelism", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Same operation on different subsets of data across cores 數據並行 4.11 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Deadlock", "u": "glossary.html#g-deadlock", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Two or more processes wait indefinitely for an event only one of the waiting processes can cause 死結 6.28 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Deferred cancellation", "u": "glossary.html#g-deferred-cancellation", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Target thread periodically checks whether it should terminate (orderly) 延遲取消 4.32 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Degree of multiprogramming", "u": "glossary.html#g-degree-of-multiprogramming", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Number of processes currently in memory 多道程式度 3.14 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Deterministic modeling", "u": "glossary.html#g-deterministic-modeling", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Evaluates algorithms on a predetermined workload; exact but specific 確定性建模 5.54 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Device driver", "u": "glossary.html#g-device-driver", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Kernel module that controls a device 裝置驅動程式 1 (summary) ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Direct communication", "u": "glossary.html#g-direct-communication", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Processes name each other explicitly; one link per pair 直接通訊 3.59 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Dispatch latency", "u": "glossary.html#g-dispatch-latency", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Time for the dispatcher to stop one process and start another 分派延遲 5.7 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Dispatcher", "u": "glossary.html#g-dispatcher", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Gives CPU control to the selected process: context switch, switch to user mode, jump to restart location 分派器 5.7 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "DLL (dynamically linked library)", "u": "glossary.html#g-dll-dynamically-linked-library", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Library loaded when needed and shared by all programs using it 動態連結庫 2.31 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "DMA", "u": "glossary.html#g-dma", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Controller transfers blocks directly between device and memory without CPU; interrupts once when done 直接記憶體存取 3.25 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Dual-mode operation", "u": "glossary.html#g-dual-mode-operation", "p": "Glossary", "x": " User mode vs kernel mode distinguished by a hardware mode bit (1 = user, 0 = kernel) 雙模式 3.27–3.28 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "dup / dup2", "u": "glossary.html#g-dup-dup2", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Duplicate a file descriptor (dup → lowest free fd; dup2 → specified fd) 複製描述符 Lab/PA1 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "EDF", "u": "glossary.html#g-edf", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Earliest-deadline-first: dynamic priority, earlier deadline = higher priority; theoretically optimal 最早截止期限優先 5.51 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Exec()", "u": "glossary.html#g-exec", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Replaces the current process image with a new program; returns only on failure 執行新程式 3.41 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Exponential averaging", "u": "glossary.html#g-exponential-averaging", "p": "Glossary", "x": " τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)τn​: predicts the next CPU burst 指數平均 5.19 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "FCFS", "u": "glossary.html#g-fcfs", "p": "Glossary", "x": " First-come first-served; non-preemptive; convoy effect 先到先得 5.9 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "fork()", "u": "glossary.html#g-fork", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Creates a child duplicating the parent's address space; returns 0 to child, child PID to parent, −1 on failure 分叉 3.35–3.37 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Hard affinity", "u": "glossary.html#g-hard-affinity", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Process specifies the subset of CPUs it may run on 硬親和性 5.45 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Hard real-time", "u": "glossary.html#g-hard-real-time", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Task must be serviced by its deadline; late = no service 硬實時 5.47 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Heap", "u": "glossary.html#g-heap", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Memory dynamically allocated at run time 堆積 3.5 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Hybrid system", "u": "glossary.html#g-hybrid-system", "p": "Glossary", "x": " OS combining several structures (Linux, Windows, macOS) 混合式 2.52 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Indirect communication", "u": "glossary.html#g-indirect-communication", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Messages sent to/received from mailboxes (ports) 間接通訊 3.60 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Interrupt", "u": "glossary.html#g-interrupt", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Signal from hardware/software requiring CPU attention; handled by an ISR 中斷 3.21–3.24 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Interrupt vector", "u": "glossary.html#g-interrupt-vector", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Table of interrupt-handler addresses indexed by interrupt number 中斷向量 3 (summary) ", "w": 6}, {"t": "I/O-bound process", "u": "glossary.html#g-i-o-bound-process", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Spends more time on I/O; many short CPU bursts I/O 密集 3.14 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "IPC", "u": "glossary.html#g-ipc", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Mechanism for cooperating processes to exchange data: shared memory or message passing 進程間通訊 3.51 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Kernel", "u": "glossary.html#g-kernel", "p": "Glossary", "x": " The one program running at all times on the computer 核心 1.24 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Kernel thread", "u": "glossary.html#g-kernel-thread", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Thread supported and scheduled by the OS 核心執行緒 4.19 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Layered approach", "u": "glossary.html#g-layered-approach", "p": "Glossary", "x": " OS divided into layers (0 = hardware … N = UI); each uses only lower layers 分層結構 2.44 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Linker", "u": "glossary.html#g-linker", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Combines relocatable object files and libraries into one executable 連結器 2.31 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Little's formula", "u": "glossary.html#g-little-s-formula", "p": "Glossary", "x": " n=λ×W (queue length = arrival rate × waiting time) 利特爾公式 5.57 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Load balancing", "u": "glossary.html#g-load-balancing", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Keep workload evenly distributed across CPUs (push / pull migration) 負載平衡 5.46 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Loadable kernel module (LKM)", "u": "glossary.html#g-loadable-kernel-module-lkm", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Core kernel plus modules linked in at boot or run time; any module can call any other 可載入核心模組 2.49–2.50 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Loader", "u": "glossary.html#g-loader", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Loads the executable file into memory for execution 載入器 2.31 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Locality (temporal / spatial)", "u": "glossary.html#g-locality-temporal-spatial", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Recently accessed items are reused soon / nearby items are accessed soon 局部性 1.33 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Long-term scheduler (job scheduler)", "u": "glossary.html#g-long-term-scheduler-job-scheduler", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Selects which jobs are brought into memory; controls degree of multiprogramming 長期排程器 3.17 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "LWP (lightweight process)", "u": "glossary.html#g-lwp-lightweight-process", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Virtual processor between user threads and a kernel thread 輕量級進程 5.36 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Many-to-one / one-to-one / many-to-many", "u": "glossary.html#g-many-to-one-one-to-one-many-to-many", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Mappings from user threads to kernel threads 多對一 / 一對一 / 多對多 4.21–4.24 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Medium-term scheduler", "u": "glossary.html#g-medium-term-scheduler", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Swaps processes out to disk and back to reduce degree of multiprogramming 中期排程器 3.18 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Memory stall", "u": "glossary.html#g-memory-stall", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Processor waits for data from memory (e.g., cache miss) 記憶體停頓 5.40 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Message passing", "u": "glossary.html#g-message-passing", "p": "Glossary", "x": " IPC via explicit send()/receive() without shared memory 訊息傳遞 3.57 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Microkernel", "u": "glossary.html#g-microkernel", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Minimal kernel; other services run in user space and communicate via message passing 微核心 2.46–2.48 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "MLFQ", "u": "glossary.html#g-mlfq", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Multilevel feedback queue: processes move between queues; approximates SRTF without burst knowledge 多級回饋佇列 5.30–5.35 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Mode bit", "u": "glossary.html#g-mode-bit", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Hardware bit: 1 = user mode, 0 = kernel mode 模式位元 3.28 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Monolithic", "u": "glossary.html#g-monolithic", "p": "Glossary", "x": " All kernel functionality in one static binary in a single address space; fast but hard to maintain 單體式 2.41 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Multicore", "u": "glossary.html#g-multicore", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Multiple computing cores on a single chip; faster on-chip communication, less power 多核心 1.39 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Multilevel queue", "u": "glossary.html#g-multilevel-queue", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Processes permanently assigned to queues by type; each queue its own algorithm 多級佇列 5.28–5.29 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Multiprogramming", "u": "glossary.html#g-multiprogramming", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Keeping several jobs in memory so the CPU always has one to execute 多道程式 1.42 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Mutex lock", "u": "glossary.html#g-mutex-lock", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Lock acquired before and released after a critical section (acquire/release atomic) 互斥鎖 6.22 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Mutual exclusion", "u": "glossary.html#g-mutual-exclusion", "p": "Glossary", "x": " If one process is in its CS, no other process can be in its CS 互斥 6.11 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Named pipe (FIFO)", "u": "glossary.html#g-named-pipe-fifo", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Bidirectional (UNIX half-duplex) pipe with a file name; no parent–child needed; persists until deleted 具名管道 3.69 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Non-preemptive scheduling", "u": "glossary.html#g-non-preemptive-scheduling", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Process keeps the CPU until it terminates or blocks 非搶佔式 5.6 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "NUMA", "u": "glossary.html#g-numa", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Each CPU has fast local memory; remote memory access slower 非一致記憶體存取 1.40 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Ordinary pipe", "u": "glossary.html#g-ordinary-pipe", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Unidirectional pipe between parent and child; fd[0] read, fd[1] write; gone after use 普通管道 3.66 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Orphan", "u": "glossary.html#g-orphan", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Child whose parent terminated without wait(); adopted by init/systemd 孤兒進程 3.50 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Parallelism", "u": "glossary.html#g-parallelism", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Performing more than one task simultaneously (needs multiple cores) 平行 4.9 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "PCB", "u": "glossary.html#g-pcb", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Process control block: state, PC, registers, scheduling, memory, accounting, I/O info 進程控制塊 3.10 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "PCS (process-contention scope)", "u": "glossary.html#g-pcs-process-contention-scope", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Thread library schedules user threads onto LWPs, among threads of the same process 進程競爭範圍 5.37 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Pipe", "u": "glossary.html#g-pipe", "p": "Glossary", "x": " IPC channel treated as a special file; read()/write() 管道 3.65 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Policy vs mechanism", "u": "glossary.html#g-policy-vs-mechanism", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Policy = what will be done; mechanism = how to do it 策略與機制 2.34 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Preemptive scheduling", "u": "glossary.html#g-preemptive-scheduling", "p": "Glossary", "x": " CPU can be taken away involuntarily (interrupt, higher-priority arrival) 搶佔式 5.6 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Priority scheduling", "u": "glossary.html#g-priority-scheduling", "p": "Glossary", "x": " CPU to highest-priority process (smallest number); starvation → aging 優先權排程 5.25 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Privileged instruction", "u": "glossary.html#g-privileged-instruction", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Instruction executable only in kernel mode (I/O, timer, interrupt management) 特權指令 3.28 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Process", "u": "glossary.html#g-process", "p": "Glossary", "x": " A program in execution; active entity with PC and resources 進程 3.4–3.5 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Processor affinity", "u": "glossary.html#g-processor-affinity", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Tendency to keep a process on the CPU whose cache holds its data 處理器親和性 5.45 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Program", "u": "glossary.html#g-program", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Passive entity — executable file on disk 程式 3.5 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Progress", "u": "glossary.html#g-progress", "p": "Glossary", "x": " If no one is in the CS, selection of the next entrant cannot be postponed indefinitely 前進 6.11 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Pull / push migration", "u": "glossary.html#g-pull-push-migration", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Idle CPU pulls tasks / a task pushes work from overloaded CPUs 拉 / 推遷移 5.46 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Race condition", "u": "glossary.html#g-race-condition", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Outcome depends on the order of concurrent access to shared data 競爭條件 6.9 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Ready queue", "u": "glossary.html#g-ready-queue", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Processes in memory ready and waiting to run 就緒佇列 3.15 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Rate-monotonic (RM)", "u": "glossary.html#g-rate-monotonic-rm", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Static priority = shorter period higher priority; optimal among static-priority algorithms 速率單調 5.49 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Response time", "u": "glossary.html#g-response-time", "p": "Glossary", "x": " (Course) end of the first run − arrival; time until the first response is produced 回應時間 5.12 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Round robin (RR)", "u": "glossary.html#g-round-robin-rr", "p": "Glossary", "x": " FCFS with a time quantum; preempted process to tail; wait ≤ (n−1)q 輪轉 5.11 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "RPC", "u": "glossary.html#g-rpc", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Remote procedure call: message-based procedure call between networked systems 遠端程序呼叫 3.70 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "SCS (system-contention scope)", "u": "glossary.html#g-scs-system-contention-scope", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Kernel schedules kernel threads onto CPUs, among all threads in the system 系統競爭範圍 5.37 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Semaphore", "u": "glossary.html#g-semaphore", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Integer accessed only via atomic wait() (P) and signal() (V) 信號量 6.24 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Shared memory", "u": "glossary.html#g-shared-memory", "p": "Glossary", "x": " IPC by reading/writing a shared region 共享記憶體 3.51 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Signal", "u": "glossary.html#g-signal", "p": "Glossary", "x": " UNIX notification to a process that an event occurred 信號 4.29 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "SJF", "u": "glossary.html#g-sjf", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Shortest-job-first: optimal minimum average waiting time; hard to know next burst 最短工作優先 5.17 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "SMP", "u": "glossary.html#g-smp", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Symmetric multiprocessing: each processor self-schedules; own registers & cache, shared memory 對稱多處理 1.38, 5.39 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Socket", "u": "glossary.html#g-socket", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Endpoint for communication identified by IP address + port 套接字 3.70 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Soft affinity", "u": "glossary.html#g-soft-affinity", "p": "Glossary", "x": " OS tries but does not guarantee to keep a process on the same CPU 軟親和性 5.45 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Soft real-time", "u": "glossary.html#g-soft-real-time", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Critical tasks get preference over non-critical ones; no guarantee 軟實時 5.47 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Spinlock", "u": "glossary.html#g-spinlock", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Lock that busy-waits; no context switch, wastes CPU; good for short CS on multiprocessors 自旋鎖 6.22 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "SRTF", "u": "glossary.html#g-srtf", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Shortest-remaining-time-first: preemptive SJF 最短剩餘時間優先 5.23 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Stack", "u": "glossary.html#g-stack", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Temporary data: function parameters, return addresses, local variables 堆疊 3.5 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Starvation", "u": "glossary.html#g-starvation", "p": "Glossary", "x": " A process waits indefinitely (low priority / semaphore queue) 飢餓 5.25, 6.28 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "System call", "u": "glossary.html#g-system-call", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Programming interface to OS services 系統呼叫 2.14 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "System program", "u": "glossary.html#g-system-program", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Utilities shipped with the OS providing a convenient environment (not part of kernel) 系統程式 2.27 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Task parallelism", "u": "glossary.html#g-task-parallelism", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Different threads performing unique operations on cores 任務並行 4.11 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "TCB", "u": "glossary.html#g-tcb", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Thread control block: registers, PC, stack pointer, scheduling info, pointer to PCB 執行緒控制塊 4.15 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Test-and-set", "u": "glossary.html#g-test-and-set", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Atomically returns the old value of a boolean and sets it to TRUE 測試並設置 6.15 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Thread", "u": "glossary.html#g-thread", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Basic unit of CPU utilization: thread ID, PC, register set, stack 執行緒 4.6 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Thread-local storage", "u": "glossary.html#g-thread-local-storage", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Data unique to each thread 執行緒區域儲存 (Sp21 MC) ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Throughput", "u": "glossary.html#g-throughput", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Number of processes completed per unit time 吞吐量 5 (summary) ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Timesharing (multitasking)", "u": "glossary.html#g-timesharing-multitasking", "p": "Glossary", "x": " CPU switches among jobs so frequently that users can interact with each 分時 1.42 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Trap / exception", "u": "glossary.html#g-trap-exception", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Software-generated interrupt caused by an error or a system call request 陷阱 / 例外 3.24, 3.31 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Turnaround time", "u": "glossary.html#g-turnaround-time", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Completion time − arrival time 周轉時間 5.8 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "User thread", "u": "glossary.html#g-user-thread", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Thread managed by a user-level library, invisible to the kernel 使用者執行緒 4.19 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Virtualization", "u": "glossary.html#g-virtualization", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Abstracts one computer's hardware into multiple execution environments (VMs) 虛擬化 1.43 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "VMM / hypervisor", "u": "glossary.html#g-vmm-hypervisor", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Creates and manages VMs, presenting an interface identical to the host 虛擬機監控器 1.44 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "von Neumann architecture", "u": "glossary.html#g-von-neumann-architecture", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Stored-program: instructions and data in the same memory; fetch–decode–execute 馮紐曼架構 1.26 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Waiting time", "u": "glossary.html#g-waiting-time", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Time spent in the ready queue = turnaround − burst 等待時間 5.8 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "Zombie", "u": "glossary.html#g-zombie", "p": "Glossary", "x": " Terminated process whose parent has not yet called wait(); PID & PCB remain 殭屍進程 3.49 ", "w": 6}, {"t": "S26-MC1 Interrupts", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements about interrupts is false? Interrupts may be generated by hardware devices Interrupts may be generated by software exceptions or traps All interrupts are serviced in exactly the same way, with no difference in priority Interrupts are handled by the kernel 解：interrupt 有 priority levels，high-priority 可以 preempt low-priority interrupt [3.24]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC2 DMA", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements is true about DMA? DMA is used to move data for slow devices, while CPU is used for fast devices DMA allows data transfer between device and memory with less CPU involvement DMA eliminates interrupts completely CPU and DMA can't execute concurrently 解：DMA 用於 fast devices / large data（A 相反）；完成後仍然 interrupt CPU（C 錯）；CPU 同 DMA 可以 concurrent（D 錯）[3.25]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC3 Storage hierarchy", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements is true about storage hierarchy? Registers are slower than main memory Main memory is nonvolatile Hard-disk drives are smaller than volatile memory Main memory is byte addressable 解：each address refers to one byte [1.30]。Registers 最快；main memory volatile；HDD 大過 memory。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC4 von Neumann", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is a core characteristic of the von Neumann architecture? Instructions and data are stored in separate physical memories Instructions and data are stored in the same memory space, accessed by address The CPU does not contain an ALU; all computations are done by an external co-processor The Program Counter (PC) is stored in external mass storage devices 解：stored-program architecture [1.26]。A 係 Harvard architecture。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC5 OS services", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is false about services of operating systems? User interface can be CLI or GUI The logging service of OS should keep track of computer resources that each user uses Errors only occur in memory and I/O devices Resource allocation service should allocate resources like CPU cycles, memory, file storage 解：errors 可以喺 CPU and memory, I/O devices, user programs [2.5]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC6 API", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is an advantage of using APIs instead of directly invoking system calls? APIs provide a more convenient programming interface APIs always execute faster than system calls APIs can bypass kernel protection APIs eliminate the need for linkers and loaders 解：API 好處 = portability + hide complex details（convenient） [2.17]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC7 Microkernel", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is false about microkernel system structure? It is easier to extend a microkernel operating system It typically has better performance than a monolithic kernel It runs memory management in user space It is easier to port a microkernel operating system to new architectures 解：microkernel performance suffers（message passing overhead）[2.48]；microkernel 保留 minimal memory management 喺 kernel，所以 C 都算錯。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC8 printf vs write", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What's the relationship between printf() and write()? Both printf() and write() are system calls provided directly by the kernel printf() invokes write() system call to get service from operating system The program enters kernel mode immediately when printf() is called write() system call invokes printf() to perform the print function 解：printf() 係 C library function，佢 call write() system call [2.26]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC9 Layered design", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements about a layered operating-system design is correct? Any layer may directly access any other layer The interfaces between adjacent layers are usually well defined Layered design eliminates all performance overhead Pure layered design is the dominant structure in all modern operating systems 解：每層只用下層服務、interface well defined（information hiding）[2.44–2.45]。A 係 LKM 特色；layered 有 overhead；好少 pure layered。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC10 fork count", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "int main() { int i; for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) { if (fork() == 0) { fork(); printf(\"X \"); return 1; } } printf(\"Y \"); return 0; } What is the total number of processes (including the original), the number of \"X \" printed, and the number of \"Y \" printed? Total processes = 4, \"X \" = 3, \"Y \" = 1 Total processes = 4, \"X \" = 1, \"Y \" = 3 Total processes = 7, \"X \" = 6, \"Y \" = 1 Total processes = 7, \"X \" = 1, \"Y \" = 6 解：只有 original 繼續 loop；每輪生 1 個 child，child 再 fork → 2 個印 X 然後 return。3 輪 × 2 = 6 X；origina", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC11 Context switch", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement is true about context switch? Context switch means switching from user mode to kernel mode During context switch, OS saves the state of one process/thread and restores another Context switch requires the same hardware on all CPU architectures Context switch is shorter if the OS is more complex 解：[3.20]。A 係 mode switch；越複雜 OS/PCB → context switch 越長。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC12 Ordinary pipes", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about ordinary pipes is false? They can be accessed like file descriptors They can be accessed by arbitrary processes They disappear after the communicating processes terminate They are typically used between parent and child processes 解：ordinary pipe cannot be accessed from outside the process that creates it（只限 parent–child）[3.65–3.66]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC13 Zombie", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC13", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about zombie processes is correct? A zombie process is still executing user code normally A zombie process has terminated but its parent has not yet collected its status A zombie process is created when exec() fails A zombie process owns no process identifier 解：[3.49] terminated 但 parent 未 call wait()；PID、process-table entry、PCB 仍然存在。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC14 Leaving running state", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC14", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which event typically causes a process to leave the running state? It issues an I/O request It calls a function It increases a local variable It reads from cache 解：I/O request → running → waiting [3.9]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC15 Multithreaded process", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC15", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is true about the multithreaded process? Each thread has its own address space and file descriptors Threads can't run concurrently on multiple cores if they belong to the same process Thread creation is usually faster than process creation It can't take advantage of processing capabilities on multicore systems 解：Economy — thread creation cheaper [4.4, 4.7]；threads share address space & files。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC16 Concurrency vs parallelism", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC16", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement is correct about concurrency and parallelism in multicore systems? Concurrency means a system can perform more than one task at exactly the same time Parallelism means tasks only make progress over time on a single processor core Concurrency can be supported on a single processor core through time multiplexing by the scheduler Testing and debugging become easier in multicore systems because execution paths are fixed 解：[4.9]。A 係 parallelism 定義；debugging 變難。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC17 Amdahl's Law", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC17", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which combination gives a speedup between 1.6 and 1.7? parallel 81%, 3 cores parallel 81%, 5 cores parallel 61%, 3 cores parallel 61%, 5 cores 解：speedup=S+(1−S)/N1​。A：0.19+0.271​=2.17；B：2.84；C：0.39+0.20331​=1.69；D：1.95。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC18 Signals", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC18", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about signal handling is false? Signals can be received synchronously or asynchronously Users can define custom signal handlers for certain signals All signals should be delivered to all threads in a process Different signals can have different handlers in the same process 解：synchronous signals 只 deliver 畀 causing thread；只有部分 asynchronous（e.g. Ctrl-C terminate）先畀所有 threads [4.30–4.31]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC19 Scheduling criteria", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC19", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about scheduling criteria is correct? Waiting time is the amount of time from process arrival until the process finishes execution Turnaround time is the amount of time a process spends waiting in the ready queue Response time is the amount of time from when a request is submitted until the first response is produced Throughput is the fraction of time the CPU is busy 解：A 係 turnaround；B 係 waiting；D 係 CPU utilization [5.8, 5.12]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC20 MLFQ", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC20", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about MLFQ is correct? Processes never move between queues Each queue can use a different scheduling algorithm It requires burst times to be known exactly in advance It is a non-preemptive algorithm 解：[5.30–5.31]。MLFQ 可以搬 queue、唔需要知 burst、preemptive。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC21 EDF", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC21", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which scheduling algorithm assigns priorities according to deadlines? RM EDF FCFS RR 解：EDF = dynamic priority by deadline；RM = static by period [5.51]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC22 Multiprocessor scheduling", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC22", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about multiprocessor scheduling is false? For asymmetric multiprocessing, the master server can become a potential bottleneck In a multithreaded system, another thread may run when one thread stalls while waiting for memory The motivation of placing multiple processor cores on the same physical chip is to simplify the scheduler Processor affinity tries to keep a process on the same CPU to improve cache performance 解：multicore 係為咗 faster + less power，而且令 scheduling 更複雜 [5.40]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC23 Mutex + semaphore output", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC23", "p": "Quiz", "x": "int counter = 0; pthread_mutex_t mtx; sem_t sem; /* sem_init(&sem, 0, 0) */ void *worker1(void *arg) { pthread_mutex_lock(&mtx); counter = counter + 2; printf(\"A %d;\", counter); pthread_mutex_unlock(&mtx); sem_post(&sem); return NULL; } void *worker2(void *arg) { sem_wait(&sem); pthread_mutex_lock(&mtx); counter = counter + 3; printf(\"B %d;\", counter); pthread_mutex_unlock(&mtx); return NULL; } /* main creates t1 (worker1) and t2 (worker2), then joins both */ What is the output? A 2; B 5; B 3; A", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC24 Hardware primitive", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC24", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which hardware primitive is commonly used to build synchronization tools? exec() test_and_set() fork() open() 解：atomic hardware instructions：test_and_set()、compare_and_swap() [6.14]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-MC25 Bounded-waiting TAS", "u": "quiz.html#S26-MC25", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In the bounded-waiting mutual exclusion algorithm using test_and_set() (waiting[], key, lock; exit section scans j = (i+1)%n … and either sets lock = false or waiting[j] = false), which statement is not correct? test_and_set(&lock) releases the lock after the critical section waiting[i] is used to indicate that process i wants to enter the critical section waiting[j] = false indicates that process i releases the lock (hands it to j) Process i can re-enter the critical section after executing loc", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC1 Locality", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Locality is essential to ____ Memory Cache Hard disks Non-volatile memory (NVM) 解：caching works because of temporal/spatial locality [1.33]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC2 Virtualization", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "____ allows a guest OS to run as an application on a native OS. Operating system Emulation User interface Virtualization 解：[1.43] \"allows an OS to run as an application within another OS\"。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC3 Interrupt", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements about interrupts is NOT true? Interrupts enable hardware to interact with the operating system Interrupt alerts the CPU that some event requires attention Interrupt allows software to access hardware including the CPU Interrupt can be caused by both hardware and software 解：C 講緊 system call 嘅功能。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC4 API", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Programmers interface with ____ to obtain services from the OS. Shell System call interfaces APIs (application programming interfaces) System calls 解：programmers 用 API（best answer）[2.15]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC5 Parameter passing", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "How can parameters be passed to the OS in system calls? Registers Stack Block or table All of the above 解：三種方法 [2.20]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC6 LKM", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "____ refers to the OS design that maintains a set of core components in the kernel and adds additional services via modules during run time. Layered approach Loadable kernel modules (LKMs) Microkernel Monolithic 解：[2.49]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC7 fork", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements on fork() is NOT true? The parent process waits until the completion of the child process The child process duplicates the entire address space of the parent process The parent and child processes run the same program The parent and child processes run concurrently in the system 解：等 child 係 wait() 嘅工作，fork 本身唔會等。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC8 Address space", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is contained in the address space of a process? Stack Code Data All of the above 解：text (code)、data、heap、stack [3.5]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC9 Named pipes", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "____ enables communication for multiple processes with no parent-child relationship, independent from network. RPCs Ordinary pipes Named pipes Socket 解：named pipes 唔需要 parent-child、多 processes 用 [3.69]；socket/RPC 要 network。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC10 Medium-term scheduler", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "____ swaps a process out of memory and temporarily stores it on disk. The long-term scheduler A device scheduler The CPU scheduler The medium-term scheduler 解：swapping [3.18]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC11 Not shared by threads", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which item below is NOT shared by threads of the same process? Code Stack Files Data 解：每個 thread 有自己 stack, registers, PC [4.6]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC12 Thread library", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "____ provides the programmer with an API for creating and managing threads. Thread library (such as POSIX Pthreads) Signals System calls Inter-process communications 解：[4.20]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC13 Multithreading models", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC13", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements is NOT true? Kernel threads are scheduled by the OS Programmers or users manage user threads A user thread can be mapped to more than one kernel thread One user thread can be mapped to at most one kernel thread 解：冇 one-to-many mapping；many-to-many 係 many user → smaller or equal kernel threads。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC14 Minimum average waiting time", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC14", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Given a set of processes, which algorithm produces the minimum average waiting time? FCFS RR SJF SRTF 解：SJF optimal among non-preemptive；SRTF optimal among preemptive [5.17, 5.24]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "F20-MC15 Hard to implement", "u": "quiz.html#F20-MC15", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following scheduling algorithms is difficult to implement in practice? SJF RR MLFQ FCFS 解：好難知道 next CPU burst length [5.17]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC1 von Neumann", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In a von Neumann architecture, a processing unit contains a/an ____ and processor registers. arithmetic logic unit (ALU) instruction register (IR) program counter (PC) all of the mentioned 解：[1.26] \"a CPU that contains an ALU and processor registers\"；IR 屬 control unit。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC2 Interrupt trigger", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following events will directly trigger an interrupt? kernel code execution I/O job execution user code execution I/O job completion 解：device controller 完成操作 → interrupt CPU [3.21]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC3 Dual mode", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The two separate modes of operation in an operating system are ____. supervisor mode and system mode physical mode and logical mode user mode and kernel mode kernel mode and privileged mode 解：[3.27]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC4 System calls", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "____ provide an interface to the services provided by an operating system. Inter-process communications Remote procedure calls System calls User interfaces 解：[2.14]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC5 Original UNIX", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which operating system structure is adopted by the original UNIX? layered structure microkernel monolithic structure modular approach 解：[2.40–2.41]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC6 Degree of multiprogramming", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The ____ refers to the number of processes in the memory. program counter degree of multi-programming long-term scheduler CPU scheduler 解：[3.14]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC7 Amdahl", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Using Amdahl's Law, what is the speedup for an application that is 30% serial on 7 processing cores? 2 2.5 3 3.5 解：0.3+0.7/71​=0.41​= 2.5。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC8 Process owns", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In an operating system, each process has its own ____. address space local/global variables and open files pending alarms, signals and signal handlers all of the above 解：process = address space + resources。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC9 Stack", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which section of a process contains temporary data like function parameters, return addresses and local variables? text section data section heap stack 解：[3.5]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC10 Short-term scheduler", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following selects from among the processes in the ready queue to execute on the CPU? Short-term scheduler Medium-term scheduler Long-term scheduler Context switcher 解：= CPU scheduler [3.17]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC11 fork incorrect", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "During process creation with fork(), which of the following is incorrect? The child process can run concurrently with the parent The child process can call wait() in order to finish earlier than the parent The child process can call exec() to load a new program into it The child can be an exact duplicate of the parent 解：wait() 係等 child 完成，唔係令自己早完。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC12 Pipes", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following statements about pipes is not true? Named pipes allow bi-directional communication Only the parent and child processes can use ordinary pipes for communication Named pipes cease to exist after the communicating processes have finished Reading and writing to ordinary pipes on both UNIX and Windows can be performed like ordinary file I/O 解：named pipes continue to exist until explicitly deleted [3.69]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC13 Leave the CPU", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC13", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following events might force a process to leave the CPU? make a fork system call make an I/O request interrupt all of the above 解：system call / I/O / interrupt 都可以令 process 離開 CPU。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC14 fork with ||", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC14", "p": "Quiz", "x": "if (fork() || fork()) { fork(); } How many processes are created in total (including the original)? 3 4 5 6 解：P0（true，跳過第二個 fork）→ fork → 2；C1（false）→ fork2：C1 true → fork → 2；C2 false → 1。2 + 2 + 1 = 5 ✅ ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC15 fork in loop", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC15", "p": "Quiz", "x": "for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) { if (fork() == 0) fork(); } How many processes in total? 3 8 27 64 解：每輪每個 process 變 3 個 → 33=27 ✅ ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC16 TCB", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC16", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is not in the Thread Control Block (TCB)? Execution state Scheduling information Pointer to enclosing process Process identifier 解：TCB 有 execution state、scheduling info、accounting、pointers、pointer to enclosing PCB [4.15]；PID 喺 PCB。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC17 Concurrency", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC17", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which refers to the capability to allow multiple tasks to make progress on a single processor? parallelism concurrency data parallelism task parallelism 解：[4.9]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC18 Multithreading models", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC18", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following is not true about multithreading models? Many-to-one means many user-level threads are mapped to a single kernel thread One-to-one model provides more concurrency than many-to-one model The many-to-many model is easy to implement in practice Most operating systems now use the one-to-one model 解：many-to-many difficult to implement [4.26]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC19 Thread-local storage", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC19", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Thread-local storage is data that ____. is not associated with any process has been modified by the thread, but not yet updated to the parent process is generated by the thread independent of the thread's process is unique to each thread 解：TLS = unique to each thread（Windows TEB 入面有 TLS [4.36]）。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC20 When scheduling occurs", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC20", "p": "Quiz", "x": "CPU scheduling decisions may take place when ____. a process switches from running to waiting state a process terminates a process switches from running to ready state all of the above 解：四種情況 [5.6]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC21 Priority problem", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC21", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The main problem with a priority scheduling algorithm is ____. how to set the priority of a process some processes may never execute how to determine the length of the next CPU burst how to determine the length of the time quantum 解：starvation；solution = aging [5.25]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC22 RR large quantum", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC22", "p": "Quiz", "x": "For RR scheduling, when the time quantum q gets too large, it degenerates to ____. SJF FCFS Shortest-remaining-time-first Multilevel queue 解：[5.14, 5.16]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC23 Must be non-preemptive", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC23", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which of the following scheduling algorithms must be non-preemptive? FCFS SJF Priority scheduling Multi-level feedback queue 解：FCFS 一定 non-preemptive；SJF / priority 有 preemptive 版本；MLFQ preemptive [5.11]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC24 MLFQ true statement", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC24", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In the multilevel feedback queue algorithm, which statement is true? Prior knowledge on the next CPU burst time is required for scheduling Classification of the ready queue is permanent It handles interactive jobs well by delivering similar performance as SJF It won't suffer from starvation problem 解：[5.35]。B 係 multilevel queue（冇 feedback）；MLFQ 都可能 starvation（要 reshuffle/aging）。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC25 Real-time", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC25", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about real-time CPU scheduling is true? Real-time scheduling doesn't demand performance guarantee In hard real-time systems, a task can be serviced after its deadline Soft real-time systems guarantee only that the process will be prioritized over non-critical processes The scheduler for a real-time system must support a priority-based algorithm without preemption 解：[5.47]。RT scheduler 要 priority-based with preemption。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC26 Starvation-free", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC26", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which algorithms are free from starvation? 1. FCFS 2. Non-preemptive SJF 3. Preemptive SJF 4. Round Robin 5. Multi-level Queue 1 4 1 2 4 1 3 4 1 2 4 5 解：SJF/SRTF 長 job 可能 starve；multilevel queue（fixed priority）低 queue 可能 starve；FCFS、RR 唔會。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC27 Mutual exclusion", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC27", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Mutual exclusion implies that ____. if a process is executing in its critical section, then no other process can be executing in their critical sections if a process is executing in its critical section, then only one of the other processes can be executing in its critical section if a process is executing in its critical section, then all the resources of the system must be blocked until it finishes execution none of the above 解：[6.11]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC28 CS requirements", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC28", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A solution to the critical section problem does not have to satisfy which requirement? mutual exclusion progress synchronization bounded waiting 解：三個 requirements = ME + progress + bounded waiting。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC29 Race condition", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC29", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A situation where several processes access and manipulate the same data concurrently and the outcome depends on the particular order of access is called ____. data consistency race condition aging starvation 解：[6.9]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Sp21-MC30 Entry section", "u": "quiz.html#Sp21-MC30", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Suppose the binary variable lock is initialized to 0. Which can be an implementation of the entry section to solve the critical-section problem? while (compare_and_swap(&lock, 0, 1) == 0) ; while (compare_and_swap(&lock, 1, 0) != 0) ; while (test_and_set(&lock)) ; while (!test_and_set(&lock)) ; 解：TAS 返回 old value；lock = 0 時第一個 process 得 0 → 跳出 loop 入 CS [6.16]。A 嘅條件相反（應該 != 0）。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC1 DMA", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about I/O operations and DMA is NOT true? In programmed I/O, the CPU executes special I/O instructions to move data between a device controller and memory A DMA controller can transfer a block of data between an I/O device and memory without continuous CPU involvement During a DMA transfer, the DMA controller sends an interrupt to the CPU after every byte is transferred After a DMA block transfer completes, the DMA controller can interrupt the CPU 解：DMA block 完成先 interrupt 一次 [3.", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC2 Interrupts and traps", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about interrupts and traps is NOT true? Hardware interrupts are generally asynchronous to the instruction currently being executed Interrupts can only be generated by I/O devices A trap or exception may be caused by an error or by a user request such as a system call Interrupts may have different priority levels 解：trap/exception = software-generated interrupt [3.24]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC3 Resource allocator", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Several jobs are ready to run on a shared computer. The OS decides which job receives CPU time and how memory is allocated. Which role does this illustrate? The OS provides a user interface The OS provides a communication service The OS acts as a resource allocator The OS acts as an application program 解：[1.23]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC4 NUMA", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about multiprocessor, multicore, and NUMA systems is NOT true? In SMP, processors have their own registers and local caches but share physical memory A multicore system places multiple computing cores on a single physical chip In NUMA, a CPU can access its local memory faster than remote memory connected to another CPU group In NUMA, every CPU accesses every part of memory with exactly the same latency 解：NUMA = non-uniform latency [1.40]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC5 OS structures", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about operating-system structures is true? A monolithic kernel moves most OS services into user-space processes that communicate by message passing A microkernel keeps only essential functions in the kernel and provides other services outside the kernel through communication mechanisms such as message passing In a layered OS, each layer may directly call any other layer without restriction A loadable kernel module always requires the entire kernel to be recompiled and the machine", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC6 read() path", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A C program calls read() to obtain data from a file. Which sequence best describes how the request reaches the OS? The application directly executes the device's privileged I/O instructions while remaining in user mode The read() API completes the entire file operation in user mode without invoking the kernel The API and run-time environment use the system-call interface to invoke the corresponding kernel service, and control later returns to the application The OS creates a new process to execu", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC7 Privileged instruction", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What normally happens if a user program attempts to execute a privileged instruction in user mode? The hardware treats the instruction as illegal and traps to the operating system The CPU automatically changes the instruction into a non-privileged instruction The user program changes the mode bit by itself and continues in kernel mode The instruction is always executed, but its result is discarded 解：[3.28]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC8 Virtualization", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about virtualization is NOT true? A virtual machine manager creates and manages virtual machines An application always runs faster inside a virtual machine than directly on the physical machine Each guest OS runs in its own VM and behaves as if it has access to hardware Virtualization allows multiple operating systems to run on one physical machine 解：VM 只會 minor performance decrease [1.46]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC9 fork", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about fork() is NOT true? After a successful fork(), the child receives 0 and the parent receives the child's PID The parent and child both continue execution at the instruction following fork() If the child changes a global variable after fork(), the parent's copy changes automatically The execution order of parent and child depends on scheduling unless synchronization is used 解：各自有 own copy。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW1-MC10 Pipes", "u": "quiz.html#HW1-MC10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about pipes is NOT true? An ordinary pipe is typically used between processes with a parent-child relationship A named pipe can be used by processes that do not have a parent-child relationship An ordinary pipe is typically unidirectional A named pipe ceases to exist as soon as the communicating processes terminate 解：named pipe 要 explicitly deleted。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC1 Responsiveness", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "An interactive application continues responding to the user while one of its threads is blocked. Which benefit does this illustrate? Economy Responsiveness Resource sharing Scalability 解：[4.7]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC2 Data parallelism", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which arrangement is an example of data parallelism? Threads on different cores each perform a different operation One thread processes all elements of an array sequentially One thread handles user input while another thread performs spelling checks Threads on different cores perform the same operation on different subsets of an array 解：A、C = task parallelism；B = serial [4.11]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC3 Signal classification", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement correctly classifies signals? Division by zero can generate a synchronous signal, whereas timer expiration can generate an asynchronous signal Division by zero can generate an asynchronous signal, whereas timer expiration can generate a synchronous signal Both generate synchronous signals Both generate asynchronous signals 解：[4.29]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC4 Deferred cancellation", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement describes deferred thread cancellation? The target thread is terminated immediately when cancellation is requested Requesting cancellation terminates every thread in its process The target thread periodically checks whether it should terminate, allowing it to terminate in an orderly manner The target thread can be canceled only after it has finished its normal execution 解：A 係 asynchronous cancellation [4.32]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC5 Preemptive scheduling", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which event is an example of preemptive CPU scheduling? A running process completes its execution and terminates A running process requests I/O and enters the waiting state A time quantum expires, and the running process is moved back to the ready queue A running process calls wait() and enters the waiting state 解：running → ready（involuntary）= preemptive [5.6]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC6 Convoy effect", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which situation illustrates the convoy effect in FCFS? A waiting process gradually gains a higher priority Several short processes wait behind a long process that arrived first A process moves to a lower-priority queue after using its time quantum A running process is preempted when its RR quantum expires 解：[5.10]。A = aging；C = MLFQ demotion。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC7 Aging", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What is the purpose of aging in priority scheduling? To address starvation by gradually increasing the priority of a waiting process To keep each process permanently in the same priority queue To predict the length of a process's next CPU burst To give higher priority to periodic tasks with shorter periods 解：[5.25]。D = RM。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC8 PCS vs SCS", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In a many-to-many model, a thread library selects a user thread to run on an available LWP. Which scheduling scope applies? SCS, because several user threads are competing SCS, because the library schedules kernel threads onto CPUs PCS, because all kernel threads in the system are competing PCS, because the competing threads belong to the same process 解：[5.37]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC9 Exponential averaging", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The predicted CPU burst is 10 ms, the actual burst is 6 ms, α=0.25. What is the next prediction? 6 ms 7 ms 9 ms 10 ms 解：τn+1​=0.25×6+0.75×10= 9。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "HW2-MC10 Little's formula", "u": "quiz.html#HW2-MC10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "3 processes arrive per second on average; average queue length is 12. Average waiting time? 0.25 s 4 s 9 s 36 s 解：W=n/λ=12/3= 4 s [5.57]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "Lab4-MC Threads share memory, processes do not", "u": "quiz.html#Lab4-MC", "p": "Quiz", "x": "void *child(void *arg) { int *counter = (int *) arg; (*counter)++; return NULL; } int main() { int counter = 0; pthread_t thread; pthread_create(&thread, NULL, child, (void *)&counter); pthread_join(thread, NULL); printf(\"%d\\n\", counter); counter = 0; pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid) wait(NULL); else { child((void *)&counter); exit(0); } printf(\"%d\\n\", counter); } What is the output? 0 then 0 1 then 0 0 then 1 1 then 1 解：thread 同 main 共用 counter → 印 1；fork 出嚟嘅 child 改嘅係自己嗰份 copy → parent 印 0。Threads", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-1 Storage managed by", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "According to the storage characteristics table, which level of storage is managed by the compiler? Registers Cache Main memory Solid-state disk 解：registers – compiler；cache – hardware；main memory / SSD / disk – OS [1.34]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-2 Bootstrap", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Where is the bootstrap program (the first program run at power-on) typically stored? DRAM Cache EEPROM Hard disk 解：[1.30] bootstrap program stored on EEPROM（firmware）。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-3 Multiprocessor speedup", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Why is the speed-up ratio with N processors less than N? Each processor runs a different instruction set Overhead, e.g., contention for shared resources such as the bus or memory Only one processor can execute user processes Processors must share a single register set 解：[1.37]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-4 Multiprocessor advantages", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is NOT an advantage of multiprocessor systems listed in the lecture? Increased throughput Economy of scale Increased reliability (graceful degradation) Simpler parallel programming 解：multiprocessor 令 parallel programming 更複雜 [1.37, summary]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-5 NUMA drawback", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What is the potential drawback of a NUMA system? All CPUs must share a single slow bus Increased latency when a CPU must access remote memory across the system interconnect CPUs cannot share one physical address space It consumes more power than multicore chips 解：[1.40]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-6 Cloud models", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A database server provided as a ready-to-use software stack over the Internet is an example of: SaaS PaaS IaaS MaaS 解：SaaS = applications；PaaS = software stack (e.g. database server)；IaaS = servers/storage [1.48]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-7 VMM", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about virtualization components is correct? The guest OS creates and manages the virtual machine manager The VMM (hypervisor) creates and manages VMs, providing an interface identical to the host The host is the virtual copy given to each guest Programs in a VM usually run several times faster than natively 解：[1.44]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-8 Timesharing", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement is correct? Multiprogramming is an extension of timesharing Timesharing switches jobs so frequently that users can interact with each job while it runs Timesharing requires that only one program be in memory Multiprogramming is not used in modern systems 解：timesharing = logical extension of multiprogramming [1.42]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-9 Mechanical vs electrical storage", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Compared with mechanical storage (e.g., HDD), electrical storage (NVM) is generally: Larger and cheaper per byte More costly, smaller, more reliable and faster Slower but more reliable Volatile 解：[1.31]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch1-10 AMAT", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch1-10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A cache has hit ratio 95%, hit time 1 ns and miss time 100 ns. The average access time is: 1.0 ns 5.0 ns 5.95 ns 100 ns 解：0.95×1+0.05×100=0.95+5= 5.95 ns。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-1 ioctl", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In UNIX, ioctl() belongs to which category of system calls? Process control File management Device management Protection 解：[2.25] device management：ioctl()、read()、write()。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-2 chmod", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "chmod(), umask() and chown() are examples of which category of system calls? Information maintenance Communications Protection File management 解：[2.25]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-3 Parameter passing limit", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which parameter-passing methods do not limit the number or length of parameters? Registers only Block (table) and stack methods Registers and stack None of them 解：[2.20]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-4 Policy vs mechanism", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Deciding how long the timer should be set for a particular user is an example of: A mechanism A policy A system call A system program 解：timer 本身 = mechanism；設幾耐 = policy [2.34]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-5 Shell", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What is the primary functionality of a shell? Schedule kernel threads Fetch a command from the user, interpret it and execute it Manage virtual memory Load device drivers 解：[2.8]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-6 DLL", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What is a benefit of dynamically linked libraries (DLLs)? They are linked statically into every executable They are loaded when needed and shared by all programs using the same version They remove the need for a loader They make executables larger 解：[2.31]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-7 MS-DOS", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about MS-DOS is correct? It is a multitasking system with dual mode Application programs could access I/O directly, and it had no dual mode or hardware protection It is a microkernel design It creates a new process for every program 解：[2.36, 2.39]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-8 Microkernel services", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A microkernel typically provides: File systems and device drivers only Minimal process and memory management, plus a communication facility The GUI and shells All OS services in kernel mode 解：[2.46]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-9 Darwin", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Darwin, the kernel environment of macOS/iOS, consists of: Linux kernel and glibc Mach microkernel and BSD UNIX kernel Windows NT kernel and personalities ART and JNI 解：[2.54, 2.57]（hybrid，XNU kernel）。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-10 Android", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about Android is false? Java applications execute on the Android RunTime (ART) Programs written using JNI are generally portable across hardware Android uses the Bionic C library instead of glibc The HAL abstracts hardware to provide a consistent view 解：JNI programs generally not portable [2.58]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-11 Windows structure", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Windows is described as: Pure microkernel Largely monolithic with microkernel-like subsystems (personalities) and loadable modules Pure layered Simple structure like MS-DOS 解：[2.52]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch2-12 High-level language", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch2-12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is a main advantage of implementing an OS in a high-level language? Always faster than assembly Easier to port to other hardware Less storage needed No compiler needed 解：[2.35]；缺點 = reduced speed、more storage。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-1 Default file descriptors", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "On UNIX, each process by default has three open file descriptors. Which are they? stdin (0), stdout (1), stderr (2) stdin (1), stdout (2), stderr (3) pipe read end, pipe write end, stderr stdin, stdout, swap 解：[3.7]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-2 Linux PCB", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In Linux, the PCB is represented by: struct pcb in a hash table struct task_struct elements in a circular doubly linked list (task list) TCB in a binary tree ETHREAD 解：[3.13]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-3 Long-term scheduler", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about the long-term scheduler is correct? It is invoked every few milliseconds It controls the degree of multiprogramming and is mainly used in mainframes, not PCs It swaps processes out to disk It selects the next process from the ready queue 解：[3.17]。C = medium-term；D = short-term。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-4 Register sets", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Some processors (e.g., SUN UltraSPARC) provide multiple register sets. What is the effect on context switch? Context switch becomes impossible A context switch may just change the pointer to a different register set The PCB is no longer needed Context switch time increases 解：[3.20]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-5 Copy-on-write", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In Linux, fork() uses copy-on-write. What does this achieve? Parent and child never share any pages Copying of the address space is delayed until a page is written, speeding up process creation The child always runs exec() first All pages are copied immediately 解：[3.46]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-6 Mode bit", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The hardware mode bit is ____ in user mode and ____ in kernel mode. 0, 1 1, 0 1, 1 0, 0 解：[3.28]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-7 Protection rings", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "On Intel processors with four protection rings: Ring 3 is kernel mode and ring 0 is user mode Ring 0 is kernel mode and ring 3 is user mode All four rings are heavily used Ring 1 is for the VMM only 解：[3.29]；rings 1、2 rarely used。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-8 Trap", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A segmentation fault (protection violation) causes which type of mode transfer? System call Interrupt Trap or exception Context switch only 解：trap/exception = internal synchronous event [3.31]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-9 Bounded buffer", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The shared-memory bounded-buffer solution with in and out and no counter can hold at most: BUFFER_SIZE items BUFFER_SIZE − 1 items BUFFER_SIZE + 1 items Unlimited items 解：[3.54]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-10 Direct communication", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is a property of direct communication in message passing? Messages go through mailboxes A link is associated with exactly one pair of processes A link may be associated with more than two processes It provides better modularity than indirect communication 解：[3.59]；缺點 = limited modularity。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-11 Zero capacity", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "With a zero-capacity message queue: The sender never waits The sender waits only if the queue is full The sender must wait for the receiver (rendezvous) Messages are lost 解：[3.64]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-12 UNIX FIFO", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about named pipes (FIFOs) in UNIX is correct? They support full-duplex transmission over networks They are created with mkfifo(), appear as files, support half-duplex, and processes must be on the same machine They disappear when the creating process exits They require a parent-child relationship 解：[3.69]；Windows named pipes 先係 full-duplex、可跨機。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-13 Ports", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-13", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about client–server sockets is correct? Servers initiate the connection to clients All ports below 1024 are well known; clients pick a port above 1024 HTTP uses port 22 A socket is identified by the MAC address only 解：[3.71]；HTTP 80、SSH 22、FTP 21。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-14 Orphan", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-14", "p": "Quiz", "x": "When a parent terminates without calling wait(), its child process: Is immediately deleted by the kernel in all OSes Becomes an orphan and is re-parented to init/systemd, which periodically calls wait() Becomes a zombie forever Takes over the parent's PID 解：[3.50]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-15 exec", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-15", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is true about a successful exec() call? It returns the PID of the new program It returns 0 to the child It does not return; the process image is replaced It creates a new process 解：exec 唔會建立新 process，只係 replace 現有 process 嘅 image [3.41]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-16 fork count", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-16", "p": "Quiz", "x": "How many processes are there in total after: fork(); fork() && fork(); fork(); 8 10 12 16 解：2 × 3 × 2 = 12（fork() && fork() 令每個 process 變 3 個）。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-17 Interrupt vector", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-17", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The interrupt vector is: A list of processes waiting for I/O A table of addresses of interrupt handlers, indexed by interrupt number The register that holds the next instruction address A queue of DMA requests 解：Ch3 summary。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch3-18 Nonmaskable interrupt", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch3-18", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A nonmaskable interrupt is reserved for events such as: Keyboard input Unrecoverable memory errors and power failure Timer expiry for RR Disk I/O completion 解：Ch3 summary；maskable 用於 device controllers。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-1 Multicore challenges", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is NOT one of the multicore programming challenges listed in the lecture? Dividing tasks Data dependency Testing and debugging Reducing the number of system calls 解：五大：dividing tasks、balance、data splitting、data dependency、testing & debugging [4.9]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-2 Windows TEB", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In Windows, which thread data structure lives in user space and contains the thread id, user-mode stack and thread-local storage? ETHREAD KTHREAD TEB PCB 解：[4.36]。ETHREAD、KTHREAD 喺 kernel space。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-3 clone()", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which call creates a Linux task that behaves like a thread? clone(SIGCHLD, 0) clone(CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND, 0) fork() followed by exec() pthread_kill() 解：share address space、fs info、files、signal handlers [4.38]；A = normal fork。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-4 Linux threads", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "How does Linux treat threads? As lightweight processes with separate TCB and PCB structures As tasks — a thread is a process that shares certain resources with other tasks Only user-level threads are supported Through the many-to-one model 解：[4.37]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-5 Signal delivery", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A synchronous signal such as division by zero should be delivered to: Every thread in the process The thread that caused the signal A random thread The parent process 解：[4.31]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-6 Pthread cancellation default", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In Pthreads, the default cancellation type is: Asynchronous Deferred Disabled permanently Immediate 解：[4.32]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-7 Amdahl limit", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "An application is 50% parallel. As the number of cores approaches infinity, the maximum speedup is: 1.5 2 4 Infinite 解：1/S=1/0.5= 2。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-8 One-to-one drawback", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What is the main drawback of the one-to-one model? One blocking call blocks the whole process Threads cannot run in parallel Creating a user thread requires creating a kernel thread, so the number of threads may be restricted It is difficult to implement 解：[4.23]。A、B 係 many-to-one；D 係 many-to-many。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-9 Thread context switch", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Why is a context switch between two threads of the same process cheaper than between processes? Threads have no registers The address space stays the same Threads do not have stacks The OS does not save any state 解：[4.14]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch4-10 fork in multithreaded process", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch4-10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "If exec() is called immediately after fork() in a multithreaded process, which fork() version is appropriate? Duplicate all threads Duplicate only the calling thread Duplicate no threads It does not matter for correctness or efficiency 解：exec 會 replace 成個 process，複製所有 threads 係浪費 [4.28]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-1 Dispatcher", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which is NOT a function of the dispatcher? Switching context Switching to user mode Jumping to the proper location in the user program Selecting the next process using the scheduling algorithm 解：揀 process 係 CPU scheduler；dispatcher 只負責交 CPU 畀佢 [5.7]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-2 RR waiting bound", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "With n processes and quantum q under RR (ignoring context switches), no process waits more than: nq (n − 1)q q / n n + q 解：[5.11]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-3 Quantum rule of thumb", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A common rule of thumb for choosing the RR quantum is: q should be smaller than the context-switch time 80% of CPU bursts should be shorter than q q should equal the longest CPU burst q should be 1 ms 解：[5.16]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-4 Exponential averaging α = 0", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "With exponential averaging, if α=0 then: Only the last CPU burst counts Recent history does not count; the prediction never changes All bursts are weighted equally The prediction is always 0 解：τn+1​=τn​ [5.22]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-5 SJF as priority", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "SJF is a special case of priority scheduling where the priority is: The arrival time The inverse of the predicted next CPU burst The process ID The period of the task 解：[5.25]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-6 MLFQ preemption rule", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In the lecture's MLFQ, when a job running from Q1 is preempted by a new arrival in Q0, it: Is demoted to Q2 Joins the tail of Q1 with a fresh quantum Joins the head of Q1 with its remaining quantum Is moved to Q0 解：[5.31]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-7 Logical CPUs", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A quad-core processor with 2 hardware threads per core appears to the OS as how many logical processors? 2 4 6 8 解：4 × 2 = 8 [5.42]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-8 Push migration", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Push migration means: An idle processor pulls a waiting task from a busy processor A specific task periodically checks loads and moves tasks from overloaded to idle/less-busy CPUs A process specifies the CPUs it may run on A thread is pinned to one core forever 解：A = pull migration [5.46]；C = hard affinity [5.45]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-9 Affinity system call", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In Linux, which system call supports hard affinity? sched_setaffinity() setpriority() clone() nice() 解：[5.45]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-10 RM optimality", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Rate-monotonic scheduling is optimal in the sense that: It always achieves 100% CPU utilization If a task set cannot be scheduled by RM, it cannot be scheduled by any other static-priority algorithm It never misses deadlines It handles aperiodic tasks best 解：[5.52]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-11 EDF requirement", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "What is the only requirement EDF places on a process? It must be periodic It must have a constant CPU burst It must announce its deadline to the scheduler when it becomes runnable Its period must be a power of 2 解：[5.52]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-12 RM utilization", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Two periodic tasks: P1 (period 50, burst 25), P2 (period 80, burst 35). Under RM: Total utilization is 75% and no deadline is missed Total utilization ≈ 94% and P2 misses its deadline at 80 (finishes at 85) P1 misses its deadline EDF would also miss a deadline 解：[5.50–5.51]；EDF 可以全部 meet。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-13 Deterministic modeling", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-13", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement about deterministic modeling is correct? It uses probability distributions of CPU bursts It takes a predetermined workload and gives exact numbers, but results apply only to that workload It requires running the real system It is the most accurate method 解：[5.54]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-14 Response time (course definition)", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-14", "p": "Quiz", "x": "RR with q = 4; P1 (24), P2 (3), P3 (3) all arrive at 0 in that order. Using the course definition, the average response time is: 3.67 5.67 7 15.67 解：end of first run − arrival = 4, 7, 10 → avg 7 [5.13]。（3.67 係課本「first start」定義；5.67 係 avg WT；15.67 係 avg TAT） ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-15 Non-preemptive kernel / scheduling", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-15", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Scheduling that happens only when a process switches from running to waiting or terminates is called: Preemptive Non-preemptive (cooperative) Real-time Multilevel 解：[5.6]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch5-16 SMP common queue issue", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch5-16", "p": "Quiz", "x": "With a single common ready queue in SMP, what must the system guard against? Two processors choosing to schedule the same thread (race condition) Processor affinity being too strong Too many push migrations Memory stalls 解：[5.39]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-1 Disabling interrupts", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-1", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Why is disabling interrupts not a good critical-section solution on multiprocessors? It is not allowed in kernel mode It is inefficient — interrupts must be disabled on all processors It causes race conditions on uniprocessors It requires semaphores 解：[6.13]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-2 CAS return value", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-2", "p": "Quiz", "x": "compare_and_swap(&value, expected, new_value) returns: new_value expected The original value of *value TRUE if the swap happened 解：[6.17]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-3 Negative semaphore", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-3", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In the blocking (no busy waiting) semaphore implementation, a value of −3 means: The semaphore is broken Three processes are waiting on the semaphore Three resources are available Three signals were lost 解：[6.26]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-4 Spinlock usage", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-4", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Spinlocks are most appropriate when: Locks are held for a long time on a uniprocessor Locks are held for short times on multiprocessor systems Processes must sleep while waiting Context switches are free 解：[6.22]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-5 Preemptive kernel", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-5", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Which statement is correct? A preemptive kernel is free from race conditions A non-preemptive kernel is essentially free from race conditions in kernel mode (on a single processor) Non-preemptive kernels are harder to make correct on SMP than preemptive ones Kernel code never has race conditions 解：[6.12]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-6 Kernel race example", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-6", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The lecture's kernel race-condition example involves two processes calling fork() concurrently. Which variable is at risk? counter next_available_pid lock turn 解：same PID could be assigned to two processes [6.8]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-7 P and V", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-7", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In Dijkstra's notation, P() and V() correspond to: signal() and wait() wait() and signal() acquire() and test_and_set() lock() and fork() 解：P = proberen (test)，V = verhogen (increment) [6.24]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-8 Semaphore deadlock", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-8", "p": "Quiz", "x": "P0: wait(S); wait(Q); … P1: wait(Q); wait(S); … with S = Q = 1. What can happen? Starvation only Deadlock Race condition on S only Nothing; this is always safe 解：[6.28]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-9 Starvation in semaphore queue", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-9", "p": "Quiz", "x": "Starvation (indefinite blocking) can occur with semaphores when: Processes are removed from the waiting queue in LIFO order The semaphore is initialized to 1 signal() is atomic The waiting queue is FIFO 解：[6.28]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-10 Atomic increment", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-10", "p": "Quiz", "x": "In do { temp = *v; } while (temp != compare_and_swap(v, temp, temp + 1));, why might the loop repeat? Because CAS always fails the first time Because another thread changed *v between reading temp and the CAS, so the CAS returned a different value Because temp + 1 overflows It never repeats 解：[6.21]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-11 Counting semaphore initial value", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-11", "p": "Quiz", "x": "A counting semaphore controlling access to 5 identical printers should be initialized to: 0 1 5 −5 解：initialized to the number of resources available [6.25]。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "P-ch6-12 Simple TAS lock", "u": "quiz.html#P-ch6-12", "p": "Quiz", "x": "The simple lock while (test_and_set(&lock)); /*CS*/ lock = false; satisfies: Mutual exclusion and bounded waiting Mutual exclusion but not bounded waiting Bounded waiting but not mutual exclusion None of the requirements 解：要用 waiting[] 版本 [6.19] 先有 bounded waiting。 ", "w": -2}, {"t": "S26-Q2.1 Race condition (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q2.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "What is a race condition? Give an example. A race condition occurs when two or more processes/threads access and manipulate shared data concurrently, and the final result depends on the order in which the accesses take place; since the order is unpredictable, the result may be incorrect or inconsistent. (2.5) Example: two threads increment the same counter without synchronization — counter++ is load/add/store, so both may load the same old value and one update is lost. (1.5) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q2.2 Caching (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q2.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "What is the fundamental idea of caching? Is a high cache hit ratio alone enough to guarantee good performance? Explain by considering hit time and miss penalty. Caching temporarily copies a subset of information from a slower but larger storage into a faster but smaller storage so that frequently used data can be accessed more quickly. (2) No — hit ratio is a major criterion but not sufficient (1); performance is the average access time = hit ratio × hit time + miss ratio × miss time, which also depends on the hit time and the miss penalty (1). A slow cache or a huge miss penalty can still giv", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q2.3 Custom policy fᵢ = 1/(a·cᵢ + b) (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q2.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "The OS keeps ci​ = average past CPU burst of Pi​ and picks the process with the highest fi​=aci​+b1​ (a,b>0), running it for one quantum or until it gives up the CPU. (a) Does this prioritize I/O-bound over CPU-bound processes? (b) Can it cause starvation? (a) Yes — I/O-bound processes have small CPU bursts ci​, hence a larger fi​ and higher priority. (b) Yes — a CPU-bound process (large ci​, low fi​) can starve if higher-priority I/O-bound processes keep arriving. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q2.4 swap spinlock (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q2.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": "swap(x, v) atomically sets x to v and returns the old value of x. locked = 1 when held, 0 otherwise. (a) Write the line that acquires the spinlock with a busy-spin loop. (b) Advantage and disadvantage of a spinlock. (a) while (swap(locked, 1) == 1); (release: locked = 0;) (b) Disadvantage: wastes CPU cycles due to busy waiting. Advantage: no context switch is needed when a process must wait on a lock (a context switch can take considerable time) — good for short critical sections on multiprocessors. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q3.1 fork tree with break (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (fork()) fork(); else break; } (a) n = 2: draw the process tree; total processes and number of breaks. (b) Relationship between total processes and n. (a) 7 processes, 3 breaks. Tree: P0 → P1 (break), P2, P3 (break), P4; P2 → P5 (break), P6. (b) Each active process per iteration creates 2 children: the first breaks, the second keeps looping → active count doubles: Ak​=2k. After n iterations: active =2n, stopped =1+2+⋯+2n−1=2n−1. Total =2n+1−1 (breaks =2n−1). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q3.2 fork + exec scenarios (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q3.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "int ret = fork(); if (ret == 0) { printf(\"Hello1\\n\"); exec(\"some_executable\"); printf(\"Hello2\\n\"); } else if (ret > 0) { wait(NULL); printf(\"Hello3\\n\"); } else { printf(\"Hello4\\n\"); } List all possible scenarios and outputs. Case I — fork succeeds, exec succeeds: Hello1, Hello3 (exec replaces the image; Hello2 never runs). Case II — fork succeeds, exec fails (returns −1): Hello1, Hello2, Hello3. Case III — fork fails: Hello4. (Hello3 always last because the parent waits.) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q3.3 pipe + dup fill-in (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q3.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Child counts even numbers in 1.txt, parent in 2.txt (file: length then elements, read with scanf); child sends its count via pipe; parent prints the total. Blank 1: close(0) · Blank 2: dup(fin) (stdin now refers to the file) Blank 3: if (number % 2 == 0) cnt++ Blank 4.1: fd[1] · 4.2: &cnt (child: write(fd[1], &cnt, sizeof(int))) Blank 5.1: fd[0] · 5.2: &child_cnt (parent: read(fd[0], &child_cnt, sizeof(int))) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q4.1 MLFQ Q0 RR4 / Q1 RR8 / Q2 FCFS (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q4.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (0, 9), P2 (2, 25), P3 (6, 7), P4 (14, 18), P5 (45, 15), P6 (58, 6). New jobs → Q0; full quantum → demote; Q0 arrival preempts Q1/Q2 immediately; preempted job → head of its queue; no demotion when preempted. Gantt: 0 P1 4 P2 8 P3 12 P1 14 P4 18 P1 21 P2 29 P3 32 P4 40 P2 45 P5 57 P2 58 P6 64 P2 71 P4 77 P5 80 (P5 45–49 Q0 then 49–57 Q1; P6 58–62 Q0 then 62–64 Q1). Waiting = completion − arrival − burst: P1 21−0−9 = 12; P2 71−2−25 = 44; P3 32−6−7 = 19; P4 77−14−18 = 45; P5 80−45−15 = 20; P6 64−58−6 = 0 → avg =6140​=23.33. Response (end of first run − arrival): 4, 6, 6, 4, 4 (49−45), 4 (62−5", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q4.2 RM / EDF with D < T (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q4.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "(t, d, p): P1 (1, 3, 4), P2 (2, 4, 5), P3 (2, 6, 7), P4 (1, 5, 8); all arrive at 0; draw 0–16; ties → smaller ID; count missed deadlines. RM (P1 > P2 > P3 > P4 by period): 0 P1 1 P2 3 P3 4 P1 5 P2 7 P3 8 P1 9 P3 10 P2 12 P1 13 P3 14 P3 15 P2 16 → missed: P3 (d 6, finished 8), P4 (d 5, never), P3 (d 13, finished 14), P4 (d 13, never) → 4 missed. (U = 1.06 > 1.) EDF: 0 P1 1 P2 3 P4 4 P3 6 P1 7 P2 9 P1 10 P3 12 P4 13 P2 15 P1 16 → missed: P2 (d 14, finished 15), P1 (d 15, finished 16) → 2 missed. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q4.3 Dynamic priority (9 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q4.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Larger value = higher priority; waiting changes priority at rate α, running at rate β; tie → larger ID. P1 (0, 7), P2 (3, 5), P3 (6, 6), P4 (9, 4). (a) α=0, β=3, initial 0: 0 P1 7 P3 13 P4 17 P2 22 (b) α=1, β=0, only P1, P2, check every 3: 0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 9 P2 11 P1 12 (c) α=1, β=−2, initial 30, check every 3: 0 P1 3 P2 6 P3 9 P4 12 P1 15 P2 17 P3 20 P4 21 P1 22 ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S26-Q5 Semaphore ordering (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#S26-Q5", "p": "Past papers", "x": "A depends on B and C; B depends on C. s0, s1, s2 initialised to 0. workerA: BLANK1; wait(&s1); print A. workerB: BLANK2; print B; signal(&s2). workerC: BLANK3; print C; BLANK4; signal(&s1). BLANK1 wait(&s2) · BLANK2 wait(&s0) · BLANK3 /* Nothing */ · BLANK4 signal(&s0) → order always C, B, A. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q1.1 Linker and loader (2 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q1.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "What do the linker and loader do, respectively? The linker combines relocatable object files (and libraries) into a single binary executable file; the loader loads the binary executable file into memory so it can run. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q1.2 Locality (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q1.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "What are temporal locality and spatial locality? Temporal locality: a memory location accessed recently is likely to be accessed again in the near future. Spatial locality: locations near a recently accessed location are likely to be accessed soon. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q1.3 Why separate API and system calls (2 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q1.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Two reasons for separating APIs from the underlying system calls? (1) Program portability — the API stays the same across platforms. (2) Hide the complex details of system calls from programmers. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q1.4 LKM vs layered (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q1.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Similarity and difference between the loadable-kernel-module approach and the layered approach? Similarity: functionality is divided into modules, each with a well-defined interface. Difference: LKM is more flexible — any module can call any other module (no higher/lower layer restriction), and modules can be loaded/removed at run time. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.1 State transitions (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Fill in the actions for: running→ready, ready→running, running→waiting, waiting→ready. running→ready: interrupt; ready→running: scheduler dispatch (CPU scheduling); running→waiting: I/O or event wait; waiting→ready: I/O or event completion. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.2 Socket (2 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Four pieces of information that uniquely define a socket connection? Source IP address, source port number, destination IP address, destination port number. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.3 Thread unique vs shared (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Three elements unique to each thread and three shared. Unique: program counter, registers (state), stack (thread ID). Shared: code, data (globals/heap), open files. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.4 Shell system calls (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Two system calls a shell must execute to start a new process on UNIX? fork() — clones the calling process (creates a child); exec() — overlays/replaces the child's address space with a new program (loads the program to run). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.5 Multicore vs SMP, CMT (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.5", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Two advantages of multicore vs conventional SMP; reason for chip multithreading (hyper-threading). Multicore is faster (on-chip communication) and consumes less power than separate single-core chips. CMT exploits memory stall: processors are much faster than memory, so a core may spend up to ~50% of its time waiting for data; with multiple hardware threads the core switches to another thread while one waits. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.6 Lines printed vs n (5 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.6", "p": "Past papers", "x": "for (i = 0; i < n; ++i) { if (fork()) printf(\"Fork!\\n\"); else printf(\"Fork!\\n\"); } Each iteration doubles the processes and every process prints one line: iteration i prints 2i lines → total 2+4+⋯+2n=2n+1−2 ✅ ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q2.7 fork()==fork() || fork()!=fork() (5 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q2.7", "p": "Past papers", "x": "if (fork() == fork() || fork() != fork()) fork(); printf(\"Fork!\\n\"); 23. After fork()==fork() there are 4 processes; only the one that got 0 twice is true → it skips the rest of || and forks → 2 lines. The other 3 run fork()!=fork() → 12 processes; 9 are true and fork again → 18; 3 false → 3. Total 2 + 18 + 3 = 23 ✅ ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q3.1 Which threads does the OS schedule (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Which threads are scheduled by the OS? How does mapping user threads onto kernel threads/LWPs differ from CPU scheduling? The OS schedules kernel-level threads. Scheduling user threads onto LWPs/kernel threads (PCS) is done by the thread library, usually by programmer-set priority, among threads of the same process; CPU scheduling (SCS) chooses which kernel thread runs on a CPU among all kernel threads in the system. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q3.2 FCFS problem; RR worse than FCFS (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q3.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " FCFS: convoy effect — short processes stuck behind a long one. RR has worse turnaround than FCFS when all processes have the same CPU burst length that is larger than the quantum (they all finish near the end). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q3.3 MLFQ approximates SJF (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q3.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Short interactive jobs finish in the top (high-priority) queue, while long CPU-bound jobs sink to lower queues — so short jobs are served first, like SJF. Advantage: no prior knowledge of CPU burst times is needed. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q3.4 EDF to t = 38 (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q3.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (period = deadline 10, t 4), P2 (16, 2), P3 (20, 8). 0 P1 4 P2 6 P3 14 P1 18 P2 20 P1 24 P3 32 P1 36 P2 38 (deadline ties at t = 10 and 30 → the running P3 continues). No deadline missed. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q3.5 Least Used First (12 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q3.5", "p": "Past papers", "x": "LUF picks the process that has used the fewest CPU cycles; tie → FCFS; preempted process goes to the end of the queue; scheduler runs on completion and on new arrival. P1 (0, 25), P2 (15, 25), P3 (25, 5), P4 (40, 5). (a) 0 P1 15 P2 25 P3 30 P2 40 P4 45 P1 55 P2 60 (b) TAT / RT / WT: P1 55 / 15 / 30; P2 45 / 10 / 20; P3 5 / 5 / 0; P4 5 / 5 / 0. (c) Similar to SRTF: short jobs tend to get high priority. Different: under LUF a process that has already run a long time is likely to starve; under SRTF a process with a long burst is likely to starve from the beginning. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q3.6 FCFS / SJF / RR4 / SRTF (12 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q3.6", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (0, 8), P2 (7, 13), P3 (9, 1), P4 (11, 5), P5 (20, 7), P6 (27, 1). FCFS 0 P1 8 P2 21 P3 22 P4 27 P5 34 P6 35 — WT 0,1,12,11,7,7 → 6.33 SJF 0 P1 8 P2 21 P3 22 P4 27 P6 28 P5 35 — WT 0,1,12,11,8,0 → 5.33 RR q=4 0 P1 8 P2 12 P3 13 P4 17 P2 21 P4 22 P5 26 P2 30 P5 33 P6 34 P2 35 — WT 0,15,3,6,6,6 → 6.0 (corrected official) SRTF 0 P1 8 P2 9 P3 10 P2 11 P4 16 P2 20 P5 27 P6 28 P2 35 — WT 0,15,0,0,0,0 → 2.5 (official; keeping P2 on the tie at t=20 also gives 2.5) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q4.1 CS requirements (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q4.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": " (1) Mutual exclusion — if a process is executing in its CS, no other process can be in its CS. (2) Progress — if no process is in its CS and some want to enter, the selection of the next one cannot be postponed indefinitely. (3) Bounded waiting — there is a bound on the number of times other processes may enter their CS after a process has requested entry and before that request is granted. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q4.2 Binary semaphore vs mutex (3 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q4.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " No. A binary semaphore initialised to 1 can be used as a mutex lock, but it can also be used for other synchronization purposes (e.g., initialised to 0 for ordering/signalling). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q4.3 Possible values of c (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q4.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "a = 7, b = 2, c = 5. T1: if (a < 0) c = b - a; else c = b + a; T2: b = 8; a = -6; 14, 2, 9, 15, −4 — (a<0 branch: 8−(−6) = 14); else-branch: T2 done before c computed: 8+(−6) = 2; T2 after: 2+7 = 9; b written before read but a after: 8+7 = 15; b read before write, a written before read: 2+(−6) = −4. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F19-Q4.4 Semaphores L = 3, R = 0 with putc (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F19-Q4.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1: wait(L); putc('C'); signal(R). P2: wait(R); putc('A'); putc('B'); signal(R). P3: wait(R); putc('D'). (a) 3 D's. (b) A's: min 0, max infinite. (c) CABABDCDABCABD — No (after \"CABABDCD\", R = 0, so P2 cannot print the next A before a third C). (d) CABACDBCABDD — Yes. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q2.1 Microkernel (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q2.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Main idea: remove all nonessential components from the kernel and implement them as system/user-level programs in separate address spaces; communicate by message passing. Advantage: small kernel, easier to extend and port, more secure and reliable. Disadvantage: performance suffers due to increased system-function overhead (user↔kernel message passing). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q2.2 Linker and loader (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q2.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Linker: combines several relocatable object modules into a single binary executable image (stored on disk). Loader: loads the executable into memory for execution. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q2.3 Heavyweight vs lightweight (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q2.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": " A process is heavyweight because it owns an address space and resources; creating one duplicates program and data. A thread is lightweight — only an execution unit (PC, registers, stack) that shares code, data and files with other threads of its process, so creating it needs no duplication. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q2.4 Common vs per-processor ready queue (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q2.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Common queue: easy load balancing (no CPU idle while the queue is non-empty) but hard to keep processor affinity (and needs locking). Per-processor queues: natural processor affinity but need push/pull migration for load balancing. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q2.5 MLFQ benefits (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q2.5", "p": "Past papers", "x": " (1) Resembles SJF/SRTF performance without prior knowledge of the next CPU burst (2 pts); (2) RR-like good response time for interactive jobs (1); (3) fair — all processes make progress (1). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q3.1 Possible outputs (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "if (fork() == 0) { if (fork() > 0) { printf(\"1\"); wait(NULL); printf(\"2\"); } else printf(\"3\"); } else printf(\"4\"); Unbuffered: 8 outputs — \"2\" after \"1\" and \"3\"; \"4\" anywhere: 1342, 1432, 3142, 4132, 3412, 4312, 1324, 3124. Buffered (\"1\" and \"2\" flushed together): 3 outputs — 4312, 3412, 3124. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q3.2 Count \"a\" (13 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q3.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "if (!fork()) { for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (fork()) fork(); } fork(); printf(\"a\"); First fork: the child enters the loop; each iteration every process becomes 3 → 3n processes from the child branch. Plus the original → 3n+1. The final fork doubles → 2(3n+1) \"a\"s ✅ ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q4.1 FCFS / SJF / SRTF (9 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q4.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (0, 9), P2 (3, 3), P3 (8, 2), P4 (10, 6), P5 (16, 8), P6 (20, 5). FCFS 0 P1 9 P2 12 P3 14 P4 20 P5 28 P6 33 → (0+6+4+4+4+8)/6 = 4.33 SJF 0 P1 9 P3 11 P2 14 P4 20 P6 25 P5 33 → (0+8+1+4+9+0)/6 = 3.67 SRTF 0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 8 P3 10 P1 14 P4 20 P6 25 P5 33 → (5+0+0+4+9+0)/6 = 3.0 ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q4.2 Two-level feedback queue with aging (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q4.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Q0 RR 4 ms (high), Q1 FCFS; new → Q0; Q0 arrival preempts Q1; preempted Q1 process → head of Q1 with timer reset; a Q1 process whose timer reaches 8 ms rejoins Q0. P1 (0, 9), P2 (3, 3), P3 (8, 2), P4 (10, 9), P5 (13, 4), P6 (18, 5). 0 P1 4 P2 7 P1 8 P3 10 P4 14 P5 18 P1 22 P6 26 P4 30 P6 31 P4 32 → WT (13+1+0+13+1+8)/6 = 6. (P1 ages back to Q0 at 16; P4 at 22.) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q4.3 Priority + RR (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q4.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Smaller number = higher priority; preemptive; same priority → RR q = 4. P1 (0, 9, 2), P2 (3, 3, 1), P3 (8, 5, 4), P4 (10, 2, 4), P5 (16, 8, 5), P6 (20, 5, 3). 0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 12 P3 16 P4 18 P3 19 P5 20 P6 25 P5 32 → (3+0+6+6+8+0)/6 = 3.83 ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F20-Q5 Possible values of c (15 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F20-Q5", "p": "Past papers", "x": "a = 7, b = 2. T1: if (b < 0) c = b - a; else c = b + a; T2: b = -8; a = 6; −14, −15, −2, −1, 8, 9 (2.5 marks each). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q1.1 pid1/pid2 (5 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q1.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "pid1 = fork(); pid2 = fork(); if (pid1 > 0) if (pid2 == 0) fork(); 5 — 4 processes after two forks; only the child created by the parent's second fork has pid1 > 0 and pid2 == 0, so one more process ✅ ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q1.2 Nested loop (5 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q1.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "if (fork() == 0) { for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) if (fork() == 0) fork(); } fork(); (35+1)×2=488 ✅ ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q1.3 Output orderings (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q1.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "printf(\"A\\n\"); if (fork() == 0) printf(\"B\\n\"); else { printf(\"C\\n\"); ___A___ } printf(\"D\\n\"); (a) empty: ABDCD, ABCDD, ACBDD, ACDBD. (b) wait(NULL): ABDCD, ABCDD, ACBDD. (c) printf(\"E\\n\"): ABDCED, ABCDED, ABCEDD, ACBDED, ACBEDD, ACEBDD, ACEDBD. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q2.1 FCFS / SJF / RR8 / SRTF (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q2.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (0, 2), P2 (4, 12), P3 (6, 6), P4 (9, 4), P5 (13, 5). FCFS 7.2 · SJF 6.6 · RR(8) 7.6 · SRTF 4.2 (Gantt charts in Ch5 §14.9; note the idle gap 2–4). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q2.2 MLFQ q=2, q=4, FCFS (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q2.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (0, 10), P2 (10, 8), P3 (8, 30), P4 (16, 2), P5 (22, 8), P6 (6, 22). 0 P1 2 P1 6 P6 8 P3 10 P2 12 P6 16 P4 18 P3 22 P5 24 P2 28 P5 32 P1 36 P6 52 P3 76 P2 78 P5 80 → WT 26, 60, 38, 0, 50, 24 → 33. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q2.3 EDF vs RM (8 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q2.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (1, 3, 3), P2 (2, 4, 4), 0–14, soft real-time. EDF 0 P1 1 P2 3 P1 4 P2 6 P1 7 idle 8 P2 10 P1 11 idle 12 P1 13 P2 14; RM 0 P1 1 P2 3 P1 4 P2 6 P1 7 idle 8 P2 9 P1 10 P2 11 idle 12 P1 13 P2 14. EDF is better: dynamic deadline-based priorities can meet more deadlines (up to 100% utilization) than RM's static priorities. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q3.1 compare_and_reset spinlock (4 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": " struct spinlock { int value = 0; }; · acquire: while (compare_and_reset(&lock->value, 0, 1) == 0) ; · release: lock->value = 0; ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Sp21-Q3.2 Fix the bounded-waiting algorithm (7 pts)", "u": "bank.html#Sp21-Q3.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Problems: (1) only processes with index > i are checked → lower-index processes can wait unboundedly; (2) the lock is never released when nobody is waiting. Fix: cyclic scan j = (i + 1) % n; while ((j != i) && !waiting[j]) j = (j + 1) % n; if (j == i) lock = 0; else waiting[j] = false; ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q2.1 AMAT + locality (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q2.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Hit ratio 90%, hit time 2 ns, miss time 50 ns. 0.9×2+0.1×50= 6.8 ns. Temporal: recently accessed items are likely accessed again (e.g., loop counter, loop body instructions). Spatial: items near recent accesses are likely accessed soon (e.g., sequential array elements a[i], a[i+1]). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q2.2 Multiprogramming vs time sharing (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q2.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Multiprogramming keeps several jobs in memory; when one waits for I/O, the CPU switches to another, so the CPU always has work → higher utilization. Time sharing switches among jobs so frequently (timer + CPU scheduling) that users can interact with each program while it runs → short response time, interactive computing. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q2.3 State sequence (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q2.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": " New → Ready → Running → Waiting → Ready → Running → Ready → Running → Terminated. When leaving Running, the PCB saves the process state, program counter, CPU registers, plus updated scheduling, accounting and I/O-status information. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q2.4 read(): user → kernel → user (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q2.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": " (1) read() in libc puts the system-call number and parameters in registers and executes a trap instruction. (2) The trap switches to kernel mode; the kernel indexes the system-call table and runs the read service. (3) The kernel stores the return value and executes return-from-trap, which switches back to user mode and resumes after the call. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q2.5 Parent → child through a pipe (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q2.5", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Call pipe(fd) before fork(); after fork, the parent (writer) closes fd[0] and writes to fd[1]; the child (reader) closes fd[1] and reads from fd[0]; close the used ends when done. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q3.1 Values of x (15 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": " C1: 3, C2: 5, P: 2 (each process has its own copy of x). Outputs: \"C1: 3, C2: 5, P: 2\" or \"C2: 5, C1: 3, P: 2\". Moving the first wait before x *= 2 guarantees C1, C2, P order; values unchanged. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q3.2 exec worker (15 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q3.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " 3 processes (parent → child → grandchild; the grandchild execs worker). X is not printed (successful exec never returns). Outputs: A P W C D · A W P C D · A W C P D. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW1-Q3.3 Pipe parent → child (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW1-Q3.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": " (a) Parent closes fd[0], writes to fd[1]; child closes fd[1], reads fd[0]; parent → child. (b) \"Child: 12\" then \"Parent: done\" — read blocks until data arrives, and the parent waits for the child. (c) No — separate pipe() calls after fork create two unrelated pipes, with no shared descriptors. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q2.1 Different PC/registers (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q2.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Yes — each thread has its own PC, register set and stack; saved in its TCB when not running. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q2.2 Many-to-one on 4 cores (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q2.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": " No — all user threads map to one kernel thread, which runs on one core at a time; only concurrency, no parallelism (and one blocking call blocks all). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q2.3 Ctrl+C (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q2.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": " Asynchronous (generated by an event external to the process). Yes — install a user-defined handler with signal(SIGINT, handler) to override the default (terminate). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q2.4 Hard affinity (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q2.4", "p": "Past papers", "x": " No — hard affinity restricts T to CPU 0 and CPU 1; load balancing may move it between those two only. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q2.5 RM vs EDF at t = 50 (6 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q2.5", "p": "Past papers", "x": " No. RM picks A (shorter period, static) → B finishes at 85 and misses 80. EDF picks B (deadline 80 < 100) → B finishes at 60, A at 85; both meet deadlines. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q3.1 Amdahl (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": " (a) 0.2+0.8/41​= 2.5. (b) N→∞: 0.21​= 5. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q3.2 SRTF and RR q=3 (20 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q3.2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "P1 (0, 7), P2 (1, 3), P3 (4, 5), P4 (10, 2). SRTF 0 P1 1 P2 4 P3 9 P1 10 P4 12 P1 17 → avg WT 2.5, avg TAT 6.75. RR(3) 0 P1 3 P2 6 P1 9 P3 12 P1 13 P4 15 P3 17 → avg WT 4.75, avg TAT 9. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "HW2-Q3.3 MLFQ (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#HW2-Q3.3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Q0 RR4, Q1 RR8, Q2 FCFS; P1 (0, 20), P2 (1, 5), P3 (11, 2); preempted Q1 job keeps the unused part of its quantum. 0 P1(Q0) 4 P2(Q0) 8 P1(Q1) 11 P3(Q0) 13 P1(Q1) 18 P2(Q1) 19 P1(Q2) 27. At t = 11, P1 goes to the head of Q1 (ahead of P2) with 5 ms of its Q1 quantum left. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F22-Q3 fork() == fork() in a loop (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F22-Q3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "int main() { int i, n; scanf(\"%d\", &n); for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (fork() == fork()) { printf(\"fork() == fork()!\\n\"); fflush(stdout); } } return 0; } (a) Number of processes and of printed lines for n = 1, 2, 3. (b) In terms of n. (a) n = 1: 4 processes, 1 line; n = 2: 16, 5; n = 3: 64, 21. (b) Each iteration every process does two forks → ×4, so 4n processes. In each iteration only the grandchild that received 0 from both forks sees equal return values, so the iteration that starts with 4k processes prints 4k lines. Total lines =40+41+⋯+4n−1=34n−1​. ✅ (verified) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "F24-P3.1 fork in nested loops (10 pts)", "u": "bank.html#F24-P3.1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "for (i = 0; i < M; i++) { pid_t pid = fork(); // line X if (!pid) { for (j = 0; j < N; j++) // line Y fork(); } } (a) Which process executes line Y? (b) M = N = 1: how many processes? (c) In terms of M and N. (a) The child — !pid is true only where fork() returned 0. (b) 3 — the parent forks a child (2), the child forks once in the inner loop (3). (c) Let f(k) be the number of processes one process becomes with k outer iterations left: the parent continues (f(k−1)) and the child becomes 2N processes that all continue (2Nf(k−1)), so f(k)=(2N+1)f(k−1) and the total is (2N+1)M. ✅ (verified) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S25-MC12 Nested short-circuit forks", "u": "bank.html#S25-MC12", "p": "Past papers", "x": "if (fork() || fork()) { if (fork() && !fork()) { fork(); } } How many processes are there in total (suppose all fork() succeed)? 9. fork() || fork(): 2 processes enter the if-clause, 1 the else-clause. Inside, fork() && !fork(): 1 process enters the inner if and forks once (→ 2), 2 processes skip it → each outer-if process becomes 4. Total 2×4+1=9 (8 newly created). ✅ (verified) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "S23-PRR Preemptive priority + RR with different arrivals (q = 2)", "u": "bank.html#S23-PRR", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Smaller number = higher priority; processes with equal priority share the CPU round-robin with q = 2; a higher-priority arrival preempts. Process table reconstructed from the official answer: P1 (0, 5, 2), P2 (1, 3, 1), P3 (2, 8, 2), P4 (8, 2, 1), P5 (9, 6, 2). 0 P1 1 P2 4 P1 5 P3 7 P1 8 P4 10 P1 11 P3 13 P5 15 P1 16 P3 18 P5 20 P3 22 P5 24 (official Gantt). A preempted process keeps the remaining part of its quantum — P1 runs only 1 unit at 4–5 and at 10–11. Resetting the quantum is the common mistake. Avg WT =511+0+12+0+9​=6.4. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab2-Q1 Counting printed values", "u": "bank.html#Lab2-Q1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "pid_t pid = fork(); int cnt = 0; if (pid == 0) { cnt += 10; pid = fork(); if (pid != 0) { cnt += 10; pid = fork(); } } printf(\"%d \\n\", pid); printf(\"%d \\n\", cnt); How many non-zero PIDs, how many '0's and how many '10's are printed? 4 processes print (pid, cnt): P0 (≠0, 0), C1 (≠0, 20), C2 (0, 10), C3 (0, 20) → 2 non-zero PIDs, 3 zeros, 1 ten. ✅ (verified) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab2-Q2 Force the order process 3 → 2 → 1", "u": "bank.html#Lab2-Q2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "int x = 0; if (BLANK1) { x = x + 1; BLANK2; printf(\"The value x in process 1 is %d\\n\", x); } else if (BLANK3) { x = x + 1; BLANK4; printf(\"The value x in process 2 is %d\\n\", x); } else { x = x + 1; printf(\"The value x in process 3 is %d\\n\", x); } Fill the blanks so the output is always process 3, then 2, then 1 (each printing x = 1). BLANK1 fork() · BLANK2 wait(0) · BLANK3 fork() · BLANK4 wait(0) (fork() > 0 / wait(NULL) also fine). Each process waits for the child it created, so 3 prints before 2, and 2 before 1; every process has its own copy of x, so each prints 1. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab4-EX1 Two ordinary pipes for two-way communication", "u": "bank.html#Lab4-EX1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Parent sends \"Parent\" to the child and the child sends \"Child\" to the parent using p2c[2] and c2p[2]. Fill BLANK1–BLANK6 (create pipes; child writes then reads; parent reads then writes). BLANK1 pipe(p2c) · BLANK2 pipe(c2p) · BLANK3 write(c2p[1], msg, 6) · BLANK4 read(p2c[0], buffer, 7) · BLANK5 read(c2p[0], buffer, 6) · BLANK6 write(p2c[1], msg, 7) (5 / 6 bytes also accepted). Output: \"Parent received the message: Child\" and \"Child received the message: Parent\". ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q1 Semaphores for strict thread order", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q1", "p": "Past papers", "x": "T1: wait(S1); CS; signal(S2); T2: wait(S2); CS; signal(S3); T3: wait(S3); CS; signal(S1); — initial values for the order T1, T2, T3, T1, …? S1 = 1, S2 = 0, S3 = 0 (only T1 can start; each thread passes the turn to the next). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q2 P0 → P1 → P2 ordering blanks", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q2", "p": "Past papers", "x": "S0 = BLANK1, S1 = BLANK2, S2 = BLANK3; P0: wait(S0); CS; BLANK4; P1: BLANK5; CS; BLANK6; P2: BLANK7; CS; signal(S0); S0 = 1, S1 = 0, S2 = 0; BLANK4 signal(S1); BLANK5 wait(S1); BLANK6 signal(S2); BLANK7 wait(S2). ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q3 Mutex blanks", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q3", "p": "Past papers", "x": "void *add(void *arg) { pthread_mutex_t* m = BLANK1; for (5000 times) { BLANK2; ++counter; BLANK3; } } — two threads run add with &mutex. Missing code? Purpose? Final counter? BLANK1 (pthread_mutex_t*) arg · BLANK2 pthread_mutex_lock(m) · BLANK3 pthread_mutex_unlock(m). Purpose: protect the shared variable counter (critical section). Final value 10,000. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q4 Possible outputs with semaphores X, Y = 0", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q4", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Process A: wait(X); print \"b\"; signal(Y); wait(Y); print \"c\"; signal(Y); Process B: signal(X); print \"a\"; wait(Y); print \"d\"; If both complete: abcd, bacd, bcad. If B takes Y first, A blocks forever at its second wait(Y) → bad, abd (A never finishes). ✅ (verified by exhaustive search) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q5 Counting semaphore permits", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q5", "p": "Past papers", "x": "S0 = 1, S1 = S2 = S3 = 0. P0: while(true){ wait(S0); print '1'; release(S1); release(S2); release(S3); } P1: wait(S1); release(S0); P2: wait(S2); release(S0); wait(S3); release(S0); — how many '1's? 4 — S0 permits: initial 1 + P1 gives 1 + P2 gives 2. After the first print P0 releases S1, S2, S3, so P1 and P2 can produce all of them; then P0 blocks forever. ✅ (verified) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q6 Readers–writers with two mutexes", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q6", "p": "Past papers", "x": "Fill the reader's blanks (mutex protects read_count; rw_mutex protects the data). Entry: pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); read_count++; if (read_count == 1) pthread_mutex_lock(&rw_mutex); pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); Exit: pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); read_count--; if (read_count == 0) pthread_mutex_unlock(&rw_mutex); pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); The first reader locks writers out and the last reader lets them in. ", "w": 1}, {"t": "Lab6-Q7 Race condition values", "u": "bank.html#Lab6-Q7", "p": "Past papers", "x": "a = 1, b = 3, c = 0. Thread 1: a = b + 1; c = a - b; Thread 2: a = 13; b = 10; c = 2; All possible final values of c? −6, 1, 2, 3, 10 — 2 if T2's c = 2 is last; 1 (T1 reads a=4, b=3 then T2 runs; or T2 first then a = 11, c = 11 − 10); −6 (T1 reads a=4, then T2 sets b=10); 3 (T2 runs after T1's a = 4, T1 reads a=13, b=10); 10 (T1 reads a=13 and b=3 between T2's writes). ✅ (verified by exhaustive search) ", "w": 1}, {"t": "X1-01 OS definition, intermediary, four components", "u": "answers.html#X1-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is an operating system? Explain its role among the four components of a computer system. [1.20–1.21] An OS is system software that acts as an intermediary between users / application programs and the computer hardware; it controls and coordinates the use of computing resources among various applications and users. Four components: hardware (CPU, memory, I/O devices) → operating system → application programs (define how resources are used to solve problems, e.g., editors, browsers, games) → users (people, machines, other computers). Abstraction: the OS hides the complicated details of the ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-02 OS goals in different computing environments", "u": "answers.html#X1-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How do OS design goals differ for shared computers, workstations, mobile devices, embedded systems and real-time systems? [1.22] The OS design first and foremost depends on the target device: Environment Main OS goal / feature Shared computers (mainframe, minicomputer) keep all users satisfied: balance performance vs. fairness Individual systems (workstations) dedicated resources: performance rather than fairness; may use shared resources from servers Mobile devices (smartphones, handhelds) resource constrained; sensors; touch screen and voice control (e.g., Siri); optimized for usability and ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-03 OS goals, resource allocator, control program", "u": "answers.html#X1-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What are the main goals of an OS? Explain the views of the OS as a resource allocator and as a control program. [1.23] Two main goals: user convenience (an environment to execute programs in a convenient, safe, protected and efficient manner) and resource allocation (allocate resources fairly and efficiently). Resource allocator: the OS manages all resources, hardware (CPU, memory, I/O) and software (e.g., signals, locks), and decides between conflicting requests for efficient and fair resource use. Control program: the OS controls the execution of programs to prevent errors and improper use o", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-04 Scenario: which OS role", "u": "answers.html#X1-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which OS role does each case show? (a) Two programs request the only printer at the same time. (b) A program writing into another program's memory is stopped. (c) A user opens a photo by file name, not by disk block numbers. [1.21, 1.23] (a) Resource allocator: the OS decides between conflicting requests (one job gets the printer, the other waits), so the device is shared fairly and efficiently. (b) Control program: the OS controls program execution to prevent errors and improper use; each program may access only its own memory. (c) Abstraction / user convenience: the OS hides hardware details", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-05 Kernel, middleware, system and application programs", "u": "answers.html#X1-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish the kernel, middleware, system programs and application programs. Which of them are part of the OS? [1.23–1.24] There is no universally accepted definition of an OS (\"everything a vendor ships\" is only an approximation); commonly, OS = always-running kernel + middleware frameworks + system programs. Term Meaning Part of OS? Example Kernel \"the one program running at all times on the computer\"; the essential functions yes process and memory management Middleware software frameworks that provide additional services to application developers yes (popular in iOS, Android) databases, mu", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-06 Computer-system organization: bus, controllers, drivers", "u": "answers.html#X1-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the organization of a modern computer system. Why do the CPUs and devices compete for memory? [1.25, 1.27] A computer has one or more CPU cores and device controllers connected through a common bus that provides access to shared memory. CPUs and devices execute concurrently, so they compete for memory cycles through the shared bus (a memory controller keeps the access orderly). Each device controller is in charge of a particular device; the OS controls it through a device driver (the kernel module that controls a device). Basic I/O hardware elements: buses, device controllers and the ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-07 von Neumann architecture, stored-program concept", "u": "answers.html#X1-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the main components of the von Neumann architecture. What does \"stored-program\" mean? [1.26] Processing unit (CPU): an arithmetic logic unit (ALU) and processor registers: PC (program counter), AC (accumulator), MAR (memory address register), MDR (memory data register). Control unit: an instruction register (IR) and the program counter (PC). Memory that stores both data and instructions (with caches), external mass storage (secondary storage), and input and output mechanisms. Stored-program: instructions and data are stored in the same memory and accessed by address, so programs and d", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-08 Instruction-execution cycle steps", "u": "answers.html#X1-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the steps of the instruction-execution cycle on a von Neumann machine. [1.26] Fetch instruction: the instruction at the address in the PC is fetched from memory (or cache) into the instruction register (IR). Decode instruction. Fetch data: the operands are fetched from memory (or cache) into data registers. Execute instruction: the ALU operates on the operands. Write back (if any): the result may be stored back in memory or cache. The cycle then repeats for the next instruction. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-09 Why programs must be in memory; MMU", "u": "answers.html#X1-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why must a program be loaded into main memory before it can run? What does the memory management unit (MMU) \"see\"? [1.26, 1.30] Because main memory is the only large storage the CPU can access directly; the CPU accesses secondary storage only indirectly, so code and data on disk must first be brought into memory. In the von Neumann (stored-program) model, the CPU fetches every instruction and operand from memory (or cache / registers). The MMU sees only a stream of memory addresses; it does not know how they are generated, or whether they are instructions or data. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-10 I/O subsystem: buffering, caching, spooling, drivers", "u": "answers.html#X1-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What services does the kernel I/O subsystem provide? Distinguish buffering, caching and spooling, and state what a device driver does. (Ch1 summary, Lab 2) The kernel I/O subsystem provides I/O scheduling, buffering, caching, spooling, device reservation and error handling. Buffering: stores data temporarily while it is being transferred (e.g., between a device and an application). Caching: keeps a copy of data on faster storage. Difference: a buffer may hold the only existing copy of a data item, whereas a cache only holds a copy of an item that resides elsewhere. Spooling: holds output for a", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-11 Storage units: bits, bytes, words, KB–PB", "u": "answers.html#X1-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "(Calculation) A computer has 64-bit registers and addressing. (a) Bytes per word? (b) Words in 1 KB? (c) Bytes in 4 GB? (d) Time to send a 1 GB file over a 1 Gb/s link? [1.28] Use 1 byte = 8 bits; KB = 210 = 1,024 bytes, MB = 10242, GB = 10243, TB = 10244, PB = 10245 bytes; a word is the architecture's native unit of data; networks are measured in bits per second (Mb/s, Gb/s). (a) 64 bits / 8 = 8 bytes per word. (b) 1,024 / 8 = 128 words. (c) 4×230=232 = 4,294,967,296 bytes (manufacturers round this to about 4 billion). (d) 230×8 = 8,589,934,592 bits; at 109 bits/s this takes about 8.6 s (abou", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-12 Storage hierarchy: speed, cost, capacity, volatility", "u": "answers.html#X1-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the storage hierarchy. How do speed, cost, capacity and volatility change as we move down it? [1.29] Storage is organized in a hierarchy by speed, cost per byte, capacity and volatility: registers → cache → main memory → nonvolatile memory (e.g., SSD) → hard-disk drives → optical disk → magnetic tapes. Higher levels (closer to the CPU) are faster, smaller and more expensive per byte. Moving down, cost per byte decreases, access time increases (slower) and capacity increases. Registers, cache and main memory are volatile (primary storage); NVM and HDDs are nonvolatile (secondary storag", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-13 Why secondary storage is needed", "u": "answers.html#X1-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why can't all programs and data be kept permanently in main memory? What is secondary storage for? [1.30–1.31] Because main memory is volatile (it loses its contents when power is turned off) and is too small and too costly per byte to hold all programs and data. Secondary storage is an extension of main memory that provides large nonvolatile storage capacity, holding large quantities of data permanently. The most common devices are hard-disk drives (HDDs) and nonvolatile memory (NVM) devices such as SSDs; programs are loaded from them into memory when they run. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-14 Main memory properties, bootstrap in EEPROM", "u": "answers.html#X1-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe main memory. Where is the bootstrap program stored, and why not in main memory? [1.30] Main memory is the only large storage the CPU can access directly; it is volatile, typically DRAM, accessed by load and store instructions to specific addresses, and byte addressable (each address refers to one byte). The bootstrap program, the first program run at power-on, is stored in EEPROM (electrically erasable programmable read-only memory, i.e., firmware). Reason: DRAM is volatile and empty at power-on, so start-up code must be in nonvolatile memory; the bootstrap program initializes the sys", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-15 Mechanical vs electrical (NVM) storage", "u": "answers.html#X1-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare mechanical and electrical secondary storage. [1.31] Mechanical storage is larger and cheaper per byte; electrical storage (NVM) is more costly, smaller, more reliable and faster. Mechanical Electrical (NVM) Examples HDDs, optical disks, holographic storage, magnetic tape flash memory, SSD, FRAM, NRAM Capacity larger smaller Cost per byte less expensive more costly Speed slower faster Reliability lower (moving parts) more reliable Both are nonvolatile and hold programs and data permanently. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-16 Cache hit, miss, cache management", "u": "answers.html#X1-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain what happens on a cache hit and on a cache miss. What are the main cache-management issues? [1.32] Caching temporarily copies a subset of information from a slower, larger storage into a faster, smaller storage, making the frequently used case faster. Every access first checks whether the information is in the cache: Hit: it is in the cache, so it is used directly from the cache (fast). Miss: it is not, so the data is copied from the slower storage into the cache and used there. Cache management: the cache is much smaller than the storage being cached, so the key issues are the cache s", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-17 Where caching is used in systems", "u": "answers.html#X1-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Caching is \"performed at many levels\". Give examples of caching in a computer system or OS. [1.32] Caching is used whenever a subset of content can be kept on a faster device: Hardware caches keep recently used parts of main memory close to the CPU. Recent address translations are cached for fast lookup. File blocks, file names and file directories: e.g., part of the file directory (a kernel data structure stored on disk) is cached in main memory, so main memory acts as a cache for the disk. Frequently used network routes are cached. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-18 Average access time; miss time vs penalty", "u": "answers.html#X1-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "(Calculation) Hit ratio 98%, hit time 1 ns, miss time 60 ns: find the average access time. What if 60 ns is the miss penalty instead? [1.32] Average access time = (hit ratio × hit time) + (miss ratio × miss time), where miss ratio = 1 − hit ratio: Tavg​=h×Thit​+(1−h)×Tmiss​ Miss time 60 ns: Tavg​=0.98×1+0.02×60=0.98+1.2= 2.18 ns. If 60 ns is the miss penalty (extra time on top of the hit time), then Tmiss​=1+60=61 ns and Tavg​=0.98×1+0.02×61=0.98+1.22= 2.20 ns (same as Thit​+(1−h)×penalty=1+0.02×60). State which interpretation you use. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-19 Higher hit ratio is not always better", "u": "answers.html#X1-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "(Calculation) Cache A: hit ratio 95%, hit time 4 ns, miss time 100 ns. Cache B: hit ratio 90%, hit time 1 ns, miss time 60 ns. Which is better, and what does this show? [1.32] Cache B is better, even though its hit ratio is lower. Formula: Tavg​=h×Thit​+(1−h)×Tmiss​. A: 0.95×4+0.05×100=3.8+5= 8.8 ns. B: 0.90×1+0.10×60=0.9+6= 6.9 ns. This shows that hit ratio alone does not determine cache performance: the average access time also depends on the hit time and the miss time (miss penalty). ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-20 Required hit ratio and caching speedup", "u": "answers.html#X1-20", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "(Calculation) Hit time 2 ns, miss time 82 ns, and an access without the cache takes 80 ns. What hit ratio gives an average access time of at most 6 ns? What is the speedup over no cache? [1.32] A hit ratio of at least 95% is needed, giving a speedup of about 13.3 times. h×2+(1−h)×82≤6⇒82−80h≤6⇒80h≥76⇒h≥0.95. General form: h=(Tmiss​−Ttarget​)/(Tmiss​−Thit​) = (82 − 6) / (82 − 2) = 76 / 80 = 0.95. Check: 0.95×2+0.05×82=1.9+4.1=6 ns; speedup = 80 / 6 ≈ 13.3 compared with accessing memory directly. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-21 Locality: temporal, spatial, when caching fails", "u": "answers.html#X1-21", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why does caching work? Identify the temporal and spatial locality in a loop that sums an array. When would a cache not help? [1.33] Caching works because programs show locality of reference: for (i = 0; i < n; i++) sum += a[i]; Temporal locality (locality in time): recently accessed items are likely to be accessed again soon; here sum, i and the loop instructions are reused in every iteration. Spatial locality (locality in space): items near recently accessed items are likely to be accessed shortly; a[0], a[1], a[2], ... are contiguous and accessed in order (instructions are also sequential), ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-22 Who manages each storage level", "u": "answers.html#X1-22", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which component manages each level of the storage hierarchy? Is movement between levels explicit or implicit? [1.34] Registers are managed by the compiler, the cache by hardware, and main memory, SSD and disk by the OS. Level Access time (ns) Managed by Backed by Registers 0.25–0.5 compiler cache Cache 0.5–25 hardware main memory Main memory 80–250 operating system disk Solid-state disk 25,000–50,000 operating system disk Magnetic disk 5,000,000 operating system disk or tape Movement between levels can be explicit or implicit: cache ↔ memory transfers are implicit (done by hardware), while mem", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-23 Timescales: why switch jobs during disk I/O", "u": "answers.html#X1-23", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Using typical latencies, explain why the OS should run another job while one job waits for a disk access. [1.35, 1.42] A disk access is about 100,000 times slower than a memory access, so a waiting CPU would waste millions of instruction times. Main memory reference ≈ 100 ns; disk seek ≈ 10 ms = 107 ns, so 107/102=105 times slower (an L1 cache reference is only ≈ 0.5 ns). Therefore, under multiprogramming, when the running job waits for I/O the OS switches the CPU to another job, so the CPU always has a job to execute. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-24 Single-processor system, special-purpose processors", "u": "answers.html#X1-24", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A PC has one single-core CPU plus a disk controller and a GPU that contain their own processors. Is it a multiprocessor system? Explain. [1.36] No, it is still a single-processor system. It has one general-purpose CPU with a single processing core, which executes a general-purpose instruction set, including user processes. The disk controller and GPU are special-purpose (device-specific) processors: they run a specific and limited instruction set and usually do not execute instructions from user processes. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-25 Multiprocessor advantages; speed-up below N", "u": "answers.html#X1-25", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give three advantages of multiprocessor systems. Why is the speed-up with N processors less than N? What is the main disadvantage? [1.37] Increased throughput: more computing capability, so more work is done in less time. Economy of scale: processors share I/O devices, power supplies, housings and peripherals, so it costs less than several single-processor systems. Increased reliability: graceful degradation (service continues in proportion to the surviving hardware) or fault tolerance (operation continues despite a component failure). Speed-up is less than N because of overhead, e.g., content", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-26 SMP vs AMP comparison", "u": "answers.html#X1-26", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) and asymmetric multiprocessing (AMP). [1.37–1.38] In AMP a master processor assigns tasks to slaves and handles I/O; in SMP all processors are equal and each performs all tasks. Asymmetric (AMP) Symmetric (SMP) Relationship master–slave: the master assigns specific tasks to the slaves all processors treated equally OS functions and user processes run as the master assigns each processor performs all tasks, including OS functions and user processes I/O handled by the master only handled by any processor Weakness the master can become a bottleneck adding C", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-27 Cache coherency with private caches", "u": "answers.html#X1-27", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In an SMP system each CPU has its own private cache. What problem does this create, and who usually solves it? [1.38] Cache coherency: the same data item can be copied in several CPUs' private caches, so after one CPU updates its copy, the other caches may hold stale values. The system must ensure that an update in one cache is immediately reflected in all other caches holding that item, so every CPU reads the most recent value. This is usually handled by hardware, below the OS level. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-28 Multicore benefits for laptops and mobile devices", "u": "answers.html#X1-28", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is one multicore chip preferred to several single-core chips, especially in laptops and mobile devices? [1.39] Faster communication: cores on one chip communicate on-chip, which is faster than between-chip communication. Lower power: one chip with multiple cores uses significantly less power than multiple single-core chips, which matters for battery-powered devices such as mobile devices and laptops. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-29 NUMA: motivation, structure, drawback", "u": "answers.html#X1-29", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why was NUMA introduced? Describe its structure, its drawback and its implication for the OS. [1.40] Motivation: adding more CPUs to an SMP system may not scale, because contention for the system bus becomes a bottleneck. Structure: each CPU (or group of CPUs) has its own local memory accessed via a small, fast local bus; the CPUs are connected by a shared system interconnect, and all CPUs share one physical memory address space. Local memory is fast, remote memory is slower (non-uniform access time). Drawback: increased latency when a CPU must access remote memory across the system interconne", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-30 CPU, processor, core, multicore, multiprocessor", "u": "answers.html#X1-30", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define CPU, processor, core, multicore and multiprocessor. [1.41] A processor (chip) contains one or more CPUs, and the core is the unit that actually executes instructions: Term Definition CPU the hardware that executes instructions Processor a physical chip that contains one or more CPUs Core the basic computation unit of the CPU; it executes instructions and has registers for storing data locally Multicore multiple computing cores on a single physical processor chip Multiprocessor a system including multiple processors ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-31 Multiprogramming: why, how, OS support", "u": "answers.html#X1-31", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is multiprogramming needed? What happens when the running job waits for I/O, and what OS support does it need? [1.42] A single program cannot keep the CPU or I/O devices busy at all times, so multiprogramming keeps several jobs in memory at the same time and organizes them so the CPU always has one to execute, raising CPU utilization (efficiency). When the running job waits for I/O, the OS switches the CPU to another job in memory instead of leaving the CPU idle. OS support: job scheduling (choose queued jobs to load into memory), CPU scheduling (several jobs ready at the same time), memor", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-32 Multiprogramming vs time sharing compared", "u": "answers.html#X1-32", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare multiprogramming (batch) and time sharing (multitasking). [1.42] Time sharing is a logical extension of multiprogramming: both keep several jobs in memory, but time sharing switches the CPU so often that users can interact with their programs. Multiprogramming (batch) Time sharing (multitasking) Goal efficiency: keep the CPU busy (CPU utilization) fast response time for interactive users When the CPU switches when the running job must wait (e.g., for I/O) frequently, even if the job is not waiting (short time slices, enforced by a timer) User interaction none while jobs run (jobs are s", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-33 Virtualization: host, VMM, guest OS", "u": "answers.html#X1-33", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is virtualization? Describe the roles of the host, the VMM (hypervisor) and the guest OS. [1.43–1.44] Virtualization abstracts the hardware of a single computer into multiple different execution environments (virtual machines, VMs), creating the illusion that each user or program runs on its own \"private computer\". It allows an OS to run as an application within another OS, so one physical machine can run multiple operating systems concurrently. Host: the underlying hardware system. Virtual machine manager (VMM) or hypervisor: creates and manages virtual machines by providing an interface", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-34 Virtualization motivations: consolidation, testing scenario", "u": "answers.html#X1-34", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A company runs many lightly loaded servers, each needing a different OS or OS version, and its developers must test software on several OSes. How does virtualization help? [1.43] Server consolidation: virtualization consolidates multiple OSes onto fewer hardware platforms; each service runs in its own VM with the OS / version it needs, on far fewer physical machines. Testing and debugging: developers can run many OS types and versions on just one machine, which makes testing and debugging easier. Desktop use: a user can run one OS (e.g., Linux) and still use native applications of another plat", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-35 Virtualization vs emulation; VM performance", "u": "answers.html#X1-35", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish virtualization from emulation. Does a program run faster inside a VM? [1.46] Virtualization: the guest OS is natively compiled for the same CPU as the host; most guest instructions run directly on the hardware under VMM control, so programs show only minor performance decreases (they pass through more layers of software). Emulation: simulates a different CPU architecture in software (source CPU differs from target CPU); every instruction must be translated, so it is much slower. So a program in a VM is not faster than on the physical machine; it is usually slightly slower. (History", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-36 Cloud computing; public, private, hybrid clouds", "u": "answers.html#X1-36", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is cloud computing, and why is it a \"logical extension of virtualization\"? Distinguish public, private and hybrid clouds. [1.47–1.48] Cloud computing delivers computing, storage and applications as a service over a network, on demand. It is a logical extension of virtualization because it uses virtualization as the base for its functionality; e.g., Amazon EC2 has millions of servers running tens of millions of VMs, and users pay based on usage. Public cloud: available via the Internet to anyone willing to pay. Private cloud: run by a company for the company's own use. Hybrid cloud: includ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-37 SaaS, PaaS, IaaS classification", "u": "answers.html#X1-37", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain SaaS, PaaS and IaaS, and classify: (a) an online word processor; (b) a ready-to-use database server for your application; (c) rented storage for backups or virtual servers. [1.48] SaaS provides applications, PaaS a ready software stack, and IaaS servers or storage, all over the Internet. Model Provided over the Internet Example SaaS (Software as a Service) one or more applications (a) online word processor PaaS (Platform as a Service) a software stack ready for application use (b) a database server IaaS (Infrastructure as a Service) servers or storage (c) storage for backup, virtual se", "w": 2}, {"t": "X1-TF True / False quick verdicts", "u": "answers.html#X1-TF", "p": "Answer bank", "x": " Statement T/F Why (≤ 12 words) The kernel is the one program running at all times on the computer. T Middleware and system programs are part of the OS, not kernel. System programs ship with the OS and are part of the kernel. F They ship with the OS but are not part of the kernel. There is a universally accepted definition of an operating system. F \"Everything a vendor ships\" is only an approximation. Embedded systems usually have rich user interfaces. F Little or no UI; OS is pre-installed in ROM. The MMU knows whether an address refers to an instruction or data. F The MMU sees only a stream ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-01 OS services: helpful to user, efficient operation", "u": "answers.html#X2-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "List the OS services that are helpful to the user, and those that ensure the efficient operation of the system itself. [2.4–2.6] Helpful to the user: User interface: CLI, GUI, touch screen. Program execution: load a program into memory, run it, and end execution normally or abnormally (indicating error). I/O operations: a running program may require I/O on a file or an I/O device. File-system manipulation: read, write, create, delete and search files and directories; list file information; permission management. Communications: processes exchange information on the same computer or over a netw", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-02 Scenario: identify the OS service", "u": "answers.html#X2-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which OS service is shown? (a) CPU time used by each user is recorded for billing. (b) A disk read fails; the OS retries and reports it. (c) Two processes exchange data through shared memory. (d) A program is loaded, runs, and exits with an error code. (e) A user must log in with a password. [2.4–2.6] (a) Logging (accounting): tracks which users use how much and what kinds of resources. (b) Error detection: the OS is constantly aware of possible errors (here in an I/O device) and takes appropriate action. (c) Communications, using the shared-memory model (the other model is message passing). (", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-03 Protection vs security; access rights, domains", "u": "answers.html#X2-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish protection and security. What are an access right and a domain? [2.6] (Ch2 summary) Protection: controlling the access of users or processes to the resources (hardware and software) of the computer, ensuring that all access to system resources is controlled, so concurrent processes do not interfere with each other. Security: defending the system against internal and external attacks (e.g., denial-of-service, worms, viruses); security from outsiders requires user authentication and extends to defending external I/O devices from invalid access attempts. An access right is the permiss", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-04 Two categories: protection, functionality beyond hardware", "u": "answers.html#X2-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "According to the chapter summary, OS services fall into two general categories. What are they? Give an example of each. (Ch2 summary) Enforcing protection among processes running concurrently: e.g., a process may access only the memory in its own address space, and may not access hardware devices directly without OS assistance and intervention. Providing functionality not directly supported by the underlying hardware: e.g., memory management and file systems (the disk only stores blocks; the OS builds files and directories on top of them). ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-05 CLI vs GUI vs touch-screen interfaces", "u": "answers.html#X2-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the three approaches for users to interact with an OS: CLI, GUI and touch screen. [2.8–2.12] CLI = typed commands, GUI = desktop metaphor with mouse and icons, touch screen = gestures and voice. CLI (command interpreter) GUI Touch screen Interaction user enters commands directly; the shell fetches, interprets and executes them user-friendly desktop metaphor: mouse, keyboard, monitor; icons for files, programs, directories gestures, virtual keyboard, voice commands (e.g., Siri) Best for experienced users; fast and scriptable ease of learning and use devices where a mouse is not feasible", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-06 Shell: functions; kernel or system program", "u": "answers.html#X2-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is the primary function of a shell? Why is it better to implement the command interpreter as a system program than inside the kernel? [2.8, 2.37] A shell (1) fetches a command from the user, (2) interprets it and (3) executes it; in UNIX it runs a program by calling fork() to create a new process and exec() to load the program into it, then waits for it (or continues, for a background job). The command interpreter is sometimes implemented in the kernel, sometimes by system programs. As a system program: Several shells can coexist (C shell, Bourne-Again shell, Korn shell), and users can ch", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-07 cp command: sequence of system calls", "u": "answers.html#X2-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the sequence of system calls needed by cp in.txt out.txt to copy one file to another. [2.14] Even this simple command needs a sequence of system calls: Acquire the input and output file names (from the command line, or by writing a prompt to the screen and reading the keyboard). Open the input file; if it does not exist, print an error and abort. Create the output file; if it already exists, abort (or overwrite). Loop: read from the input file and write to the output file, until the read reaches end of file (or an error occurs). Close the files, write a completion message to the scree", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-08 API definition, common APIs, read() example", "u": "answers.html#X2-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is an API? Name the three most common APIs, and explain the parameters and return value of ssize_t read(int fd, void *buf, size_t count). [2.15–2.16] An Application Programming Interface (API) specifies a set of functions available to application programmers, including the parameters passed to each function and the return values it may expect. The API functions invoke the actual system calls on behalf of the programmer (e.g., Windows CreateProcess() invokes the NTCreateProcess() system call). Programmers use an API through a library provided by the OS: libc for C programs on UNIX/Linux. M", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-09 Why use APIs; API call vs system call", "u": "answers.html#X2-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain why programmers use an API rather than invoking system calls directly. Does every API call result in a system call? [2.17] Program portability: a program written to an API can compile and run on any system that supports the same API, although the implementation of system calls varies from machine to machine. Hiding complex details: actual system calls are more detailed and difficult to work with; the caller need know nothing about how the system call is implemented, only obey the API and understand what the OS will do as a result. E.g., printf() handles formatting and buffering, then c", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-10 System-call interface, RTE, system-call table", "u": "answers.html#X2-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does the system-call interface link an API call to the kernel? Explain the roles of the run-time environment, the system-call number and the system-call table. [2.18–2.19] The system-call interface, part of the run-time environment, maps each API call to a numbered system call and invokes it in the kernel. The run-time environment (RTE), the full suite of software needed to run programs in a language (compilers or interpreters, libraries, loaders), provides a system-call interface that serves as the link to the system calls of the OS. An identity number is associated with each system call,", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-11 printf() vs write(); when kernel mode starts", "u": "answers.html#X2-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain the relationship between printf() and write(). At what point does the program enter kernel mode? [2.26] printf() is a standard C library (API) function; write() is a system call. The program invokes the printf() library call, which calls the write() system call to get the service from the OS. printf() runs in user mode (formatting the string and buffering the output). Only when the library invokes write() does the program trap into kernel mode; the kernel writes the data to the file or device and then returns to user mode with the result. The standard C library (libc) thus provides the", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-12 Parameter passing: registers, block, stack", "u": "answers.html#X2-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the three methods to pass system-call parameters to the OS. A call needs 7 parameters but only 4 registers are available: which method would you use? [2.20–2.21] Use the block (or stack) method, because registers limit the number of parameters: Method How Limit Registers parameters are placed directly in CPU registers (simplest) limited: there may be more parameters than registers Block (table) parameters are stored in a block, or table, in memory, and the address of the block is passed in a register no limit on number or length Stack parameters are pushed onto the stack by the progra", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-13 Six types of system calls, classification", "u": "answers.html#X2-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Name the six categories of system calls, and classify fork(), wait(), open(), ioctl(), getpid(), sleep(), pipe(), mmap(), chmod() and umask(). [2.22–2.25] Process control, file management, device management, information maintenance, communications and protection. Category Typical operations UNIX Windows Process control create/terminate, load/execute, wait for time or event, allocate/free memory, dump memory, debugger, locks fork(), exit(), wait() CreateProcess(), ExitProcess(), WaitForSingleObject() File management create/delete, open/close, read/write/reposition, get/set file attributes open(", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-14 System programs: categories and user view", "u": "answers.html#X2-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What are system programs? List their main categories with examples. Why is the user's view of the OS defined by them? [2.27–2.30] System programs (system services, system utilities) provide a convenient environment for program development and execution; some are simply user interfaces to system calls, others are considerably more complex. File management: create, delete, copy, rename, print, list files and directories. Status information: date, time, available memory or disk space, number of users; performance, logging and debugging information. File modification: text editors; commands to sea", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-15 System calls vs system programs vs applications", "u": "answers.html#X2-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare system calls, system programs and application programs. [2.27–2.30] System calls are the low-level programming interface; system programs are higher-level utilities built on them; application programs are not part of the OS. System calls System programs Application programs Level low-level programming interface to OS services higher-level utilities shipped with the OS user programs that solve problems Used by running programs, usually through an API users, without writing programs (e.g., shell commands to create or delete a file) end users Requests basic functions: process control, fil", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-16 Compiler, linker, loader roles; relocation", "u": "answers.html#X2-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What does each of the compiler, linker and loader do? What is a relocatable object file, and what is relocation? [2.31–2.32] The compiler produces relocatable object files, the linker combines them into one executable on disk, and the loader brings that executable into memory to run. gcc -c main.c # compiler -> main.o (relocatable object file) gcc -o main main.o -lm # linker -> main (executable; -lm links the math library) ./main # loader -> program loaded into memory (+ DLLs if needed) A relocatable object file is compiled code designed to be loaded into any physical memory location. Linker: ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-17 Static linking vs dynamically linked libraries", "u": "answers.html#X2-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare static linking with dynamically linked libraries (DLLs). Why do modern OSes use DLLs? [2.31] Modern general-purpose OSes do not link libraries statically; DLLs are loaded when needed and shared, which saves memory. Static linking Dynamic linking (DLLs) When linked at link time; library code is copied into every executable loaded when needed, at load or run time Executable size larger smaller Memory use every program has its own copy loaded only once and shared by all programs using the same version of the library Unused code libraries may be linked in even if never used avoids linking ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-18 Why applications are OS-specific; ABI", "u": "answers.html#X2-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why can an executable built for one OS usually not run on another OS? How can programs be made portable? [2.15, 2.31, 2.56] Because each OS has its own system calls, executable format and binary conventions. Different system calls / APIs: each OS provides its own set of system calls (different numbers, parameters, meanings and results), e.g., Win32 vs POSIX. Different executable formats: each OS uses its own standard binary format so it knows how to load and start the program (e.g., ELF on Linux, PE on Windows). Different CPUs have different instruction sets: e.g., iOS runs on ARM and does not", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-19 OS design goals: user vs system", "u": "answers.html#X2-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What are user goals and system goals in OS design? What affects the design at the highest level? [2.33] At the highest level, the design is affected by the choice of hardware and the type of system (desktop/laptop, mobile, distributed or real-time); it starts by defining goals and specifications. User goals: the OS should be convenient to use, easy to learn and use, reliable, safe and fast. System goals (for designers and maintainers): it should be easy to design, implement and maintain, and flexible, reliable, error-free and efficient. OS design is not \"solvable\"; it is a highly creative soft", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-20 Policy vs mechanism: definition, separation", "u": "answers.html#X2-20", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish policy and mechanism with an example. Why should they be separated? [2.34] Policy: what will be done. Mechanism: how to do it. Example: a timer is a mechanism for ensuring CPU protection, but deciding how long the timer is set for a particular user is a policy decision. Why separate: policies change over time; if policy and mechanism are properly separated, a policy change needs minimal change to the mechanism, which gives flexibility. The same mechanism can support \"I/O-intensive programs have priority over CPU-intensive ones\" or the opposite policy. In a nutshell, design goals de", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-21 Scenario: classify policy or mechanism", "u": "answers.html#X2-21", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Policy or mechanism? (a) code that saves and restores registers to switch processes; (b) \"interactive jobs get higher priority than batch jobs\"; (c) a hardware timer; (d) \"each user may run at most 10 ms at a time\"; (e) checking permission bits on every open(); (f) \"only the owner may write a file\". [2.34] (a) Mechanism: how the CPU is switched between processes. (b) Policy: decides which jobs get priority. (c) Mechanism: a tool for ensuring CPU protection. (d) Policy: how long the timer is set for a user. (e) Mechanism: how access control is enforced. (f) Policy: what access is allowed. Rule:", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-22 OS in a high-level language: pros, cons", "u": "answers.html#X2-22", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What are the advantages and disadvantages of implementing an OS in a high-level language (C/C++) rather than assembly? [2.35] The advantages outweigh the small costs, so most OSes are now written in C or C++ (the lowest levels of the kernel may still be in assembly; e.g., Android's system libraries are in C/C++ and its application frameworks mostly in Java). Advantages: the code can be written faster, is more compact, and is easier to understand and debug; advances in compiler technology improve the generated code for the entire OS by simple recompilation; and the OS is far easier to port to o", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-23 MS-DOS vs FreeBSD: running a program", "u": "answers.html#X2-23", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare how MS-DOS and FreeBSD run a user program. [2.36–2.37] MS-DOS is single-tasking and runs a program without creating a process; FreeBSD is multitasking and runs each program in a new process (fork() + exec()). MS-DOS FreeBSD (UNIX variant) Tasking single-tasking multitasking Running a program the shell (invoked at boot) loads the program into memory, overwriting all but the kernel; no process is created the shell calls fork() to create a new process, then exec() to load the program into it Memory single memory space each process has its own address space While it runs only that program ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-24 Simple structure: why MS-DOS was vulnerable", "u": "answers.html#X2-24", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why was MS-DOS vulnerable to buggy or malicious programs? [2.39] Because nothing protected the OS and hardware from application programs. MS-DOS has a simple structure: it was written to provide the most functionality in the least space, was not carefully divided into modules, and its interfaces and levels of functionality are not well separated. Application programs can access I/O directly, bypassing the OS. It was written for the Intel 8088, which had no dual mode (kernel vs. user mode) and no hardware protection, so one faulty program could crash the whole system. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-25 Monolithic kernel: pros, cons, Linux", "u": "answers.html#X2-25", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a monolithic kernel? Why do UNIX, Linux and Windows still use this structure despite its drawbacks? [2.40–2.42] A monolithic structure places all the functionality of the kernel into a single, static binary file that runs in a single address space; in traditional UNIX, the kernel is everything below the system-call interface and above the physical hardware (UNIX = kernel + system programs). Why still used: a distinct performance advantage, since there is very little overhead in the system-call interface, and communication within the kernel is fast. Drawback: an enormous amount of funct", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-26 Tightly vs loosely coupled; modular design", "u": "answers.html#X2-26", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish a tightly coupled system from a loosely coupled (modular) one. What is the advantage of modularity? [2.43] Tightly coupled (monolithic): essentially one big program running in one address space, where changes to one part of the system affect other parts. Loosely coupled (modular): the system is divided into separate, smaller components, each with specific and limited functionality, with specified interactions between them. Advantage: changes in one component affect only that component, giving more freedom and flexibility in creating and modifying each component and simplifying debu", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-27 Layered approach: information hiding, debugging", "u": "answers.html#X2-27", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the layered approach. What is information hiding, and why is a layered OS easy to construct and debug? [2.44–2.45] The OS is divided into layers, each built on top of lower layers: layer 0 (bottom) is the hardware; layer N (top) is the user interface. Each layer has data structures and a set of functions, uses the services of the layer below and offers services to the layer above. Information hiding: a layer needs to know only what the lower-layer operations do (their interface), not how they are implemented; each layer hides its own data structures, operations and hardware from highe", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-28 Why few OSes are purely layered", "u": "answers.html#X2-28", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why do few operating systems use a pure layered approach? What is the trend instead? [2.45, 2.53] Difficulty of defining the layers: there are significant challenges in appropriately defining the layers and their functionalities, since each layer may use only lower layers. Performance: the overhead of requiring a program to traverse multiple layers to obtain a service. Trend: fewer layers with more functionality and modularized code, avoiding the problems of complex layer definition and interaction. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-29 Microkernel: file access by message passing", "u": "answers.html#X2-29", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In a microkernel OS, describe how an application reads a file. Why does this hurt performance? [2.46–2.47] The application and the file server never interact directly; they exchange messages through the microkernel. The microkernel keeps only minimal process and memory management plus a communication facility (IPC); services such as the file server and device drivers run as user-level programs in separate address spaces. To read a file, the application sends a request message to the microkernel, which passes it to the file server; the file server does the work and replies by message through th", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-30 Microkernel: advantages, drawback, Windows NT", "u": "answers.html#X2-30", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is a microkernel OS easier to extend and port, and more secure and reliable? What is its main drawback? Give an example. [2.48] Easier to extend: new services are added in user space without modifying the kernel; when the kernel must change, the changes are fewer because the kernel is much smaller. Easier to port: the much smaller kernel can be more easily ported from one hardware platform to another. More secure and reliable: less code runs in kernel mode; most services run as user processes, so if a service fails, the rest of the OS remains untouched; a simpler kernel is also more reliab", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-31 LKM approach; USB device scenario", "u": "answers.html#X2-31", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is the loadable kernel module (LKM) approach? What happens when a USB device is plugged into a running Linux machine whose kernel lacks its driver? [2.49–2.51] The kernel has a set of core components and links in additional services via modules, either at boot time or during run time; this is the best current methodology in OS design. USB case: the needed driver module is loaded dynamically into the running kernel (and can be removed later), with no need to recompile the kernel or reboot. Why better: linking services dynamically is preferable to adding features directly to the kernel, whi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-32 LKM vs microkernel; LKM vs layered", "u": "answers.html#X2-32", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the LKM approach with the microkernel approach (one similarity, one difference). How does LKM also resemble a layered design? [2.50] Similarity with a microkernel: the primary module has only core functions and knowledge of how to load and communicate with other modules; other services are added separately. Difference: LKM is more efficient, because modules do not need to invoke message passing to communicate; they run inside the kernel and call each other directly, whereas microkernel services run in user space and talk through message passing. Versus layered: like a layered design, e", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-33 Hybrid systems: Linux, Windows, macOS", "u": "answers.html#X2-33", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why are most modern OSes hybrid systems? Explain how Linux, Windows and macOS combine structures. [2.52–2.53] Very few OSes adopt a single, strictly defined structure; they combine different structures to address performance, security and usability needs. Linux: monolithic (OS in a single address space, so efficient performance) and modular (new functionality is dynamically added via LKMs). Windows: largely monolithic, but keeps microkernel-like behaviour with separate subsystems (personalities) running as user-mode processes, and supports dynamically loadable kernel modules. macOS / iOS: laye", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-34 Compare monolithic, layered, microkernel, LKM", "u": "answers.html#X2-34", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the monolithic, layered, microkernel and loadable-kernel-module structures. [2.38–2.53] Monolithic is fastest but hardest to maintain; microkernel is the most extensible and reliable but slowest; LKM combines modularity with near-monolithic speed. Monolithic Layered Microkernel LKM (modular) Idea all kernel functions in one static binary, one address space layer 0 = hardware ... layer N = UI; each layer uses only lower layers minimal kernel (process and memory management, IPC); other services in user space core kernel + modules loaded or removed at boot or run time Communication direct", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-35 Scenario: choose an OS structure", "u": "answers.html#X2-35", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which structure would you choose, and why, for: (a) a server where speed matters most; (b) a safety-critical system where a failed service must not bring down the OS; (c) a desktop OS that must add drivers and file systems without rebooting; (d) an OS to be ported to many hardware platforms? [2.41–2.53] (a) Monolithic (possibly with LKMs): very little overhead in the system-call interface and fast communication within the kernel. (b) Microkernel: services run as user processes in separate address spaces, so if a service fails, the rest of the OS remains untouched; less code in kernel mode make", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-36 Mac OS X structure: Darwin, Mach, BSD", "u": "answers.html#X2-36", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the structure of Mac OS X. What is Darwin, and what do Mach and BSD each provide? [2.54–2.57] Mac OS X is hybrid and layered: Aqua GUI (for mouse or trackpad) → application environments and services (Cocoa programming environment with an Objective-C API; Core frameworks for graphics and media, e.g., QuickTime, OpenGL) → kernel environment, Darwin. Darwin = Mach microkernel + BSD UNIX kernel (the hybrid XNU kernel), with two system-call interfaces: Mach system calls (known as traps) and BSD system calls (POSIX functionality), plus rich libraries (standard C library, networking, securit", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-37 macOS vs iOS: similarities, differences", "u": "answers.html#X2-37", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare macOS and iOS. [2.54–2.56] Similar: both share the same architecture of user interface, programming (language) support, graphics and media services, and the kernel environment Darwin (Mach microkernel + BSD UNIX kernel); iOS is structured on Mac OS X with added functionality. macOS iOS User interface Aqua (mouse or trackpad) Springboard (touch devices) Programming API Cocoa (Objective-C API) Cocoa Touch (Objective-C API for touch-screen devices) Graphics and services Core frameworks (QuickTime, OpenGL) Media services (graphics, audio, video) and Core services (cloud computing, database", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-38 iOS vs Android: similarities, differences", "u": "answers.html#X2-38", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare iOS and Android. [2.58] Similarities: (1) both are based on existing kernels (Android on Linux, iOS on the Mac OS X kernel); (2) both use a layered software-stack architecture; (3) both provide rich frameworks for developers (graphics, audio, hardware features). iOS Android Source closed-source; runs only on Apple mobile devices open-source; runs on a variety of mobile platforms (Open Handset Alliance, led by Google) App language Objective-C Java, using a separate Android API Execution executes code natively apps run on a virtual machine, the Android RunTime (ART) ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-39 Android architecture: ART, JNI, HAL, Bionic", "u": "answers.html#X2-39", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain the roles of ART, JNI, HAL and Bionic in the Android architecture. [2.58–2.59] ART (Android RunTime): the virtual machine on which Java applications execute, optimized for mobile devices with limited memory and CPU processing capabilities. JNI (Java Native Interface): lets developers bypass the virtual machine and write Java programs that access specific hardware features; such programs are generally not portable from one hardware to another. HAL (hardware abstraction layer): abstracts all hardware (camera, GPS chip, other sensors) and gives applications a consistent view independent o", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-40 History: from batch to time sharing", "u": "answers.html#X2-40", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Briefly describe how operating systems evolved, and why the focus moved from hardware utilization to user convenience. [2.60] Early on, hardware was expensive and humans relatively cheap, so OSes maximized hardware utilization; later hardware became cheap and humans expensive, so OSes focused on users. Hand-programmed machines (1945–1955) had low hardware utilization, followed by batch processing (1955–1965) and multiprogramming (1965–1980): multiple jobs in memory, multiplexed over the CPU(s). Time sharing: users share servers through cheap terminals. Personal computers: utilization is not a ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X2-TF True / False quick verdicts", "u": "answers.html#X2-TF", "p": "Answer bank", "x": " Statement T/F Why (≤ 12 words) printf() is a system call provided by the kernel. F It is a library function that calls write(). Every C library (API) function makes a system call. F Some, e.g., strlen(), run entirely in user mode. Windows CreateProcess() invokes the NTCreateProcess() system call. T API functions invoke system calls for the programmer. read() returns 0 at end of file and −1 on error. T Otherwise it returns the number of bytes read. Using an API lets a program bypass kernel protection. F APIs only wrap system calls; protection still applies. An API caller must know how the syst", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-01 Program vs process; loading a program", "u": "answers.html#X3-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is the difference between a program and a process? When does a program become a process, and can one program be associated with several processes? A program is a passive entity — an executable file stored on disk. A process is an active entity — a program in execution, with a program counter specifying the next instruction to fetch and execute and a set of associated resources (address space, registers, open files). A program becomes a process when its executable file is loaded into main memory (double-click an icon or enter a command in the shell): the OS (1) loads the code and static da", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-02 Memory layout: text, data, heap, stack", "u": "answers.html#X3-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the sections of a process in memory. In the program below, where are x, y, values, i, the five ints from malloc(), argc/argv and the code of main stored? int x; int y = 15; int main(int argc, char *argv[]) { int *values; int i; values = (int *)malloc(sizeof(int) * 5); for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) values[i] = i; return 0; } Section Contents Text the program code Data global variables, divided into initialized data and uninitialized data Heap memory dynamically allocated during run time (malloc() / free()) Stack temporary data for function calls: function parameters, return addresses, local ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-03 Process states and transition events", "u": "answers.html#X3-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Name the five process states. Give the transition caused by each event: (a) admitted, (b) selected by the CPU scheduler, (c) timer interrupt, (d) a disk read is issued, (e) it calls wait() for its child, (f) the disk read completes, (g) it calls exit(). New (being created — allocating resources), ready (waiting to be assigned to a CPU core), running (instructions are being executed), waiting (waiting for some event to occur), terminated (finished — deallocating resources). Event Transition (a) admitted new → ready (b) scheduler dispatch (CPU scheduling) ready → running (c) timer interrupt runn", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-04 PCB contents; process table; Linux task_struct", "u": "answers.html#X3-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a Process Control Block (PCB) and what does it contain? How does the OS keep track of all PCBs, e.g., in Linux? The PCB (task control block) is a kernel data structure holding all information associated with a process: Process state — new, ready, running, waiting, … Program counter — location of the next instruction to execute CPU registers — accumulators, index registers, stack pointers, general-purpose registers, condition codes CPU-scheduling information — priority, pointers to scheduling queues, scheduling parameters Memory-management information — memory allocated (page / segment ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-05 Threads within a process", "u": "answers.html#X3-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How is a thread represented, and what do the threads of one process share? Why use several threads in a process? A thread is a single unique execution context: its own program counter, registers, execution flags and stack. It is executing when this context is resident in the CPU registers. Threads of a process run within the same address space — they share code, data and open files, while each keeps its own thread state. So a process can perform more than one task at a time (e.g., a web browser with one thread displaying images or text and another retrieving data from the network), and on mult", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-06 I/O-bound vs CPU-bound; good process mix", "u": "answers.html#X3-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define I/O-bound and CPU-bound processes. Why should the long-term scheduler select a good process mix? I/O-bound — spends more time doing I/O than computation; many short CPU bursts. CPU-bound — generates I/O requests infrequently, mostly computing; few very long CPU bursts. A good mix keeps both the CPU and the I/O devices busy. If all processes are I/O-bound, the ready queue is almost always empty and the CPU idles; if all are CPU-bound, the I/O wait queues are almost always empty and the devices sit unused. Hence the long-term scheduler (mainframes) strives for a good process mix to maximi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-07 Scheduling queues and the queueing diagram", "u": "answers.html#X3-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which scheduling queues does the OS maintain, and how does a process migrate among them during its lifetime? Ready queue — processes in main memory, ready and waiting to execute. Wait queues — processes waiting for an event such as I/O completion (one per device or event). The queues link PCBs (the PCB holds pointers to scheduling queues). A new process joins the ready queue until it is dispatched. While running, it leaves the CPU when it: issues an I/O request → I/O wait queue → back to ready when the I/O completes; uses up its time slice → straight back to the ready queue; creates a child an", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-08 Long-, short- and medium-term schedulers compared", "u": "answers.html#X3-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the long-term, short-term and medium-term schedulers. What is the degree of multiprogramming, and why must the short-term scheduler be fast? Long-term (job) scheduler Short-term (CPU) scheduler Medium-term scheduler Role selects which processes are brought into memory from the job queue (allocates memory) selects a process from the ready queue and allocates a CPU core swaps a process out to disk and back in later Frequency very infrequent (seconds, minutes) — may be slow very frequent (milliseconds) — must be fast when the workload is too heavy Degree of multiprogramming dictates it no", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-09 Swapping and the medium-term scheduler", "u": "answers.html#X3-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is swapping? When and why does the medium-term scheduler swap a process out? Swapping: the medium-term scheduler removes a partially executed process from memory, stores it temporarily on secondary storage (swap space), and later brings it back into memory to continue execution where it left off. When: the degree of multiprogramming must be reduced — memory is over-committed / the workload is heavy and execution becomes slow (or to improve the process mix). Why: it frees memory and other resources for the remaining processes, so they run faster; the swapped-out process resumes when resour", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-10 Context switch: saved state and kernel actions", "u": "answers.html#X3-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a context switch? Describe the actions the kernel takes, and name events that cause one. A context switch occurs when the CPU switches from one process (or thread) to another: the OS must save the state of the old process and load the saved state of the new process. The context is represented in the PCB — CPU registers, program counter, stack pointer, process state, memory-management information. Kernel actions: (1) an interrupt or system call enters the kernel; (2) save the running process's context in its PCB, set its state (ready / waiting) and queue it; (3) the short-term scheduler", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-11 Context-switch overhead; multiple register sets", "u": "answers.html#X3-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is context-switch time overhead, and what does it depend on? Some CPUs have multiple register sets: what happens if the new context is already in a register set, and if it is not? Context-switch time is pure overhead — the system does no useful work while switching; the more complex the OS and the PCB, the longer the switch. It is highly dependent on the hardware: memory speed, the number of registers to copy, and special instructions (e.g., a single instruction to load or store all registers). Multiple register sets (e.g., SUN UltraSPARC): if the new context is already loaded, the switch ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-12 Context switch vs mode switch", "u": "answers.html#X3-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Is a context switch the same as switching from user mode to kernel mode? Explain. No. A mode switch flips the mode bit (user ↔ kernel) on a system call, interrupt or trap; the CPU saves the running process's state, runs kernel code and may return to the same process — e.g., getpid() returns at once and no other process runs. A context switch changes which process runs: the kernel saves the old process's state in its PCB and loads another process's saved state. It is performed by the kernel (in kernel mode), typically after a timer interrupt or a blocking system call. So every context switch re", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-13 Interrupt-driven I/O and device controllers", "u": "answers.html#X3-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain how the CPU and a device controller cooperate to perform interrupt-driven I/O. I/O devices and the CPU execute concurrently and asynchronously. Each device controller is in charge of a particular device and has a local buffer. The device driver starts the operation by loading the controller's registers; the controller moves data between the device and its local buffer while the CPU continues other work. When finished, the device controller informs the CPU by causing an interrupt (signal on the interrupt-request line): for input, data is available in the local buffer; for output, the I/", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-14 Interrupt handling and the interrupt vector", "u": "answers.html#X3-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is an interrupt? Describe how the CPU handles one, and explain why an interrupt vector is used. An interrupt is a signal from hardware to the CPU, through an interrupt-request line, that an event requires CPU attention (e.g., I/O completion). It transfers control to an interrupt service routine (interrupt handler), which is part of the kernel. Handling: the CPU senses the interrupt-request line after executing every instruction; on a signal it saves the state of the interrupted process (PC, registers), reads the interrupt number, uses it as an index into the interrupt vector and jumps to ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-15 Maskable vs nonmaskable interrupts; priority levels", "u": "answers.html#X3-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish maskable and nonmaskable interrupts. What are interrupt priority levels for? Most CPUs have two interrupt-request lines: Nonmaskable — cannot be turned off; reserved for events such as unrecoverable memory errors and power failure. Maskable — can be turned off by the CPU before executing critical instruction sequences that must not be interrupted; used by device controllers to request service, which can be delayed. Interrupt priority levels let the CPU defer low-priority interrupts without masking all interrupts, and let a high-priority interrupt preempt the execution of a low-prio", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-16 Programmed I/O vs DMA; DMA transfer steps", "u": "answers.html#X3-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is DMA used instead of programmed I/O? Describe how a DMA transfer is set up and completed. Programmed I/O: the CPU runs special I/O instructions to move one byte at a time between memory and a device — acceptable for slow devices (keyboard, mouse), but for fast devices and large transfers (disk) it wastes CPU time on data movement. DMA: the DMA controller bypasses the CPU and transfers a whole block directly between the I/O device and memory; the CPU/OS only initializes it, which relieves the CPU to run other work concurrently. Set-up: the OS writes a DMA command block into memory (source", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-17 Dual-mode operation, mode bit, privileged instructions", "u": "answers.html#X3-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is dual-mode operation and why is it needed? How does the hardware support it, and what happens when a user program executes a privileged instruction? Dual-mode operation separates user mode (user code) from kernel mode (OS code); it allows the OS to protect itself and other system components — the OS from errant users, and errant users from one another. A mode bit provided by hardware shows the current mode: user = 1, kernel = 0. Some instructions are privileged — executable only in kernel mode (e.g., I/O control, timer management, interrupt management), and devices are accessed only in ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-18 Which operations must be privileged", "u": "answers.html#X3-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which of these must be privileged, and why? (a) set the value of the timer (b) read the clock (c) clear memory (d) issue a trap instruction (e) disable interrupts (f) modify entries in the device-status table (g) access an I/O device. Privileged: (a), (c), (e), (f), (g) — set the timer, clear memory, disable interrupts, modify the device-status table, access I/O devices. A user program doing these could monopolize the CPU (timer, interrupts), wipe out the OS or other processes (clear memory), or bypass the OS's control of devices. Not privileged: (b) read the clock (harmless, read-only) and (d", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-19 Timer: OS always regains control", "u": "answers.html#X3-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does the OS ensure that a user program cannot keep the CPU forever (e.g., one stuck in an infinite loop)? With a timer: before giving control to a user program, the OS sets the timer to interrupt after a specified period (a counter decremented by the clock; an interrupt occurs when it reaches 0). The timer interrupt transfers control back to the OS in kernel mode, which may treat it as a fatal error, give the program more time, or schedule another process (time slice expired). Instructions that modify the timer are privileged, so a user program cannot turn it off — hence a user program alw", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-20 Three types of mode transfer", "u": "answers.html#X3-20", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the three ways control passes from user mode to kernel mode: system call, interrupt, and trap/exception. Type Nature Examples System call a user process requests a service from the OS — like a function call, but outside the user process read(), write(), exit(), fork() Interrupt external, asynchronous event, independent of the user process timer, I/O completion Trap / exception internal, synchronous event caused by the running process protection violation (segmentation fault), divide by zero, privileged instruction in user mode All three set the mode bit to kernel mode, save the running", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-21 System-call path: printf() to write()", "u": "answers.html#X3-21", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In terms of user mode and kernel mode, explain what happens when a program calls printf(\"hi\\n\"). printf() is a C library (API) function running in user mode; it formats the text into a buffer and invokes the write() system call on fd 1 (buffering may delay this until a newline or flush). The write() wrapper puts the system-call number and parameters in registers and executes a trap → the hardware sets the mode bit to kernel mode (0) and jumps to the kernel's system-call handler. The kernel indexes the system-call table with the number, runs the write service routine (device driver) and stores ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-22 Beyond two modes: rings, VMM, machine switch", "u": "answers.html#X3-22", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How can dual-mode operation be extended beyond two modes? What is a machine switch? Intel processors have four protection rings: ring 0 = kernel mode, ring 3 = user mode; rings 1 and 2 could run OS services but are rarely used. ARMv8 systems have seven modes. CPUs that support virtualization have a separate mode for the virtual machine manager (VMM), with more privileges than user processes but fewer than the kernel. A machine switch is the VMM's counterpart of a context switch: it saves the entire machine state of one guest OS — registers, PC and, unlike a context switch, any privileged hardw", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-23 Process creation options; tree of processes", "u": "answers.html#X3-23", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "When a parent creates a child, what are the options for (a) sharing resources, (b) execution, and (c) the child's address space? Give UNIX examples. Processes form a tree of processes: a parent creates children, which create their own children; on Linux the root is systemd (PID 1), and each process has a unique pid. The child needs CPU time, memory, files and I/O devices, and the parent may pass it initialization data. (a) Resource sharing: parent and children share almost all resources — fork() (the child gets copies of everything); children share a subset of the parent's resources — clone();", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-24 fork() return values and why two", "u": "answers.html#X3-24", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What does fork() return, and why must the parent and the child receive different values? fork() takes no parameters; each process gets exactly one return value: > 0 in the parent — the PID of the newly created child; 0 in the child; −1 in the original process if it fails — no child is created (errno is set). The child duplicates the parent's entire address space, including the program counter, so both continue at the instruction right after fork() with identical code; the return value is the only way to tell which process is running, so the two can take different branches. The parent also lear", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-25 fork() steps, copy-on-write, clone()", "u": "answers.html#X3-25", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What steps does the kernel take to implement fork()? Why is this expensive, and how does Linux speed it up? Steps: (1) create and initialize the PCB in the kernel (unique PID); (2) create a new address space (allocate memory); (3) initialize it with a copy of the entire contents of the parent's address space — time consuming; (4) inherit the parent's execution context (e.g., open files); (5) inform the CPU scheduler that the child is ready to run. The copy is wasted when the child calls exec() immediately and discards it. Copy-on-write (COW): parent and child share a single copy of the pages, ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-26 After fork(): copied, shared, different", "u": "answers.html#X3-26", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "After a successful fork(), what does the child get from the parent and what is different? Which of the stack, heap and shared-memory segments are shared? Duplicated (the child gets its own copy): the address space — text, data, heap, stack, hence global and local variables; the program counter and registers; open file descriptors (referring to the same files and pipes); current and root directory, process resources, resource limits; customized signal handlers. Different: the PID (and parent PID), the return value of fork(), running time and running state. Stack and heap are copied, not shared ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-27 Output: child modifies a global array", "u": "answers.html#X3-27", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is printed at LINE X and LINE Y? #define SIZE 5 int nums[SIZE] = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}; int main() { int i; pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { for (i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) { nums[i] *= -i; printf(\"CHILD: %d \", nums[i]); /* X */ } } else if (pid > 0) { wait(NULL); for (i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) printf(\"PARENT: %d \", nums[i]); /* Y */ } return 0; } LINE X (child): CHILD: 0 CHILD: -1 CHILD: -4 CHILD: -9 CHILD: -16 LINE Y (parent): PARENT: 0 PARENT: 1 PARENT: 2 PARENT: 3 PARENT: 4, printed after the child's output because of wait(NULL). fork() gives the child its own copy of the parent's data section, s", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-28 Output: fork() return value vs getpid()", "u": "answers.html#X3-28", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "The actual PIDs of the parent and the child are 2600 and 2603. What values are printed at lines A, B, C and D? pid_t pid, pid1; pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { /* child */ pid1 = getpid(); printf(\"child: pid = %d\", pid); /* A */ printf(\"child: pid1 = %d\", pid1); /* B */ } else if (pid > 0) { /* parent */ pid1 = getpid(); printf(\"parent: pid = %d\", pid); /* C */ printf(\"parent: pid1 = %d\", pid1); /* D */ wait(NULL); } A = 0 (fork() returns 0 in the child); B = 2603 (getpid() gives the child's own PID); C = 2603 (fork() returns the child's PID to the parent); D = 2600 (the parent's own PID). Key: ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-29 Output: unflushed printf buffer copied by fork()", "u": "answers.html#X3-29", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is printed (a) if every printf is flushed immediately, (b) if stdout is buffered? Why can ./a.out > out.txt show A twice even with printf(\"A\\n\")? int main() { printf(\"A\"); fork(); printf(\"B\\n\"); return 0; } (a) Flushed immediately: A once, B twice → AB then B. A is printed before fork(), and both processes execute printf(\"B\\n\"). (b) Buffered: AB is printed twice. The unflushed \"A\" is still in the stdio buffer inside the process's address space, and fork() copies the address space, buffer included, so each process later flushes its own \"A\" followed by \"B\\n\". When stdout is a file or pipe i", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-30 exec(): success, failure, execlp vs execvp", "u": "answers.html#X3-30", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What does a successful exec() do to the calling process? Does it create a new process or change the PID? When is the statement after exec() executed? Contrast execlp() and execvp(). exec() changes the program being run by the current process: it creates a new process image from an executable file, replacing the address space (text, data, heap, stack). It does not create a new process — the PID, PCB and open file descriptors stay the same (so redirection set up before exec() still applies). A successful exec() never returns, so the code after it is never executed; only if it fails (e.g., progra", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-31 Output: exec in a loop succeeds/fails", "u": "answers.html#X3-31", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How many processes are created and what is printed if (a) execlp() succeeds, (b) execlp() fails? What should the child do after a failed exec? int main() { for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) execlp(\"echo\", \"echo\", \"hi\", NULL); } while (wait(NULL) > 0) ; printf(\"done\\n\"); return 0; } (a) exec succeeds: 4 processes (parent + 3 children). Each child becomes echo, prints hi and terminates — it never returns to the loop. The parent reaps all three and prints done once, last: hi, hi, hi, done. (b) exec fails: execlp() returns −1 and the child continues the loop as a copy", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-32 Why a shell uses fork, exec, wait", "u": "answers.html#X3-32", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why does a shell create a child with fork() and call exec() in the child, instead of calling exec() directly? Why is it useful that UNIX separates the two calls? If the shell called exec() itself, its own image would be replaced by the command, and the shell would be gone when the command ended. So the shell fork()s a child, the child exec()s the command (overlays the new program), and the parent wait()s for the child, then reads the next command. For a background command (a.out &) the shell does not wait. Most OSes create a process and load a program in one step; UNIX splits this into fork() ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-33 PA1: cd and exit as built-ins", "u": "answers.html#X3-33", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In PA1, why can cd not be executed with exec*(), and how is it implemented? Why is exit also a built-in? The current working directory is a per-process attribute. If the shell forked a child to change directory, only the child's directory would change, and the change is lost when the child exits — a child cannot change its parent's directory, so the shell would stay where it was. So cd is a built-in command run inside the shell process itself, without fork(), using chdir(path): 0 on success, −1 on error (print \"Myshell cd command error\"); it accepts absolute and relative paths, including . and", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-34 PA1: why system() and popen() are banned", "u": "answers.html#X3-34", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why are system() and popen() forbidden in PA1? Why must pipes be used with open/read/write/close rather than fopen/fread/fwrite/fclose? system() creates a system shell (e.g., bash) that runs the command, and the parent is blocked until it finishes; popen() also has a shell run the command concurrently and returns a FILE* for one-way communication with it. Using them hands all process creation, redirection and piping to another shell, defeating the purpose of PA1 (process management and IPC through system calls); they are detected by pattern matching and score 0 marks. PA1 must use fork, exec*,", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-35 PA1: Ctrl-C (SIGINT) handling with signal()", "u": "answers.html#X3-35", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does PA1 customize Ctrl-C handling? What is a signal, and what does the handler receive? A signal is a software-generated interrupt that notifies a process that an event has occurred. Ctrl-C makes the OS send SIGINT (signal number 2), whose default handler terminates the process; SIGINT is asynchronous (caused by an event outside the process). PA1 overrides the default with a user-defined handler registered at start-up; the signal number is passed to the handler as its parameter: void handler(int sig) { /* sig = 2 */ printf(\"Myshell (pid=%d) terminates\" \" by Ctrl-C with signal: %d\\n\", getp", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-36 Termination: exit, wait, abort, cascading", "u": "answers.html#X3-36", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe how a process terminates normally and how its parent obtains the exit status. Why might a parent terminate a child, and what is cascading termination? After its last statement a process asks the OS to delete it with exit(), called directly or indirectly (the C run-time library calls exit() when main() returns). The OS de-allocates and reclaims its resources: memory (program, data, heap, stack), open files, I/O buffers. It may return a status value (integer) to its waiting parent: pid = wait(&status); returns the PID of the terminated child (so the parent knows which child ended) and s", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-37 Zombie vs orphan; role of init/systemd", "u": "answers.html#X3-37", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare zombie and orphan processes, and explain the role of init/systemd in process termination. Zombie Orphan Definition has terminated, but its parent has not yet called wait() its parent terminated without calling wait() What remains only its PID, process-table entry and PCB (holding the exit status); all other resources released everything — it may still be running Runnable? no — it has no address space yes (if cascading termination is not used) Cleaned up by the parent's wait(), which releases the PID, entry and PCB init/systemd, its new parent All processes pass through the zombie state", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-38 Zombie or orphan? Two scenarios", "u": "answers.html#X3-38", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What happens to the child in each case? (A) The child calls exit(0) at once; the parent calls sleep(30) and then wait(NULL). (B) The child calls sleep(30) and then prints; the parent returns from main() at once without wait(). (A) The child is a zombie for about 30 seconds: it has terminated, but its PID, process-table entry and PCB (exit status) remain until the parent's wait(NULL) reaps it; then they are released. (B) The child becomes an orphan: the parent ends first, so the shell prompt reappears before the child's output. The child keeps running, is re-parented to init/systemd (PID 1), pr", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-39 Independent vs cooperating processes", "u": "answers.html#X3-39", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish independent and cooperating processes. Give reasons for allowing processes to cooperate. Independent processes cannot affect or be affected by other processes; cooperating processes can affect or be affected by other processes (e.g., they share data), so they need an interprocess communication (IPC) mechanism to exchange data. Reasons: information sharing — several applications need the same information (e.g., copy and paste); computation speedup — split a task into parallel subtasks (e.g., machine-learning jobs on multiple cores); modularity — divide system functions into separate", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-40 Shared memory vs message passing", "u": "answers.html#X3-40", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the two IPC models, shared memory and message passing, giving an advantage of each. Shared memory Message passing How a region of memory is shared; processes read and write the shared region — implicit communication explicit messages exchanged with send() / receive() Kernel role system calls only to set up the region, then ordinary memory accesses kernel intervention (system call) for every message Speed faster slower for large data Synchronization the processes must coordinate themselves (e.g., producer–consumer) provided by the IPC facility (e.g., blocking send/receive) Suits large d", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-41 Bounded buffer: empty, full, BUFFER_SIZE − 1", "u": "answers.html#X3-41", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In the shared-memory bounded-buffer solution with in and out, when is the buffer empty or full, and why can it hold only BUFFER_SIZE - 1 items? With BUFFER_SIZE = 10, in = 3, out = 7, how many items are in the buffer? Unbounded buffer: no practical limit on size (only the consumer may wait). Bounded buffer: fixed size — the consumer waits when it is empty, the producer waits when it is full. Empty: in == out. Full: (in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE == out. The producer stores buffer[in] and advances in = (in + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE; the consumer takes buffer[out] and advances out the same way; each busy-waits", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-42 Message passing: link and design choices", "u": "answers.html#X3-42", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What must processes P and Q do to communicate by message passing? List the design choices for implementing the link, and compare fixed- and variable-size messages. P and Q must establish a communication link between them, then exchange messages with the primitives send(message) and receive(message). Design choices for the link: direct or indirect communication (naming); synchronous (blocking) or asynchronous (non-blocking) send/receive; automatic or explicit buffering. Fixed-size messages keep the system-level implementation straightforward but make programming harder; variable-size messages n", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-43 Direct vs indirect communication; mailbox sharing", "u": "answers.html#X3-43", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare direct and indirect communication. If P1, P2 and P3 share mailbox A, P1 sends a message, and both P2 and P3 call receive(), who gets it? Direct Indirect (mailbox) Naming processes name each other explicitly: send(P, msg), receive(Q, msg) send to / receive from a mailbox: send(A, msg), receive(A, msg); each mailbox has a unique id Link set-up automatic; each needs only the other's identity only if both processes share a mailbox Processes per link exactly one pair may be more than two Links per pair exactly one several, one per shared mailbox Drawback limited modularity: changing a proce", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-44 Blocking vs non-blocking send/receive; rendezvous", "u": "answers.html#X3-44", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain blocking and non-blocking send and receive. What is a rendezvous, and how does it simplify producer–consumer? Blocking (synchronous) Non-blocking (asynchronous) send() the sender is blocked until the message is received by the receiving process or mailbox the sender sends and resumes its operation receive() the receiver blocks until a message is available the receiver retrieves either a valid message or null When both send() and receive() are blocking, we have a rendezvous between the sender and the receiver. Producer–consumer becomes trivial: the producer calls send(next_produced) and", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-45 Buffering: zero, bounded, unbounded capacity", "u": "answers.html#X3-45", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the three ways to implement the queue of messages on a link. When must the sender wait? Capacity Queue Sender waits? Zero capacity no messages are queued on the link always — it must wait for the receiver (rendezvous) Bounded capacity finite length of n messages only if the link is full Unbounded capacity infinite length never Messages reside in this temporary queue whether communication is direct or indirect. Zero capacity means no buffering; bounded and unbounded are automatic buffering. A UNIX pipe behaves like bounded capacity: it has a limited size, so a writer blocks when the pi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-46 Pipe design issues; ordinary vs named pipes", "u": "answers.html#X3-46", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "List the four issues to consider when implementing a pipe, and answer them for ordinary pipes and UNIX named pipes (FIFOs). Issue Ordinary pipe Named pipe (UNIX FIFO) Unidirectional or bidirectional? unidirectional (one-way, producer → consumer) bidirectional Half or full duplex? one direction only half-duplex — two FIFOs for both directions at once Relationship needed? parent–child none; several processes, typically several writers Over a network? no — same machine no — same machine Creation pipe(int fd[2]): fd[0] = read end, fd[1] = write end mkfifo(); appears as a typical file in the file s", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-47 When to use ordinary vs named pipes", "u": "answers.html#X3-47", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give a situation in which an ordinary pipe is more suitable than a named pipe, and one in which a named pipe is more suitable. Ordinary pipe: simple one-way communication between a parent and its child on one machine — e.g., a shell running ls | sort, or a parent writing a file's contents into a pipe while its child counts the characters. No name is needed, and the pipe disappears automatically when they terminate. Named pipe: unrelated processes (no parent–child relationship) or many writers — e.g., several processes write log messages to a named pipe and a logger server reads it and appends ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-48 Why close unused pipe ends (EOF)", "u": "answers.html#X3-48", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why must every process close the pipe ends it does not use? In the lab's ls | wc -l program, what happens if the process that runs wc does not close pfds[1]? read() on a pipe returns 0 (end-of-file) only when every write end is closed in every process. If any process — even the reader itself — keeps a write end open, the reader blocks forever once the data is used up, and the program hangs. In the lab the parent runs close(0); dup(pfds[0]); close(pfds[1]); wait(0); execlp(\"wc\", ...). Without close(pfds[1]), wc holds a write end itself, so after ls exits, wc never sees EOF and never prints the ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-49 What happens: blocking pipe and FIFO calls", "u": "answers.html#X3-49", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What happens when: (a) a process reads an empty pipe whose write end is still open; (b) it reads after all write ends are closed; (c) a process writes to a full pipe; (d) a process opens a FIFO for writing while no process has it open for reading; (e) two processes read the same FIFO? (a) read() blocks until data is written — pipe read/write are blocking I/O. (b) read() returns 0 = end-of-file (after any remaining data has been read). (c) write() blocks until the reader removes data — a pipe has a limited size (lab: a 4 KB page; 64 KB on some systems). (d) open() blocks until a reader opens th", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-50 Output: parent-to-child pipe program", "u": "answers.html#X3-50", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "List all possible outputs (assume each printf is flushed immediately). int pfds[2]; char buf[30]; pipe(pfds); pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid != 0) { printf(\"PARENT: writing to pipe\\n\"); close(pfds[0]); write(pfds[1], \"test\", 5); wait(NULL); printf(\"PARENT: exiting\\n\"); } else { printf(\"CHILD: reading from pipe\\n\"); close(pfds[1]); read(pfds[0], buf, 5); printf(\"CHILD: read \\\"%s\\\"\\n\", buf); } printf(\"world!\\n\"); Two outputs — only the first two lines can swap: PARENT: writing to pipe / CHILD: reading from pipe / CHILD: read \"test\" / world! / PARENT: exiting / world! CHILD: reading from pipe / PARE", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-51 Two-way communication with ordinary pipes", "u": "answers.html#X3-51", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How can a parent and child communicate in both directions using ordinary pipes? Why not use one pipe? An ordinary pipe is unidirectional, so create two pipes before fork(): p2c (parent → child) and c2p (child → parent). Parent: close(p2c[0]); close(c2p[1]);, then writes to p2c[1] and reads from c2p[0]. Child: close(p2c[1]); close(c2p[0]);, then reads from p2c[0] and writes to c2p[1]. With one pipe, a process that writes and then reads may read back its own message. (A UNIX FIFO is likewise half-duplex; two FIFOs are used for simultaneous two-way traffic.) Order the calls so that one side write", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-52 Shell pipelines and multi-level pipes", "u": "answers.html#X3-52", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does a shell implement ls | grep x? How is this extended to an n-segment pipeline such as PA1's ls | sort -r | sort | ...? The shell calls pipe(fd) before forking, then forks two children: child 1 does dup2(fd[1], 1) (stdout → write end) and execs ls; child 2 does dup2(fd[0], 0) (stdin ← read end) and execs grep x (it prints only the lines of ls output containing x). Every process closes the pipe ends it does not use, and the shell closes both ends and waits for both children. Neither program knows about the pipe — pipes let separate tools be combined without being designed to work togethe", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-53 dup vs dup2; I/O redirection", "u": "answers.html#X3-53", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What do dup() and dup2() do? How is wc -l < in.txt > out.txt implemented, and why is the redirection done in the child after fork() and before exec()? What appears on the screen and in log.txt below? int fd = open(\"log.txt\", O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, S_IRUSR | S_IWUSR); printf(\"A\\n\"); int saved = dup(1); dup2(fd, 1); printf(\"B\\n\"); fflush(stdout); dup2(saved, 1); printf(\"C\\n\"); dup(oldfd) copies an open descriptor into the lowest-numbered unused fd (so close(1); dup(fd); makes fd 1 refer to the file). dup2(oldfd, newfd) copies it into newfd, closing newfd first if it is open. Both return the new fd ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-54 Sockets, ports and the 4-tuple", "u": "answers.html#X3-54", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a socket? How does client–server communication work, and how are two connections from the same client host to the same web server told apart? A socket is an endpoint for communication; two processes communicating over a network use a pair of sockets, one at each end. A socket is identified by an IP address (host) and a port number (process or application). The server is always running with a well-known address and port, listening for requests; only clients can initiate communication. Ports below 1024 are well known, reserved for standard services (SSH 22, FTP 21, HTTP 80); a client pic", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-55 Remote procedure calls (RPC)", "u": "answers.html#X3-55", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a remote procedure call (RPC), and what is it commonly used for? How does it differ from using sockets directly? RPC abstracts the procedure-call mechanism for use between systems with network connections: a client calls a procedure on a remote host as if it were a local call. It is a message-based communication scheme to provide remote services. A client-side stub packs the procedure name and parameters into a message and sends it to an RPC daemon on the server, which executes the procedure and sends the result back in a message. Common use: distributed file systems, implemented as a ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X3-TF True / False quick verdicts", "u": "answers.html#X3-TF", "p": "Answer bank", "x": " Statement T/F Why (≤ 12 words) A program is an active entity and a process is a passive one. F Reversed: a program is passive; a process is active. Function parameters, return addresses and local variables are on the stack. T Heap holds malloc() memory; data section holds globals. A process can move directly from waiting to running. F It goes waiting → ready, then is dispatched. Issuing an I/O request moves a process from running to waiting. T It sits in a wait queue until the I/O completes. On a single-core CPU, two processes can be running at the same instant. F At most one runs; the others", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-01 thread definition, thread vs process", "u": "answers.html#X4-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a thread? Compare a thread with a process. [4.6, 4.14–4.16] A thread is a basic unit of CPU utilization: an independently scheduled instance of execution within a process, represented by a thread ID, a program counter (PC), a register set and a stack. A process is an address space (code, data, open files, other resources) plus one or more threads. Process Thread Role \"passive\" part: address space encapsulates protection \"active\" component: encapsulates concurrency Memory own address space, separate from other processes shares the address space of its process Creation heavyweight (new a", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-02 shared vs private per-thread state", "u": "answers.html#X4-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What do threads of the same process share, and what does each thread keep private? Why must each thread have its own stack and registers? [4.6, 4.16] Shared by all threads of a process: code (text), data (global variables), heap, open files (file descriptors, network connections) and signals (signal handlers), i.e., the address space and OS resources. Private to each thread (kept in its TCB): thread ID, program counter, register set and stack. Why private: each thread is an independent flow of control at a different instruction, so it needs its own PC and registers; its stack holds its own cal", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-03 four benefits of multithreading, examples", "u": "answers.html#X4-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "State the four benefits of multithreaded programming and give an example of each. [4.7] Responsiveness: may allow continued execution if part of a process (one thread) is blocked, which matters for interactive applications. E.g., a word processor keeps responding to keystrokes while another thread does background spelling and grammar checking. Resource sharing: threads share the resources of a process by default (same address space), easier than shared memory or message passing between processes. E.g., a browser thread retrieves data from the network into a buffer that the display thread reads", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-04 thread creation and switching cheaper", "u": "answers.html#X4-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain why creating a thread, and switching between two threads of the same process, are cheaper than doing the same for processes. [4.4, 4.14] Threads of a process share its address space and resources, so only the thread-specific state has to be created or switched. Creation: a new process needs a new address space and copies of program, data and resources (PCB, memory, open files), so it is heavyweight. A new thread needs only a new TCB and a stack; the process-specific data structures are unchanged, so it is lightweight. Context switch T1 → T2 (same process): save T1's register state and ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-05 disadvantages of threads vs processes", "u": "answers.html#X4-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give two disadvantages of using multiple threads instead of multiple processes. When would separate processes be preferable? [4.9, 4.16] (1) There is no protection between threads, and (2) shared data needs synchronization. All threads run in one address space, and the address space is what encapsulates protection. A buggy thread can corrupt shared data or crash the whole process, e.g., an illegal memory access terminates the program and all its threads. Separate address spaces keep a buggy program from thrashing the entire system. Shared globals and heap invite race conditions: if there is a ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-06 multicore programming challenges", "u": "answers.html#X4-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "List and briefly explain the challenges of programming for multicore systems. [4.9] Five challenges: Dividing tasks: find separate, concurrent tasks in the application. Balance: each task should perform an \"equal\" amount of work; a task that contributes little value may not be worth a separate core. Data splitting: data used by the tasks must be divided to run on separate cores. Data dependency: when one task uses data produced by another, synchronization is required. Testing and debugging: different paths of execution (many possible interleavings) make debugging difficult. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-07 concurrency vs parallelism", "u": "answers.html#X4-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish concurrency from parallelism. Can there be concurrency without parallelism? Parallelism without concurrency? [4.9–4.10] Concurrency supports more than one task making progress; on a single processor core, the scheduler provides concurrency by multiplexing over time. Parallelism means a system can perform more than one task simultaneously, which requires multiple cores or processors. Concurrency without parallelism: yes, e.g., 4 threads time-sliced on one core. Parallelism without concurrency: no. Tasks running simultaneously are all making progress, so every parallel system is also", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-08 data vs task parallelism, classify examples", "u": "answers.html#X4-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain data and task parallelism using an array of N integers on a dual-core system. Classify: (a) a blur filter applied to four image quadrants on four cores; (b) a video player with one thread decoding video, one decoding audio and one handling the UI. [4.11] Data parallelism distributes subsets of the same data across multiple cores and performs the same operation on each core. Thread A on core 0 sums elements 0 … N/2 − 1, thread B on core 1 sums N/2 … N − 1, and the two partial sums are added. Task parallelism distributes threads (tasks) across cores, each thread performing a unique opera", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-09 Amdahl's law statement, limit 1/S", "u": "answers.html#X4-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "State Amdahl's Law. What happens to the speedup as the number of cores N → ∞, and why does the serial portion have a \"disproportionate effect\"? [4.12–4.13] speedup≤1/(S+(1−S)/N), where S = serial portion, 1 − S = parallel portion, N = number of processing cores. It gives the theoretical speedup in latency of a task at a fixed workload gained by adding cores. As N → ∞, (1 − S)/N → 0, so the speedup approaches 1/S: the serial part takes the same time however many cores are added. Disproportionate effect: with only 10% serial (S = 0.1), 8 cores give 1/(0.1+0.9/8)=1/0.2125≈4.71, not 8, and unlimit", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-10 Amdahl speedup, 60% parallel", "u": "answers.html#X4-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "An application is 60% parallel. Using Amdahl's Law, find the maximum speedup on 4 cores, on 16 cores, and with unlimited cores. [4.12] S = serial portion = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4 (the 60% is the parallel part). N = 4: 1/(0.4+0.6/4)=1/(0.4+0.15)=1/0.55≈ 1.82 N = 16: 1/(0.4+0.6/16)=1/(0.4+0.0375)=1/0.4375≈ 2.29 N → ∞: 1/S=1/0.4= 2.5 Four times as many cores raise the speedup only from 1.82 to 2.29, because the 40% serial part dominates. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-11 Amdahl serial fraction from measured speedup", "u": "answers.html#X4-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A program runs 4 times faster on 8 cores than on 1 core. Assuming Amdahl's Law holds with equality, what fraction of it is serial? What is the maximum speedup with unlimited cores? [4.12] 4=1/(S+(1−S)/8) → S+(1−S)/8=0.25 → multiply by 8: 8S+1−S=2 → 7S=1 → S = 1/7 ≈ 0.143 (14.3% serial, 85.7% parallel). Maximum speedup as N → ∞ =1/S= 7. Template: measured speedup X on N cores gives S=(N/X−1)/(N−1). Check: (8/4−1)/(8−1)=1/7. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-12 Amdahl cores needed for target speedup", "u": "answers.html#X4-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "An application is 10% serial. How many cores are needed for a speedup of at least 5? Can it ever reach a speedup of 12? [4.12] 1/(0.1+0.9/N)≥5 → 0.1+0.9/N≤0.2 → 0.9/N≤0.1 → N ≥ 9 cores. Check: 1/(0.1+0.9/9)=1/0.2=5, while 8 cores give only 4.71. 12 is impossible: the speedup approaches 1/S = 1/0.1 = 10 as N → ∞ and never exceeds it. Template: N≥(1−S)/(1/X−S), valid only when the target X < 1/S. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-13 doubling cores vs halving serial part", "u": "answers.html#X4-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A program has serial fraction S = 0.2 and runs on N = 4 cores. Which helps more: doubling the cores or halving the serial portion? Does the answer change if N = 8? [4.12] N = 4 (now 1/(0.2+0.8/4)=2.5): doubling the cores gives 1/(0.2+0.8/8)=1/0.3≈ 3.33; halving S gives 1/(0.1+0.9/4)=1/0.325≈ 3.08. So doubling the cores helps more. N = 8 (now 1/0.3≈3.33): doubling the cores gives 1/(0.2+0.8/16)=1/0.25= 4; halving S gives 1/(0.1+0.9/8)=1/0.2125≈ 4.71. So halving the serial part helps more. Rule: halving S beats doubling N exactly when S > 1/N (S·N > 1). As cores increase, the serial portion domi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-14 why real speedup is lower", "u": "answers.html#X4-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Measured speedups on N cores are usually lower than Amdahl's Law predicts. Why? [4.9, 4.12] Amdahl's Law is a theoretical upper bound (≤): it assumes the parallel part splits perfectly over N cores with zero overhead. In practice there is also: Overhead of creating, managing and context-switching threads. Synchronization: data dependencies force threads to wait for each other (locks, joins). Imbalance: tasks rarely perform an \"equal\" amount of work, so the slowest thread decides the finish time. Contention for shared memory, bus and caches, and other processes competing for the same cores. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-15 multithreading on a single core", "u": "answers.html#X4-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Can a multithreaded program finish faster than its single-threaded version on a single-core CPU? Explain. [4.9, 4.14] Yes if the threads perform I/O (or otherwise block); no for purely CPU-bound work. One core gives only concurrency (multiplexing over time), not parallelism, so CPU-bound threads gain no speedup and add context-switch overhead. Threads enable overlap of I/O with other activities within a single program: one thread runs on the CPU while another thread of the process is doing I/O. The total time drops and the program stays responsive. This needs kernel threads (one-to-one or many", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-16 Thread Control Block contents", "u": "answers.html#X4-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What does the OS keep in a Thread Control Block (TCB)? How is the TCB related to the PCB? [4.15] Each thread has a TCB holding its private execution state, its scheduling and accounting information, and a pointer to its enclosing process (PCB). Execution state: CPU registers, program counter, pointer to stack (saved here while the thread is not running). Scheduling info: thread state, priority, CPU time. Accounting info. Various pointers that link the TCB into scheduling queues. Pointer to enclosing process (PCB): process-wide information (process ID, memory/address space, open files) stays in", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-17 process state with multiple threads", "u": "answers.html#X4-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A process has three threads: one running, one waiting for a disk read, one ready. What is the state of the process? Where are the threads' TCBs? [4.17–4.18] Each thread has its own state (new, ready, running, waiting, terminated), recorded in its TCB; the threads are scheduled individually, so one thread of the process can be running while another waits and a third is ready. Running thread: executing on the CPU; it is resident in the registers. Ready thread: its TCB is in the ready queue. Waiting thread: its TCB is in the disk unit's device queue. TCBs are organized into queues based on their ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-18 user threads vs kernel threads", "u": "answers.html#X4-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish user-level threads from kernel-level threads. [4.19–4.20] User threads are created and managed by a user-level thread library and are unknown to the kernel; kernel threads are supported, managed and scheduled by the OS. User threads Kernel threads Managed by user-level threads library operating system Known to kernel no, visible only to programmers yes; each has a TCB Scheduling cannot be scheduled on a CPU directly the only threads the OS schedules on CPUs Creation cost lower more overhead (kernel allocates resources) Examples Pthreads / Windows / Java thread APIs Windows, Linux, ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-19 thread libraries, Pthreads specification", "u": "answers.html#X4-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a thread library? Name the three primary thread libraries. Is Pthreads an implementation? [4.20] A thread library provides the programmer with an API for creating and managing threads (user threads) in programs. Three primary libraries: POSIX Pthreads (POSIX API, UNIX/Linux), Windows threads (Win32 API), Java threads (Java API). Pthreads is a specification, not an implementation: the POSIX standard (IEEE 1003.1c) defines the API for thread creation and synchronization, and different UNIX-like OSes implement it differently. User threads are the concurrent and modular entities of a progr", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-20 pthread_create arguments, pthread_join purpose", "u": "answers.html#X4-20", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain the arguments of pthread_create() and the purpose of pthread_join(). What can go wrong if main() never calls pthread_join()? [4.20, 4.33] pthread_create() starts a new thread that runs start_routine(arg); pthread_join() makes the caller wait until that thread terminates. int pthread_create(pthread_t *tid, const pthread_attr_t *attr, void *(*start_routine)(void *), void *arg); tid receives the new thread identifier; attr holds the thread attributes (stack size, scheduling info; NULL or pthread_attr_init() = default); start_routine is the function where the thread begins execution; arg i", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-21 passing arguments, returning thread results", "u": "answers.html#X4-21", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A start routine takes only one void * argument. How do you pass several values to a thread, and how does the thread return a result to the thread that joins it? [4.20, 4.33] Pack the values in a struct, pass its address as arg, and return a pointer with return or pthread_exit(); the joining thread receives it through pthread_join(). struct args { int lo, hi; long sum; }; void *worker(void *p) { struct args *a = (struct args *)p; /* cast back */ a->sum = 0; for (int i = a->lo; i <= a->hi; i++) a->sum += i; return a; /* = pthread_exit(a) */ } /* in main: */ struct args a = {1, 100, 0}; void *res", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-22 passing &i to threads bug", "u": "answers.html#X4-22", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is wrong with this code? What can it print, and how would you fix it? [4.20, 4.33] void *show(void *arg) { printf(\"%d\\n\", *(int *)arg); return NULL; } int main() { pthread_t t[3]; int i, j; for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) pthread_create(&t[i], NULL, show, &i); for (j = 0; j < 3; j++) pthread_join(t[j], NULL); } All three threads get the address of the same variable i, which main keeps changing. This is a race condition, so the output is unpredictable. Thread k reads i only when it gets scheduled, so it prints some value from k to 3, in any order: 0 1 2, 1 2 3, 2 3 3 and 3 3 3 are all possible. Fi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-23 pthread_exit vs return vs exit", "u": "answers.html#X4-23", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In a multithreaded program, what is the difference between a thread calling pthread_exit(), returning from its start routine, and calling exit()? What happens when main() returns? [4.20, 4.33] pthread_exit(v) or return v from the start routine terminates only the calling thread; v goes to the thread that calls pthread_join() on it. The other threads keep running. exit() called by any thread terminates the entire process, i.e., all of its threads. Returning from main() is the same as calling exit(). So main() should pthread_join() its workers first, or end with pthread_exit(), which ends only t", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-24 Pthreads trace, possible outputs", "u": "answers.html#X4-24", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "f(arg) prints the string arg. How many different outputs are possible? List them. [4.20, 4.33] printf(\"M1\\n\"); pthread_create(&t1, NULL, f, \"A\"); pthread_create(&t2, NULL, f, \"B\"); printf(\"M2\\n\"); pthread_join(t1, NULL); pthread_join(t2, NULL); printf(\"M3\\n\"); 6 possible outputs. M1 is always first (no thread exists yet) and M3 is always last (both joins return only after A and B are printed). A, B and M2 can appear in any order, because a created thread is only placed in the ready queue and runs independently, depending on scheduling: 3! = 6. M1, then one of {M2 A B, M2 B A, A M2 B, B M2 A, A", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-25 compare four multithreading models", "u": "answers.html#X4-25", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare the many-to-one, one-to-one, many-to-many and two-level models. [4.21–4.26] They differ in how user threads are mapped to kernel threads, which decides blocking behaviour and parallelism: Model Mapping One thread makes a blocking system call Parallel on multicore? Main drawback Examples Many-to-one many user threads → one kernel thread entire process blocks No: only one kernel thread can be active at a time no parallelism; blocking Solaris Green Threads, GNU Portable Threads One-to-one each user thread → its own kernel thread others keep running Yes creating a user thread requires crea", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-26 blocking system call, many-to-one vs one-to-one", "u": "answers.html#X4-26", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A process has 4 user threads on a 4-core machine. Thread T1 calls read() on a slow disk. What happens to T2–T4 under (a) the many-to-one model, (b) the one-to-one model? [4.22–4.23] (a) Many-to-one: T2–T4 are blocked too, because the entire process is blocked. All four user threads are mapped to one kernel thread; T1's blocking system call blocks that kernel thread, and the OS has no other kernel thread of this process to schedule. Even before the call, only one thread can run at a time, so 3 cores are idle for this process (concurrency, no parallelism). (b) One-to-one: T2–T4 keep running. Eac", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-27 why most OSes use one-to-one", "u": "answers.html#X4-27", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "The one-to-one model has more overhead than many-to-one, yet most operating systems (Windows, Linux) use it. Why? [4.23, 4.26] It gives maximum concurrency and true parallelism, and its cost (a limit on the number of kernel threads) matters less as the number of cores grows. A blocking thread does not block the others, and multiple threads run in parallel on multiprocessor or multicore systems. Drawback: creating a user-level thread mandates creating a corresponding kernel thread; kernel threads consume resources (memory, I/O, etc.), so the number of threads per process may be restricted. But ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-28 many-to-many and two-level models", "u": "answers.html#X4-28", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain the many-to-many and two-level models. What do they offer over one-to-one, and why is many-to-many rarely used? [4.24–4.26] Many-to-many multiplexes many user-level threads onto a smaller or equal number of kernel threads, specific to a particular application or machine: an application may be allocated more kernel threads on an 8-core system than on a 4-core system. The thread library decides which user thread runs on which kernel thread (process-contention scope, PCS). It offers: programmers can create as many user threads as needed, not restricted by the number of kernel threads; the", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-29 LWP, scheduler activations, upcalls", "u": "answers.html#X4-29", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a lightweight process (LWP)? How do the kernel and the thread library cooperate under the many-to-many model? An LWP is an intermediate data structure between user and kernel threads. To the user-thread library, an LWP appears to be a virtual processor on which the application can schedule a user thread to run (many-to-one / many-to-many mapping; this choice is PCS). Each LWP is attached to a kernel thread, and it is the kernel threads that the OS schedules on the CPUs. If a kernel thread blocks (e.g., waiting for I/O), the LWP blocks as well, and so does the user thread running on it.", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-30 thread pool benefits", "u": "answers.html#X4-30", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a thread pool? Why is serving requests from a thread pool better than creating a new thread for each request? [4.24] A thread pool is a set of threads created in advance (at start-up) that wait in a pool for work. A request is handed to an idle pool thread, which returns to the pool when done instead of terminating. (The many-to-many model likewise lets the OS create a sufficient number of kernel threads in advance.) Faster: an existing thread services the request at once, with no thread-creation delay per request. Bounded resources: the pool limits the number of threads that exist at ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-31 fork() and exec() in multithreaded process", "u": "answers.html#X4-31", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "If one thread of a multithreaded process calls fork(), should the child duplicate all threads or only the calling thread? What happens when a thread calls exec()? [4.28] It depends on whether the child calls exec(); some UNIX systems provide two versions of fork() for this reason. If exec() is called immediately after forking, duplicate only the calling thread. Duplicating all threads is unnecessary, as the program specified in exec() will replace the entire process. If the child does not call exec(), duplicate all threads, so the child is a full copy of the parent. (Linux/POSIX fork() duplica", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-32 fork + pthread_create loop with join", "u": "answers.html#X4-32", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How many processes are there in total, how many threads are created by pthread_create(), and how many times are T and M printed? (Assume each printf is output immediately.) [4.28] void *work(void *arg) { printf(\"T\\n\"); return NULL; } int main() { pthread_t tid; for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) { fork(); pthread_create(&tid, NULL, work, NULL); pthread_join(tid, NULL); } printf(\"M\\n\"); } 4 processes and 6 threads created; T is printed 6 times and M 4 times. i = 0: fork gives 2 processes; each creates and joins one thread: 2 threads, 2 × T. i = 1: each process forks, giving 4 processes; each creates a", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-33 fork + pthread_create in a branch", "u": "answers.html#X4-33", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Assume fork() duplicates only the calling thread (Linux). How many processes (including the original) are there, and how many threads are created by pthread_create()? [4.28] pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid > 0) { pthread_create(&t1, NULL, work, NULL); fork(); } pthread_create(&t2, NULL, work, NULL); 3 processes and 4 threads created. First fork: P0 (parent, pid > 0) and P1 (child, pid == 0). Only P0 enters the if: it creates thread t1, then forks P2. P2 gets only a copy of the calling (main) thread, not t1. P2 continues after that fork(), so it also reaches the last line. P0, P1 and P2 each run th", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-34 thread shares, fork copies global", "u": "answers.html#X4-34", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is printed? [4.6] int count = 0; void *inc(void *arg) { count++; return NULL; } int main() { pthread_t t; pthread_create(&t, NULL, inc, NULL); pthread_join(t, NULL); if (fork() == 0) { pthread_create(&t, NULL, inc, NULL); pthread_join(t, NULL); printf(\"child: %d\\n\", count); return 0; } wait(NULL); printf(\"parent: %d\\n\", count); } child: 2, then parent: 1. The first thread shares the process's global data, so its count++ updates main's count to 1. fork() gives the child its own copy of the address space (count = 1); the child's thread increments the child's copy to 2. The parent's copy sta", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-35 fork called inside a thread", "u": "answers.html#X4-35", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Assume fork() duplicates only the calling thread (Linux). How many processes are there, and how many times are A and B printed? [4.28] void *work(void *arg) { fork(); printf(\"A\\n\"); return NULL; } int main() { pthread_t t; pthread_create(&t, NULL, work, NULL); pthread_join(t, NULL); printf(\"B\\n\"); } 2 processes; A is printed 2 times and B once. The worker thread calls fork(), so the child process contains only a copy of the calling (worker) thread, not main. Parent: the worker prints A; main's pthread_join() returns and main prints B. Child: its only thread prints A and returns from work. As t", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-36 fork + pthread_create loop, formula", "u": "answers.html#X4-36", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Assume fork() duplicates only the calling thread. For n = 3, how many processes exist after the loop, and how many threads are created by pthread_create()? Give formulas in n. [4.28] for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { fork(); pthread_create(&tid[i], NULL, work, NULL); } n = 3: 8 processes and 14 threads created. In general: 2n processes and 2+4+⋯+2n=2n+1−2 threads. In each iteration, every process's main thread forks (2, 4, 8 processes), then each process creates one thread (+2, +4, +8 = 14). A child receives only a copy of the calling main thread, not the worker threads already running in its pare", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-37 what is a signal, handling steps", "u": "answers.html#X4-37", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a signal? Describe how a signal is handled. Can a program change how a signal is handled? [4.29–4.30] A signal is used in UNIX systems to notify a process that a particular event has occurred. A signal handler processes it in this pattern: The signal is generated by a particular event (e.g., division by 0, Ctrl-C, a timer expiring, process termination). The signal is delivered to a process (or thread). The signal is handled by one of two signal handlers: default or user-defined. Every signal has a default handler that the kernel uses: some signals are ignored, while others (e.g., an il", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-38 synchronous vs asynchronous signals", "u": "answers.html#X4-38", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish synchronous and asynchronous signals, with two examples of each. Why is the first kind called synchronous? [4.29] Synchronous signals are caused by an operation of the running process itself, e.g., an illegal memory access (segmentation fault) or a division by 0. They are delivered to the same process that performed the operation that caused the signal, which is why they are called synchronous. Asynchronous signals are generated by an event external to the running process, e.g., terminating a process with Ctrl-C, a timer expiring, or another process calling kill(). A signal may be ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-39 signal delivery in multithreaded process", "u": "answers.html#X4-39", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Where should a signal be delivered in a multithreaded process? A process has threads T1–T3; T2 divides by zero, and later the user presses Ctrl-C. Where should each signal go? [4.30–4.31] The delivery method depends on the type of signal. There are four options: Deliver the signal to the thread to which the signal applies. Deliver the signal to every thread in the process. Deliver the signal to certain threads in the process. Assign a specific thread to receive all signals for the process. T2's division by zero is synchronous, so it is delivered to the thread causing the signal (T2) and not to", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-40 asynchronous vs deferred cancellation", "u": "answers.html#X4-40", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is thread cancellation? Compare asynchronous and deferred cancellation. Which is preferred, and why? [4.32] Thread cancellation means terminating a thread before it has finished; the thread to be cancelled is the target thread. Asynchronous cancellation terminates the target thread immediately, possibly in the middle of a critical update to shared data, which leaves the data inconsistent; the OS may not be able to reclaim all resources (memory, locks, files). Deferred cancellation: the target thread periodically checks whether it should terminate, allowing it an opportunity to terminate i", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-41 pthread_cancel only requests cancellation", "u": "answers.html#X4-41", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Thread A calls pthread_cancel(tid) and then pthread_join(tid, NULL). Is the target terminated immediately? What if it is in a long computation loop with no function calls, or has cancellation disabled? [4.32–4.33] No. Invoking pthread_cancel() only requests cancellation; the actual cancellation depends on the target thread's state and type. The default type is deferred, so the request stays pending until the target reaches a cancellation point. A pure computation loop has none, so the thread is not cancelled until it calls, e.g., pthread_testcancel() or a blocking call that is a cancellation p", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-42 thread-local storage", "u": "answers.html#X4-42", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is thread-local storage (TLS)? How does it differ from local variables and from global data? Give an example. [4.36] Thread-local storage is data that is unique to each thread: every thread has its own copy. vs global / static data: globals are shared by all threads of the process, while TLS gives each thread a private copy that, like static data, persists across function calls. vs local variables: locals live on the stack and exist only during a single function invocation, while TLS is visible across all function invocations of that thread. Example: each thread's own errno, or a unique t", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-43 Windows thread components, ETHREAD KTHREAD TEB", "u": "answers.html#X4-43", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "List the components of a Windows thread and its primary data structures. Where does each data structure reside? [4.35–4.36] Windows uses the one-to-one mapping: each user-level thread maps to an associated kernel thread. A thread has: a thread ID, a register set (processor state), a program counter, separate user and kernel stacks, and a private data storage area used by run-time libraries and DLLs. The register set, stacks and private storage area are known as the context of the thread. ETHREAD (executive thread block): pointer to the process the thread belongs to and to its KTHREAD; in kerne", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-44 Linux tasks, task_struct vs Windows", "u": "answers.html#X4-44", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does Linux represent threads? Contrast Linux with Windows for a process that has two threads. [4.37] Linux does not differentiate between threads and processes: each is a task. A thread is a process that may share certain resources (e.g., the address space) with other tasks; each thread has its own unique task_struct and appears as a normal process. Windows (explicit kernel support for threads): one PCB describes the shared resources (address space, open files) and points to the two threads' TCBs, each describing the resources that thread alone possesses. Linux: simply two tasks with norma", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-45 clone() flags vs fork()", "u": "answers.html#X4-45", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does Linux create a thread with clone()? Explain the flags and how clone() differs from fork(). [4.38] Threads are created like normal tasks, except that clone() is passed flags naming the resources the child task shares with its parent: clone(CLONE_VM | CLONE_FS | CLONE_FILES | CLONE_SIGHAND, 0); CLONE_VM: share the address space; CLONE_FS: share file-system information (e.g., current directory); CLONE_FILES: share the open files (file descriptors); CLONE_SIGHAND: share the signal handlers. With these flags, the child task behaves like a thread. Instead of copying all data structures like", "w": 2}, {"t": "X4-TF True / False quick verdicts", "u": "answers.html#X4-TF", "p": "Answer bank", "x": " Statement T/F Why (≤ 12 words) Threads of a process share the heap, but each thread has its own stack. T Heap and globals shared; stack, PC, registers private. Each thread has its own set of open file descriptors. F Open files are shared by all threads of the process. The process identifier (PID) is stored in each thread's TCB. F PID is in the PCB; TCB points to the PCB. Each thread has its own state (ready/running/waiting) kept in its TCB. T TCBs are organized into queues by state. Most operating-system kernels are multithreaded. T E.g., Linux boot creates kernel threads for devices, memory.", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-01 CPU–I/O burst cycle and burst distribution", "u": "answers.html#X5-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the CPU–I/O burst cycle. What does the histogram of CPU-burst times show, and why does it matter for scheduling? Process execution consists of a cycle of CPU execution and I/O wait — a CPU burst followed by an I/O burst, alternating until the process ends. While one process waits for I/O, the CPU scheduler gives the CPU to another ready process, so some process is running at all times → maximize CPU utilization (the goal of multiprogramming). Measured CPU bursts follow a long-tail distribution: many short CPU bursts and a small number of long ones. An I/O-bound program has many short ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-02 Distinguish I/O-bound from CPU-bound processes", "u": "answers.html#X5-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is it important for the scheduler to distinguish I/O-bound processes from CPU-bound processes? Because they use the CPU very differently: an I/O-bound process runs only short CPU bursts and seldom uses its full quantum, while a CPU-bound process runs long bursts and uses its whole quantum. Giving I/O-bound processes higher priority lets them start their next I/O quickly, keeping I/O devices busy and giving short response time to interactive jobs; the CPU-bound process uses the CPU while they wait → better CPU and device utilization. Treating them alike (e.g., FCFS) makes I/O-bound jobs wai", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-03 Four scheduling decision points, which preemptive", "u": "answers.html#X5-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "List the four situations in which CPU-scheduling decisions take place. Which of them are preemptive? CPU scheduling may take place when a process: switches from running to waiting (e.g., I/O request, wait()); terminates; switches from running to ready (e.g., an interrupt — timer / quantum expiry); switches from waiting to ready (e.g., I/O completion), or a new process arrives in the ready queue (new → ready) with a higher priority. Cases 1 and 2 are non-preemptive — the process gives up the CPU voluntarily and a new process must be selected. Cases 3 and 4 are preemptive — the CPU can be taken ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-04 Preemptive vs non-preemptive scheduling issues", "u": "answers.html#X5-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish preemptive and non-preemptive scheduling. What problems does preemption introduce? Non-preemptive: once a process gets the CPU it keeps it until it terminates or blocks (releases it voluntarily). Simple, low overhead, but a long process can monopolize the CPU (poor response time, convoy effect). E.g., FCFS, SJF. Preemptive: the OS can take the CPU away on a timer interrupt (quantum expiry) or when a higher-priority / shorter process becomes ready. Better response time, no monopolization. E.g., RR, SRTF, preemptive priority, MLFQ. Problems of preemption: (1) more context switches (o", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-05 Dispatcher and dispatch latency", "u": "answers.html#X5-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is the dispatcher and what does it do? What is dispatch latency, and why should it be small? The dispatcher is the module that gives control of the CPU to the process selected by the CPU scheduler. It involves: (1) switching context from one process to another; (2) switching to user mode; (3) jumping to the proper location in the user program to restart it. Dispatch latency is the time it takes for the dispatcher to stop one process and start another running. It is pure overhead paid on every context switch (typically hundreds per second, visible with vmstat on Linux), so the dispatcher m", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-06 Scheduling criteria to maximize or minimize", "u": "answers.html#X5-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define the CPU-scheduling criteria and state whether each should be maximized or minimized. Criterion Definition Goal CPU utilization fraction of time the CPU is busy maximize Throughput number of processes completed per unit time maximize Turnaround time time to execute a process = completion − arrival (waiting + CPU + I/O time) minimize Waiting time total time spent waiting in the ready queue = turnaround − CPU burst minimize Response time time from submission until the first response is produced (this course: end of first run − arrival) minimize Fairness adequate CPU allocation to every pro", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-07 Response time definition and computation", "u": "answers.html#X5-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define response time. How is it computed in this course for RR and for non-preemptive algorithms, and why is it important? Response time is the amount of time from when a request is submitted until the first response is produced; in this course RT = completion of the first run on the CPU − arrival time. RR / MLFQ: the first run ends at the end of the first quantum (or at completion if the burst < q). E.g., RR q = 4 with P1 24, P2 3, P3 3 (all at 0): RT = 4, 7, 10 → avg 7 (FCFS: 24, 27, 30 → avg 27). Non-preemptive (FCFS, SJF, non-preemptive priority): the first run is the whole burst, so RT = ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-08 Conflicting scheduling criteria", "u": "answers.html#X5-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain how these pairs of criteria conflict: CPU utilization vs response time; average turnaround vs maximum waiting time; I/O device utilization vs CPU utilization. Scheduling criteria conflict, so an algorithm improves some at the expense of others: CPU utilization vs response time: utilization is highest when context switches are rare (long quanta, little overhead), but short response time needs frequent switching (short quanta), which adds overhead. Average turnaround vs maximum waiting time: average turnaround is minimized by running the shortest jobs first (SJF), but long jobs may then ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-09 FCFS and the convoy effect", "u": "answers.html#X5-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe FCFS scheduling. What is the convoy effect, and why is FCFS unsuitable for interactive systems? FCFS serves processes in their order of arrival to the ready queue (FIFO). It is non-preemptive: a process keeps the CPU until it completes its current CPU burst. It is simple and starvation-free. Convoy effect: short processes get stuck behind a long process, so the average waiting time is long and depends purely on the arrival order — bursts 24, 3, 3: order P1, P2, P3 → avg WT (0 + 24 + 27)/3 = 17; order P2, P3, P1 → (6 + 0 + 3)/3 = 3. With one CPU-bound and many I/O-bound processes, the ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-10 Round-robin mechanism and waiting bound", "u": "answers.html#X5-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain how Round-Robin scheduling works. What does it guarantee with n processes and time quantum q? RR is FCFS with preemption: each process gets a small unit of CPU time, the time quantum q (usually 10–100 ms). When the quantum expires, a timer interrupt preempts the process and it is added to the tail of the ready queue; if its (remaining) burst is shorter than q, it releases the CPU itself and the next process runs at once. With n ready processes, each gets 1/n of the CPU time in chunks of at most q, and no process waits more than (n − 1)q time units for its next turn (context-switch time", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-11 Choosing q from the waiting bound", "u": "answers.html#X5-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Ten interactive processes share a CPU under RR, and each must wait at most 90 ms in the ready queue before running again. Find the largest quantum (a) ignoring context switches, (b) if every context switch costs 1 ms. (a) RR bound: no process waits more than (n − 1)q → (10−1)q≤90 → q ≤ 10 ms. (b) Between two turns of a process, the other n − 1 processes each run one quantum and n context switches occur → worst wait = (n−1)q+ns: 9q+10×1≤90 → q≤80/9 → q ≤ 8.9 ms. Trade-off: a smaller q gives a tighter bound (better response) but more switching overhead (efficiency q/(q + s)). ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-12 Time quantum size effects, 80% rule", "u": "answers.html#X5-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How does the size of the time quantum affect RR performance? State the rule of thumb for choosing it. q too large (larger than every CPU burst) → each burst finishes in its first quantum → RR degenerates to FCFS. q too small → too many context switches; q must be large with respect to the context-switch time (usually < 10 µs), otherwise the overhead is too high. In the limit (tiny q, zero switch cost) RR becomes processor sharing: each of n processes appears to have its own processor running at 1/n the speed. Average turnaround time does not necessarily improve as q increases; it improves when", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-13 RR CPU efficiency with context-switch overhead", "u": "answers.html#X5-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "(a) RR with context-switch cost s = 0.1 ms: CPU efficiency for q = 10, 1 and 0.1 ms when all processes are CPU-bound? (b) Ten I/O-bound tasks (each computes 1 ms, then does a 10 ms I/O) and one CPU-bound task, s = 0.1 ms: CPU utilization for q = 1 ms and q = 10 ms? (a) Each quantum of useful work q is followed by one switch s → efficiency = q/(q + s): q = 10 ms: 10/10.1 = 99.0%; q = 1 ms: 1/1.1 = 90.9%; q = 0.1 ms: 0.1/0.2 = 50% → a quantum close to the switch time wastes the CPU. (A process that blocks after a burst b < q gives b/(b + s).) (b) Each 10 ms I/O finishes before the task's next tu", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-14 Utilization and throughput with switch cost", "u": "answers.html#X5-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "P1 (10 ms), P2 (4 ms), P3 (6 ms) arrive at time 0; RR with q = 4 ms; each context switch takes 1 ms. Draw the Gantt chart with the switches; find CPU utilization, throughput and average turnaround time, and compare with FCFS. Assume no switch before the first process and a 1 ms switch (CS) whenever the CPU moves to a different process. RR: 0 P1 4 CS 5 P2 9 CS 10 P3 14 CS 15 P1 19 CS 20 P3 22 CS 23 P1 25 → 5 switches; 20 ms of useful work in 25 ms. CPU utilization = 20/25 = 80%; throughput = 3 processes / 25 ms = 0.12 per ms (120 per second); completions P1 25, P2 9, P3 22 → avg TAT = (25 + 9 +", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-15 RR versus FCFS with equal jobs", "u": "answers.html#X5-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Is RR always better than FCFS? Four jobs, each needing 6 ms, arrive at time 0. Compare FCFS and RR (q = 2) in average turnaround, waiting and response time. No. FCFS: 0 P1 6 P2 12 P3 18 P4 24 → TAT 6, 12, 18, 24 → avg 15; WT 0, 6, 12, 18 → avg 9; RT = TAT → avg 15. RR (q = 2): 0 P1 2 P2 4 P3 6 P4 8 P1 10 P2 12 P3 14 P4 16 P1 18 P2 20 P3 22 P4 24 → TAT 18, 20, 22, 24 → avg 21; WT 12, 14, 16, 18 → avg 15; RT 2, 4, 6, 8 → avg 5. When all jobs have the same length, longer than q, RR has worse turnaround and waiting time — the jobs are interleaved and all finish near the end, while FCFS finishes th", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-16 SJF optimality and proof sketch", "u": "answers.html#X5-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe SJF scheduling and explain why it is optimal. SJF (shortest-next-CPU-burst) associates each process with the length of its next CPU burst and runs the process with the shortest next burst (ties → FCFS). The idea: get short jobs out of the system sooner — a big gain for short jobs, a small cost for long ones. SJF is optimal: it gives the minimum average waiting time for a given set of processes. Proof sketch: moving a short process before a long one decreases the waiting time of the short process more than it increases the waiting time of the long process. Swapping an adjacent pair (lo", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-17 SJF implementation difficulty and approximation", "u": "answers.html#X5-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is SJF difficult to implement in practice? How can it be approximated, and what other drawbacks does it have? The difficulty is knowing the length of the next CPU request — the scheduler cannot see the future, so SJF/SRTF are hard to implement and are mainly used as a benchmark for practical algorithms. Approximation: predict the next CPU burst from the lengths of previous bursts using exponential averaging, τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)τn​, then pick the process with the shortest predicted next burst (a wrong prediction gives a sub-optimal schedule). SJF can be applied to an entire program (e.g., a ru", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-18 Exponential averaging formula and alpha", "u": "answers.html#X5-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain the exponential averaging formula used to predict the next CPU burst. What happens when α = 0 and when α = 1? τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)τn​, 0≤α≤1: tn​ = actual length of the n-th CPU burst (most recent information), τn​ = the previous prediction (past history), α = the relative weight of recent vs past history (commonly α = 1/2). α = 0 → τn+1​=τn​: recent history does not count. α = 1 → τn+1​=tn​: only the actual last CPU burst counts. Expanded: τn+1​=αtn​+(1−α)αtn−1​+⋯+(1−α)jαtn−j​+⋯+(1−α)n+1τ0​ → the prediction depends on all past bursts (with different weights) and the initial estimate τ0​; ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-19 Exponential averaging calculation, alpha 0.5", "u": "answers.html#X5-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "With α = 0.5 and τ0 = 12 ms, the next four actual CPU bursts are 8, 6, 10 and 4 ms. Predict τ1 to τ4. τn+1​=0.5tn​+0.5τn​: τ1 = 0.5(8) + 0.5(12) = 4 + 6 = 10 ms τ2 = 0.5(6) + 0.5(10) = 3 + 5 = 8 ms τ3 = 0.5(10) + 0.5(8) = 5 + 4 = 9 ms τ4 = 0.5(4) + 0.5(9) = 2 + 4.5 = 6.5 ms Check with the expanded form (weights 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16 on bursts 4, 10, 6, 8 and 1/16 on τ0 = 12): τ4 = 2 + 2.5 + 0.75 + 0.5 + 0.75 = 6.5 ms. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-20 Exponential averaging: alpha 0.25, solve alpha", "u": "answers.html#X5-20", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "(a) α = 0.25, τ0 = 16 ms, actual bursts 8, 2, 15 ms: find τ1, τ2, τ3. (b) A prediction of 10 ms is followed by an actual burst of 4 ms and the new prediction is 8.5 ms: find α. (c) Using the data of (a), what is τ3 when α = 0 and when α = 1? (a) τn+1​=0.25tn​+0.75τn​: τ1 = 2 + 12 = 14 ms; τ2 = 0.5 + 10.5 = 11 ms; τ3 = 3.75 + 8.25 = 12 ms. A small α reacts slowly — the 15 ms burst raises the guess only from 11 to 12. (b) 8.5=4α+10(1−α)=10−6α → α=1.5/6= 0.25. (c) α = 0: history is ignored → τ3 = τ0 = 16 ms. α = 1: only the last burst counts → τ3 = t2 = 15 ms. ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-21 SRTF definition and comparison with SJF", "u": "answers.html#X5-21", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe SRTF scheduling. When must the scheduler be invoked, and how does SRTF compare with SJF? SRTF (shortest-remaining-time-first) is preemptive SJF: when a process joins the ready queue (new → ready, or waiting → ready after I/O) with a CPU burst shorter than the remaining time of the running process, it preempts the running process (equal remaining time → no preemption). Hence the scheduler must run at every arrival to the ready queue (scheduling condition 4), not only at completions. SRTF is optimal among preemptive algorithms for average waiting time and is always at least as good as S", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-22 FCFS, SJF, SRTF, RR averages calculation", "u": "answers.html#X5-22", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "P1 (arrival 0, burst 6), P2 (2, 5), P3 (3, 2), P4 (5, 4). Draw Gantt charts for FCFS, non-preemptive SJF, SRTF and RR (q = 2); find the average waiting, turnaround and response times. Algorithm Gantt chart WT P1–P4 → avg TAT P1–P4 → avg RT P1–P4 → avg FCFS 0 P1 6 P2 11 P3 13 P4 17 0, 4, 8, 8 → 5 6, 9, 10, 12 → 9.25 = TAT → 9.25 SJF 0 P1 6 P3 8 P4 12 P2 17 0, 10, 3, 3 → 4 6, 15, 5, 7 → 8.25 = TAT → 8.25 SRTF 0 P1 3 P3 5 P1 8 P4 12 P2 17 2, 10, 0, 3 → 3.75 8, 15, 2, 7 → 8 3, 15, 2, 7 → 6.75 RR q = 2 0 P1 2 P2 4 P1 6 P3 8 P2 10 P4 12 P1 14 P2 15 P4 17 8, 8, 3, 8 → 6.75 14, 13, 5, 12 → 11 2, 2, 5,", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-23 Priority scheduling and its relation to SJF", "u": "answers.html#X5-23", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe priority scheduling (preemptive and non-preemptive). How is SJF related to it? Each process has a priority number; the CPU is allocated to the process with the highest priority (smallest integer = highest priority unless stated otherwise); equal-priority processes are scheduled in FCFS order (or RR). Preemptive: a newly arrived process with a higher priority preempts the running process. Non-preemptive: a higher-priority arrival is put at the head of the ready queue and waits until the running process finishes its burst. SJF is a special case of priority scheduling where the priority ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-24 Starvation and aging", "u": "answers.html#X5-24", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is starvation? Explain how aging prevents it, with a numerical example. Starvation (indefinite blocking): a process is ready to run but waits indefinitely for the CPU, because there are always higher-priority processes (or, in SJF/SRTF, shorter jobs) ahead of it. Aging: gradually increase the priority of a process the longer it waits, so it eventually becomes high enough to run (implemented with a timer for each process in the ready queue). Example: priorities range from 127 (lowest) to 0 (highest); raise a waiting process's priority by 1 every second → even a priority-127 process reaches", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-25 Preemptive vs non-preemptive priority calculation", "u": "answers.html#X5-25", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "P1 (arrival 0, burst 5, priority 3), P2 (1, 3, 1), P3 (2, 4, 4), P4 (3, 2, 2); smaller number = higher priority. Draw Gantt charts for preemptive and non-preemptive priority scheduling; find the average waiting and turnaround times. Version Gantt chart WT P1–P4 → avg TAT P1–P4 → avg RT P1–P4 → avg Preemptive 0 P1 1 P2 4 P4 6 P1 10 P3 14 5, 0, 8, 1 → 14/4 = 3.5 10, 3, 12, 3 → 28/4 = 7 1, 3, 12, 3 → 19/4 = 4.75 Non-preemptive 0 P1 5 P2 8 P4 10 P3 14 0, 4, 8, 5 → 17/4 = 4.25 5, 7, 12, 7 → 31/4 = 7.75 = TAT → 7.75 Preemptive: at t = 1, P2 (priority 1) preempts P1 (3); at t = 3, P4 (2) cannot preem", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-26 Priority with RR, remaining quantum rule", "u": "answers.html#X5-26", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Preemptive priority scheduling with RR (q = 2) among equal priorities, smaller number = higher priority: P1 (arrival 0, burst 5, priority 2), P2 (0, 4, 2), P3 (3, 2, 1), P4 (4, 3, 2). Draw the Gantt chart; find the average waiting time. Rule (Lab 5, S23 official answer): a process preempted by a higher-priority arrival returns to the head of its priority level and keeps its remaining quantum; a process whose quantum expires goes to the tail. Gantt: 0 P1 2 P2 3 P3 5 P2 6 P1 8 P4 10 P2 12 P1 13 P4 14 t = 2: P1's quantum expires → [P2, P1]. t = 3: P3 (priority 1) preempts P2 after 1 unit → P2 goe", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-27 Dynamic priority alpha beta gives FCFS or LIFO", "u": "answers.html#X5-27", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A preemptive scheduler uses dynamic priorities (larger number = higher): a waiting process's priority changes at rate α, a running process's at rate β, and every process enters the ready queue with priority 0. Which algorithm results from (a) β > α > 0, (b) α < β < 0? (a) β > α > 0 → FCFS. Every priority rises from 0, so a new process (priority 0) is lower than every process already in the system and never preempts; the running process rises fastest (β > α), so it is never preempted; among the waiting processes, the one that has waited longest has the highest priority → processes run in arriva", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-28 Least-recent-CPU policy favours I/O-bound", "u": "answers.html#X5-28", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A scheduler favours the processes that have used the least processor time in the recent past. Why does it favour I/O-bound processes, yet not permanently starve CPU-bound processes? It favours I/O-bound processes because they run only short CPU bursts before blocking for I/O, so their recent CPU usage is small and they get high priority. CPU-bound processes do not starve permanently because the I/O-bound processes give up the CPU frequently to do their I/O, and the CPU-bound process runs in those gaps. Also, usage is measured only over the recent past: while a CPU-bound process waits, its rece", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-29 Multilevel queue scheduling", "u": "answers.html#X5-29", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe multilevel queue scheduling. How is the CPU shared among the queues? The ready queue is partitioned into separate queues based on process type (e.g., real-time, system, interactive, batch). The priority is assigned statically: a process remains in the same queue for its whole runtime. Each queue can have its own scheduling algorithm (e.g., RR for interactive/foreground, FCFS for batch/background). Scheduling among the queues: (1) fixed-priority preemptive scheduling — a lower queue runs only when all higher queues are empty, and an arrival in a higher queue preempts it → low queues ca", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-30 Multilevel queue versus MLFQ", "u": "answers.html#X5-30", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare multilevel queue scheduling with multilevel feedback queue (MLFQ) scheduling. Aspect Multilevel queue MLFQ Queue membership permanent (by process type) process can move between queues Priority static dynamic, based on observed CPU usage Movement rules none demote after using a full quantum; upgrade by aging Starvation possible for low queues (fixed priority) possible, but handled by aging / periodic reshuffling Needs burst knowledge no (classified by type) no (learns from behaviour) Flexibility / cost inflexible, low overhead flexible, but the most complex (many parameters) MLFQ theref", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-31 MLFQ defining parameters", "u": "answers.html#X5-31", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What parameters define a multilevel feedback queue scheduler? An MLFQ scheduler is defined by: the number of queues; the scheduling algorithm for each queue (e.g., RR with its own quantum per queue, FCFS in the last queue); the method used to determine when to upgrade a process to a higher-priority queue (e.g., aging); the method used to determine when to demote a process to a lower-priority queue (e.g., after it uses a full quantum); the method used to determine which queue a process will enter when it needs service (e.g., every new job enters Q0). Because a process can move between queues, a", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-32 MLFQ rules: demotion, preemption, head of queue", "u": "answers.html#X5-32", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "MLFQ with Q0 (RR, q = 8 ms), Q1 (RR, q = 16 ms), Q2 (FCFS). (a) Where does a new job go, and when is it demoted? (b) A Q1 job that has used 5 ms of its quantum is preempted by a new arrival: where does it go, with how much quantum? (c) Can a Q1 job preempt a Q2 job? (d) Which jobs never reach Q2? (a) A new job enters the tail of Q0 (RR, FCFS order) and preempts a running Q1 or Q2 job. A job that uses its full quantum without finishing is demoted to the tail of the next queue (Q0 → Q1 → Q2). A running Q0 job is never preempted by an arrival (the arrival waits in Q0). (b) It returns to the head ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-33 MLFQ disadvantages and remedies", "u": "answers.html#X5-33", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What are the disadvantages of MLFQ, and how can they be handled? Starvation: with fixed priority among queues, long CPU-bound jobs in the lowest queue may never run if new or short jobs keep arriving → handled by aging / reshuffling jobs among the queues periodically, e.g., after some period, move all jobs to the top queue. Complexity: it is the most general but most complex scheduler — the number of queues, each queue's quantum and algorithm, and the upgrade/demote/entry rules must all be chosen and tuned for the workload. Changing behaviour: a job demoted during a CPU-bound phase stays at lo", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-34 MLFQ Gantt calculation, remaining quantum", "u": "answers.html#X5-34", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "MLFQ: Q0 RR (q = 4), Q1 RR (q = 8), Q2 FCFS; new jobs enter Q0 and preempt Q1/Q2; a preempted job returns to the head of its queue with its remaining quantum. P1 (arrival 0, burst 14), P2 (3, 5), P3 (9, 3), P4 (15, 2). Draw the Gantt chart; find the average waiting, turnaround and response times. Gantt: 0 P1 4 P2 8 P1 9 P3 12 P1 15 P4 17 P1 21 P2 22 P1 24 Queues: P1@Q0 0–4, P2@Q0 4–8, P1@Q1 8–9, P3@Q0 9–12, P1@Q1 12–15, P4@Q0 15–17, P1@Q1 17–21, P2@Q1 21–22, P1@Q2 22–24. t = 4: P1 used its Q0 quantum → Q1. t = 8: P2 → Q1 behind P1; P1 runs from Q1. t = 9: P3 preempts P1 (1 of 8 used) → P1 to t", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-35 Algorithm property comparison table", "u": "answers.html#X5-35", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare FCFS, SJF, SRTF, RR, priority, multilevel queue and MLFQ: preemptive? need the next-burst length? can starve? main property. Algorithm Preemptive? Needs next-burst length? Starvation? Key property FCFS No (must be non-preemptive) No No simplest; convoy effect SJF No Yes (predicted) Yes (long jobs) minimum avg WT among non-preemptive SRTF Yes (on arrival) Yes (predicted) Yes (long jobs) minimum avg WT (preemptive) RR Yes (timer) No No fair, good response; depends on q Priority Either No Yes → aging SJF is a special case Multilevel queue Yes (fixed priority among queues) No Yes (low queu", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-36 Which algorithm: quick judgement answers", "u": "answers.html#X5-36", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Which scheduling algorithm (a) minimizes average waiting time, (b) suits interactive systems best, (c) is free from starvation, (d) is hard to implement, (e) must be non-preemptive, (f) is used in BSD Unix, Solaris and Windows? Question Answer Reason (a) Minimum average waiting (and turnaround) time SJF (non-preemptive), SRTF (preemptive) provably optimal: short jobs first (b) Best for interactive systems / response time RR or MLFQ short quanta; MLFQ keeps interactive jobs in the top queues (c) Free from starvation FCFS and RR every process reaches the head of the queue (d) Difficult to implem", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-37 Equivalences between scheduling algorithms", "u": "answers.html#X5-37", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "State the relation between: (a) RR and FCFS; (b) SJF and priority; (c) priority and FCFS; (d) MLFQ and FCFS; (e) SJF/SRTF and FCFS; (f) RR and SJF. (a) RR with a quantum larger than every CPU burst = FCFS; RR with a very small quantum ≈ processor sharing. (b) SJF = priority scheduling with priority = inverse of the predicted next CPU burst; SRTF = preemptive priority on the remaining time. (c) FCFS = priority scheduling with priority = arrival time (the job that has been in the system longest has the highest priority); priority scheduling with all-equal priorities is FCFS. (d) The lowest queue", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-38 How FCFS, RR, MLFQ treat short jobs", "u": "answers.html#X5-38", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain how much FCFS, RR and MLFQ discriminate in favour of short processes. FCFS — discriminates against short jobs: a short job that arrives after long jobs must wait for all of them (convoy effect). RR — treats all jobs equally: each job gets the same quantum in turn, so a short job finishes after only a few quanta and leaves the system sooner, but it gets no extra priority. MLFQ — strongly favours short jobs: short (interactive, I/O-bound) jobs finish in the high-priority top queues, while long CPU-bound jobs are demoted to lower queues — similar to SJF/SRTF without knowing the burst leng", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-39 PCS versus SCS and LWP", "u": "answers.html#X5-39", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish process-contention scope (PCS) from system-contention scope (SCS). What is an LWP? On systems with both thread levels, the OS schedules kernel-level threads; user-level threads are managed by the thread library (the OS is unaware of them). PCS SCS Who schedules thread library kernel (CPU scheduler) What user-level threads onto an available LWP (kernel thread) kernel threads onto a CPU Competition among threads of the same process all threads in the system Models many-to-one, many-to-many all; one-to-one systems (Windows, Linux, Solaris) use only SCS Policy usually priority set by t", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-40 AMP versus SMP", "u": "answers.html#X5-40", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare asymmetric multiprocessing (AMP) and symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) for CPU scheduling. AMP SMP Scheduling all scheduling decisions, I/O processing and system activities are handled by one processor — the master server each processor is self-scheduling Kernel data only the master accesses kernel data structures → no data sharing needed all processors access shared kernel data → must synchronize Other processors execute only user code run both kernel and user code Ready queue kept by the master one common ready queue or a private queue per processor Drawback the master can become a bot", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-41 Common versus per-processor ready queues", "u": "answers.html#X5-41", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In SMP, what race condition can a single common ready queue cause? Compare common and per-processor ready queues in load balancing, processor affinity and locking. With a common queue, two processors may choose to schedule the same thread (or a thread may be lost) unless the queue is locked — a race condition. Common ready queue Per-processor (private) ready queues Load balancing automatic — no CPU is idle while the queue is non-empty needs push / pull migration Processor affinity hard to keep — a thread may run on a different CPU each time (cold cache) natural — affinity \"for free\" (warm cach", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-42 Memory stall and chip multithreading", "u": "answers.html#X5-42", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a memory stall, and how do multithreaded multicore processors (CMT) exploit it? How many logical CPUs does the OS see on a quad-core processor with 2 hardware threads per core? Multicore = multiple processor cores on the same chip: faster and consumes less power, but complicates scheduling. Memory stall: when a processor accesses memory it spends a significant amount of time waiting for the data, because modern processors are much faster than memory, especially on a cache miss. Chip multithreading (CMT; Intel: hyperthreading) gives each core multiple hardware threads, each with its own", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-43 Two levels of multicore thread scheduling", "u": "answers.html#X5-43", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the two levels of scheduling on a multithreaded multicore processor, and the full mapping from user threads to cores. OS level: the operating system decides which software (kernel) thread runs on each logical CPU (hardware thread) — this is the CPU scheduling of this chapter, using any algorithm. Core level: each core decides which of its hardware threads runs on the physical core, e.g., simple round-robin (UltraSPARC T3). Full mapping: user thread → LWP / kernel thread (thread library, PCS) → kernel (software) thread → hardware thread / logical CPU (OS, SCS) → hardware thread → physi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-44 Processor affinity: soft, hard, NUMA", "u": "answers.html#X5-44", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is processor affinity? Distinguish soft and hard affinity, and explain how NUMA affects it. Processor affinity: a thread has affinity for the processor it runs on, because that processor's cache holds the thread's recently accessed memory. Migration means invalidating and repopulating caches (high cost), so most SMP systems try to avoid migrating processes; per-processor ready queues provide affinity for free. Soft affinity: the OS attempts to keep a process on the same processor but does not guarantee it — the process may migrate during load balancing. Hard affinity: a process specifies ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-45 Load balancing: push, pull, affinity conflict", "u": "answers.html#X5-45", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain push and pull migration. Why does load balancing conflict with processor affinity? Load balancing keeps the workload evenly distributed across processors; it is needed when each processor has its own private ready queue (a common queue balances itself). Push migration: a specific task periodically checks the load on each processor and, if it finds an imbalance, pushes tasks from overloaded CPUs to idle or less-busy CPUs. Pull migration: an idle processor pulls a waiting task from a busy processor. They are not mutually exclusive — often both are implemented (e.g., Linux CFS). Conflict:", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-46 Hard versus soft real-time", "u": "answers.html#X5-46", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish hard and soft real-time systems. What must a real-time CPU scheduler support? Hard real-time: stricter — a task must be serviced by its deadline; service after the deadline has expired is the same as no service at all (e.g., airbag or flight control). Soft real-time: no guarantee as to when a critical real-time process will be scheduled; it guarantees only that the real-time process will be given preference over non-critical processes (e.g., video streaming). Real-time scheduling demands performance guarantees — predictability. The scheduler must support a priority-based algorithm ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-47 Event, interrupt and dispatch latency", "u": "answers.html#X5-47", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define event latency, interrupt latency and dispatch latency in a real-time system. (Textbook topic; the slides define only dispatch latency, slide 5.7.) Event latency: the time from when an event occurs to when it is serviced. Two latencies affect real-time performance: Interrupt latency: the time from the arrival of an interrupt at the CPU to the start of the routine that services it — the CPU finishes the current instruction, determines the interrupt type and saves the state of the current process. For hard real-time it must be bounded (interrupts may be disabled only briefly). Dispatch lat", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-48 Periodic tasks, utilization, admission control", "u": "answers.html#X5-48", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the characteristics of a periodic real-time task. What is the CPU utilization of a task set, and what is admission control? A periodic task requires the CPU at constant intervals (periods): processing time t, deadline d, period p, with 0 ≤ t ≤ d ≤ p; its rate is 1/p. One job is released every p time units and must finish by its deadline (often d = p, i.e., by the start of the next period). CPU utilization of task i = ti​/pi​; total U=∑i​ti​/pi​. E.g., (t, p) = (20, 50) and (35, 100): U = 0.40 + 0.35 = 75%. If U > 1, the CPU is overloaded and some deadlines must be missed. Admission co", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-49 Rate-monotonic scheduling and missed deadlines", "u": "answers.html#X5-49", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain rate-monotonic (RM) scheduling. Why can RM miss a deadline even when U < 1? RM schedules periodic tasks using a static priority policy with preemption: priority = inverse of the period — shorter period (higher rate) = higher priority, because tasks that need the CPU more often get higher priority. Priorities never change. RM is optimal among static-priority algorithms: if a task set cannot be scheduled by RM, no other static-priority algorithm can schedule it. It handles only periodic tasks with a constant processing time. Deadlines can be missed with U < 1, because the static priority", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-50 Earliest-deadline-first scheduling", "u": "answers.html#X5-50", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain EDF scheduling and its advantages over RM. Why can it not reach 100% CPU utilization in practice? EDF assigns priorities dynamically according to the deadline: the earlier the (absolute) deadline, the higher the priority; priorities are re-evaluated whenever a job becomes runnable; it is preemptive. EDF does not require processes to be periodic, nor a constant amount of CPU time per burst — the only requirement is that a process announces its deadline when it becomes runnable. Theoretically optimal: it can schedule processes so that each meets its deadline with CPU utilization up to 10", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-51 RM versus EDF comparison", "u": "answers.html#X5-51", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare rate-monotonic and earliest-deadline-first scheduling. Rate-monotonic (RM) Earliest-deadline-first (EDF) Priority static — inverse of period (shorter = higher) dynamic — earlier absolute deadline = higher Task model periodic, constant processing time periodic or not, burst may vary; must announce its deadline Preemption yes yes Optimality optimal among static-priority algorithms theoretically optimal overall Schedulable if (d = p) U≤N(21/N−1) guarantees it (sufficient only) U≤1 (necessary and sufficient) Cost priorities fixed once: simple, predictable priorities recomputed at each rele", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-52 Schedulability test with utilization bound", "u": "answers.html#X5-52", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Using CPU utilization (deadline = period), decide whether each task set (t, p) is schedulable by RM and by EDF: (a) (1, 4), (2, 6), (1, 10); (b) (2, 5), (4, 7); (c) (2, 4), (4, 8); (d) (3, 5), (3, 6). RM bound N(21/N−1): N = 1: 1.000; N = 2: 0.828; N = 3: 0.780; N = 4: 0.757; N = 5: 0.743; large N: 0.693. EDF: schedulable iff U ≤ 1. (a) U = 1/4 + 2/6 + 1/10 = 0.250 + 0.333 + 0.100 = 0.683 ≤ 0.780 → RM guaranteed; EDF also schedulable. (b) U = 2/5 + 4/7 = 0.400 + 0.571 = 0.971 > 0.828 → RM not guaranteed — draw the timeline (RM actually misses at t = 7; see the next entry); EDF schedulable (U ≤", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-53 RM and EDF timelines, missed deadlines", "u": "answers.html#X5-53", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Soft real-time: P1 (t = 2, period = deadline = 5) and P2 (t = 4, period = deadline = 7), both released at 0. Draw the RM and EDF schedules for 0–14 and count the missed deadlines. U = 2/5 + 4/7 = 0.971. Releases and deadlines: P1 at 0, 5, 10 (deadlines 5, 10, 15); P2 at 0, 7 (deadlines 7, 14). RM (P1 has higher priority, period 5 < 7): 0 P1 2 P2 5 P1 7 P2 10 P1 12 P2 14. At t = 5, P1 preempts P2 (P2 has 1 unit left); at t = 7, P2's first job is unfinished → misses deadline 7; it keeps running (soft real-time) and finishes at 8; P2's second job runs 8–10 and 12–14 → meets deadline 14. RM: 1 mis", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-54 Algorithm evaluation methods", "u": "answers.html#X5-54", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How are CPU-scheduling algorithms evaluated? Compare deterministic modeling, queueing models, simulation and implementation. First define the criteria and their relative importance, e.g., maximize CPU utilization under the constraint that the maximum response time is 300 ms, or maximize throughput such that turnaround time is (on average) linearly proportional to total execution time. Then evaluate: Method How Advantages Disadvantages Deterministic modeling takes a particular predetermined workload and computes each algorithm's performance (Gantt chart) simple, fast, exact numbers requires exa", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-55 Little's formula calculations", "u": "answers.html#X5-55", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "State Little's formula and its assumptions. (a) n = 14, λ = 7/s: find W. (b) λ = 20/s, W = 0.5 s: find n. (c) n = 6, W = 300 ms: find λ. (d) 120 processes arrive per minute and each waits 250 ms on average: find n. Little's formula: n=λ×W, where n = average queue length, λ = average arrival rate into the queue, W = average waiting time in the queue. It holds in steady state (processes leaving the queue = processes arriving; the arrival rate must be smaller than the service rate) and is valid for any scheduling algorithm and arrival distribution. (a) W = n/λ = 14/7 = 2 s. (b) n = λW = 20 × 0.5 ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X5-TF True / False quick verdicts", "u": "answers.html#X5-TF", "p": "Answer bank", "x": " Statement T/F Why (≤ 12 words) Scheduling when a process switches from running to waiting is non-preemptive. T The process gives up the CPU voluntarily (condition 1). A process moving from waiting to ready can trigger preemption. T Condition 4: it may have a higher priority. The dispatcher decides which process runs next. F The scheduler selects; the dispatcher performs the context switch. CPU utilization and throughput measure the same thing. F Utilization = busy fraction; throughput = completions per unit time. A better scheduling algorithm shortens a process's CPU burst. F It only changes ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-01 Race condition: counter++ / counter-- interleaving", "u": "answers.html#X6-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define a race condition. Using counter++ (producer) and counter-- (consumer) with counter = 5, show at the machine level how the result can be wrong. [6.7, 6.9] A race condition occurs when several processes access and manipulate shared data concurrently and the outcome depends on the particular order in which the accesses take place. counter++ and counter-- are not atomic: each becomes three machine instructions, and the two sequences can be interleaved: S0: producer register1 = counter /* register1 = 5 */ S1: producer register1 = register1 + 1 /* register1 = 6 */ S2: consumer register2 = cou", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-02 All possible final values of counter", "u": "answers.html#X6-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "counter = 5. The producer executes counter++ once and the consumer executes counter-- once, concurrently and without synchronization. Give all possible final values of counter. 4, 5 or 6. 5: the two updates do not overlap (one stores before the other loads), which is the correct result. 4: both load 5; the producer stores 6, then the consumer stores 4, so the increment is lost. 6: both load 5; the consumer stores 4, then the producer stores 6, so the decrement is lost. No other value is possible: each process stores (the value it loaded) ± 1, and it loads either 5 or the other process's result", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-03 Unprotected counter in a loop: lost updates", "u": "answers.html#X6-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In Lab 6 Q3, two threads each execute ++counter 5,000 times (counter = 0). If the pthread_mutex_lock() / pthread_mutex_unlock() calls are removed, is the final value still 10,000? No. The result is non-deterministic: any value from 2 to 10,000 is possible (10,000 only if no update is lost). ++counter is load, add, store; if the other thread stores in between, this thread's store overwrites (loses) the other's increments: a race condition. Worst case 2: T1 loads 0 and is preempted; T2 does 4,999 increments; T1 stores 1; T2 loads 1 and is preempted; T1 does its other 4,999 increments; T2 stores ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-04 Race condition inside the kernel", "u": "answers.html#X6-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give an example of a race condition in the kernel. Which kernel data structures must be protected? [6.8, 6.12] Processes P0 and P1 call fork() at the same time. Both read the kernel variable next_available_pid before either one updates it, so the same pid could be assigned to two different processes, unless updates of the variable are mutually exclusive. Other kernel data structures subject to race conditions: the list of open files (two processes open files simultaneously), structures for memory allocation, the process list / process table, and interrupt handling. How the kernel protects them", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-05 Critical section; entry, exit, remainder sections", "u": "answers.html#X6-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is a critical section? Describe the general structure of a process in the critical-section problem. [6.9–6.10] A critical section is a segment of code in which a process accesses or updates shared data (changing shared variables, updating a table, writing a file), so a race condition may occur. The critical-section problem is to design a protocol such that when one process is executing in its critical section, no other process is executing in its critical section. do { entry section /* request permission to enter */ critical section exit section /* give up permission, let others in */ rem", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-06 Three requirements with violation examples", "u": "answers.html#X6-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "State the three requirements a solution to the critical-section problem must satisfy, and give one way each can be violated. [6.11] Mutual exclusion: if process Pi​ is executing in its critical section, no other process can be executing in its critical section. Violated by: counter++ without a lock, or a lock whose test and set are not atomic. Progress: if no process is in its critical section and some processes wish to enter, the selection of the process that enters next cannot be postponed indefinitely (a process in its remainder section must not block the others). Violated by: strict altern", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-07 Preemptive vs non-preemptive kernels", "u": "answers.html#X6-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Compare preemptive and non-preemptive kernels with respect to race conditions. Why might a preemptive kernel still be preferred? [6.12] Preemptive kernel: allows a process to be preempted while it is running in kernel mode, so it is not free from race conditions on kernel data, and it is increasingly difficult to design on SMP architectures. Non-preemptive kernel: a kernel-mode process runs until it exits kernel mode, blocks, or voluntarily yields the CPU, so on a single processor only one process is active in the kernel at a time: it is essentially free of race conditions in kernel mode (on a", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-08 Disabling interrupts: uniprocessor vs multiprocessor", "u": "answers.html#X6-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Can the critical-section problem be solved by disabling interrupts? Answer for uniprocessor and multiprocessor systems. [6.13] Uniprocessor: yes. With interrupts disabled while the shared data is modified, the current code executes without being preempted or interrupted, so its instructions run in order without interleaving. Multiprocessor / SMP: no, it is generally inefficient. Disabling interrupts on one CPU does not stop the other CPUs from entering their critical sections; disabling them on all CPUs requires a message to every processor, which is time-consuming, delays entry into each crit", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-09 Peterson's solution: code and assumption (Lab 6)", "u": "answers.html#X6-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Write Peterson's solution for two processes Pi​ and Pj​. Explain the shared variables and the assumption it relies on. int turn; /* shared: whose turn it is */ boolean flag[2] = {false, false}; /* shared: flag[i] = Pi wants in */ do { /* process Pi, j = 1 - i */ flag[i] = true; /* I want to enter ... */ turn = j; /* ... but you may go first */ while (flag[j] && turn == j) ; /* busy wait */ /* critical section */ flag[i] = false; /* I have left */ /* remainder section */ } while (true); flag[i] == true means Pi​ is ready (wants) to enter its critical section; turn decides which process enters w", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-10 Peterson satisfies all three requirements", "u": "answers.html#X6-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Prove that Peterson's solution satisfies mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting. Mutual exclusion: Pi​ enters only if flag[j] == false or turn == i. If both try, flag[0] == flag[1] == true, and turn is either 0 or 1, not both, so only one process can pass its while loop (the one that wrote turn last waits). Progress: Pi​ is stuck only while flag[j] == true && turn == j. If Pj​ does not want to enter, flag[j] == false and Pi​ enters immediately; if both want to enter, turn admits one of them at once. Bounded waiting: when Pj​ leaves, it sets flag[j] = false; if it wants to re-enter, it ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-11 Peterson on modern hardware: memory barriers", "u": "answers.html#X6-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why is Peterson's solution not guaranteed to work on modern computer architectures? How can it be fixed, and what are its other drawbacks? To improve performance, processors and/or compilers may reorder read and write operations that have no dependencies. Peterson's entry section writes flag[i] and turn and then reads flag[j], all different variables. If the two writes are reordered, or Pi​'s read happens before its own write becomes visible to Pj​, both processes can be in their critical sections at the same time. Fix: a memory barrier (memory fence), an instruction that forces all earlier lo", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-12 Atomic hardware instructions", "u": "answers.html#X6-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What does \"atomic\" mean? Why are atomic hardware instructions useful for synchronization, and why can't every operation simply be made atomic? [6.14] Atomic = non-interruptible: the instruction (possibly several machine-level steps, e.g., read and write a word) executes as one unit that cannot be interrupted, so no race condition can occur inside it. If two such instructions run simultaneously on different cores, they are executed sequentially in some order (serialization). They are the building blocks of more sophisticated tools (spinlocks, mutex locks, semaphores, atomic variables). The two ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-13 test_and_set(): definition, lock, bounded waiting", "u": "answers.html#X6-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give the definition of test_and_set(), use it to implement mutual exclusion, and state which requirement the solution does not satisfy. [6.15–6.16] boolean test_and_set(boolean *target) { /* executed atomically */ boolean rv = *target; *target = TRUE; return rv; /* returns the OLD value */ } boolean lock = FALSE; /* shared */ do { while (test_and_set(&lock)) ; /* spin: lock was held */ /* critical section */ lock = FALSE; /* remainder section */ } while (true); Mutual exclusion holds: the first caller gets FALSE (lock free) and enters; the same atomic call sets lock = TRUE, so every other call", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-14 compare_and_swap(): definition and lock", "u": "answers.html#X6-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give the definition of compare_and_swap() and use it to implement a lock. Why is the loop condition != 0? [6.17–6.18] int compare_and_swap(int *value, int expected, int new_value) { int temp = *value; /* executed atomically */ if (*value == expected) *value = new_value; return temp; /* always the OLD value */ } int lock = 0; /* 0 = free, 1 = held */ do { while (compare_and_swap(&lock, 0, 1) != 0) ; /* spin */ /* critical section */ lock = 0; /* remainder section */ } while (true); compare_and_swap(&lock, 0, 1) sets lock to 1 only if it is 0 and returns the old value. A return of 0 means the lo", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-15 Bounded-waiting TAS: waiting[], key, lock", "u": "answers.html#X6-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In the algorithm below, explain the roles of waiting[], key and lock, and why the exiting process sets waiting[j] = false instead of lock = false. [6.19] /* shared: boolean waiting[n] (all false), lock = false; local: key, j */ do { waiting[i] = true; key = true; while (waiting[i] && key) key = test_and_set(&lock); waiting[i] = false; /* critical section */ j = (i + 1) % n; while ((j != i) && !waiting[j]) j = (j + 1) % n; if (j == i) lock = false; /* no one is waiting, so release the lock */ else waiting[j] = false; /* unblock process j */ /* remainder section */ } while (true); waiting[i] == ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-16 Bounded-waiting TAS: sketch proof", "u": "answers.html#X6-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give a sketch proof that the bounded-waiting test_and_set() algorithm satisfies mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting. [6.20] Mutual exclusion: Pi​ enters only if waiting[i] == false or key == false. key becomes false only by executing test_and_set(), and only the first process to execute it finds key == false; all others must wait. waiting[i] becomes false only when another process leaves its critical section, and only one waiting[j] is set to false per exit. So at most one process is in its critical section. Progress: a process exiting its critical section either sets lock = false o", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-17 Broken variants of the bounded-waiting algorithm", "u": "answers.html#X6-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In the bounded-waiting TAS algorithm, which requirement fails if (a) the whole exit section is just lock = false;, or (b) the exit scan always starts from index 0 instead of i + 1? (a) Bounded waiting fails (mutual exclusion and progress still hold). The lock is never handed over, so the algorithm becomes the simple test_and_set spinlock: whichever process executes test_and_set first after the release wins, and a process can be overtaken indefinitely. (b) Bounded waiting fails for n ≥ 3 (mutual exclusion and progress still hold). The exiting process always prefers low-index waiters, e.g., P0​ ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-18 Atomic variables: increment() with CAS", "u": "answers.html#X6-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is an atomic variable? Implement increment() on an atomic integer using compare_and_swap(), and explain why a loop is needed. [6.21] An atomic variable provides atomic (uninterruptible) updates on basic data types such as integers and Booleans; e.g., increment(&sequence) increments sequence without interruption. It is built on compare_and_swap: void increment(atomic_int *v) { int temp; do { temp = *v; /* read the current value */ } while (temp != compare_and_swap(v, temp, temp + 1)); } CAS writes temp + 1 only if *v still equals temp, i.e., no other thread changed v since it was read. Bec", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-19 Mutex lock: why acquire() must be atomic", "u": "answers.html#X6-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give the mutex-lock acquire() / release() code. Why must acquire() be atomic? Show what can go wrong otherwise. [6.22–6.23] acquire() { release() { while (!available) available = true; ; /* busy wait */ } available = false; } do { acquire lock; critical section; release lock; remainder section; } while (true); A process acquires the lock before entering its critical section and releases it when it exits; the Boolean available indicates whether the lock is free. If acquire() were not atomic, its test and set could interleave: T1 sees available == true, T2 also sees available == true, both set i", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-20 Spinlocks: when to use, not on uniprocessors", "u": "answers.html#X6-20", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "When are spinlocks preferable to blocking (sleeping) locks? Why is a spinlock a poor choice on a single-processor system? [6.22] Spinlocks are preferable on multiprocessor (multicore) systems when locks are held for short times: a waiting thread spins on one processor while the lock holder runs its short critical section on another processor and releases the lock soon. No context switch is required, and a context switch may take considerable time (rule of thumb: spin if the lock is held for less than the time of two context switches). On a single processor, the spinning thread occupies the onl", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-21 Semaphore: definition, wait/signal, P and V", "u": "answers.html#X6-21", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Define a semaphore and its two operations (busy-waiting definition). What do P and V stand for? [6.24] A semaphore S is an integer variable (a generalized lock, introduced by Dijkstra, and the main synchronization primitive of the original UNIX) that, apart from initialization, is accessed only through two standard atomic operations: wait() and signal(), originally P() (proberen, \"to test\") and V() (verhogen, \"to increment\"). wait(S) { signal(S) { while (S <= 0) S++; ; /* busy wait */ } S--; } wait(S): while S ≤ 0 the process waits; then it decrements S (takes a permit). signal(S): increments ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-22 Why wait() and signal() must be atomic", "u": "answers.html#X6-22", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why must wait() and signal() be executed atomically? How is this guaranteed on uniprocessor and multiprocessor systems? Otherwise two processes could both test S > 0 (e.g., S = 1) before either decrements it, so both pass wait() and enter the critical section (mutual exclusion violated); S can even end at −1, or an update of S can be lost. Hence no two processes may execute wait()/signal() on the same semaphore at the same time; this is itself a critical-section problem. Uniprocessor: disable interrupts while wait() and signal() execute, so their instructions cannot be interleaved. Multiproces", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-23 Counting vs binary semaphore; semaphore vs mutex", "u": "answers.html#X6-23", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Distinguish counting and binary semaphores, and explain how a semaphore differs from a mutex lock. [6.25] Counting semaphore: its integer value ranges over an unrestricted domain. It controls access to a resource with a finite number of instances and is initialized to the number of resources available (wait() takes an instance, signal() returns one). Binary semaphore: its value is only 0 or 1. Initialized to 1, it behaves like a mutex lock, but it can also be used in other ways, e.g., initialized to 0 to enforce an execution order (S1 before S2). Semaphore vs mutex lock: a mutex lock has a bin", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-24 Mutual exclusion with a semaphore: initial value", "u": "answers.html#X6-24", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Show how a semaphore provides mutual exclusion among n processes. What happens if it is initialized to 0 or to 2 instead of 1? semaphore mutex = 1; /* shared */ do { wait(mutex); /* critical section */ signal(mutex); /* remainder section */ } while (true); Initialized to 1: the first wait() takes the only permit (mutex = 0) and all others block until signal(mutex), so at most one process is in its critical section. Initialized to 0: there is no permit, so no process can ever enter; all of them block forever (deadlock). Initialized to 2: two processes can be in their critical sections at the sa", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-25 Ordering S1 before S2 (synch = 0)", "u": "answers.html#X6-25", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Processes P1 and P2 run concurrently. Use a semaphore to guarantee that statement S1 (in P1) executes before statement S2 (in P2). Why must it be initialized to 0? [6.25] semaphore synch = 0; P1: S1; signal(synch); P2: wait(synch); S2; If P2 reaches wait(synch) first, synch is 0, so P2 blocks until P1 finishes S1 and calls signal(synch). If P1 runs first, synch becomes 1 and P2 passes wait() immediately, but S1 is already done. Either way S2 starts only after S1 completes. If synch were initialized to 1, P2 could pass wait() before S1 runs, so the order would not be enforced. General rule: \"X ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-26 Counting semaphore: at most k users", "u": "answers.html#X6-26", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A server has 3 identical database connections, so at most 3 threads may use them at the same time. Write the synchronization with a semaphore. What happens if 5 threads request a connection together? semaphore conn = 3; /* number of available instances */ wait(conn); /* take a connection (block if none left) */ /* use the connection */ signal(conn); /* return it, waking one waiter if any */ A counting semaphore initialized to k (the number of instances) lets at most k threads pass wait() until someone calls signal(). With 5 requests, 3 proceed and 2 block; in the waiting-queue implementation c", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-27 Rendezvous and barrier with semaphores", "u": "answers.html#X6-27", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Threads A and B: a1 must execute before b2, and b1 before a2 (a rendezvous). Give a semaphore solution, then extend it to a barrier for n threads. semaphore aArrived = 0, bArrived = 0; A: a1; signal(aArrived); wait(bArrived); a2; B: b1; signal(bArrived); wait(aArrived); b2; Each thread signals its own arrival first, then waits for the other, so neither second statement starts before both first statements finish. If both wait before signalling, each waits for a signal that is never sent: deadlock. Barrier for n threads (no thread passes until all n have arrived): int count = 0; semaphore mutex ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-28 Semaphore without busy waiting: block() and wakeup()", "u": "answers.html#X6-28", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "How can a semaphore be implemented without busy waiting? Give the code and explain block() and wakeup(). [6.26–6.27] Each semaphore has an integer value and a waiting queue (a list of processes): typedef struct { int value; struct process *list; } semaphore; wait(semaphore *S) { S->value--; if (S->value < 0) { add this process to S->list; block(); } } signal(semaphore *S) { S->value++; if (S->value <= 0) { remove a process P from S->list; wakeup(P); } } block() suspends the calling process and places it on the semaphore's waiting queue (running → waiting) instead of spinning, so no CPU time is", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-29 Tracing a semaphore value with a waiting queue", "u": "answers.html#X6-29", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "A semaphore S (waiting-queue implementation) is initialized to 2. Five processes call wait(S), and then another process calls signal(S) twice. What is the value of S, and how many processes are still blocked? S = −1, and one process is still blocked. Five waits: 2−5=−3, so the first two processes pass and three are blocked in the queue (∣−3∣=3). Two signals: −3+2=−1; each signal finds the value ≤ 0 and wakes one blocked process (moves it to the ready queue), so one remains blocked (∣−1∣=1). Rule: S ≥ 0 means S permits are free and nobody waits; S < 0 means no permit is free and ∣S∣ processes a", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-30 Deadlock with two semaphores", "u": "answers.html#X6-30", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "S and Q are semaphores initialized to 1. Show how P0 and P1 below can deadlock, and how to prevent it. [6.28] P0: wait(S); wait(Q); ... signal(S); signal(Q); P1: wait(Q); wait(S); ... signal(Q); signal(S); Deadlock: two or more processes are waiting indefinitely for an event that can be caused only by one of the waiting processes. P0 executes wait(S) and then P1 executes wait(Q). When P0 executes wait(Q), it must wait until P1 executes signal(Q); but P1 is waiting in wait(S) until P0 executes signal(S). Since these signal() operations can never be executed, P0 and P1 are deadlocked. It happens", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-31 Starvation (indefinite blocking) vs deadlock", "u": "answers.html#X6-31", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What is starvation in the context of semaphores, and how does it differ from deadlock? [6.28] Starvation (indefinite blocking): a process may never be removed from the semaphore queue in which it is suspended, e.g., if processes are removed from the queue in LIFO (last-in, first-out) order or by priority, newer or higher-priority processes can always be chosen ahead of it. Remedy: remove waiting processes in FIFO order, so every waiting process is eventually woken. Deadlock: a set of processes all wait for each other and none can proceed. Starvation: the system keeps running and other processe", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-32 Verdict table for classic CS protocols", "u": "answers.html#X6-32", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "For each entry/exit protocol (two processes with j = 1 − i, unless noted), state whether mutual exclusion (ME), progress and bounded waiting (BW) hold. Protocol (entry … exit) ME Progress BW Reason while (turn != i); … turn = j; Yes No Yes¹ strict alternation: a process in its remainder section blocks the other while (flag[j]); flag[i] = true; … flag[i] = false; No Yes No both test before either sets its flag flag[i] = true; while (flag[j]); … flag[i] = false; Yes No Yes¹ both set their flags, then both wait forever (deadlock) Peterson: flag[i] = true; turn = j; while (flag[j] && turn == j); …", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-33 Strict alternation using only turn", "u": "answers.html#X6-33", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Shared int turn = 0; Process Pi​ (j = 1 − i): do { while (turn != i); /* CS */ turn = j; /* remainder */ } while (true); Does this solve the critical-section problem? No. It satisfies mutual exclusion but violates progress. Mutual exclusion: yes. Pi​ enters only when turn == i, and turn has a single value, so only one process can be in its critical section. Progress: no. It forces strict alternation: if turn == 1 and P1​ stays in its remainder section (does not want to enter), P0​ cannot enter although the critical section is free; the decision is postponed indefinitely by a process that is no", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-34 Flag checked before it is set", "u": "answers.html#X6-34", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Shared boolean flag[2] = {false, false}; Process Pi​: while (flag[j]); flag[i] = true; /* CS */ flag[i] = false; Which requirements hold? Mutual exclusion is violated, so it is not a solution. Interleaving: P0​ tests flag[1] (false) and passes; before P0​ sets flag[0], P1​ tests flag[0] (false) and passes; both set their flags and both enter the critical section. The test and the set are not one atomic step. Progress holds: a process waits only while the other's flag is up, i.e., while the other is in its critical section, and the flag is cleared on exit. Bounded waiting fails: Pi​ raises its ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-35 Flag set before checking: deadlock", "u": "answers.html#X6-35", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Shared boolean flag[2] = {false, false}; Process Pi​: flag[i] = true; while (flag[j]); /* CS */ flag[i] = false; Which requirements hold? Mutual exclusion holds, but progress is violated: the processes can deadlock. Mutual exclusion: each process raises its flag before testing the other's, so if both are interested, the one that raises its flag later sees the other's flag and waits; they cannot both enter. Progress: no. P0​ sets flag[0] = true, then P1​ sets flag[1] = true; now each waits for the other's flag to become false, forever (deadlock), although the critical section is empty. Bounded ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-36 Peterson variants that break mutual exclusion", "u": "answers.html#X6-36", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In Peterson's solution, what happens if (a) flag[i] = true; and turn = j; are swapped, or (b) a process sets turn = i; instead of turn = j;? Both versions violate mutual exclusion (progress still holds; bounded waiting is lost). (a) turn = j; flag[i] = true;: P0​ sets turn = 1; P1​ sets turn = 0, sets flag[1] = true, finds flag[0] == false and enters; P0​ sets flag[0] = true, finds turn == 0 (not 1) and enters. Both are in their critical sections. This is exactly what instruction reordering can do to the correct code, hence memory barriers. (b) turn = i: P0​ sets flag[0] = true; turn = 0;, fin", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-37 Semaphores for a precedence graph", "u": "answers.html#X6-37", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Six concurrent processes P1–P6 execute statements S1–S6 with precedence S1→S2, S1→S3, S2→S4, S3→S4, S3→S5, S4→S6, S5→S6. Enforce it with semaphores and give their initial values. Rule: for each edge X → Y use a semaphore initialized to 0; X calls signal() after it finishes, and Y calls wait() before it starts. Compactly, use one semaphore per statement that has predecessors, waited on once per incoming edge: semaphore s2 = 0, s3 = 0, s4 = 0, s5 = 0, s6 = 0; P1: S1; signal(s2); signal(s3); P2: wait(s2); S2; signal(s4); P3: wait(s3); S3; signal(s4); signal(s5); P4: wait(s4); wait(s4); S4; signal", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-38 Strict alternation of threads with semaphores", "u": "answers.html#X6-38", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Thread A prints \"ping\" and thread B prints \"pong\" in infinite loops. Make the output strictly alternate, starting with ping. Generalize to k threads in a fixed cyclic order. semaphore ping = 1, pong = 0; A: while (true) { wait(ping); print(\"ping\"); signal(pong); } B: while (true) { wait(pong); print(\"pong\"); signal(ping); } Exactly one permit circulates: ping = 1 lets A go first; each thread waits on its own semaphore and signals the next thread's, so the output is always ping pong ping pong …. k threads T1 → T2 → … → Tk → T1: one semaphore per thread, the first initialized to 1 and all others", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-39 Fill in the blanks: TAS, CAS, increment", "u": "answers.html#X6-39", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Fill in the blanks (lock is initially false / 0): /* (a) */ while (__1__) ; /* critical section */ __2__; /* (b) */ while (compare_and_swap(&lock, __3__, __4__) __5__ 0) ; /* critical section */ __6__; /* (c) */ void increment(atomic_int *v) { int temp; do { temp = __7__; } while (temp != __8__); } 1 test_and_set(&lock) · 2 lock = false 3 0 · 4 1 · 5 != · 6 lock = 0 7 *v · 8 compare_and_swap(v, temp, temp + 1) Reason: both instructions return the old value. A process has the lock exactly when the old value was free (false / 0), so it spins while the returned value says \"held\"; release is an or", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-40 Spinlock from a new instruction: fetch_and_add", "u": "answers.html#X6-40", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "fetch_and_add(int *x, int a) atomically adds a to *x and returns the old value of *x. Use it to build a spinlock that also satisfies bounded waiting. A ticket lock: each process takes a ticket and waits until its number is served. int next_ticket = 0, now_serving = 0; /* shared */ void acquire() { int my = fetch_and_add(&next_ticket, 1); /* unique ticket */ while (now_serving != my) ; /* spin */ } void release() { fetch_and_add(&now_serving, 1); /* serve the next ticket */ } Mutual exclusion: fetch_and_add() is atomic, so tickets are unique, and only the process whose ticket equals now_serving", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-41 Possible values of c: worked example", "u": "answers.html#X6-41", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Shared int a = 3, b = 5, c = 0; Thread 1: if (a > 0) c = a + b; else c = b - a; Thread 2: a = -1; b = 10; Reads and writes are atomic, each thread's order is preserved, and expressions read their operands left to right. Give all possible final values of c. c ∈ {4, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13}. Method: split Thread 1 into atomic steps (read a for the condition, then the branch's reads in order, then write c). Thread 2's writes keep their order (a = -1 before b = 10) but may fall into any gap. Branch on what each read sees: Branch Where Thread 2's writes fall Thread 1 reads c then (condition saw a = 3) both", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-42 Can both threads read 0?", "u": "answers.html#X6-42", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Shared x = 0, y = 0; T1: x = 1; r1 = y; T2: y = 1; r2 = x; Which results (r1, r2) are possible? Can both be 0? (0, 1), (1, 0) and (1, 1) are possible; (0, 0) is impossible when each thread's order is preserved. (0, 1): T1 runs entirely before T2. (1, 0): T2 runs entirely before T1. (1, 1): both writes happen before both reads (e.g., x = 1; y = 1; r1 = y; r2 = x;). (0, 0) would need r1 = y before y = 1 and r2 = x before x = 1; with program order this forms the cycle x = 1 → r1 = y → y = 1 → r2 = x → x = 1, which cannot happen. On modern CPUs that reorder a write after a later independent read, ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-43 Possible outputs with two semaphores", "u": "answers.html#X6-43", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Semaphores s = 0, t = 0. P1: print(\"A\"); signal(s); wait(t); print(\"C\"); P2: print(\"B\"); wait(s); signal(t); print(\"D\"); List all possible outputs. ABCD, ABDC, BACD, BADC: four outputs, and the program never deadlocks. Program order: A before C and B before D. P2 passes wait(s) only after P1's signal(s), which follows A, so A comes before D. P1 passes wait(t) only after P2's signal(t), which follows B, so B comes before C. Hence A and B (in either order) both precede C and D (in either order): 2 × 2 = 4 outputs. Method: write the program-order constraints, add a \"signal before the matching wai", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-44 Counting permits: what gets printed", "u": "answers.html#X6-44", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Semaphores A = 2, B = 0. P1: while (true) { wait(A); print(\"x\"); signal(B); } P2: while (true) { wait(B); wait(B); print(\"y\"); signal(A); } What is printed? Always exactly xxyx; then both processes block forever (deadlock). A's 2 initial permits let P1 print x x (A = 0, B = 2). P2 needs two B permits per y: it prints y (B = 0) and returns one A permit, so P1 prints one more x (A = 0, B = 1). Now P2 takes the single B permit and blocks on its second wait(B), while P1 blocks on wait(A); nobody can signal, so the processes are deadlocked. In total, 3 x's and 1 y are printed. Method: count permits", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-45 Misusing wait() and signal() around a CS", "u": "answers.html#X6-45", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "mutex is a semaphore initialized to 1. What goes wrong if a process (a) swaps the calls: signal(mutex); CS; wait(mutex); (b) writes wait(mutex); CS; wait(mutex); (c) omits wait(mutex), (d) omits signal(mutex), or (e) calls signal(mutex) twice? (a) Mutual exclusion violated: signal() first raises mutex to 2, so several processes may be executing in their critical sections simultaneously. (b) Deadlock: the process permanently blocks on the second wait() (mutex is 0), and every other process then blocks on its first wait(). (c) Mutual exclusion violated: the process enters its critical section wi", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-46 Bounded buffer: wrong order of wait()", "u": "answers.html#X6-46", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "In the bounded-buffer producer, what happens if wait(empty); wait(mutex); is changed to wait(mutex); wait(empty);? Is swapping signal(mutex); signal(full); harmful too? Deadlock is possible. When the buffer is full (empty = 0), the producer acquires mutex and then blocks on wait(empty) while holding mutex; the consumer then blocks on wait(mutex), so it can never remove an item and call signal(empty). Both wait forever. (Symmetrically, a consumer doing wait(mutex); wait(full); on an empty buffer deadlocks.) Rule: wait on the counting semaphore (empty / full) first, then on mutex; never block wh", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-47 Bounded-buffer solution (Ch7 preview / Lab 6)", "u": "answers.html#X6-47", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give a semaphore solution to the bounded-buffer (producer–consumer) problem with n buffers, and justify each initial value. semaphore mutex = 1; /* mutual exclusion for the buffer pool */ semaphore empty = n; /* number of empty buffers */ semaphore full = 0; /* number of full buffers (items) */ /* producer */ while (true) { /* produce an item in next_produced */ wait(empty); wait(mutex); /* add next_produced to the buffer */ signal(mutex); signal(full); } /* consumer */ while (true) { wait(full); wait(mutex); /* remove an item from the buffer to next_consumed */ signal(mutex); signal(empty); /", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-48 First readers–writers solution (Ch7 preview / Lab 6)", "u": "answers.html#X6-48", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give the semaphore solution to the first readers–writers problem. Why can writers starve? If a writer is writing and n readers arrive, where do the readers wait? semaphore rw_mutex = 1; /* used by writers and the first/last reader */ semaphore mutex = 1; /* protects read_count */ int read_count = 0; /* readers reading or waiting to read */ /* writer */ wait(rw_mutex); /* writing is performed */ signal(rw_mutex); /* reader */ wait(mutex); read_count++; if (read_count == 1) wait(rw_mutex); /* first reader locks out writers */ signal(mutex); /* reading is performed */ wait(mutex); read_count--; i", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-49 Dining philosophers: deadlock and remedies (Ch7 preview)", "u": "answers.html#X6-49", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Each chopstick is a semaphore initialized to 1, and philosopher i executes wait(chopstick[i]); wait(chopstick[(i + 1) % 5]); eat; signal(chopstick[i]); signal(chopstick[(i + 1) % 5]);. How can this deadlock, and how can deadlock be prevented? Deadlock: if all five philosophers become hungry at the same time and each picks up its left chopstick, all chopsticks are taken and each waits forever for its right chopstick. Remedies: (1) allow at most four philosophers at the table at the same time; (2) let a philosopher pick up the chopsticks only if both are available (picking them up in a critical ", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-50 POSIX condition variables (Lab 6)", "u": "answers.html#X6-50", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Why does pthread_cond_wait() take a mutex as its second argument, and why is it called inside a while loop? /* waiting thread */ /* signalling thread */ pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); while (!ready) ready = 1; pthread_cond_wait(&cond, &mutex); pthread_cond_signal(&cond); pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); A condition variable is always used with a mutex that protects the condition (ready), and the mutex must be held when calling wait or signal. pthread_cond_wait() atomically releases the mutex and puts the caller to sleep; otherwise no other th", "w": 2}, {"t": "X6-TF True / False quick verdicts", "u": "answers.html#X6-TF", "p": "Answer bank", "x": " Statement T/F Why (≤ 12 words) A race condition requires two processes to access shared data at exactly the same instant. F Concurrent (interleaved) access is enough; not necessarily simultaneous. counter++ can compile into several machine instructions that may be interleaved. T Load, add, store; another thread can run in between. Progress allows a process in its remainder section to block others from entering. F Only processes wishing to enter take part in the decision. Bounded waiting bounds how many times others may enter before a waiting process. T Definition of bounded waiting [6.11]. A ", "w": 2}, {"t": "XI-01 read() system call: user → kernel → DMA → interrupt → return", "u": "answers.html#XI-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe what happens, step by step, from the moment a process calls read(fd, buf, n) on a disk file until it gets the data. The program calls the C library wrapper read() (API). The wrapper puts the system-call number and parameters in registers and executes a trap (software interrupt) → mode bit set to kernel mode. The kernel's system-call handler looks up the system-call table, checks the parameters and calls the file system / device driver. If the block is not already in memory, the driver programs the disk controller for a DMA transfer (source, destination address in memory, count). The p", "w": 2}, {"t": "XI-02 Timer interrupt ends a time quantum (RR)", "u": "answers.html#XI-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What happens when the time quantum of the running process expires under round-robin scheduling? The timer (loaded by the OS with a privileged instruction) counts down and raises a timer interrupt at the end of the quantum. The CPU switches to kernel mode and jumps to the timer interrupt handler. The OS saves the running process's context (PC, registers, state) in its PCB, changes its state running → ready and puts it at the tail of the ready queue. The short-term (CPU) scheduler selects the next process (RR: the head of the ready queue). The dispatcher restores that process's context from its ", "w": 2}, {"t": "XI-03 How a shell runs `ls -l > out.txt` and `ls | wc -l`", "u": "answers.html#XI-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain which system calls a shell uses to execute ls -l > out.txt and ls | wc -l. ls -l > out.txt: the shell parses the line → fork() → in the child: fd = open(\"out.txt\", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644), dup2(fd, 1) (stdout now refers to the file), close(fd), execvp(\"ls\", argv) (the redirection survives exec) → the parent (shell) calls waitpid() and then prints the next prompt. The shell's own stdout is untouched because only the child redirected. ls | wc -l: the shell calls pipe(fd) before forking; child 1 does dup2(fd[1], 1) and execs ls; child 2 does dup2(fd[0], 0) and execs wc -l. Eve", "w": 2}, {"t": "XI-04 Life cycle of a process through all states", "u": "answers.html#XI-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Describe the life cycle of a process using the process states, naming the event that causes each transition. new: created by fork(); the OS allocates a PCB and memory. new → ready: admitted (long-term scheduler), placed in the ready queue. ready → running: dispatched by the short-term scheduler. running → ready: interrupt / time quantum expires / preempted by a higher-priority process. running → waiting: requests I/O or waits for an event (e.g. wait(), read() on an empty pipe). waiting → ready: I/O completion (interrupt) or the event occurs. A process never goes waiting → running directly. run", "w": 2}, {"t": "XI-05 From source file to running process (compiler, linker, loader, exec)", "u": "answers.html#XI-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Explain how a C source file prog.c becomes a running process. Compiler: gcc -c prog.c → relocatable object file prog.o (machine code with unresolved addresses/symbols). Linker: combines object files and libraries into one executable (e.g. ELF); static libraries are copied in, dynamically linked libraries (DLLs / shared libraries) are only referenced and linked at load or run time (saves memory and disk; one shared copy). Launch: typing ./prog makes the shell fork() a child that calls exec(). The loader brings the executable into memory (text, data, heap, stack), relocates addresses and loads n", "w": 2}, {"t": "XI-06 What happens when you press Ctrl-C", "u": "answers.html#XI-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "What happens when the user presses Ctrl-C while a program runs in the shell, and how can a program change this? The terminal driver sends the signal SIGINT (signal number 2) to the foreground process. A signal is a software notification of an event; it is asynchronous (generated outside the receiving process). Default action for SIGINT: terminate the process. A program can install its own signal handler with signal(SIGINT, handler) (PA1 prints a message and exits), or ignore it with signal(SIGINT, SIG_IGN). SIGKILL (9) and SIGSTOP cannot be caught or ignored. In a multithreaded process the sig", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-01 fork + wait + exit status", "u": "answers.html#XC-01", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Write a program in which the child prints its own PID and its parent's PID, and the parent waits for the child and prints the child's exit status. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid < 0) { /* fork failed */ perror(\"fork\"); exit(1); } else if (pid == 0) { /* child: fork() returned 0 */ printf(\"child: pid=%d parent=%d\\n\", getpid(), getppid()); exit(7); /* status passed to the parent */ } else { /* parent: fork() returned the child's PID */ int status; waitpid(pid, &status, 0); /* or wait(&status) */ if (WI", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-02 N children (fan) + reap all", "u": "answers.html#XC-02", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "The parent creates N child processes; child i prints i and terminates; the parent waits for all children before printing \"done\". #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> #define N 4 int main(void) { for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) { pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { /* child i */ printf(\"child %d: pid=%d\\n\", i, getpid()); exit(0); /* MUST exit: otherwise the child continues the loop and forks too */ } } while (wait(NULL) > 0) /* reap every child (no zombies); wait returns -1 when none left */ ; printf(\"parent: done\\n\"); return 0; } Exactly N + 1 processes.", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-03 Chain of processes", "u": "answers.html#XC-03", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Create a chain P0 → P1 → … → PN in which each process creates exactly one child and waits for it. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> #define N 3 int main(void) { for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) { pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid > 0) { /* parent: wait for its only child, then stop forking */ wait(NULL); break; } /* child: continue the loop and create the next process */ } printf(\"pid=%d parent=%d\\n\", getpid(), getppid()); return 0; } N + 1 processes; the deepest prints first, each parent prints after its child ends (reverse order of creation). ", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-04 fork + execvp a command line", "u": "answers.html#XC-04", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "The parent reads a command line (e.g. \"ls -l -a\"), runs it in a child with execvp, and waits for it. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <string.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { char line[] = \"ls -l -a\"; char *argv[9]; /* at most 8 arguments + NULL terminator */ int argc = 0; for (char *tok = strtok(line, \" \"); tok != NULL && argc < 8; tok = strtok(NULL, \" \")) argv[argc++] = tok; argv[argc] = NULL; /* execvp needs a NULL-terminated array */ pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { execvp(argv[0], argv); /* replaces the child's image; returns only on error */", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-05 Pipe: parent sends a string to child", "u": "answers.html#XC-05", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Use an ordinary pipe so that the parent sends the string \"Greetings\" to the child, which prints it. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <string.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { int fd[2]; /* fd[0] = read end, fd[1] = write end */ char buf[100]; if (pipe(fd) == -1) { perror(\"pipe\"); exit(1); } /* create BEFORE fork so both share it */ pid_t pid = fork(); if (pid == 0) { /* child = reader */ close(fd[1]); /* close unused write end */ ssize_t n = read(fd[0], buf, sizeof(buf) - 1); buf[n > 0 ? n : 0] = '\\0'; printf(\"child read: %s\\n\", buf); close(fd[0]); ex", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-06 Pipe: child computes, parent receives", "u": "answers.html#XC-06", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "The child computes 1 + 2 + … + 100 and sends the result to the parent through a pipe; the parent prints it. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { int fd[2]; pipe(fd); if (fork() == 0) { /* child: writer */ close(fd[0]); int sum = 0; for (int i = 1; i <= 100; i++) sum += i; write(fd[1], &sum, sizeof(sum)); /* pass the ADDRESS and the SIZE */ close(fd[1]); exit(0); } close(fd[1]); /* parent: reader */ int result; read(fd[0], &result, sizeof(result)); /* blocks until data arrives */ close(fd[0]); wait(NULL); printf(\"sum = %d\\n\", result);", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-07 Two pipes: two-way communication", "u": "answers.html#XC-07", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Parent sends \"Parent\" to the child and the child replies \"Child\", using two ordinary pipes. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <string.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { int p2c[2], c2p[2]; /* parent->child, child->parent */ char buf[20] = {0}; pipe(p2c); pipe(c2p); if (fork() == 0) { /* child */ close(p2c[1]); close(c2p[0]); /* keep: read p2c, write c2p */ read(p2c[0], buf, sizeof(buf) - 1); printf(\"child received: %s\\n\", buf); write(c2p[1], \"Child\", 6); /* 6 bytes include the '\\0' */ close(p2c[0]); close(c2p[1]); exit(0); } close(p2c[0]); close(c2p[1])", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-08 Redirection: cmd < in > out", "u": "answers.html#XC-08", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Implement sort < in.txt > out.txt: run sort in a child with stdin from in.txt and stdout to out.txt. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <fcntl.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { if (fork() == 0) { int in = open(\"in.txt\", O_RDONLY); int out = open(\"out.txt\", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644); /* 0644: rw-r--r-- */ if (in < 0 || out < 0) { perror(\"open\"); exit(1); } dup2(in, STDIN_FILENO); /* fd 0 now refers to in.txt */ dup2(out, STDOUT_FILENO); /* fd 1 now refers to out.txt */ close(in); close(out); /* originals no longer needed */ execlp(\"sort\", \"sort", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-09 Pipeline: ls | wc -l", "u": "answers.html#XC-09", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Implement the shell command ls | wc -l with pipe, fork, dup2 and exec. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> int main(void) { int fd[2]; pipe(fd); if (fork() == 0) { /* child 1: ls writes into the pipe */ dup2(fd[1], STDOUT_FILENO); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(\"ls\", \"ls\", (char *)NULL); exit(1); } if (fork() == 0) { /* child 2: wc reads from the pipe */ dup2(fd[0], STDIN_FILENO); close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); execlp(\"wc\", \"wc\", \"-l\", (char *)NULL); exit(1); } close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]); /* parent MUST close both ends, else wc never sees EOF */ wa", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-10 Multi-level pipe (PA1 Feature 5)", "u": "answers.html#XC-10", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Write run_pipeline(cmds, n) that runs cmd0 | cmd1 | … | cmd(n-1) (n ≤ 8), where cmds[i] is a NULL-terminated argv array. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> void run_pipeline(char **cmds[], int n) { int fds[8][2]; /* n-1 pipes */ for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) pipe(fds[i]); for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { if (fork() == 0) { if (i > 0) dup2(fds[i - 1][0], STDIN_FILENO); /* read the previous pipe */ if (i < n - 1) dup2(fds[i][1], STDOUT_FILENO); /* write the next pipe */ for (int k = 0; k < n - 1; k++) { close(fds[k][0]); close(fds[k][1]); } execvp(cmd", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-11 Mini shell loop: built-in cd, exit, Ctrl-C", "u": "answers.html#XC-11", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Sketch the main loop of a simple shell (PA1): prompt, read a line, handle exit and cd as built-ins, run other commands in a child, and print a message on Ctrl-C. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <string.h> #include <signal.h> #include <unistd.h> #include <sys/wait.h> void on_sigint(int sig) { /* SIGINT = 2 */ printf(\"\\nshell (pid=%d) terminated by Ctrl-C, signal %d\\n\", getpid(), sig); exit(0); } int main(void) { char line[1024], *argv[9]; signal(SIGINT, on_sigint); /* register the handler */ while (1) { printf(\"$$ 3511> \"); fflush(stdout); if (fgets(line, sizeof(line), stdin) ==", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-12 Named pipe (FIFO) writer / reader", "u": "answers.html#XC-12", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Two unrelated processes communicate through a named pipe \"/tmp/myfifo\": the writer sends a string, the reader prints it. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <string.h> #include <fcntl.h> #include <sys/stat.h> #include <sys/wait.h> #include <unistd.h> #define FIFO \"/tmp/myfifo\" void writer(void) { /* program 1 */ int fd = open(FIFO, O_WRONLY); /* blocks until a reader opens the FIFO */ const char *msg = \"hello via FIFO\"; write(fd, msg, strlen(msg) + 1); close(fd); } void reader(void) { /* program 2 */ char buf[100]; int fd = open(FIFO, O_RDONLY); /* blocks until a writer opens the F", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-13 Pthreads: partial sums with N threads", "u": "answers.html#XC-13", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Use N threads to sum an array: thread i sums its own slice, and main joins all threads and adds the partial sums. #include <stdio.h> #include <pthread.h> #define N 4 #define SIZE 1000 int a[SIZE]; long partial[N]; /* one slot per thread → no shared write → no race */ void *worker(void *arg) { int id = *(int *)arg; long s = 0; for (int i = id * (SIZE / N); i < (id + 1) * (SIZE / N); i++) s += a[i]; partial[id] = s; return NULL; /* or pthread_exit(NULL) */ } int main(void) { pthread_t tid[N]; int ids[N]; for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) a[i] = i + 1; for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) { ids[i] = i; /* pa", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-14 Thread returns a value to main", "u": "answers.html#XC-14", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Create a thread that computes the square of a number passed as its argument and returns the result to main through pthread_join. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <pthread.h> void *square(void *arg) { int x = *(int *)arg; int *res = malloc(sizeof(int)); /* heap: survives after the thread ends (not a local!) */ *res = x * x; pthread_exit(res); /* same as: return res; */ } int main(void) { pthread_t t; int x = 7; int *res; pthread_create(&t, NULL, square, &x); pthread_join(t, (void **)&res); /* receives the thread's return value */ printf(\"%d squared = %d\\n\", x, *res); /* 49 */ fre", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-15 Mutex-protected shared counter", "u": "answers.html#XC-15", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Two threads each increment a shared counter 100000 times. Make the final value always 200000. #include <stdio.h> #include <pthread.h> int counter = 0; pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; /* or pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL) */ void *add(void *arg) { for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) { pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); /* entry section */ counter++; /* critical section */ pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock); /* exit section */ } return NULL; } int main(void) { pthread_t t1, t2; pthread_create(&t1, NULL, add, NULL); pthread_create(&t2, NULL, add, NULL); pthread_join(t1, NULL); pthread_join(t2, NU", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-16 Semaphores: force the order T1 → T2 → T3", "u": "answers.html#XC-16", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Three threads each print their name. Use POSIX semaphores so the output is always T1, T2, T3. #include <stdio.h> #include <pthread.h> #include <semaphore.h> sem_t s12, s23; /* both start at 0 = \"not yet allowed\" */ void *t1(void *a) { printf(\"T1\\n\"); sem_post(&s12); return NULL; } void *t2(void *a) { sem_wait(&s12); printf(\"T2\\n\"); sem_post(&s23); return NULL; } void *t3(void *a) { sem_wait(&s23); printf(\"T3\\n\"); return NULL; } int main(void) { pthread_t a, b, c; sem_init(&s12, 0, 0); /* pshared = 0: shared by threads of this process */ sem_init(&s23, 0, 0); pthread_create(&c, NULL, t3, NULL);", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-17 Bounded buffer (producer–consumer) with semaphores", "u": "answers.html#XC-17", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Implement a producer and a consumer sharing a bounded buffer of size 5, using empty/full counting semaphores and a mutex. #include <stdio.h> #include <pthread.h> #include <semaphore.h> #define BUF 5 #define ITEMS 20 int buffer[BUF], in = 0, out = 0; sem_t empty, full; /* empty = free slots (init BUF), full = items (init 0) */ pthread_mutex_t mutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; void *producer(void *arg) { for (int i = 1; i <= ITEMS; i++) { sem_wait(&empty); /* wait for a free slot */ pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex); buffer[in] = i; in = (in + 1) % BUF; pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex); sem_post(&full); /", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-18 First readers–writers (semaphores)", "u": "answers.html#XC-18", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Give a solution where many readers may read together but a writer needs exclusive access (readers have priority). #include <stdio.h> #include <pthread.h> #include <semaphore.h> sem_t rw_mutex; /* init 1: writers' exclusive access / held by the reader group */ sem_t mutex; /* init 1: protects read_count */ int read_count = 0, data = 0; void *writer(void *arg) { sem_wait(&rw_mutex); data++; /* writing */ sem_post(&rw_mutex); return NULL; } void *reader(void *arg) { sem_wait(&mutex); read_count++; if (read_count == 1) sem_wait(&rw_mutex); /* first reader locks out writers */ sem_post(&mutex); pri", "w": 2}, {"t": "XC-19 Zombie and orphan demos", "u": "answers.html#XC-19", "p": "Answer bank", "x": "Write short programs that create (a) a zombie process and (b) an orphan process, and explain what happens. #include <stdio.h> #include <stdlib.h> #include <unistd.h> int main(void) { /* (a) zombie: child exits, parent is alive but has not called wait() */ if (fork() == 0) exit(0); sleep(1); /* during this time the child is a zombie (ps shows <defunct>) */ /* (b) orphan: parent exits first, child is still running */ if (fork() == 0) { sleep(2); printf(\"orphan: my parent is now %d\\n\", getppid()); /* re-parented to init / systemd (or a subreaper) */ exit(0); } return 0; /* parent ends; the zombie", "w": 2}, {"t": "CPU Scheduling Simulator (Gantt chart tool)", "u": "tools.html#scheduler", "p": "Tools", "x": "FCFS SJF SRTF RR priority MLFQ RM EDF dynamic priority Gantt chart simulator waiting turnaround response", "w": 4}, {"t": "Calculators: Amdahl, AMAT, exponential averaging, Little, RM bound, fork", "u": "tools.html#calculators", "p": "Tools", "x": "Amdahl speedup average access time exponential averaging Little's formula utilization rate monotonic bound fork count", "w": 4}];
